{"input": "", "answers": ["The Occupy LA encampment got a stay of execution this morning—though a few arrests have been reported beyond the boundaries of City Hall Park. Two hours after protesters defied city hall's order to vacate the park by 12:01am, lawyer and Occupy supporter Jim Lafferty told Reuters he expected police to give the camp a two-day reprieve. At around 5am, police told protesters over a loudspeaker that they didn't intend to clear the park, the LA Times reports. Police do, however, intend to clear protesters out of the street, and several people who refused to move out of an intersection have already been arrested. \"We definitely need to get morning traffic in,\" a police commander explained. Everything also seems calm at Occupy Philadelphia, where protesters defied a 5pm Sunday deadline to disperse. But as of last night, a police inspector said they didn't intend to forcibly evict anyone. \"We look forward to working with Occupy Philadelphia and a resolution of the problem,\" he told the Philadelphia Inquirer. \"Confrontation is never good.\""], "length": 2099, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Occupy Protests Continue Across the Country**\n\nThe Occupy Wall Street movement, which began two months ago, continues to gain momentum and attention across the United States. In Philadelphia, members of Occupy Philadelphia remained on site at City Hall into the evening of November 28, despite a deadline set by Mayor Nutter for them to leave. The protesters, who had set up a camp on the plaza, were served squash soup and water, and chanted slogans such as \"What does democracy look like? This is what democracy looks like.\" Police looked on calmly, with Chief Inspector Joseph Sullivan stating that the police had no plans to evict anyone and that confrontation was not good.\n\nIn Los Angeles, police in riot gear closed in on anti-Wall Street activists in City Hall Park on Monday morning, but stopped short of clearing the encampment. The protesters, who had defied a midnight deadline to vacate the camp, had taken over a downtown intersection, but were eventually forced to retreat into the park. Four demonstrators were arrested during the brief confrontation, accused of being present at an unlawful assembly. The Los Angeles encampment, which had grown to roughly 400 tents and 700 to 800 people, was one of the largest on the West Coast.\n\nIn contrast to other cities, where police have used force to clear out Occupy camps, the Los Angeles police showed restraint, with Mayor Antonio Villaraigosa complimenting the protesters on Sunday for staying peaceful. However, the mayor also stated that it was time for Occupy LA to move from focusing on a particular patch of park land to spreading the message of economic justice.\n\nIn Philadelphia, the city's 5 p.m. Sunday deadline for Occupy protesters to move from a similar encampment came and went without incident. Dozens of people heeded the order, but many tents and other structures stayed put. Police sources said authorities were hoping the rest of the protesters would relocate voluntarily and that no major actions were expected before Tuesday.\n\nThe Occupy movement, which began as a protest against economic inequality and excesses of the financial system, has gained widespread attention and support across the country. The movement has inspired protests and demonstrations in cities across the United States, and has sparked a national conversation about economic justice and the role of corporations in society.\n\n**Key Developments:**\n\n* Occupy Philadelphia protesters remain on site at City Hall, despite a deadline set by Mayor Nutter for them to leave.\n* Police in Los Angeles closed in on anti-Wall Street activists in City Hall Park on Monday morning, but stopped"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["In our own obituary of AK-47 inventor Mikhail Kalashnikov last month, we included this quote: The rifle was invented \"for the protection of the Motherland,\" he said. \"I have no regrets and bear no responsibility for how politicians have used it.\" It turns out \"no regrets\" might not have exactly been true. The AFP picks up a report from Russia's Izvestia that Kalashnikov penned a letter to the head of the Russian Orthodox Church nine months before his December death in which he wrote of \"unbearable ... spiritual pain\" and asked about his culpability. It contains this line: \"I keep having the same unsolved question: if my rifle took away people's lives, then can it be that I ... am guilty for people's deaths, even if they were enemies?\" AFP notes that Izvestia ran a copy of the letter, which is typed on Kalashnikov's personal stationery and signed \"with a wavering hand.\" A rep for Patriarch Kirill confirms that the letter was sent, and says the church leader wrote a reply—one that very may well have calmed his fears. Says the rep, \"The Church has a very definite position: when weapons serve to protect the Fatherland, the Church supports both its creators and the soldiers who use it.\" But the BBC notes that it's not clear whether every word came from Kalashnikov: Izvestia reports that his daughter believes a priest helped him write the letter."], "length": 1377, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Mikhail Kalashnikov's Legacy: A Life of Guilt and Redemption**\n\nMikhail Kalashnikov, the inventor of the iconic AK-47 assault rifle, passed away in December at the age of 94, leaving behind a complex legacy. In a surprising revelation, it has been reported that Kalashnikov wrote a lengthy emotional letter to the head of the Russian Orthodox Church, Patriarch Kirill, expressing his deep-seated guilt over the countless lives lost due to his invention.\n\nThe letter, written in May 2012, reveals Kalashnikov's spiritual pain and his struggle to come to terms with the devastating impact of his creation. He questioned whether he was responsible for the deaths of those who used his rifle, and wondered why God allowed humans to have \"devilish desires\" of envy, greed, and aggression.\n\nKalashnikov's letter is a stark contrast to his earlier stance, where he refused to accept responsibility for the lives lost due to his rifle. He had previously blamed the policies of other countries that acquired the AK-47 for its misuse. However, in his later years, Kalashnikov seemed to have undergone a change of heart, seeking solace in the Russian Orthodox Church.\n\nThe inventor's journey to faith began at the age of 91, when he first entered a church. He was later baptized, and his letter to Patriarch Kirill reflects his newfound spirituality. The Patriarch's press secretary, Alexander Volkov, confirmed that the Church leader received Kalashnikov's letter and wrote a personal reply, stating that the Church supports those who create weapons to protect their country, but not those who use them for terrorist purposes.\n\nKalashnikov's legacy is a complex one, marked by both pride and guilt. His invention, the AK-47, has become a symbol of armed insurgent movements and has been used by child soldiers. However, it has also been a reliable and effective tool for defending one's country, as Kalashnikov intended.\n\nAs the world reflects on Kalashnikov's life and legacy, it is clear that his story is one of redemption and spiritual growth. Despite his guilt and pain, Kalashnikov found solace in his faith, and his letter to Patriarch Kirill serves as a testament to the power of human spirit and the search for meaning in life.\n\n**Timeline:**\n\n* 1996: Alexa Internet begins donating crawl data to the Internet Archive, which is added to the Wayback Machine after an embargo period.\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Unnamed officials say TSA officer Gerardo Hernandez lay bleeding for 33 minutes at LAX before being taken to an ambulance. For 28 of those minutes, alleged shooter Paul Ciancia was in police custody, though officers hadn't yet declared the area safe to enter. Officers checked on Hernandez, who was just 20 feet from an exit, then moved on while paramedics waited 150 yards away, the AP reports, noting it's not clear if immediate attention might have saved Hernandez's life. Now under review: the conversations took place between police and fire commanders to determine when it was safe enough to enter, and whether paramedics could have gone into the terminal earlier. While an officer who checked on Hernandez about 5 minutes after he was shot told numerous officers he was dead, the AP reports that it's not known whether that officer had the training needed to make that determination; no first-aid was administered. \"I basically think there's a lack of coordination between entities at this airport. That lack of coordination may have led to something that shouldn't have happened,\" an LAX employee says. \"We may be talking about Officer Hernandez as a survivor.\" The results of the investigation could be months away, the AP adds. Ciancia was on Tuesday upgraded from critical to fair condition at UCLA Medical Center."], "length": 1422, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Deadly Shooting at Los Angeles International Airport: Investigation and Aftermath**\n\nOn November 1, 2013, a gunman opened fire at Los Angeles International Airport (LAX), killing one Transportation Security Administration (TSA) officer and wounding three others. The suspect, 23-year-old Paul Ciancia, was shot by airport police officers four times and taken into custody. He remains in fair condition at Ronald Reagan UCLA Medical Center.\n\nThe investigation into the shooting has raised questions about the response of police and fire officials, who waited 33 minutes to enter the terminal and provide medical attention to the victims. TSA Officer Gerardo Hernandez, 39, was shot in the chest and died at Harbor-UCLA Medical Center despite efforts to revive him. An investigation is underway to determine whether a lack of coordination between police and fire officials contributed to the delay in treatment.\n\nAccording to officials, police broadcast over their radios that Ciancia was in custody at 9:25 a.m., five minutes after Hernandez was shot. However, no officers rendered first aid on the scene, and it was not until 33 minutes later that Hernandez was wheeled out of the terminal to an ambulance. Trauma surgeon David Plurad said Hernandez had no signs of life when he arrived at the hospital, and doctors worked for about an hour to revive him despite significant blood loss.\n\nThe incident has sparked concerns about the training of police officers in first aid and CPR. A recent audit by the Los Angeles Police Commission found that the LAPD had a zero percent compliance rate in providing refresher training to officers. However, airport police have received the training.\n\nCiancia is charged with killing Hernandez and wounding three others, including two other TSA officers. The charges carry a potential death sentence. A public memorial service was held on Tuesday to honor Hernandez, the first TSA officer killed in the line of duty.\n\nThe incident has also highlighted the need for improved coordination between police and fire officials in responding to active shooter situations. Los Angeles County Fire Battalion Chief Larry Collins said that firefighters are often held back from entering the scene until police have cleared it, which can take hours. However, in active shooter situations, firefighters may need to enter the scene immediately to provide medical attention to victims."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Under circumstances that sound straight out of a Le Carre novel, Mohamed Morsi has met with an outsider for the first time since being dumped by Egypt's military, holding a midnight meeting with the European Union's top diplomat at an \"undisclosed location\" last night, the Wall Street Journal. Though many have feared for Morsi's well-being after almost a month in captivity, Catherine Ashton, the EU's foreign affairs and security policy chief, reports that he is well and has access to TV and newspapers, Reuters reports. The pair had a \"very long and in-depth conversation,\" Ashton says. Ashton, who has met with several other Egyptian political figures while in the country, says she wasn't there to help Morsi break out, but rather to facilitate discussions toward a peaceful end to the country's political unrest, the Washington Post reports. \"First of all, we are here to help. We are not here to impose,\" she says. \"The people of Egypt will determine their own future.\" But will Morsi play ball? \"He's a very proud man,\" says a source close to the former leader, per the Journal. \"I think the idea of doing anything on (the) army's terms will not work with him.\""], "length": 1480, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Egypt's Ousted President Morsi Meets with EU Diplomat, Amid Ongoing Crisis**\n\nIn a significant development, European Union foreign policy chief Catherine Ashton met with deposed Egyptian President Mohamed Morsi on Monday night, marking the first contact he has had with an independent official since his ouster nearly a month ago. The meeting, which lasted for two hours, was described by Ashton as \"friendly and open\" and took place at an undisclosed location.\n\nAccording to Ashton, Morsi was in good condition and had access to newspapers and television. She also stated that she had informed Morsi's family about his well-being. The meeting was seen as a positive step towards resolving Egypt's ongoing crisis, which has seen repeated violence between security forces and Morsi's supporters.\n\nThe meeting was facilitated by the Egyptian military, which has been accused of human rights abuses and suppressing pro-Morsi demonstrations. The military has also announced that Morsi is under investigation for espionage and murder, allegations that his supporters have dismissed as politically motivated.\n\nAshton's visit to Egypt was part of a broader effort to mediate a resolution to the crisis. She met with a range of Egyptian political figures, including interim Vice President Mohamed ElBaradei, who expressed support for Morsi's Muslim Brotherhood allies being part of the new political \"road map\" going forward.\n\nThe visit appeared to have a calming effect on the capital, with no violence reported on Tuesday night. However, Morsi's supporters rallied in Cairo and marched towards the military intelligence headquarters, although no violence had been reported by early Wednesday.\n\nThe international community has been watching the situation closely, with US Defense Secretary Chuck Hagel speaking by telephone with Egyptian General Abdel Fatah al-Sissi, urging restraint in dealing with protesters and calling for an inclusive reconciliation process.\n\nThe crisis in Egypt continues, with Morsi's supporters demanding his return to power and the restoration of constitutional legitimacy. The Muslim Brotherhood has stated that any political solution must be based on the return of the president, and that demonstrations will continue until this goal is achieved."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A government backed by Russia is accused of killing its own civilians. That applies not only to the current situation in Syria but to the 1999 situation in Kosovo. And as the New York Times reports, top US officials are keeping Kosovo very much in mind as they weigh their options. President Obama would likely never get the approval of the UN Security Council for a military response because of Russia's veto power. When Bill Clinton was in the same boat in 1999, he turned to NATO and got its blessing for 78 days of airstrikes. “It’s a step too far to say we’re drawing up legal justifications for an action, given that the president hasn’t made a decision,” a government official tells the Times. “But Kosovo, of course, is a precedent of something that is perhaps similar.” Obama was meeting today with national security aides to determine next steps if the US concludes that Bashar al-Assad used chemical weapons. (Assad in turn accused the rebels of doing the same today.) Meanwhile, the US Navy is keeping two destroyers positioned in the Mediterranean in case the green light is given for a cruise-missile attack, reports the Wall Street Journal."], "length": 626, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Middle East Crisis: US and Russia Take Different Stances on Syria**\n\nThe situation in Syria remains volatile as the international community grapples with the aftermath of a chemical attack in the country. The US Navy has taken a proactive stance, with a destroyer remaining in the Mediterranean to be in striking distance of Syria in case of a military intervention. The ship, which has Tomahawk cruise missiles on board, is prepared to launch a strike against targets in Syria if the President decides to take action.\n\nMeanwhile, Russia has called on Syrian President Bashar al-Assad to allow United Nations investigators to access the site of the attack, but diplomats say this move does not reflect a shift in Russia's support for Assad or its opposition to punitive measures in the Security Council. Russia's foreign ministry has instead placed the blame on the Syrian opposition forces, suggesting that the attack was a provocation by the rebels.\n\nHowever, US officials have detected activity at locations known to be chemical weapons sites before the attack, which they believe may have been preparations for the assault. This suggests that the Syrian government may have been involved in the attack, contradicting Russia's claims.\n\nThe Syrian government has not commented on the allegations, while the opposition forces have been accused of not cooperating with the investigation by United Nations experts. The situation remains tense, with the international community waiting for further developments.\n\n**Key Developments:**\n\n* US Navy destroyer remains in the Mediterranean to be in striking distance of Syria\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Even though Ellen Pao lost her gender-discrimination suit against a former employer, she's still widely hailed as a hero for bringing the boys' club atmosphere of Silicon Valley under a microscope. But now she's got a tough choice on her hands: The venture-capital firm she sued, Kleiner Perkins Caufield & Byers, says she owes almost $1 million in legal fees—but it will drop its pursuit of the money if she doesn't appeal last month's ruling, reports the Wall Street Journal. Pao's attorneys say they will have a decision in a few weeks. Kleiner Perkins says it offered Pao about $1 million as a settlement before the trial began, but received no response from her legal team. Because of that offer, the company can now go after Pao for expensive witness fees, explains the New York Times. One factor that will surely weigh on the decision: A review of previous cases suggests that Pao has only a slim chance of winning on appeal, reports Reuters."], "length": 1166, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Kleiner Perkins Seeks to Recover Costs from Ellen Pao in High-Profile Gender Discrimination Case**\n\nKleiner Perkins Caufield & Byers, a prominent venture capital firm in Silicon Valley, is seeking to recover approximately $973,000 in costs from Ellen Pao, a former partner who recently lost a high-profile sex-discrimination case against the firm. The case, which drew widespread attention to the issue of gender diversity in the tech industry, was decided in favor of Kleiner Perkins in March.\n\nAccording to court filings, Kleiner Perkins is seeking to recover costs related to expert witness fees, deposition and court reporter costs, and other expenses. The firm has offered to waive its attempt to recover costs if Pao agrees not to pursue an appeal in the case. Pao's legal team is considering the proposal, with a response expected within the next two weeks.\n\nThe case has sparked a wider discussion about gender diversity in the tech industry, with many experts and advocates calling for greater efforts to promote women in technology. Kleiner Perkins has stated that it believes women in technology would be best served by focusing on making progress on issues of gender diversity outside of continued litigation.\n\nThe firm's offer to waive costs in exchange for Pao's agreement not to appeal has been seen as a strategic move to avoid further controversy and negative publicity. However, some experts have criticized the firm's approach, suggesting that it comes across as punitive rather than conciliatory.\n\nThe case has also highlighted the high costs of litigation in employment disputes, with Kleiner Perkins seeking to recover significant expenses related to expert witness fees and other costs. The firm's offer to settle the case for almost $1 million before it went to trial has also been revealed, with Pao's lawyers failing to respond to the offer.\n\nOverall, the case has raised important questions about the role of women in technology and the need for greater diversity and inclusion in the industry. The outcome of the case and the response of Kleiner Perkins will be closely watched by advocates and experts in the field."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A man convicted four years ago in the oldest cold case ever tried was freed Friday after an Illinois judge vacated his conviction and subsequent life sentence, CNN reports. According to CBS News, 76-year-old Jack McCullough was convicted of kidnapping a 7-year-old girl, choking her, and stabbing her to death in 1957. The case was reopened in 2008, and McCullough was arrested in 2011 and convicted the following year. After an appeal by McCullough, Illinois state's attorney Richard Schmack launched a six-month investigation that found what he calls \"clear and convincing evidence\" that McCullough is innocent. While the judge Friday vacated McCullough's conviction, he stopped short of declaring him innocent, and a new trial will be held, the Chicago Tribune reports. McCullough, who lived in the same neighborhood as the kidnapped girl, says he was 40 miles away at an Air Force recruiting center at the time of the kidnapping. It's an alibi that passed a polygraph test in 1957 and made it impossible for him to be the culprit based on the FBI's original timeline for the kidnapping. That timeline was later changed by police, and Schmack says documents—including phone and Air Force records—supporting McCullough's alibi were wrongly not allowed at his trial. A friend of the kidnapped girl picked McCullough out of a photo lineup when the case was reopened. But his was the only non-yearbook photo in the lineup, and she picked a different man out of a photo lineup 50 years earlier. That info wasn't allowed at McCullough's trial either. No physical evidence was ever found to support McCullough's conviction."], "length": 2195, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Cold Case Conviction Overturned: 76-Year-Old Man Released from Prison**\n\nIn a stunning turn of events, a 76-year-old man, Jack McCullough, has been released from prison after serving a life sentence for the 1957 murder of 7-year-old Maria Ridulph. McCullough, who was convicted in 2012, was granted a new trial and ordered released by Judge William Brady on Friday.\n\nThe case, which was one of the oldest unsolved cold cases in the US, has been reopened after a six-month review of evidence by DeKalb County State's Attorney Richard Schmack. Schmack concluded that McCullough was wrongly convicted and that there was \"clear and convincing evidence\" that he was not involved in Maria's disappearance and murder.\n\nThe key evidence that led to McCullough's release was a newly discovered phone record that showed he made a collect call to his parents from a phone booth in Rockford, Illinois, at 6:57 p.m. on the night Maria disappeared. This evidence supported McCullough's long-held alibi that he was in Rockford, attempting to enlist in the US Air Force, at the time of the crime.\n\nMaria's family, however, remains convinced that McCullough is guilty and has sought the appointment of a special prosecutor to keep him behind bars. Maria's brother, Charles Ridulph, has expressed disappointment with the state prosecutor's office for overturning the conviction.\n\nThe case has been a source of controversy for decades, with many questions raised about the evidence used to convict McCullough. The investigation into Maria's disappearance was reopened in 2008 after a tip from McCullough's half-sister, and McCullough was arrested in 2011. He was convicted in 2012 and sentenced to life in prison.\n\nWith McCullough's release, the case is now closed, and the family of Maria Ridulph will have to come to terms with the fact that the man they believed was responsible for their loved one's death may not have been guilty after all."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Rush Limbaugh may be bleeding sponsors—one count now has the number fleeing his show up to 98—because of his \"slut\" comment, but Saturday Night Live had some suggestions last night about who might fill the advertising void. A few of the highlights, as per Mediaite: Misaki Dolphin Poppers: \"Start your day off right, with bits of dolphin.\" Depends for Racists: \"If you pee a little every time you see a Mexican, you need Depends for Racists.\" Schoder's fake rape whistles: \"Help is not on the way.\" Syria Tourism Board: Because, \"Ah! No! There's nowhere to hide!\""], "length": 495, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Advertisers Flee Rush Limbaugh's Show Amid Controversy**\n\nIn the wake of his highly criticized comments about Georgetown student Sandra Fluke, Rush Limbaugh's radio show has lost a significant number of advertisers. According to a memo obtained by Radio-Info.com, 98 advertisers have informed Premiere Radio Networks, the syndicator of Limbaugh's show, that they no longer want to advertise on his program or other shows deemed \"offensive or controversial.\" The list of advertisers includes major brands such as Ford, General Motors, Toyota, Allstate, Geico, Prudential, State Farm, McDonald's, and Subway.\n\nThe advertisers have specifically asked that their commercials be scheduled in dayparts or programs free of content that may be deemed offensive or controversial, including shows hosted by other conservative radio personalities such as Mark Levin, Tom Leykis, Michael Savage, Glenn Beck, and Sean Hannity.\n\nThe backlash against Limbaugh's comments has been swift and severe, with many advertisers pulling their ads from his show. The online feed at WABC, Limbaugh's flagship station, has been forced to air free public service announcements during his show as a result.\n\nThe controversy was also addressed on Saturday Night Live, which opened its show with a sketch featuring Taran Killam as Limbaugh, chastising his sponsors for deserting him. The sketch was a humorous take on the situation, but it also highlighted the reality that Limbaugh's comments have had a significant impact on his show's advertising revenue.\n\nOverall, the fallout from Limbaugh's comments has been significant, with many advertisers choosing to distance themselves from his show and its associated controversy."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["\"Any guy that can do a body-slam, he's my guy,\" President Trump shouted to a cheering Montana audience Thursday night as he simulated slamming someone to the ground, then noting, \"I shouldn't say that.\" Trump was discussing Rep. Greg Gianforte, who assaulted Guardian reporter Ben Jacobs last year during the special election for the House seat vacated by Ryan Zinke, a misdemeanor assault to which he pleaded guilty and completed community service and an anger management class. Trump said after the incident, he feared Gianforte had blown his chance at the congressional seat. \"I said, 'Oh, this is terrible, he's gonna lose the election,'\" Trump recounted. \"Then I said, 'Well, wait a minute—I know Montana pretty well. I think it might help him.' And it did.\" Trump was in Big Sky Country stumping for Matt Rosendale in his race for Democrat Jon Tester's Senate seat. Reaction came quickly: John Mulholland, US editor of the Guardian, put out a statement after the rally blasting Trump's words as \"an attack on the First Amendment,\" asking for an apology and adding, \"In the aftermath of the murder of Jamal Khashoggi, it runs the risk of inviting other assaults on journalists both here and across the world.\" ABC News reports on what the Toronto Star's Daniel Dale calls the \"most significant\" and \"truly horrible\" moment in the rally: \"The president is gleefully applauding violence against a journalist.\" Trump also praised Gianforte as \"smart\" and then advised the crowd to \"never wrestle him.\" (Trump seems to acknowledge Khashoggi is dead.)"], "length": 1550, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Summary of Recent News**\n\n**Montana Congressman-Elect Pleads Guilty to Assault**\n\nGreg Gianforte, the Montana congressman-elect, pleaded guilty to misdemeanor assault on Monday morning. The incident occurred during his congressional campaign last year, when he \"body-slammed\" a reporter, Ben Jacobs. Gianforte was sentenced to a 180-day deferred sentence, 40 hours of community service, 20 hours of anger management, and a $300 fine. He has apologized to Jacobs and pledged to donate $50,000 to the Committee to Protect Journalists.\n\n**President Trump Defends Gianforte's Actions**\n\nPresident Trump has defended Gianforte's actions, saying that he was a \"tough cookie\" and that the incident was a \"tremendous success\" in Montana. Trump's comments have been widely criticized by the White House Correspondents' Association, which condemned the president's praise for a violent assault on a reporter.\n\n**Trump Campaigns for Republican Senate Candidate**\n\nPresident Trump visited Montana on Friday to campaign for Republican Senate candidate Matt Rosendale, who is running against Democratic Senator Jon Tester. Trump has made it a personal mission to unseat Tester, who he blames for the failed nomination of White House physician Adm. Ronny Jackson. Trump also spoke about immigration, falsely claiming that Democrats are supporting a caravan of immigrants traveling up from Central America.\n\n**Twitter Update**\n\nTwitter has updated its policy to allow users to add location information to their tweets, including their city or precise location. Users can also delete their tweet location history.\n\n**Montana Senate Race Heats Up**\n\nThe Montana Senate race is heating up, with Tester maintaining a slight lead over Rosendale in recent polls. The race has become one of the most expensive in the state's history, with millions of dollars pouring in from both sides. Tester has touted his bipartisan record in the Senate, while Rosendale has criticized him for being a \"radical liberal.\""}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Superbugs such as MRSA are already bad enough. But it turns out that something can make them even nastier: cigarette smoke. Researchers found that antibiotic-resistant bacteria exposed to the smoke became more aggressive and far more difficult to kill, they explain at Eureka Alert. It's almost as if the smoke sets off an alarm that causes the bacteria to strengthen its defenses, explains the Union-Tribune of San Diego. Scientists made the discovery by exposing immune cells to two different batches of MRSA, one normal and one treated with cigarette smoke extract. The bigger the dose of smoke, the bigger the effect, notes Medical Daily. \"We already know that smoking cigarettes harms human respiratory and immune cells, and now we've shown that, on the flipside, smoke can also stress out invasive bacteria and make them more aggressive,\" says lead author Laura Crotty Alexander of UC San Diego. It's a double-whammy: Smokers already have weakened immune systems that make them more susceptible to infectious diseases, and the smoke they inhale seems to make invading pathogens even more dangerous. In this study, the smoke helped MRSA survive longer and kill more mice with pneumonia. (The latest antibiotic-resistant bug to make headlines is a nasty stomach virus.)"], "length": 1487, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Cigarette Smoke Fuels the Rise of Superbugs: A Growing Concern**\n\nRecent research has shed light on the alarming effects of cigarette smoke on the virulent rampage of superbugs, specifically Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA). Studies conducted by researchers at the University of California, San Diego School of Medicine have found that cigarette smoke not only harms human respiratory and immune cells but also makes MRSA more resistant to antibiotics and immune defenses.\n\nAccording to the studies, MRSA exposed to cigarette smoke extract becomes harder to kill, with macrophages (immune cells) struggling to eliminate the bacteria. The smoke-exposed MRSA was found to be more resistant to reactive oxygen species and antimicrobial peptides, which are key components of the immune system's defense against pathogens. Furthermore, the bacteria were able to adhere better to human cells, making it easier for them to invade and cause infections.\n\nThe research suggests that cigarette smoke alters the cell walls of MRSA, allowing it to repel toxic substances and enhance its survival against immune responses. This effect was found to be dose-dependent, with higher levels of smoke exposure leading to greater resistance in the bacteria.\n\nThe studies also found that the increased resistance in MRSA continued for 24 hours after exposure, suggesting that it could be inherited. This is particularly concerning, as MRSA can divide rapidly, generating millions of descendants in a short period.\n\nThe findings of these studies have significant implications for public health, highlighting the need for individuals to refrain from smoking. Cigarette smoke not only harms smokers but also makes them more susceptible to infectious diseases, including MRSA. The research also suggests that cigarette smoke may be an additional contributing factor to the rise of antibiotic-resistant bacteria.\n\nIn summary, the studies demonstrate that cigarette smoke fuels the virulent rampage of superbugs, making them more resistant to antibiotics and immune defenses. The findings emphasize the importance of quitting smoking and highlight the need for further research into the effects of cigarette smoke on the spread of infectious diseases."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["One of the most renowned hairstylists in Los Angeles was murdered on Monday by intruders who made their getaway in his Porsche, police say. Fabio Sementilli was stabbed several times and was found on his home's patio by his wife and daughter, CBS Los Angeles reports. Paramedics were called but the 49-year-old was bleeding profusely and died at the scene. Police say the Canadian-born hairstylist's 2008 Porsche 911 Carrera was missing from the scene and they believe it was stolen by two men who broke into the upscale Woodland Hills home and attacked Sementilli. Police aren't sure whether the murder was random or a targeted attack, the AP reports. Sementilli gained international recognition during his 30 years as a hairstylist and also served as an exec at companies including Wella, the Los Angeles Daily News reports. He was \"recognized as an exceptional stylist, educator, and visionary,\" Wella said in a statement that describes him as \"an icon in the hairdressing industry.\" A post at Modern Salon says he was a VP of education for cosmetics company Coty, adding that he \"mentored tens of thousands of hairdressers with a hands-on approach either on a one-to-one basis or on a grander scale.\" (This man was murdered just two months after President Obama commuted his sentence.)"], "length": 2237, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Renowned Hairdresser Fabio Sementilli Found Dead in Woodland Hills**\n\nFabio Sementilli, a 49-year-old internationally known hairdresser and beauty company executive, was found beaten and stabbed to death outside his home in the upscale Woodland Hills neighborhood of Los Angeles on Monday, January 23, 2017. Paramedics responded to a medical emergency call and found Sementilli bleeding profusely on the patio of his gated house. Despite attempts to save him, Sementilli died at the scene.\n\nSementilli, a Canadian-born hairstylist, was a vice president of education for Wella, a major German hair-care company. He was a decorated competition hair stylist and had mentored tens of thousands of hairdressers throughout his career. Sementilli was known for his positive attitude, integrity, and leadership, and was affectionately called \"big daddy\" by his colleagues and friends.\n\nThe Los Angeles Police Department's Homicide Bureau is investigating the case, and detectives are trying to determine if the attack was random or targeted. Sementilli's black 2008 Porsche 911 Carrera was missing from the scene, and police are reviewing security footage from the neighborhood to identify potential suspects.\n\nSementilli's death has sent shockwaves through the hairdressing industry, with many colleagues and friends paying tribute to his legacy. Eden Sassoon, the daughter of Vidal Sassoon and a star of Real Housewives Of Beverly Hills, called Sementilli a mentor and friend, saying \"he gave a (expletive) that made a difference.\" Wella Education posted on Facebook, \"Our hearts are broken. He will be sorely missed.\"\n\nAs the investigation continues, police are urging anyone with information about the case to come forward. Sementilli's family and friends are also seeking to honor his memory, with a local Facebook group set up to share tributes and memories of the beloved hairstylist."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Using methods borrowed from Google, a group of researchers has analyzed all Wikipedia pages and determined that, at least on the English language version of the site, Frank Sinatra is the world's most important person. Second place goes to Michael Jackson, and third to Pope Pius XII. When factoring in all 24 language editions of the online encyclopedia, the team found that Adolf Hitler ranked the most important person, while Michael Jackson was again second and Madonna third, reports the Guardian. \"Our analysis shows that most important historical figures across Wikipedia language editions are born in western countries after the 17th century, and are male,\" the authors write. The researchers combined two algorithms to reach these conclusions. First, Google's PageRank algorithm, which determines a page's importance based on how many other pages link to it. In using PageRank on Wikipedia, the most important person in the world was 18th-century Swedish naturalist Carl Linnaeus, likely because the pages of organism after organism link back to the \"father of taxonomy.\" So the researchers added an algorithm called CheiRank, which determines importance based on the number of outgoing links, the thought being that an important person would himself be connected to other important people, things, and events. Using the two together—an approach they dubbed 2DRank—no one beat out Frank Sinatra on English pages or Adolf Hitler across all language versions. Last year, in a separate study analyzing academic references, one team deemed Karl Marx to be the world's most important scholar, according to the Smithsonian. (Click to read about one of Sinatra's quirks.)"], "length": 1414, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**The Most Influential People in the World: A Study of Wikipedia**\n\nA recent study by the University of Toulouse's Young-Ho Eom has shed light on the most influential people in the world, based on their presence on Wikipedia. The study used a combination of algorithms, including PageRank and CheiRank, to analyze the links between Wikipedia articles and determine which individuals have the most important articles linking to them.\n\nThe initial results were surprising, with Frank Sinatra topping the list as the most important person in the English-language world, followed by Michael Jackson and Pope Pius XII. However, when the study was expanded to include all 24 major language editions of Wikipedia, the results changed. Adolf Hitler emerged as the most important historical figure, followed by Madonna and Michael Jackson.\n\nHowever, a second study by the same researcher, Eom, has revealed that the most influential thinker in the world is actually Swedish naturalist Carl Linnaeus. Linnaeus, known as the \"Father of Taxonomy,\" is credited with inventing the scientific system of naming that is still in use today. His influence is due to the fact that many Wikipedia pages reference scientific names, which all lead back to him.\n\nThe study's findings have sparked debate about the nature of influence and how it is measured. While some argue that Linnaeus' influence is due to his groundbreaking work in taxonomy, others point out that his influence is also due to the way Wikipedia is structured and the algorithms used to analyze the data.\n\nIn related news, the Internet Archive is working to archive web pages and preserve links to ensure that broken links are fixed. The organization is using crawlers to archive pages as they are created and to preserve links to referenced pages.\n\nOverall, the studies highlight the complexities of measuring influence and the importance of considering multiple perspectives and methods when evaluating the impact of individuals on the world."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Arnold Schwarzenegger seems to think Donald Trump needs a lesson in making friends after the president-elect chose to criticize his Celebrity Apprentice successor. \"Wow, the ratings are in and [new host] Arnold Schwarzenegger got 'swamped' (or destroyed) by comparison to the ratings machine, DJT,\" Trump tweeted Friday after the Trump-less show premiered Monday to 4.9 million viewers, according to Nielsen. The last season hosted by Trump averaged 7.6 million viewers, per Fortune. \"So much for … being a movie star,\" Trump continued. \"But who cares, he supported [John] Kasich & Hillary [Clinton].\" Schwarzenegger offered up a reply on Twitter, tweeting, \"I wish you the best of luck and I hope you'll work for ALL of the American people as aggressively as you worked for your ratings.\" He next shared a video he made after the election in which he quotes Abraham Lincoln's inaugural speech, beginning, \"We are not enemies, but friends. We must not be enemies.\" \"Please study this quote ... @realDonaldTrump,\" he tweeted with the video. \"It inspired me every day I was Governor, and I hope it inspires you.\" (The New York Times is unimpressed with the new season.)"], "length": 740, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Ratings Comparison: Trump's Celebrity Apprentice vs. Schwarzenegger's Hosting Debut**\n\nIn a recent ratings battle, Donald Trump's Celebrity Apprentice franchise has been compared to its new iteration hosted by Arnold Schwarzenegger. According to Nielsen's tracking numbers, the premiere of The New Celebrity Apprentice, which aired on Monday night, averaged 4.9 million total viewers across its two-hour premiere. This is a significant drop from the ratings of Trump's most recent season hosting, which averaged 7.6 million nightly viewers in the 2014-2015 season.\n\nThe ratings for Schwarzenegger's debut were also lower than expected, especially when compared to the premiere of ABC's The Bachelor, which pulled in nearly 6.6 million viewers. CBS also averaged more than 6 million viewers between four half-hour sitcoms that aired between 8 p.m. and 10 p.m.\n\nThe ratings for The New Celebrity Apprentice were down more than 22% from Trump's most recent season hosting, and the ratings for the key demographic (viewers aged 18 to 49) were roughly 46% lower than what Schwarzenegger's debut scored on Monday.\n\nDespite the mediocre start, NBC's ratings guru Tom Bierbaum noted that the premiere still provided a strong lead-in for new NBC gameshow The Wall, which retained most of the Apprentice's viewers to share the top ratings in the 10 p.m. time-slot among the key younger demographic.\n\nThe controversy surrounding Trump's continued executive producer credit on the show, despite his presidency, has also been a topic of discussion. Trump has a large stake in the show that he co-created with fellow producer Mark Burnett, but will reportedly be compensated by MGM, which owns the majority stake in the production company that owns the rights to the Apprentice franchise.\n\nOverall, the ratings comparison between Trump's Celebrity Apprentice and Schwarzenegger's hosting debut suggests that the franchise may be struggling to maintain its previous level of success without its former host."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Own a Samsung smart TV? Then take note: Your television is not only listening, it's sending your data to a third party, the Independent reports. The TV's voice-recognition software lets you give it orders, but the product's privacy policy warns that \"personal or other sensitive information ... will be among the data captured and transmitted to a third party through your use of Voice Recognition.\" Samsung tells the BBC that the third party is Nuance, the company that does Samsung's speech-to-text conversion. But intellectual property lawyer Corynne McSherry says it would be nice to see that clarified in Samsung's policy, the Daily Beast reports. \"And I’d definitely like to know whether my words were being transmitted in a secure form,\" she says. An activist tweets that Samsung's policy sounds a lot like the description of \"telescreens\" in George Orwell's dystopian novel, 1984—which reads in part, \"Any sound that Winston made, above the level of a very low whisper, would be picked up by [the screen].\" Unnervingly, it wasn't clear how often \"the Thought Police plugged in on any individual wire was guesswork.\" Samsung, however, says it doesn't keep voice data, and customers can opt to turn off voice-recognition or even unplug the TV from Wi-Fi. And Samsung isn't alone: the Verge reports that XBoxes already use voice recognition while being connected to the Internet. \"Samsung's privacy policy is not unique; its rhetoric just happens to be similar to that of a well-known sci-fi novel,\" the Verge says."], "length": 2580, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Smart TVs and Consoles: Always Listening, Always Collecting Data**\n\nIn recent news, several major technology companies have been making headlines for their new features that allow their devices to listen to and collect data from users, even when they are not actively using them. Samsung's new smart TVs and Microsoft's new Xbox One console are two examples of devices that are spark