icrosoft's Xbox One console have sparked concerns about user privacy and the potential for data misuse. Some have compared the features to the dystopian novel 1984, where the government has the ability to listen to and monitor citizens' conversations. Others have raised concerns about the potential for data breaches and the use of data for targeted"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A New Jersey court on Wednesday gave the Flight Crew—aka the New York Jets cheerleaders—something to cheer about: a $325,000 settlement of the class-action lawsuit filed in 2014 by a cheerleader identified as Krystal C., CNNMoney reports. That amounts to each of the 52 cheerleaders getting $2,500 per season worked and $400 per photo shoot. The settlement covers the 2012-13 and 2013-14 seasons. New York State Sen. Diane Savino, per the New York Post, cheered the settlement, calling on the NFL to \"develop uniform rules … to ensure that all cheerleaders in every state received the employee pay and protections they deserve.\" NFL teams have sought to justify low pay for cheerleaders by saying they're independent contractors, CNN notes. In the case of the Flight Crew, Krystal C.'s suit claimed that cheerleaders were compensated $150 per game and $100 for special appearances. But, when you factor in practices and rehearsals, that pans out to $3.77 per hour. Throw in hair, makeup, and transportation expenses and \"the hourly rate goes below $1.50 an hour,\" says the cheerleaders' attorney. The Flight Crew isn't alone: Cheerleaders have taken to the courts to seek higher pay from the Cincinnati Bengals (tentative agreement reached), Tampa Bay Buccaneers (settled for $825,000), Oakland Raiders (settled for $1.25 million), and the Buffalo Bills (class-action suit on the horizon)."], "length": 859, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**NFL Cheerleaders Win Settlements in Wage Disputes**\n\nIn a series of victories for NFL cheerleaders, several teams have reached settlements in wage disputes with their squads. The most recent settlement was reached between the New York Jets and their cheerleaders, known as the Flight Crew, in a New Jersey court. The Jets agreed to pay $325,000 to the 52 cheerleaders, with each receiving $2,500 per season they worked, plus an additional $400 per photo shoot. The settlement covers the 2012-13 and 2013-14 NFL seasons.\n\nThis is not the first time an NFL team has been sued by their cheerleaders over wages. The Cincinnati Bengals, Tampa Bay Buccaneers, Oakland Raiders, and Buffalo Bills have also been sued. The Buccaneers reached a settlement of $825,000, while the Bengals have reached a tentative pact with their cheerleaders. The Raiders settled for $1.25 million in 2014, with the cheerleaders claiming they were paid less than $5 an hour.\n\nThe Buffalo Bills' cheerleaders, known as the Jills, were recently granted class-action status in a similar wage suit against the team. State Sen. Diane Savino (D-SI) applauded the Jets' settlement and called on the NFL to develop uniform rules for its teams to ensure fair pay and treatment for all cheerleaders.\n\nThe lawsuits against the NFL teams have highlighted the low pay and poor working conditions faced by cheerleaders. In the case of the Jets, the cheerleaders claimed they made only $150 per game and nothing for practice time, with some cheerleaders earning as little as $3.77 per hour. The teams have tried to justify the low pay by labeling cheerleaders as independent contractors, but the courts have disagreed with this distinction.\n\nThe settlements are a significant victory for the cheerleaders, who have been fighting for fair pay and treatment for years. The NFL has been criticized for its treatment of cheerleaders, with some teams imposing strict codes of conduct and requiring cheerleaders to pay for their own expenses, such as hair and makeup. The settlements are a step towards addressing these issues and ensuring that all cheerleaders are treated fairly and paid a living wage."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["At first, it seemed like 4-year-old Laura Carson was suffering from a simple headache. Then came rapid shallow breathing, a tremor, double vision. Within days in August 2014, she was \"a limp rag doll,\" reports Today—but it took doctors some time to diagnose her with acute flaccid myelitis, a rare disease the CDC warns is becoming increasingly more common. Not much is known about the polio-like disease that plagues mostly children, including what causes it. What is known is that AFM affects the spinal cord and causes weak limbs or paralysis, drooping in the face, and slurred speech or difficulty breathing. About 200 kids have been diagnosed since 2014, and this year's figures are especially troubling. The CDC reports 121 cases of AFM were confirmed in 2014, but that was followed by just 21 cases in 2015, reports Fox News. From January to August of this year, however, there were 50 confirmed cases in 24 states. Among that first spike of cases in 2014, the Washington Post reports that 85% of kids recovered partially, but only three recovered fully. A CDC study found 68% of patients had a fever and 81% had a respiratory illness before AFM symptoms appeared. Some had been diagnosed with the West Nile virus, and others with an enterovirus. \"August to October is typically when enteroviruses circulate\" and \"we see more acute flaccid myelitis during that season,\" a doctor tells NBC News. Washing your hands and covering your mouth when coughing or sneezing are among the ways suggested to help. (Four siblings suffer from a mysterious disease.)"], "length": 4776, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Mysterious Polio-Like Illness on the Rise: Acute Flaccid Myelitis (AFM)**\n\nA mysterious polio-like illness, known as Acute Flaccid Myelitis (AFM), is on the rise in the United States, with 50 confirmed cases reported across 24 states as of August 2016, more than double the number of cases reported in 2015. The illness, which affects the nervous system, specifically the spinal cord, has been linked to viral infections such as enteroviruses, adenoviruses, and the mosquito-borne West Nile virus.\n\n**Symptoms and Diagnosis**\n\nSymptoms of AFM include pain in the arms and legs, an inability to pass urine, and respiratory failure, which can require urgent ventilator support. The exact trigger of the illness is often elusive, and there is no specific treatment. Diagnosis is typically made through a combination of clinical evaluation, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), and laboratory tests.\n\n**Outbreaks and Cases**\n\nThe first reported outbreak of AFM occurred in 2014, with 120 cases reported between August and December, all of which were children. The average age of children affected by AFM is 7, and many have had to be placed on respirators. Although 85% of children have recovered partially, only three have fully recovered.\n\n**Current Situation**\n\nAs of August 2016, 50 cases of AFM have been reported across 24 states, with the majority of cases occurring in children. The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) is investigating the current rise of AFM cases and advising people to wash their hands, stay up-to-date on vaccinations, and protect themselves from mosquitoes, which can carry the West Nile virus.\n\n**Treatment and Prevention**\n\nThere is no specific treatment for AFM, but neurologists may prescribe therapy on a case-by-case basis. Prevention of viral infections includes general hygienic measures, such as frequent hand washing with soap and water, avoiding close contact with sick persons, and disinfecting frequently touched surfaces.\n\n**Personal Stories**\n\nSeveral families have shared their personal stories of dealing with AFM, including the Roberts family, whose 3-year-old son Carter was diagnosed with AFM in July 2016. Carter's mother, Robin, has spoken out about the need for more research and awareness about the condition.\n\n**Research and Investigation**\n\nResearchers are working to understand the causes of AFM, with some studies suggesting a link to enterovirus D"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The Shroud of Turin is supposedly the burial cloth that was wrapped around Jesus after his crucifixion; bloodstains on the linen shroud, which are said to have been transferred to it during the three days Jesus was in the tomb, form the image of a crucified man. But a new study reported in the Journal of Forensic Sciences finds the bloodstain image was likely faked. Researchers looking at the blood spatter found that the stains appeared to come from someone standing up, rather than someone who was flat on the fabric, Science Alert reports. As the researchers put it, the stains are \"totally unrealistic\" when compared to what they should look like. The shroud, which is held in the Cathedral of St. John the Baptist in Italy, is considered an icon, as opposed to a genuine religious relic, by the Vatican; Fox News notes \"the church has never weighed in on its authenticity.\" \"This is the kind of forensic work done all the time in police investigations,\" the forensic scientist who conducted the analysis tells BuzzFeed News. \"Even a crucified or hanging person should leave a distinct blood pattern on the cloth, which would be fascinating information to have.\" The study found inconsistent staining, with researchers concluding multiple poses were used to create the bloodstains—a standing model was likely used to imprint patterns on the cloth at various angles for various body parts. Another bloodstain pattern expert notes that more research could be done to see whether cleaning a body or preparing it for burial might account for the inconsistent staining, though he notes that the stains do appear to have come from flowing blood, meaning a heart that was beating at the time the stains were made. (See previous stories in the real-or-fake debate here.)"], "length": 2394, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**The Shroud of Turin: A Forensic Investigation Raises Questions about its Authenticity**\n\nThe Shroud of Turin, a 14-foot-long linen cloth bearing the image of a crucified man, has been a subject of debate and study for centuries. Recently, a team of forensic scientists conducted a thorough analysis of the bloodstain patterns on the shroud, which has led to some surprising conclusions.\n\nAccording to the researchers, the bloodstain patterns on the shroud are inconsistent with the idea that it is a genuine death shroud of Jesus Christ. The team, led by forensic scientist Matteo Borrini and organic chemist Luigi Garlaschelli, used a technique called Bloodstain Pattern Analysis to study the flow of blood on the shroud. They created a mock-up of the shroud using a mannequin and synthetic blood, and then analyzed the bloodstain patterns that resulted from different positions and movements.\n\nThe results of the study suggest that the bloodstain patterns on the shroud are more consistent with a standing model than a crucified or hanging person. The researchers found that the bloodstains on the shroud are inconsistent with each other, with some stains showing a 45-degree angle and others showing a 90-degree angle. This is not what would be expected from a person who was crucified or hung on a cross.\n\nThe study's findings have sparked debate among experts, with some arguing that the conclusions are based on a flawed methodology. However, the researchers argue that their approach is sound and that the results are consistent with the idea that the shroud is a medieval forgery.\n\nThe Shroud of Turin has been the subject of much controversy over the years, with some regarding it as a genuine relic of Jesus Christ and others dismissing it as a medieval fake. While the latest study does not provide definitive proof of the shroud's authenticity or lack thereof, it does raise important questions about the nature of the shroud and its history.\n\nThe study's findings have been published in the Journal of Forensic Sciences, and are likely to spark further debate and discussion among experts and the general public. As one of the researchers noted, \"This is just not what happens to a person on a cross.\""}
{"input": "", "answers": ["You can temporarily take Donald Trump out of real estate, but you can't take the real estate out of Donald Trump. That much was clear at a Singapore press conference Tuesday, which took place after the president's meeting with Kim Jong Un and in which he touched upon the potential of North Korea's coastline. \"They have great beaches,\" Trump noted, per the Washington Examiner. \"You see that whenever they're exploding their cannons into the ocean.\" And that spectacular view got him to thinking: \"Wouldn't that make a great condo? Instead of doing that you could have the best hotels in the world right there. Think of it from a real estate perspective, you have South Korea, you have China, and they own the land in the middle, how bad is that, right? It's great.\" The AP also reports on another post-summit activity, which it deems a \"classic alpha male move\": Trump showing off \"The Beast,\" the presidential limousine, to the North Korean leader. CNN shows a video clip of the two men approaching the limo, a Secret Service agent opening the door, and Kim peering in to take a look. \"We know that these men were trying to find ways to bond, in just like a personal, human way, and what's more natural for guys [than] to want to check out one another's car,\" Chris Cuomo notes. (Here are seven complimentary things Trump said about Kim after the summit.)"], "length": 848, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Historic Summit in Singapore: Trump and Kim Make Progress on Denuclearization and Economic Development**\n\nIn a historic summit in Singapore, President Donald Trump and North Korean leader Kim Jong Un made significant progress on denuclearization and economic development. The two leaders met for a marathon press conference, where Trump touted North Korea's untapped property market potential and showed Kim a video simulation of possible projects that could take place in the rogue nation.\n\nTrump expressed optimism that Kim was interested in the ideas presented, including the possibility of building the \"best hotel in the world\" on North Korea's coastline. The U.S. delegation used economic development as an incentive for North Korea to enter denuclearization talks, and Kim agreed to relinquish his country's nuclear weapons.\n\nIn a lighthearted moment, Trump showed Kim the presidential limousine, known as \"The Beast,\" and the two leaders were seen chatting and laughing together. Trump had said before the summit that he would be able to tell within minutes whether he'd be able to strike a deal with Kim.\n\nThe summit marked a significant step towards a lasting peace and complete denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula. Trump announced that North Korea had agreed to rewrite history and push for a lasting peace, and the two leaders are expected to meet again in the future to continue their discussions.\n\n**Key Takeaways:**\n\n* North Korea agrees to relinquish its nuclear weapons\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["President Obama today signed legislation to honor Japanese-American soldiers who fought on behalf of the Allies during World War II—even as some of their families were kept in confinement in the US. This long-awaited bill will open the door for more than 6,000 soldiers known as Nisei veterans to collectively receive the prestigious Congressional Gold Medal. For more on the vets, see the Digital Journal or Oregon Public Broadcasting."], "length": 1182, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Historic Honor for Japanese-American Veterans**\n\nPresident Barack Obama has signed a bill authorizing the Congressional Gold Medal, one of the highest civilian honors in the United States, to be awarded to over 6,000 Japanese-American veterans who served in World War II. These veterans, known as Nisei, were born to immigrant parents and were forced to endure internment camps while their families were locked away due to their ethnicity. Despite this, they stepped forward to defend their country, serving in the 442nd Regimental Combat Team, the 100th Infantry Battalion, and the Military Intelligence Service.\n\nThe 442nd Regimental Combat Team, which was made up of Americans of Japanese ancestry who volunteered to fight, is the most decorated Army unit of its size and length of service in U.S. history. They gained a reputation for toughness in France and Italy, and were famous for the rescue of the \"Lost Battalion,\" a unit of Texas National Guardsmen who had been cut off behind German lines.\n\nThe Congressional Gold Medal will be awarded to the Nisei veterans in recognition of their heroic service and sacrifice during World War II. This honor is a testament to the bravery and patriotism of these individuals, who fought for their country despite facing prejudice and discrimination.\n\nThe award is one of the highest civilian honors in the United States, and has been previously awarded to notable figures such as the Wright Brothers, Rosa Parks, and the Dalai Lama. The ceremony will be a historic moment, marking the 65th anniversary of the end of World War II and the 65th year since some of the surviving veterans returned home.\n\nThe Nisei veterans, including Art Iwasaki, Kenji Yaguchi, and Ike Iwasaki, have spoken out about their experiences, highlighting the challenges they faced and the sense of duty that drove them to serve their country. Their stories serve as a reminder of the importance of recognizing and honoring the contributions of all Americans, regardless of their background or ethnicity."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Earlier this year, President Trump noted \"I just don't want a poor person\" in charge of economic posts in his administration, then touted Commerce Secretary Wilbur Ross as \"a very rich person\" who was up to the task. But just how rich Ross really is is now up in the air, Forbes reports, noting it's yanked Ross off its billionaires list after a one-month probe revealed that $2 billion or so or Ross' alleged fortune \"never existed,\" and that Ross had engaged in an \"apparent sequence of fibs, exaggerations, omissions, fabrications, and whoppers\" with the magazine going back 13 years. The discovery came when Ross, 79, contacted Forbes in October to refute his ranking: Last year he was listed with a net worth of $2.9 billion on the Forbes 400; he claimed he was worth closer to $3.7 billion. Then Forbes talked with 10 of Ross' ex-employees at his private equity firm, who noted a \"penchant for misleading\" that affected fellow workers and investors and spurred big fines, suits, and refunds to backers. \"Wilbur doesn't have an issue with bending the truth,\" one longtime colleague says, while another is more blunt: \"He's lied to a lot of people.\" The magazine delves into Ross' back story, including how, when he first made the Forbes billionaires list in 2004 with a net worth of $1 billion, \"everyone that I knew that worked with Wilbur knew it wasn't true,\" per a former colleague. When Forbes told Ross he was being removed from its billionaires club, Ross retorted the magazine wasn't counting family trusts that he wasn't obligated to inform the feds about—in the amount of \"more than $2 billion,\" assets Ross said he put into the trusts sometime \"between the election and [my] nomination.\" When Forbes asked to see a paper trail proving that, Ross cited \"privacy issues\"—and the magazine lays out other problems with Ross' story on the supposed $2 billion transfer. Read the in-depth findings here."], "length": 4687, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Summary of News**\n\n**Wilbur Ross' Net Worth and Ethics Scandal**\n\nUS Secretary of Commerce Wilbur Ross has been embroiled in a scandal surrounding his net worth and ethics. According to a Forbes investigation, Ross claimed to have a net worth of $2.9 billion, but financial disclosure forms showed less than $700 million in assets. Ross protested, citing trusts for his family that he said he did not have to disclose in federal filings. However, Forbes found that the money never existed, and Ross had lied about his net worth.\n\n**Ross' History of Misleading and Exaggerating**\n\nThe investigation revealed that Ross has a history of misleading and exaggerating his net worth and business dealings. He claimed to have invested $3 million in his firm's first two funds, but former employees said he actually invested only $3 million. Ross also claimed to have raised $2.2 billion for a private equity fund, but most of the money came from other funds or accounts that paid little or no fee to Invesco.\n\n**Ross' Appointment as Commerce Secretary**\n\nRoss' appointment as Commerce Secretary was met with controversy, with many questioning his qualifications and ethics. Despite his questionable ethics, President Donald Trump defended Ross, saying that he wanted a \"rich person\" in charge of the economy. Trump claimed that Ross and other wealthy appointees had to \"give up a lot\" to take their jobs, but Ross' net worth has actually increased since his appointment.\n\n**Trump's Comments on Wealth and Poverty**\n\nIn a recent rally in Iowa, Trump said that he didn't want \"poor people\" in economic roles in his administration. He claimed that Ross and other wealthy appointees had to \"give up a lot\" to take their jobs, but Ross' net worth has actually increased since his appointment. Trump's comments have been criticized as tone-deaf and out of touch with the struggles of ordinary Americans.\n\n**Trump's Administration: The Wealthiest in US History**\n\nTrump's administration has been criticized for being the wealthiest in US history, with many cabinet members and appointees being millionaires and billionaires. Despite his claims of being a champion of the \"forgotten men and women,\" Trump has appointed many wealthy individuals to key positions, raising questions about his commitment to economic equality."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A guitar that once belonged to Kurt Cobain, which is likely worth millions, is now in the hands of Eeries frontman Isaiah Silva, and Cobain's daughter is fighting to get it back in the family. Frances Bean Cobain was married to Silva for 21 months, and she's been trying to get the Martin D-18E (which had the bridge flipped so Cobain, a leftie, could play it comfortably) back ever since they split in March. Silva claims she gave it to him as a wedding gift and it's rightfully his. Now, the battle is headed to court, Page Six reports. \"It’s not [Silva’s]. It’s a treasured heirloom of the family. It’s not his to take,\" Frances Cobain has said. But sources tell Page Six that Silva isn't giving in, and \"is forcing the matter to litigation.\" The guitar, the one Cobain played during Nirvana's 1993 MTV Unplugged show and also thought to be the last one he played before taking his life, was once insured for $1 million but, per experts, is probably worth several times that now. Back in August, Silva requested $25,000 a month in spousal support and half of any money Frances made during the marriage, and said the couple had no prenup, TMZ reported. A judge has since ordered Frances Cobain to pay Silva $15,000 a month as part of an interim settlement."], "length": 810, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Multimillion-Dollar Court Battle Over Kurt Cobain's Guitar**\n\nA bitter and expensive court battle is brewing over the ownership of Kurt Cobain's most prized guitar, a Martin D-18E that he played during Nirvana's iconic 1993 \"MTV Unplugged\" concert. The guitar, which is now worth several million dollars, has been at the center of a dispute between Frances Bean Cobain, Kurt's daughter with Courtney Love, and her estranged husband, Isaiah Silva.\n\nFrances Bean claims that the guitar was never given to Silva as a gift, despite his claims that it was a wedding present when they secretly married in 2014. Love has also weighed in, stating that the guitar is a \"treasured heirloom\" of the family and not Silva's to take.\n\nThe dispute has been ongoing for months, with Silva refusing to hand over the guitar despite a judge's order to do so. Frances Bean has been paying Silva $15,000 a month in spousal support as part of an interim settlement, but Silva is now requesting a much higher amount, claiming that he left a steady job to marry Frances Bean and is now struggling financially.\n\nIn a separate development, Silva has filed court documents requesting spousal support of at least $25,000 a month, citing Frances Bean's wealth and her earnings from business deals made during their two-year marriage. Frances Bean has made it clear that she is willing to pay some spousal support, but is contesting Silva's claim to half of her earnings.\n\nThe court battle is expected to be a long and expensive one, with both sides dug in and refusing to back down. The outcome will likely have significant implications for the ownership of the guitar and the financial future of both Frances Bean and Isaiah Silva."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Matt Damon opined this week that the wave of sex harassment and assault claims sweeping Hollywood and society at large include \"a spectrum of behavior\" and that \"none of us came here perfect,\" and the woman he once dumped on Oprah wasted no time in shutting him down. \"Gosh it’s so interesting (profoundly unsurprising) how men with all these opinions about women’s differentiation between sexual misconduct, assault and rape reveal themselves to be utterly tone deaf and as a result, systemically part of the problem,\" Minnie Driver tweeted. She further unloaded to the Guardian: \"I’ve realized that most men, good men, the men that I love, there is a cut-off. They simply cannot understand what abuse is like on a daily level. I honestly think that until we get on the same page, you can’t tell a woman about their abuse. ... It is so individual and so personal, it’s galling when a powerful man steps up and starts dictating the terms, whether he intends it or not.” Damon was also getting zero sympathy from Alyssa Milano, reports USA Today, who tweeted that, \"as a victim of each component of the sexual assault spectrum of which you speak,\" \"they all hurt.\" \"We are not outraged because someone grabbed our a---s in a picture. We are outraged because we were made to feel this was normal. We are outraged because we have been gaslighted. We are outraged because we were silenced for so long.\" (Damon has said he had no idea that Harvey Weinstein routinely sexually harassed and assaulted women; his full interview this week with ABC News is here.)"], "length": 4436, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Sexual Harassment and Misconduct in Hollywood: A Growing Concern**\n\nThe entertainment industry is facing a growing crisis as allegations of sexual harassment and misconduct continue to surface. Recent comments by actor Matt Damon have sparked outrage, with many calling for greater accountability and understanding of the issue.\n\nDamon, who has be