d. Milano and Driver are among many who are advocating for a culture of zero tolerance for sexual harassment and misconduct.\n\nThe industry is also seeing a shift in the way allegations are handled, with many calling for an end to confidentiality agreements and a greater emphasis on listening to and believing women who come forward with allegations. Damon stated that he believes every"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Microsoft unveiled its much-hyped Project Natal motion control system—now renamed “Kinect”—at E3 last night, in a performance long on grandiosity and short on actual revelations. After walking through a set of fake living rooms, the white-poncho-wearing audience was treated to a performance by Cirque du Soleil acrobats dressed as cavemen, as a narrator declared that “history is about to rewritten” and a boy, raising his arms, used his movements usher in the new motion-based way to play games, according to the Wall Street Journal. And what was this grand historical event all about? Well, mostly mimicking the Wii, according to CNET. Microsoft’s camera-based motion controller may have potential, but the games demoed last night mostly looked like analogues for successful Wii efforts, including a yoga game, a racing game, and a Wii Sports-esque game titled—wait for it—Kinect Sports. IGN has a full list of launch titles. Microsoft is expected to reveal more details, including pricing, at its press conference today."], "length": 2768, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Microsoft Unveils Kinect: A Revolutionary Motion-Control System**\n\nMicrosoft has officially announced the name of its highly anticipated motion-control system, previously known as Project Natal, as Kinect. The system, which allows users to play games and interact with the Xbox 360 console using gestures and spoken commands, was unveiled at a special event in Los Angeles on Sunday night.\n\n**A Spectacular Event**\n\nThe event, which was a cross between a gospel revival and a Las Vegas show, featured Cirque du Soleil acrobats, a 9-foot tall elephant puppet, and audience members wearing white ponchos with illuminating shoulder pads. The show was designed to demonstrate the potential of Kinect and its ability to revolutionize the way people play games.\n\n**Games and Features**\n\nMicrosoft showcased a range of games and features that will be available with Kinect, including:\n\n* Whitewater rafting and a racing game\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Get out your checkbooks, kids: There is a slew of famous and infamous items up for auction. The US Marshal's office's online auction of the Unabomber's personal effects ends tomorrow, and Aol's Weird News astutely observes that Ted Kaczynski's 35,000-word handwritten manifesto ($17,525) is currently going for less than the gray hoodie ($20,025) that figured so prominently into his FBI Wanted poster. Bonus: sunglasses included. Looking for more of a bargain? A bow and arrows in a Sears box is currently a steal at $743. And in other bizarre auction news: Balloon boy's dad is trying to auction off the infamous \"flying saucer\" balloon for $1 million. But don't worry, reports KTLA: Richard Heene has vowed to give all the money to charity (specifically, Japan). A bunch of celebrities are hoping JetBlue loses their luggage ... right into the hands of eager bidders. The Celebrity Baggage Auction will benefit DoSomething.org. Buy a signed Jessica Simpson-brand bag or a leather Coach bag personally used by Rosario Dawson, and get two roundtrip JetBlue tickets, too. Click for more. Maybe eBay is the place to buy stuff: Morace Park bought a film reel for $5.25 that turned out to be the only known copy of the Charlie Chaplin film Zepped. The Telegraph reports that it's expected to fetch six figures in a June 29 auction."], "length": 1551, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Auction News Roundup**\n\nThis week, several high-profile auctions have made headlines, showcasing a range of unique and valuable items. Here's a summary of the latest news:\n\n**Unabomber Memorabilia Auction**\n\nThe personal effects of Ted Kaczynski, also known as the Unabomber, are being auctioned off online to raise money for his victims and their families. The auction, which ends on June 2, features items such as Kaczynski's handwritten manifesto, a Smith-Corona typewriter, and a hand-bowed wood saw. While some items have attracted significant bids, including the manifesto ($17,525) and a iconic hoodie ($20,025), others have received less attention. The auction is expected to raise around $15 million, with all proceeds going towards restitution.\n\n**Twilight Star Kellan Lutz's Charity Auction**\n\nKellan Lutz, star of the Twilight franchise, is teaming up with JetBlue and DoSomething.org to auction off his luggage and other travel items on eBay. The charity auction aims to raise funds for youth groups across the country. Lutz's contribution, which includes his travel bag and other goodies, is part of the second round of items up for auction, which starts today (May 31).\n\n**Rare Film Reel Sells for Six-Figure Sum**\n\nA rare film reel, Charlie Chaplin in Zepped, is set to sell for a six-figure sum after being purchased by collector Morace Park in 2009. The seven-minute long reel, which shows Chaplin bringing down a German Zeppelin during a London raid, is the only known surviving copy and contains some of the earliest known animation. The film was released in 1917 as a morale-boosting film for British troops during World War One.\n\nThese auctions showcase the diverse range of items that are being sold to raise money for various causes. From the Unabomber's personal effects to a rare film reel and a celebrity's luggage, these auctions are sure to generate interest and excitement among collectors and charity supporters alike."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A former Boston TV news anchor says that last year, when her son was 18, he was at a bar in Natucket, Mass., when he met Kevin Spacey. The actor \"bought him drink after drink after drink and when my son was drunk, Spacey made his move and sexually assaulted him,\" said Heather Unruh at a press conference Wednesday. She says Spacey stuck his hand down her son's pants and onto his genitals, and tried to convince him to go to a party where they could continue to drink, USA Today reports. A bystander asked the teen if he was OK and the teen said no, at which point the woman urged the teen to flee and he took her advice, Unruh said. Unruh, who appeared with an attorney, says her son filed a police report last week and handed over evidence; she says a criminal investigation has been opened but the Nantucket Police Department has not confirmed that. The attorney with Unruh noted that her son's accusations are \"well within\" both the civil and criminal statutes of limitations. USA Today has a list of the 14 people so far accusing Spacey of sexual assault or harassment. Meanwhile Jon Bernthal, who worked with Spacey on Baby Driver, told a SiriusXM radio show Tuesday that he lost respect for Spacey while working on the film. \"I thought he was a bit of a bully,\" Bernthal said, per People. \"I didn’t really care for the way he was behaving toward some of the other people on set.\""], "length": 3331, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Kevin Spacey Accused of Sexual Misconduct by Over a Dozen Men**\n\nActor Kevin Spacey is facing a growing list of allegations of sexual misconduct, with over a dozen men coming forward to accuse him of harassment, assault, or attempted rape. The allegations span decades, with some dating back to the 1980s.\n\nThe first accusation was made by actor Anthony Rapp, who alleged that Spacey made unwanted advances towards him in 1986, when Rapp was just 14 years old. Spacey apologized for his behavior, but also came out as gay, a move that was widely criticized as an attempt to deflect attention from the allegations.\n\nSince then, several other men have come forward to accuse Spacey of similar behavior. These include:\n\n* Roberto Cavazos, a Mexican actor who said Spacey fondled him against his will at the Old Vic theater in London\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Since we last checked in, the situation in Mali following a soldiers' coup has spiraled, with rising concerns over food and gas shortages, violence, and indeed the country's \"very existence,\" according to Le Monde. An update: The coup has driven more than 200,000 people from their homes to other parts of the region, the AP reports. The UN worries that major food shortages could be imminent, and \"mayhem in these towns and cities is increasing,\" says a rep. Neighboring countries have established an embargo against the rebels who ousted the president; they've closed off their borders and frozen the country's regional bank account. That has prompted residents of Mali, which imports all its fuel, to rush to gas stations to collect fuel. The country's electricity grid may also be headed for failure. UNESCO is citing a threat to Timbuktu, a World Heritage Site full of \"architectural wonders,\" notes the BBC. Mosques there are \"essential to the preservation of the identity of the people of Mali,\" says the group. Yet \"nothing seems to be able to stop\" the Tuareg coup, Le Monde reports. West African leaders need to help Mali's military fight back before the instability spreads beyond the country's borders."], "length": 2503, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Mali Crisis Deepens: Tuareg Rebels Gain Control, Sanctions Imposed**\n\nThe situation in Mali continues to deteriorate as Tuareg rebels, backed by Islamist fighters, gain control of a third of the country, including the key northern towns of Gao, Kidal, and Timbuktu. The rebels, who want to establish an independent Tuareg state called Azawad, have been making rapid advances in the past few days, taking advantage of the power vacuum created by a military coup that ousted President Amadou Toumani Touré on March 22.\n\nThe coup, led by a junta of captains, was initially justified as a means to end the decline of Mali, but it has had the opposite effect, allowing the rebels to gain control of the north. The Malian army is falling apart, and the country's infrastructure is under threat.\n\nThe international community is responding to the crisis, with the African Union and West African nations imposing sanctions on Mali, including a ban on fuel imports and a freeze on the country's bank account. The Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS) has given the junta a 72-hour deadline to restore civilian rule, which expired on Monday.\n\nThe sanctions are expected to have a devastating impact on the country, with fuel shortages and power outages likely to affect the entire population. The landlocked country imports all its fuel, and the embargo is expected to cause widespread shortages.\n\nThe situation in Timbuktu, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, is particularly concerning, with the rebels imposing Islamic law and ransacking bars and shops. The city's unique architecture and ancient manuscripts are at risk of being destroyed, and the international community is calling on all parties to respect the site's cultural heritage.\n\nThe crisis has also led to a humanitarian emergency, with over 200,000 people displaced and 13 million people in need of food aid. The UN Security Council is due to hold an emergency meeting on the crisis later this week.\n\nThe rebels, who are divided into two groups, the National Movement for the Liberation of Azawad (MNLA) and Ansar Dine, are open to negotiations, but only with a recognized government. The junta, led by Capt. Amadou Haya Sanogo, has promised to consult local political forces to set up a transition body, but it remains to be seen whether this will be enough to resolve the crisis.\n\nThe situation in Mali is complex and volatile, with many different actors and interests at play."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["JK Rowling makes the case today that millions of kids around the world are living in appalling conditions—the stuff of Grimms' fairytales—in institutions that are supposed to be caring for them. Rowling wants to change that, not by improving these orphanages with \"pretty murals\" or teddy bears, but by eliminating them altogether, she writes in the Guardian. The big challenge is providing support for often-poor families who think they have no choice but to place their child in such an institution. If a child's biological family isn't up to the task, then foster families can step in. But Rowling argues that institutions are absolutely the wrong answer. Rowling has started a charity called Lumos (yes, after one of her Harry Potter spells), and writes that it has made genuine progress in reducing new placements in countries such as Bulgaria, Moldova, and the Czech Republic. She wants to raise awareness and donations with campaigns like this one. \"I recently committed to becoming president of Lumos for life,\" writes Rowling. \"It is my dream that, within my lifetime, the very concept of taking a child away from its family and locking it away will seem to belong to a cruel, fictional world.\" Click for her full column."], "length": 1530, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Global Efforts to Eradicate Child Institutionalization by 2050**\n\nA growing movement is underway to end the practice of institutionalizing children worldwide, with a goal of eradicating it by 2050. The charity Lumos, founded by author JK Rowling, is at the forefront of this effort. According to Lumos, 8 million children are currently separated from their families and locked away in institutions, often in atrocious conditions.\n\nThe problem is not limited to poverty-stricken countries, as even well-resourced institutions can cause irreparable harm to children. Research has shown that institutionalization can lead to stunting, developmental delays, and psychological trauma, as well as increased risk of abuse, trafficking, and poverty.\n\nHowever, there is hope. Lumos has achieved significant success in several countries, including Moldova, the Czech Republic, and Bulgaria, where the number of children in institutions has decreased by as much as 75% in some areas. The charity estimates that with investment in inclusive education and health, support for vulnerable families, and alternative care options, the practice of institutionalization can be eradicated globally.\n\nInternational donors play a crucial role in this effort, and Lumos is calling on them to prioritize funding for family-based care and community development. Ending institutionalization not only has a moral imperative but also makes economic sense, as it is far more cost-effective to support a child in a family than in an institution.\n\nTo raise awareness and build momentum for this effort, Lumos has launched a social media campaign, #Letstalklumos, which encourages people to share their stories and join the movement to end child institutionalization. With the support of the EU, the US, and other donors, the goal of eradicating institutionalization by 2050 is within reach."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The US and Pakistan have agreed to work together in the future against \"high value targets\" in Pakistan, the countries announced in a joint statement today. The news comes after Sen. John Kerry arrived in Pakistan last night, intending to send a strong message following the death of Osama bin Laden: Cooperate more when it comes to rooting out terrorists, or face \"profound\" changes in the US-Pakistan relationship—which could include kissing billions in aid goodbye. Kerry is the most senior US official to go to Pakistan since bin Laden's death, but since issuing the joint pledge, he said Secretary of State Hillary Clinton will soon announce her own plans to visit, the AP reports. Kerry's meetings with Pakistani leaders began last night, the Washington Post reports. Many in Washington think Pakistan is harboring Islamist militants, and some members of Congress are calling for the US to sever the billions of dollars in aid it provides to Pakistan. Kerry is in Pakistan to lay out the new stakes following bin Laden's death, and question officials about how bin Laden was able to hide in Pakistan for years. In Kabul, Kerry said there is \"some evidence\" that the Pakistan government has knowledge of insurgent activities, calling it \"very disturbing.\""], "length": 1688, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**US and Pakistan Agree to Cooperate on Counter-Terrorism Efforts**\n\nIn a bid to ease tensions between the two countries, the US and Pakistan have agreed to work together on counter-terrorism efforts, including operations against \"high-value targets\" in Pakistan. The agreement was announced by US Senator John Kerry, who visited Pakistan on Monday and met with Pakistani leaders.\n\nKerry defended the US decision not to inform Pakistan in advance about the raid that killed Osama bin Laden, saying that the secrecy was necessary to protect the lives of US personnel involved in the operation. He acknowledged that the raid had caused outrage in Pakistan, but emphasized that bin Laden and his followers had \"truly violated Pakistan's sovereignty\" by inspiring and conspiring with extremist groups that had killed thousands of Pakistani citizens and soldiers.\n\nAs part of the agreement, Pakistan has agreed to return the tail of a US military helicopter that was damaged during the bin Laden raid. The helicopter was destroyed by US commandos to prevent it from falling into the wrong hands, but the tail remained intact and was seen as a symbol of the US-Pakistan rift.\n\nKerry said that the US and Pakistan had agreed to increase intelligence sharing and joint operations to combat terrorism, and that a \"road map\" for cooperation would be discussed in coming days by senior White House officials. He also emphasized that the US had \"no designs\" on Pakistan's nuclear arsenal.\n\nThe agreement comes as Pakistan's Prime Minister Yousaf Raza Gillani begins a four-day visit to China, which has been a long-time ally of Pakistan. The visit is seen as a sign of Pakistan's efforts to diversify its foreign relationships and reduce its dependence on the US.\n\nThe US-Pakistan relationship has been strained since the bin Laden raid, with Pakistan accusing the US of violating its sovereignty and the US questioning Pakistan's commitment to fighting terrorism. However, Kerry said that the two countries had made significant progress in recent days and that the relationship was \"not irreparable.\"\n\nThe agreement is seen as a crucial step in maintaining stability in the region, particularly in the context of the war in Afghanistan. The US relies on Pakistan as a key supply route for its troops in Afghanistan, and Pakistan's assistance is seen as crucial to a potential negotiated end to the war.\n\nHowever, the agreement is not without its challenges. The US Congress has been critical of Pakistan's alleged support for militant groups, and some lawmakers have called for an end to US aid to Pakistan. Kerry said that the US would continue to press Pakistan to demonstrate its commitment to fighting terrorism,"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Kim Jong Nam's assassins killed him with a banned chemical weapon, Malaysian police revealed Friday. The country's police chief said toxicology reports on swabs from the face and eyes of the exiled North Korean found VX nerve agent, which the BBC notes is classed as a weapon of mass destruction by the United Nations. He said that one of two women believed to have rubbed the extremely toxic substance on Kim's face with their hands suffered from vomiting after the attack. The New York Times reports that VX agent can be created by mixing two compounds—and police suspect the two women put the substances on Kim's face, one after the other, to create a deadly dose. The police chief said the airport where Kim was attacked is now being decontaminated. North Korea—which is widely suspected to have been behind the killing of leader Kim Jong Un's half brother—never signed the Chemical Weapons Convention that banned VX, the AP notes. Pyongyang denies involvement and says Malaysia's investigation is full of \"holes and contradictions.\" The father of one of the two women being held, Vietnamese citizen Doan Thi Huong, tells the Times his daughter trained as a pharmacist and he has seen little of her in recent years. (Police say that after Malaysia refused to give Kim's body to North Korean diplomats, somebody tried to break into the morgue.)"], "length": 2917, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**North Korea's VX Nerve Agent Assassination: A Diplomatic Crisis Unfolds**\n\nIn a shocking turn of events, the half-brother of North Korean leader Kim Jong-un, Kim Jong-nam, was killed on February 13 at Kuala Lumpur International Airport in Malaysia. The 45-year-old was poisoned with a highly toxic nerve agent, VX, which is classified as a weapon of mass destruction by the United Nations. Malaysian authorities have confirmed that the substance was detected on Kim's eyes and face, and one of the two women accused of carrying out the attack also fell ill with vomiting.\n\nThe assassination has sparked a diplomatic crisis between Malaysia and North Korea, with Pyongyang denying any involvement and accusing Malaysian authorities of fabricating evidence. North Korea's reclusive government has been on the defensive, with a statement attributed to the North Korean Jurists Committee claiming that the greatest share of responsibility for the death \"rests with the government of Malaysia.\"\n\nThe use of VX nerve agent has raised serious concerns about public safety, with experts warning that the substance can remain lethal for a long period of time and can be absorbed into the body through skin contact, inhalation, or ingestion. Malaysian authorities have announced that they are decontaminating the airport and areas where the suspects are known to have visited.\n\nNorth Korea is believed to have a large arsenal of chemical weapons, including mustard, phosgene, and sarin gas, and is estimated to have a chemical weapons production capability of up to 4,500 metric tons per year. The country has never signed the Chemical Weapons Convention, which bans the production, stockpiling, and use of chemical weapons.\n\nThe case has also raised questions about the involvement of North Korean agents in the assassination, with Malaysian authorities saying that they are investigating how the lethal agent entered Malaysia. Four people are in custody, including one North Korean and the two women accused of carrying out the attack, while seven North Koreans are being sought, including a diplomat.\n\nThe death of Kim Jong-nam has marked a serious turnaround in relations between Malaysia and North Korea, with Malaysia's Foreign Minister warning North Korea's envoy that he would be expelled unless he stopped \"spewing lies\" about the attack. The case has also sparked international speculation that Pyongyang dispatched a hit squad to Malaysia to kill the exiled older sibling of North Korean leader Kim Jong-un.\n\nAs the investigation continues, experts are warning that the use of VX nerve agent poses a significant threat to public safety, and that the substance can remain lethal for a long period of time."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["San Francisco-area cops have arrested three more young men in the savage gang rape, beating, and robbery of a 15-year-old girl outside her homecoming dance while dozens more watched. Five youths are being held, ranging from a 15-year-old boy who knew the victim to a 21-year-old. The girl was drinking outside the dance Saturday night, reports the San Francisco Chronicle, and the group turned on her when she became drunk. Charges are expected today. \"This was a barbaric act. I still cannot get my head around the fact that numerous people either watched, walked away or participated in her assault,\" a Richmond cop tells the AP. \"It's one of the most disturbing crimes in my 15 years as a police officer.\" Some students began transferring out of the school. \"It's not safe there at all,\" said a 16-year-old girl. \"I'm not going back.\""], "length": 1576, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**RICHMOND HIGH SCHOOL RAPE CASE: 10 SUSPECTS ARRESTED, $20,000 REWARD OFFERED**\n\nA horrific gang rape and beating of a 15-year-old girl on the grounds of Richmond High School in Richmond, California has left the community shaken. The attack, which occurred on Saturday, October 24, 2009, has resulted in the arrest of 10 suspects, ranging in age from 15 to their early 20s.\n\nAccording to police, the victim, a sophomore, had left the school's homecoming dance and was drinking alcohol in a school courtyard with a group when she was attacked. The assault, which lasted for over two hours, was witnessed by as many as two dozen people, who did nothing to stop it. The victim was found semiconscious and naked from the waist down near a picnic table and was airlifted to a hospital with non-life threatening injuries.\n\nTwo suspects, Manuel Ortega, 19, and a 15-year-old boy who attends the school, were arrested on Monday and Tuesday, respectively. Ortega, who dropped out of Richmond High in 2007, was arrested as he ran from the scene of the attack and is being held on $800,000 bail. The 15-year-old boy, who knew the victim, was booked on suspicion of sexual assault at Contra Costa County Juvenile Hall.\n\nPolice initially said about six young men were involved in the attack, but investigators now believe that as many as 10 assailants committed a \"slew of crimes\" against the girl, including raping her and stealing her jewelry. The men laughed and took photos as they took turns assaulting the girl.\n\nThe attack has raised questions about campus security, with Richmond High having surveillance cameras that are not working. School officials said a new system should be in place in the next few months. Police are also investigating why two school security staffers left the dance early, despite the presence of four police officers and several school security staffers.\n\nA $20,000 reward is being offered for information leading to the conviction of any of the assailants. Police expect to make more arrests and are urging anyone with information to come forward.\n\nThe attack has sent shockwaves through the community, with one police official calling it \"one of the most heinous crimes\" he has ever seen. The victim's family has also spoken out, with her mother saying that she is not safe at the school and has transferred her out of the school.\n\nThe incident has also highlighted the"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The White House on Friday declassified a partisan and bitterly disputed memo on the Russia investigation, and a House committee immediately made it public. Media outlets were just beginning to assess it. You can read the document here, via the Washington Post. The White House move came over the fierce objections of the FBI and Justice Department, which have said the document prepared by Republicans on the House intelligence committee is inaccurate and missing critical context, per the AP. The memo alleges that the FBI abused US government surveillance powers in its investigation into Russian election interference. Trump, who has called the investigation a \"witch hunt,\" has supported the release of the memo in the apparent hopes that it could help undermine the probe being led by special counsel Robert Mueller. The president, dogged by the unrelenting investigation into his campaign's ties to Russia, lashed out anew Friday at the FBI and Justice Department as politically biased against Republicans. \"The top Leadership and Investigators of the FBI and the Justice Department have politicized the sacred investigative process in favor of Democrats and against Republicans - something which would have been unthinkable just a short time ago. Rank & File are great people!\" Trump tweeted."], "length": 2676, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Summary of News**\n\n**FBI Surveillance Memo Released**\n\nThe House Intelligence Committee has released a memo alleging that the FBI abused its surveillance authority, particularly when it sought a secret court order to monitor a former Trump campaign adviser, Carter Page. The memo, which was approved for release by President Trump, claims that the FBI and Justice Department withheld information from the Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Court (FISC) about the origins of the investigation into Page. The memo also alleges that the FBI relied heavily on a dossier compiled by Christopher Steele, a former British intelligence officer who was hired by the Democratic National Committee and Hillary Clinton campaign to investigate Trump's ties to Russia.\n\n**Key Findings of the Memo**\n\n* The FBI and Justice Department failed to disclose the role of the DNC and Clinton campaign in funding Steele's efforts to investigate Trump.\n* Steele was suspended and then terminated as an FBI source for unauthorized disclosure to the media of his relationship with the FBI.\n* Steele's dossier was not corroborated by the FBI, and his anti-Trump financial and ideological motivations were ignored or concealed.\n* The FBI relied heavily on Steele's dossier to obtain a FISA warrant to surveil Page, despite his questionable credibility.\n\n**Background on the Investigation**\n\n* The FBI began investigating Page in July 2016, after he was identified as a potential Russian agent.\n* The investigation was led by FBI agent Peter Strzok, who was later reassigned for sending anti-Trump text messages to his mistress, FBI Attorney Lisa Page.\n* The investigation was also influenced by Deputy Director Andrew McCabe, who testified that no surveillance warrant would have been sought from the FISC without the Steele dossier information.\n\n**Other News**\n\n* Twitter users can now add location information to their tweets, such as their city or precise location.\n* Users can also delete their tweet location history at any time.\n\nNote: This summary is a condensed version of the news and may not include all the details and nuances of the original articles."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Critics aren’t exactly howling over the new remake of The Wolfman starring Benicio del Toro, giving it mostly mediocre or failing marks. Here’s what they’re saying: “The movie is pungent with atmosphere, laying down a thick fog of creepy Victorian murk,” writes Kyle Smith of the New York Post, but the story is toothless, centering on a mystery “so simple that even Marmaduke could have sniffed it out.” “The movie is scary only as regards all its wasted potential,” laments John Anderson of the Wall Street Journal. There are good elements here, but they’re “reduced to a gruesome fondue, accessorized by actorly ham and studio cheese.” The filmmakers obviously loved the original Wolfman, but “nostalgia isn't always the best barometer,” writes James Berardinelli of ReelViews. The makeup is “inexcusably campy,” and scenes from the original are “recreated in a fashion that seems more Monty Python than unsettling.” But Ty Burr of the Boston Globe had fun. “The movie is by no means good,” he writes, “but it’s surprisingly enjoyable: a misty, moody Saturday-matinee monster-chiller-horror special.”"], "length": 3747, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Movie News Summary**\n\n**The Wolfman (2010)**\n\nThe latest remake of the classic Universal horror movie, \"The Wolfman,\" has received mixed reviews from critics. The film, directed by Joe Johnston and starring Benicio Del Toro, Emily Blunt, and Anthony Hopkins, attempts to blend traditional gothic storytelling with modern horror elements. However, the result is a hybrid that fails to work, with some critics calling it a \"toothless exercise in the obvious.\" The film's makeup and special effects, handled by veteran Rick Baker, have been criticized for being campy and dated. Del Toro's performance as Lawrence Talbot has been praised for its homage to Lon Chaney Jr.'s iconic portrayal, but has also been criticized for being neutered and muted. The film's supporting cast, including Hopkins and Blunt, has been underutilized.\n\n**Valentine's Day (2010)**\n\nIn contrast to \"The Wolfman,\" Garry Marshall's romantic comedy \"Valentine's Day\" has been panned by critics for its lack of originality and poor performances. The film, which features an all-star cast including Julia Roberts, Ashton Kutcher, and Jessica Alba, relies on tired rom-com cliches and fails to deliver any laughs. The film's tone has been described as \"sticky sweet\" and \"satanic,\" with some critics calling it a \"Repression-Era Special.\"\n\n**Ajami (2009)**\n\nOn the other hand, the Arab-Israeli drama \"Ajami\" has received widespread critical acclaim for its urgent and immediate portrayal of life in an Israeli ghetto. The film, directed by Scandar Copti and Yaron Shani, has been praised for its Tarantino-esque style and its ability to tackle complex themes in a way that is both jarring and fluid.\n\n**Red Riding Trilogy (2009)**\n\nThe \"Red Riding Trilogy,\" a British/Irish drama based on the true story of the Yorkshire Ripper murders, has been hailed as a masterpiece of contemporary filmmaking. The film, directed by Julian Jarrold, James Marsh, and Anand Tucker, features a star-studded cast and has been praised for its taut and disturbing portrayal of the darker side of human nature.\n\n**Classic Werewolf Movies**\n\nIn a separate article, critic John Anderson reviews classic werewolf movies, including the 1941 original \"The Wolf Man,\" \"An American Werewolf in London,\" and \"Wolf.\" Anderson praises the original \"Wolf Man\" for its atmospheric"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Sinn Fein leader Gerry Adams, lately the very high-profile suspect in a 1972 murder, will go free without charge, Northern Ireland police officials tell the BBC. Adams has been questioned since Wednesday in County Antrim over allegations that he, as an IRA commander ordered the murder of a 37-year-old widow and mom of 10, Jean McConville. The AP confirms the report, saying that Adams will likely be freed today, but notes that police say they'll send an evidence file to their British counterparts."], "length": 1816, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Gerry Adams Released After Four Days of Questioning in Jean McConville Murder Case**\n\nSinn Féin leader Gerry Adams was released on Sunday evening after four days of questioning by police in Northern Ireland about his alleged involvement in the 1972 murder of Jean McConville, a 37-year-old widow who was abducted, shot, and secretly buried by the IRA. Adams, who has denied any involvement in the murder, was arrested on Wednesday under the Terrorism Act and questioned at Antrim police station.\n\nAdams claimed that the timing of his arrest was politically motivated, coming as it did in the middle of an election campaign. He also accused police of using \"pernicious, coercive legislation\" to deal with a legacy issue. However, other politicians have rejected claims that the arrest was political, with the Democratic Unionist Party's Jeffrey Donaldson saying that the police had a duty to investigate the murder regardless of the election campaign.\n\nThe McConville family, who have been seeking justice for 42 years, welcomed the opportunity for Adams to be questioned, but expressed frustration that it had taken so long. Michael McConville, Jean's son, said that the family would continue to fight for justice, saying \"We know it's going to be a long road, but we're not going to stop now.\"\n\nThe police have sent a file to the Public Prosecution Service (PPS), which will decide whether to bring charges against Adams. The PPS will assess the evidence gathered during the four days of questioning, including 33 taped interviews and old photographs of Adams and other Sinn Féin leaders.\n\nThe case is part of a wider investigation into the IRA's \"Disappeared,\" a group of people who were abducted, murdered, and buried in secret during the Troubles. Last month, Ivor Bell, a former IRA leader, was charged with aiding and abetting the murder of Jean McConville, and several other arrests have been made in recent weeks.\n\nThe Boston College tapes, a series of confessional interviews with former paramilitaries, are also being used as evidence in the case. The tapes were designed to be an oral history of the Troubles, but have been used by police to gather evidence in several high-profile cases.\n\nThe release of Adams has sparked controversy, with some politicians accusing the authorities of \"bending to Sinn Féin threats and tantrums.\" However, the Northern Ireland Justice Minister, David Ford, has urged politicians to stay out of the investigation, saying that \"politicians would be extremely well advised"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Two workers at a Chicago day care were fired and arrested after a disturbing incident that left five toddlers injured. Police say surveillance video captured one of the workers burning the 2-year-olds with a hot glue gun while the other woman watched and laughed, People reports. Lizandra Cosme, 32, has been charged with five counts of aggravated battery of a child causing great bodily harm, the Chicago Sun-Times reports. Susana Gonzalez, 27, faces five misdemeanor charges of causing the circumstances of child endangerment over the Dec. 1 incident at the Children's Place day care. It's not clear how seriously the children were injured. Prosecutors say Cosme, who brought the glue gun for a Christmas project, was captured on video applying the hot glue directly to the hands and arms of the three girls and two boys. \"Each of the child victims winced and some whined at the hot glue gun application,\" a prosecutor said during a Monday court appearance. Prosecutors said Cosme tried to cover up the incident, asking a father whether his child had been burned at home, ABC7 reports. They said her actions were discovered when one child's mother, an ER physician, saw the burns and demanded to see surveillance footage. Cosme's lawyer said his client \"screwed up\" but did not mean to hurt the children."], "length": 1272, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Two Daycare Workers Charged with Injuring Five Toddlers with Hot Glue**\n\nIn a disturbing incident, two daycare workers have been arrested and charged with injuring five 2-year-old toddlers with hot glue at a Chicago daycare center. Lizandra Cosme, 32, and Susana Gonzalez, 27, were working at the Children's Learning Place in the Logan Square neighborhood when the incident occurred on December 1.\n\nAccording to surveillance footage, Cosme allegedly applied hot glue to the hands and arms of the five children, causing them to grimace in pain. Gonzalez, who was present in the room, was seen laughing as the incident occurred. The children, three girls and two boys, were left with burn marks on their hands and arms.\n\nCosme, who had been working with children for 12 years, was arrested on Sunday and charged with five counts of aggravated battery of a child under 13 years old causing great bodily harm. She was denied bail at a court appearance on Monday and is scheduled to return to court on December 26.\n\nGonzalez, who was charged with five misdemeanor counts of causing the circumstances of child endangerment, was also terminated from her position at the daycare center. The state Department of Children and Family Services is investigating the incident and has taken custody of the children.\n\nThe daycare center, which has four locations in Chicago, immediately terminated the two employees and notified the DCFS after learning of the alleged incident. The center's spokeswoman, Lissa Druss Christman, stated that the well-being of the students is paramount and that they are working with investigators to ensure the safety of the children.\n\nCosme's defense attorney claimed that his client \"screwed up\" but did not act out of malice, citing her 12 years of experience working with young children. However, prosecutors argued that Cosme's actions were reckless and caused harm to the children.\n\nThe incident has raised concerns about the safety and well-being of children in daycare centers and has sparked calls for increased oversight and regulation of childcare facilities."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Texas gubernatorial candidate Wendy Davis' new memoir is dedicated in part to daughters Amber, Dru, and Tate, and those familiar with Davis' life story might be puzzled by the last name on that list. That's because Davis is revealing for the first time that Tate is the unborn child that she and her husband aborted in the second trimester, after doctors discovered a severe brain abnormality. The San Antonio Express-News and the AP obtained advanced copies, and the news is generating headlines given that Davis shot to national fame while filibustering against an abortion bill in Texas. Davis writes that she decided to abort in 1996 after doctors said the baby would be blind, deaf, and in a vegetative state if she survived the pregnancy. Davis also felt the fetus “tremble violently\" in the womb, \"as if someone were applying an electric shock to her, and I knew then what I needed to do. She was suffering.\" Her doctor \"quieted\" the unborn child's heart and delivered her by C-section. Davis and her husband had Tate baptized. After the abortion, \"an indescribable blackness followed,\" writes Davis in Forgetting to Be Afraid, \"and when I finally did come through it, I emerged a different person.\" Davis also writes of a previously disclosed procedure to terminate an earlier ectopic pregnancy in which the embryo was implanted outside the uterus. She says she opted not to talk about either case during her famous filibuster because she feared it would \"overshadow the events of the day.\""], "length": 2373, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Texas Gubernatorial Candidate Wendy Davis Discloses Personal Abortion Story in Memoir**\n\nIn her upcoming memoir, \"Forgetting to Be Afraid,\" Texas Democratic gubernatorial candidate Wendy Davis reveals that she had two abortions in the 1990s, including one due to a severe brain abnormality in the fetus. The memoir, set to be released on Tuesday, also details Davis' experiences as a young mother, her struggles with poverty, and her journey to becoming a state senator and gubernatorial candidate.\n\nAccording to the book, Davis had an ectopic pregnancy in 1994, which was medically necessary to terminate. She also writes about ending a pregnancy in 1996 after discovering that the fetus had a severe brain abnormality. Davis, who is running for governor against Republican Attorney General Greg Abbott, had considered sharing her story during her 2013 filibuster against Texas' tough new abortion restrictions, but decided against it, fearing it would overshadow the event.\n\nDavis' decision to disclose her personal abortion story has sparked a mix of reactions from politicians and activists. Anti-abortion groups, including Texans Right to Life, expressed sympathy for Davis' difficult decision, but emphasized the value of life. Cecile Richards, president of Planned Parenthood, praised Davis' \"unwavering courage\" for sharing her story and shedding light on a subject often shrouded in shame and stigma.\n\nThe revelation is seen as a personal and emotional aspect of Davis' campaign, which has been marked by her advocacy for women's access to healthcare and her fight against abortion restrictions. While some have questioned whether the disclosure will impact her campaign, Rice University political scientist Mark Jones believes it will humanize Davis and make it more difficult for opponents to attack her on the abortion issue.\n\nThe memoir, which also details Davis' struggles with poverty, her relationships, and her faith, is set to be released on Tuesday, with Davis scheduled to sign copies in Fort Worth on Monday."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Two men have been charged with human trafficking after police arrested them for running a prostitution ring out of a senior living facility in Pittsfield, Mass. Police say Joseph Van Wert, 65, and Randy Lambach, 45, will face a hearing later this month, the New York Daily News reports. Lambach would allegedly seek out women with drug addictions and post their pictures online, then arrange meetings with clients. Some of these meetings took place in Van Wert's apartment in a senior facility. Van Wert offered his apartment in return for cash or drugs. The Berkshire Eagle reports that the police investigation began last spring in response to citizen complaints about increased prostitution across the city. Lambach would allegedly keep up to 90% of the money the women made, sometimes refusing them cash altogether and paying them in heroin or crack cocaine instead. At least one woman said Lambach drove her to an \"appointment\" across state lines. Police estimated one of the victims was 15 years old. They were also told that some of the women were Lambach's former foster children. Both men have pleaded not guilty, Lambach to four counts of human trafficking and Van Wert to conspiracy to commit human trafficking, deriving support from prostitution, maintaining a house of prostitution, and sexual conduct for a fee."], "length": 1457, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Two Men Arrested for Running Prostitution Ring in Massachusetts**\n\nIn a shocking case of human trafficking, two men, Joseph Van Wert, 65, and Randy Lambach, 45, have been arrested and charged with running a prostitution ring in Pittsfield, Massachusetts. The investigation, which began in the spring of 2017, revealed a complex network of prostitution and human trafficking that involved multiple women, some as young as 15.\n\nAccording to police, Lambach, who had been running the ring for several years, would recruit women with severe drug addictions and advertise their services on social media. He would set up meetings and rates with prospective clients, and would often transport the women to and from specific locations. Lambach would take a significant cut of the women's money, or pay them in heroin, cocaine, or prescription drugs in lieu of cash.\n\nVan Wert, who lived in a senior living facility, would allow some of the meetings to take place in his third-floor apartment in exchange for drugs and cash. Some of the women he recruited were formerly his foster children. A witness reported that Lambach had shown her photos of the prostitutes, including some who appeared to be as young as 15.\n\nThe investigation, which involved the Pittsfield Police Department, the state police detective unit, and the District Attorney's Office, revealed that Lambach had been involved in prostitution and trafficking for about 10 years. He allegedly threatened the women if he thought they were giving information to the police, and would often use violence to control them.\n\nLambach has pleaded not guilty to four counts of human trafficking, while Van Wert has pleaded not guilty to conspiracy to commit human trafficking, deriving support from prostitution, maintaining a house of prostitution, and sexual conduct for a fee. The investigation remains ongoing, and additional charges may be coming.\n\nThe case highlights the serious issue of human trafficking and prostitution in Massachusetts, and the need for law enforcement to take a proactive approach to investigating and prosecuting these crimes."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["You think Stonehenge is impressive? Archaeologists in Britain are excavating a monument ten times larger than the iconic structure, though it appears to the naked eye to be little more than farmland. A henge is a circular earthwork, and the one in question is Marden Henge, which sits a few miles north of Stonehenge in Wiltshire. National Geographic reports that roughly 4,500 years ago it featured 10-foot-tall earthen berms that encompassed some 40 acres. The Guardian writes that the henge was \"far larger than the Avebury or Stonehenge circles, and too large for any imaginable practical use.\" But over the ages Marden Henge's berms have \"slumped\" and farmers have worked the land, and archaeologists have gravitated to the more breathtaking Stonehenge. Until now. Jim Leary of the University of Reading has just kicked off a three-year study of the site; his excavations (he worked at the site in 2010, too) are the only ones to have occurred there in nearly 50 years. He hopes the berms will help explain the \"insane, utterly unsustainable\" construction boom that led to Marden Henge and four other nearby Neolithic monuments, including Stonehenge. \"Not nearly enough attention has been paid to the archaeology of the fertile valley in between these places,\" says Leary. So far he's found the remains of a 4,000-year-old teen wearing an amber necklace just outside Marden Henge, per the BBC; fancy arrowheads; and a stone building within the henge containing the bones of at least 13 pigs, suggesting a huge feast was held there. \"For all the attention that has been lavished on Stonehenge over the years, we may well find out that Marden was where it was really at during the Neolithic,\" he says. (There's a giant \"super henge\" under Stonehenge.)"], "length": 1428, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Archaeological Discoveries Unveil Secrets of Britain's Ancient Past**\n\nRecent excavations in Wiltshire, England, have uncovered a treasure trove of ancient artifacts and insights into the lives of Britain's prehistoric inhabitants. Two significant discoveries have been made at Marden Henge and Wilsford Henge, both located in the Vale of Pewsey, a region known for its rich archaeological heritage.\n\nAt Marden Henge, a 4,300-year-old house has been unearthed, believed to be one of the best-preserved examples of its kind in the UK. The rectangular building, which dates back to the Neolithic period, features beautifully worked flint arrowheads, decorated pottery, and a Roman brooch. Analysis of the site suggests that the house was used for gatherings and feasts, with pig bones and scorch marks indicating the presence of a charcoal firepit. The site's director, Dr. Jim Leary, believes that Marden Henge was a status symbol, showcasing the wealth and power of its builders.\n\nIn a separate discovery, a 4,000-year-old Bronze Age skeleton, believed to be that of a child, has been found at Wilsford Henge. The skeleton, which is approximately 1.5 meters in length, was discovered wearing an amber necklace and lying in a foetal position. Scientific analysis of the skeleton is expected to provide valuable insights into the child's diet, pathologies, and date of burial, as well as its place of origin.\n\nBoth discoveries are part of a larger excavation project, led by the University of Reading, which aims to gain a better understanding of the people who lived in the areas surrounding Stonehenge. The project, a partnership between the University of Reading, Historic England, the Arts and Humanities Research Council, and Wiltshire Museum, has already uncovered a range of artifacts, including flint arrowheads, decorated pottery, shale and copper bracelets, and a Roman brooch.\n\nThe discoveries at Marden Henge and Wilsford Henge are significant not only for their historical value but also for their potential to shed light on the lives of Britain's prehistoric inhabitants. As Dr. Leary notes, \"Finds from the first five weeks of the dig were exciting - but as so often during excavations, the best is revealed last.\" The ongoing excavation project is expected to continue for years to come, providing a wealth of new information about Britain's ancient past."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["As everyone wonders whether Conan O’Brien will stay at NBC or move to Fox, the Tonight Show host offered up a few more novel ideas yesterday: Perhaps he’ll “pretend to put my son in a giant foil balloon, then sit back and watch the offers come pouring in!” Watch him deliver the rest of his options on the video above—then check out Jay Leno, who also addressed the drama last night on his show. “NBC says the show performed exactly as they expected it would, and then they canceled it,” Leno said. “Now don’t confuse that with when we were on at late night and we performed better than expected and then they canceled us. That was totally different!” Meanwhile, the Los Angeles Times takes a closer look at Fox’s early talks with Conan—who “would be a very compatible fit for our brand,” says the president of Fox Entertainment."], "length": 791, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Summary of News Passages**\n\n**Financial News**\n\nThe article discusses the importance of understanding the formalities associated with obtaining fast cash loans, particularly those with no credit check. It highlights the potential risks of taking out emergency loans, including high fees and interest rates. However, it also emphasizes the benefits of these loans, which can provide quick access to cash for unexpected expenses such as medical bills, home renovations, and credit card dues.\n\nThe article also mentions the availability of installment payday loans, which can be repaid through comfortable monthly payments. These loans are designed to help borrowers manage their finances and avoid debt traps. Additionally, the article notes that these loans can be applied for online, making it a convenient option for those in need of quick cash.\n\n**Personal Finance Tips**\n\nThe article provides several personal finance tips, including the importance of saving for emergencies and avoiding predatory lenders. It advises readers to plan ahead and save for unexpected expenses, rather than relying on high-interest loans. The article also reminds readers that more loan money equals more interest fines, and encourages them to be cautious when taking out loans.\n\n**Lending Requirements**\n\nThe article outlines the requirements for getting approved for a payday loan in the UK. Borrowers must be permanent citizens of the UK, at least 18 years old, and in regular employment earning at least £1000 per month. Additionally, they must have a bank account that is not more than 3 months old.\n\n**Online Application Process**\n\nThe article explains the online application process for payday loans, which involves filling out a simple application form on the lender's website. The form is then verified, and if everything is in order, the loan is approved immediately.\n\n**Car Enthusiast News**\n\nThe article takes a break from financial news to discuss Jay's love of cars. It invites readers to share their favorite cars and provides a brief introduction to the world of automobiles.\n\n**Key Takeaways**\n\n* Fast cash loans can be a convenient option for unexpected expenses, but borrowers must be aware of the potential risks and fees.\n* Installment payday loans can provide a more manageable way to repay loans through monthly payments.\n* Saving for emergencies and avoiding predatory lenders is essential for good personal finance.\n* Online application processes make it easy to apply for payday loans.\n* Car enthusiasts are encouraged to share their favorite cars."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Oil giant BP lost $5.2 billion last year, but the company somehow saw fit to propose maximum bonuses for 2015 for its top executives, including a 20% pay increase for CEO Bob Dudley—a proposal that 59% of shareholders roundly rejected by proxy vote at Thursday's annual meeting, MarketWatch reports. The company had indicated earlier in the day that it may also have to reduce its dividend, the Wall Street Journal reports. Dudley is set to receive the full bonus he was eligible for, which comes to $4.2 million (including $1.4 million in cash and a portion in deferred BP shares), per an earlier MarketWatch report. This amount was bumped up from the $3 million ($1 million in cash) he received in 2014. BP's CFO was also on the list to rake in his full bonus. \"We think it sends the wrong message,\" a rep for shareholder Royal London Asset Management tells the BBC. \"It shows that the board is out of touch.\" Not only did BP suffer straight-up monetary losses as the price of oil fell, it also announced it will be getting rid of about 7,000 jobs and taking other belt-tightening measures. And the Financial Times notes that other energy company execs saw their pay slashed in 2015. But a BP spokesman says \"executives performed strongly in a difficult environment in 2015, managing the things they could control and for which they were accountable.\" Andy Critchlow, writing for the Reuters Breakingviews blog, agrees. \"Dudley has to work harder than his predecessors,\" he writes, noting the CEO has helped the company recover from the 2010 Deepwater Horizon disaster and improved safety protocol. \"The mild-mannered American has had possibly the toughest job in the oil industry. His rewards look in line with that task.\" Carl-Henric Svanberg, chairman of BP's board, says the nonbinding shareholder vote won't alter the payouts they've already decided on, but that the board will take investors' concerns into account when coming up with next year's compensation packages."], "length": 3225, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**BP Shareholders Rebel Against CEO's Pay Package**\n\nIn a stinging rebuke to Chief Executive Bob Dudley and his board, a majority of BP PLC's shareholders voted against the company's executive pay policy at the annual meeting on Thursday. Preliminary results showed 59% of investors voting by proxy rejected the company's executive compensation decisions for 2015, including a 20% increase in Dudley's total pay to $19.6 million.\n\nThe vote is non-binding, but it heaps pressure on Dudley and his board as they try to navigate low oil prices and increasing shareholder unease. Chairman Carl-Henric Svanberg defended the pay package, saying it was based on \"exceptional\" company performance during a difficult year. However, Svanberg also signaled that the company may have to reduce its dividend, a move that would be a first for the industry.\n\nThe opposition to Dudley's pay package is not surprising, given the company's $5.2 billion loss last year and the fact that other major energy company bosses took pay cuts in 2015. Glass Lewis, a leading shareholder advisory group, had previously criticized BP's decision to award its top directors their maximum bonuses for 2015, despite the company's lackluster performance.\n\nThe vote is seen as a growing trend of institutional investors and advisers taking a more aggressive stance on executive pay. Other companies, including Smith & Nephew and Citigroup, have also faced pressure to toughen bonus \"clawback\" regimes and reduce executive pay.\n\nBP's pay policy is subject to a binding shareholder vote every three years, and the company will need to revisit its compensation plan for next year. Svanberg has promised to review the terms on which pay is awarded in consultation with shareholders before next year.\n\nThe opposition to Dudley's pay package has been led by several major investors, including Aberdeen Asset Management and Royal London Asset Management. The Institute of Directors has also warned that the shareholder rebellion could determine the future of corporate governance in the UK.\n\nIn a statement, Svanberg said that the company would discuss possible changes to its compensation plan for next year, but he also emphasized that the board would not adjust Dudley's pay. The company's pay policy is seen as a key issue in the industry, and the outcome of this vote will be closely watched by investors and analysts."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The Peruvian cop who claimed to have busted a crime ring that killed dozens of people to harvest their fat has been suspended for lying. Felix Murga, the country's top organized crime investigator, said that a gang had killed 60 people to sell their fat at $15,000 a liter. Investigators now believe there was just one victim and his murder was linked to drug trafficking, the BBC reports. Murga appears to have revived an ancient Andean legend of killers who roam the mountains extracting fat from travelers, say authorities, who blame him for damaging the police force's reputation and scaring tourists away. \"This has been a ruse of bad taste,\" a local politician tells Reuters."], "length": 1088, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Peru's Top Investigator Suspended for Misleading Public**\n\nPeru's top organized crime investigator, Eusebio Felix, has been put on indefinite leave after he misled the public about a gang of serial killers who allegedly murdered up to 60 people and sold their fat. The investigation, which was initially hailed as a major breakthrough, has been widely criticized for its botched handling and sensationalized claims.\n\nAccording to police, the four suspects arrested in connection with the case had confessed to murdering people to sell their fat, which was allegedly sold for thousands of dollars a liter to Italian cosmetics makers. However, an investigation by police in Lima and Huanuco revealed that there may have been only one victim, and that the suspects may have bottled the victim's fat to intimidate their rivals in an area rife with drug trafficking and violence.\n\nThe police's initial claims were based on a legend about the \"Pishtacos,\" mythical killers who roam the Andes mountains extracting fat from travelers. However, anthropologists have criticized the police for foolishly believing the legend and using it to explain a crime that was likely linked to the cocaine trade.\n\nMedical experts have also dismissed the theory that human fat has monetary value, pointing out that it can be easily collected from plastic surgery clinics that perform tummy tucks. The police's claims have been widely ridiculed, with politicians and doctors criticizing the investigation for scaring away tourists and wasting resources.\n\nThe incident has damaged the reputation of Peru's police force, with the head of the police, General Miguel Hidalgo, apologizing for the botched investigation. The case has also raised questions about the use of sensationalized claims to distract the public from real issues facing Peru.\n\nIn related news, Peru's police chief has announced that the four suspects arrested in connection with the case will remain in custody while the investigation continues. The incident has sparked a wider debate about the handling of crime investigations in Peru and the need for greater transparency and accountability."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Hikers on the Pacific Crest Trail can expect to encounter harsh-but-beautiful landscapes, extreme fatigue, and—these days, at least—traffic. \"You can’t talk about the Pacific Crest Trail without mentioning the crowds,\" Pete Brook writes for Outside. He should know: Brook currently is trekking the 2,650-mile PCT, which runs from the Mexican border through California, Oregon, and Washington to the border with Canada. But don't take his word for it: The Pacific Crest Trail Association so far this year has issued more than 4,000 permits to people planning on tackling at least 500 miles of the trail. To put things in perspective: 300 people attempted to complete the entire PCT in 2006, according to the AP, with about 120 of those making it. In 2014, 1,000 people set off and about half completed the trail. More traffic means more stress on the land, more improperly disposed of poop, and more inexperienced hikers requiring rescue. So what's with the increase in traffic over the past few years? You can thank Cheryl Strayed and her enormously popular memoir-turned-major-motion-picture Wild. After the book came out in 2012, there was a small increase in interest in the trail, the PCTA's Jack Haskel told the AP in 2015. But after the movie, starring Reese Witherspoon, was released in 2014, interest in the PCT exploded. \"Millions are hearing about it now and are being inspired,\" Haskel said. Strayed, more or less unprepared for the journey, hiked 1,100 miles of the trail as a form of therapy as she grappled with personal demons. \"However for others that follow her strategy of deliberate ineptitude, things might not turn out so well,\" writes DontHikeLikeWild.org. \"Our advice to would-be hikers: Grow a spine first. Then get out there and hike the PCT.\" (This female hiker is the \"biggest badass you've never heard of.\")"], "length": 2416, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Pacific Crest Trail Sees Surge in Hikers Following \"Wild\" Movie**\n\nThe Pacific Crest Trail (PCT) is experiencing a significant increase in hikers, thanks in part to the popularity of Cheryl Strayed's memoir \"Wild\" and the subsequent movie adaptation. According to the Pacific Crest Trail Association (PCTA), the number of long-distance hiking permits issued has risen dramatically, with over 4,000 permits issued so far in 2016, compared to just 300 in 2006.\n\nThe influx of hikers has raised concerns about overcrowding and the potential impact on the trail and its environment. The PCTA has implemented a quota system, limiting the number of hikers to 50 per day, in an effort to mitigate wear and tear on the trail and prevent environmental damage. However, some hikers are expressing concerns that the quota system may not be enough to prevent overcrowding, and that the trail's fragile ecosystems may be at risk.\n\nThe PCTA is also urging hikers to practice \"leave no trace\" principles and to be mindful of their impact on the trail and its communities. The organization has launched a campaign, #responsiblywild, to promote safe and responsible hiking practices.\n\nDespite the concerns, many hikers are drawn to the PCT for its transformative power and natural beauty. Cheryl Strayed's story, in particular, has inspired many women to take on the challenge of hiking the PCT. According to the PCTA, the number of female hikers has increased significantly, with anecdotal evidence suggesting that women now make up around 30% of hikers.\n\nThe PCT is a challenging and rewarding experience that requires careful planning, physical endurance, and a commitment to safety and responsibility. While the surge in hikers may pose some challenges, it also presents opportunities for hikers to connect with others and experience the transformative power of the trail.\n\n**Key Statistics:**\n\n* Over 4,000 long-distance hiking permits issued in 2016\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Noticed Pete Davidson's absence from SNL as of late? Well, there's a good reason behind it: The 23-year-old has been focusing on his sobriety. After telling High Times last fall that he couldn't function without the medical marijuana he's prescribed for Crohn's disease, Davidson now says, \"I quit drugs and am happy and sober for the first time in eight years\" in an Instagram post. Rapper Kid Cudi might have inspired that sobriety, per the New York Daily News. Last fall, Davidson told a radio station, \"I would have killed myself if I didn't have Kid Cudi,\" who'd checked himself into rehab a few weeks earlier."], "length": 805, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Pete Davidson Opens Up About Sobriety and Medical Marijuana Use**\n\nComedian Pete Davidson, a cast member on Saturday Night Live, has been making headlines recently with his candid discussions about his health and sobriety. In a heartfelt post on Instagram, Davidson revealed that he has been sober for the first time in eight years, crediting his girlfriend, friends, and a newfound appreciation for life for his success.\n\nDavidson, who has been open about his struggles with Crohn's disease, has also been using medical marijuana to manage his symptoms. In an interview with High Times magazine, he explained that cannabis is the only thing that helps him eat and perform on SNL. \"I wouldn't be able to do 'SNL' if I didn't smoke weed,\" he said. \"I wouldn't be able to do anything really.\"\n\nDavidson's use of medical marijuana has been a topic of discussion, with him expressing frustration with the New York State medical marijuana program, which he finds difficult to navigate. He believes that cannabis should be legal beyond just medical purposes, and has been an outspoken advocate for its medicinal use.\n\nIn addition to his sobriety and medical marijuana use, Davidson has also spoken about the impact of music on his life. He credits Kid Cudi's music with saving his life, saying that the rapper's music helped him through a difficult time and prevented him from taking his own life.\n\nDavidson's return to social media and the show has been met with enthusiasm from fans, who have been supportive of his journey towards sobriety. His message of hope and resilience has resonated with many, and he has encouraged his followers to never give up, saying \"We are a family and I appreciate all your love and support.\"\n\nAs Davidson continues to navigate his health and sobriety, fans can look forward to seeing him on SNL, which premieres on October 1st, and in his upcoming stand-up special, \"SMD,\" which airs on October 29th."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Bryan Singer has already been hit with two lawsuits alleging sexual abuse of minors—and the lawyer who filed both of them, plus other suits against Hollywood bigwigs, says more are coming. \"I’ve heard from victims about very recent claims of being sexually exploited in Hollywood,\" Jeff Herman said at a news conference yesterday, according to Variety. He said one coming lawsuit involves another Hollywood \"sex ring.\" Herman also shared photos showing the second alleged victim (with his face redacted) and Singer at the London premiere of Singer's Superman Returns in 2006; John Doe, then 17, says Singer abused him after the premiere, the New York Daily News reports. As for the strenuous denials from all the Hollywood VIPs named in the various suits, Herman said, \"The pushback I’m getting reminds me of the early days of the clergy sex abuse scandal.\" Meanwhile, in an interview with the Daily Beast, Herman says he himself has vague memories of being sexually abused as a child. He says he's worked on some 800 sex abuse cases in his career, notably involving the Catholic Church and the Boy Scouts."], "length": 5805, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Sex Abuse Scandal Rocks Hollywood**\n\nA series of lawsuits has been filed against several high-profile Hollywood executives, including \"X-Men\" director Bryan Singer and Broadway producer Gary Goddard, alleging sexual abuse and exploitation of underage boys. The lawsuits, filed by Florida lawyer Jeff Herman, claim that Singer and Goddard preyed on vulnerable teenagers, including one who was 14 years old at the time, and subjected them to physical and emotional abuse.\n\nAccording to the lawsuits, Singer allegedly offered a Quaalude and plied a 17-year-old boy with alcohol before bringing him back to his London hotel suite with Goddard for the alleged sexual assault. The boy claims that Singer ordered him to sit on top of him and masturbate, and attempted to penetrate him anally. Goddard is also accused of sending the boy disturbing emails and gifts, including a box of Hershey's chocolates with a handwritten note signed \"Love, Gary.\"\n\nHerman, who has filed four similar suits in the past two weeks, claims that the allegations are not isolated incidents, but rather part of a larger pattern of abuse and exploitation in Hollywood. He has promised to file more lawsuits in the future, including one related to another alleged sex ring operating in Hollywood.\n\nSinger and Goddard have denied the allegations, with Singer branding the lawsuits a \"shakedown\" and Goddard's lawyer calling the claims \"false and defamatory.\" However, Herman has accused the defendants of engaging in a \"blanket denial\" similar to the early days of the clergy sex abuse scandals in the Catholic Church.\n\nThe lawsuits have sparked a wave of outrage and calls for greater accountability in Hollywood. Herman has said that he has been contacted by other possible molestation victims connected to Hollywood, and plans to file more lawsuits in the future. The scandal has also led to a petition to remove Singer's name from the University of Southern California's film school.\n\nAs the scandal continues to unfold, Herman has spoken out about the importance of giving a voice to victims of sex abuse and exploitation. He has also emphasized the need for greater awareness and education about the issue, and has developed an app to help identify potential predators.\n\nThe lawsuits against Singer and Goddard are just the latest in a series of high-profile cases of sex abuse and exploitation in Hollywood. The scandal has raised questions about the industry's handling of allegations of abuse and exploitation, and has sparked calls for greater accountability and reform."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The dad of the teenage lesbian shot dead in a Texas park called for justice yesterday. He immediately had a \"bad feeling\" when his 19-year-old daughter, Mollie, failed to show up for work, said Mario Olgin. “It was not like Mollie,\" he told KIII-TV. \"If she had some place to be she was going to be there.” Mollie and her 18-year-old girlfriend, Mary Kristene Chapa, had been shot in the head by an unknown assailant. Mollie died at the scene, but Chapa is making an \"amazing\" recovery, her brother told NBC Latino. Mollie has just finished her first semester of college and dreamed of becoming a psychiatrist. \"She was happy,\" Olgin recalled. Now, \"she's my guardian angel. I know she's looking down on us, in a better place.\" He's confident police will find her killer. \"Justice will be served,\" he declared. Texas Rangers have joined Portland police in continuing to investigate the crime, but haven't yet discussed any progress. They haven't labeled the shootings a hate crime, but aren't ruling it out."], "length": 1296, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Double Shooting in Portland, Texas: Investigation Ongoing**\n\nOn Saturday, a double shooting occurred at Violet Andrews Park in Portland, Texas, leaving one victim dead and another critically injured. 19-year-old Mollie Judith Olgin was killed, while her girlfriend, 18-year-old Mary Kristene Chapa, survived the attack despite being shot in the head. Chapa is currently in the intensive care unit and is making a remarkable recovery, with her brother, Hilario Chapa, reporting that she is \"doing amazing\" and has made significant physical progress.\n\nThe investigation into the shooting is ongoing, with the Texas Rangers assisting the Portland Police Department. While there is no evidence to suggest that the attack was a hate crime, authorities are not ruling it out. The police chief has stated that the investigation is still in its early stages, and no suspect has been identified.\n\nThe families of both victims have received an outpouring of support from the community and across the country. Mollie Olgin's father, Mario Olgin, has expressed confidence that justice will be served and that his daughter's killer will be brought to trial. He has also spoken about his daughter's dreams and aspirations, including her goal of becoming a psychiatrist.\n\nChapa's family has also received support from the gay community, with a Facebook event organized to raise awareness and funds for her medical expenses. The family has set up a donation page on WePay to help cover the costs of her treatment.\n\nA memorial service for Mollie Olgin will be held on Friday at 4:30 p.m. at Limbaugh Funeral Home in Portland, followed by a candlelight vigil at the Violet Andrews Park. The community is coming together to support the families of both victims and to ensure that justice is served.\n\n**Key Developments:**\n\n* Mary Kristene Chapa is making a remarkable recovery after being shot in the head\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Scientists are now saying there may have been \"a more colorful Jurassic World than we previously imagined,\" thanks to the recent discovery of a fossil in China's Hebei province. Reuters reports that a closer look at the \"exquisitely preserved,\" almost completely intact fossil of a crow-like dinosaur that lived about 161 million years ago revealed that Caihong juji had luminous, brilliantly hued feathers that closely resembled those of hummingbirds. The study in the journal Nature Communications documenting the find explains that \"Caihong\" means \"rainbow\" in Mandarin. Scientists think the colorful plumage, which appeared to have covered the bony-crested creature's head, neck, and chest areas, may have kept the dinosaur warm, as well as attracted potential mates; National Geographic compares it to peacock feathers. So how were researchers able to tell what color feathers the creature had from preserved bones? They used high-tech microscopes able to home in on 66 sites on the fossil, detecting tiny cell structures called melanosomes, which underlie pigmentation. Which colors they led to can be found by their shapes, and the melanosomes in Caihong were long and flat like pancakes—similar to those found in hummingbirds, which boast iridescent plumage. Although they can't pick out from the Pantone wheel Caihong's exact colors, researchers say the creature likely sported feathers with a \"rainbow glimmer.\" Study co-author Xing Zu tells National Geographic that Caihong was a predator that spent its days gliding from tree to tree. And \"glide\" is the operative word, as this dinosaur likely didn't fly: Its feathers were located on its tail, not on its wings like birds, Discover notes. (A study postulated that nearly all dinosaurs had feathers.)"], "length": 7411, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Rainbow Dinosaur Discovered in China**\n\nA team of scientists has announced the discovery of a new species of dinosaur, Caihong juji, which lived 161 million years ago in what is now northeastern China. The dinosaur, which means \"rainbow with a big crest\" in Mandarin, is a small, bird-like theropod with a distinctive crest on its head and iridescent feathers on its head, neck, and chest.\n\n**Unique Feathers**\n\nThe discovery of Caihong juji has shed new light on the evolution of feathers in dinosaurs. The dinosaur's feathers are unlike any others found in fossils, with a unique combination of long, flat melanosomes that are organized into sheets. These melanosomes are similar to those found in the iridescent throat feathers of hummingbirds, and are thought to have given Caihong a rainbow glimmer.\n\n**Social or Sexual Display**\n\nThe scientists believe that the iridescent feathers on Caihong's head and chest may have been used for social or sexual display, similar to the way modern peacocks use their brightly colored tails to attract mates. The dinosaur's frond-like tail, which has asymmetrical feathers, may have also been used for display purposes.\n\n**Flight Capabilities**\n\nWhile Caihong is a small dinosaur, it has many bird-like characteristics, including the shape of its wings and the structure of its feathers. The scientists believe that Caihong may have been able to glide from tree to tree, and may have even had some flight capabilities.\n\n**Evolutionary Significance**\n\nThe discovery of Caihong juji is significant because it provides new insights into the evolution of feathers and flight in dinosaurs. The dinosaur's unique feathers and flight capabilities make it an important find for scientists studying the origins of birds.\n\n**Other Interesting Facts**\n\n* Caihong juji is the oldest known animal to have asymmetrical feathers, a key adaptation for flight.\n* The dinosaur's frond-like tail is similar to that of the iconic bird-like dinosaur Archaeopteryx, but is much older.\n* The scientists were able to determine the color of Caihong's feathers by analyzing the melanosomes in the fossil.\n* The discovery of Caihong juji is a reminder of the importance of continued research into the evolution of dinosaurs and the origins of birds."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["In direct contrast to the \"delirious joy\" in Bahrain, the death toll keeps climbing in Libya's protests. Moammar Gadhafi's minions killed another 20 people today, bringing the five-day total to at least 104, says Human Rights Watch. Gadhafi has effectively shut off Internet service and forbid media coverage, but witnesses told AP of attacks by police and government loyalists wielding guns, knives, and even anti-aircraft missiles. While the accounts are impossible to verify—and at least one puts the death toll closer to 200—a grim cycle has emerged, reports the New York Times: \"Security forces fire on funeral marches, killing more protesters and creating more funerals.\" In Yemen, meanwhile, President Ali Abdullah Saleh's forces also opened fire on protesters, killing at least one, reports AP."], "length": 3050, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Middle East Protests Escalate as Governments Crack Down**\n\nThe wave of protests sweeping across the Middle East has taken a deadly turn as governments in Libya, Yemen, and Bahrain have responded with force to quell the demonstrations. The protests, which began in Tunisia and Egypt, have now spread to Algeria, Jordan, and the Kurdistan region of northern Iraq, with demonstrators calling for an end to authoritarian rule and the establishment of democratic governments.\n\nIn Libya, security forces fired on pro-democracy demonstrators in the city of Benghazi, killing at least 15 people and wounding many more. The government, led by Moammar Gadhafi, has been accused of using excessive force to crush the protests, which have been ongoing for five days. The Libyan government has also cut off the internet and restricted access to the city, making it difficult for protesters to communicate and organize.\n\nIn Yemen, riot police opened fire on thousands of protesters in the capital city of Sanaa, killing one person and injuring five others. The government, led by President Ali Abdullah Saleh, has been accused of using violence to suppress the protests, which have been ongoing for 10 days. Saleh has promised not to seek re-election in 2013, but protesters are demanding his immediate resignation.\n\nIn Bahrain, the government has withdrawn its military forces from the streets after two days of bloody crackdowns on protesters. However, protesters remain wary of the government's intentions and have set up barriers and a makeshift medical tent in the capital's central Pearl Square. The protests in Bahrain, which began with calls to weaken the Sunni monarchy's power and address claims of discrimination against the Shiite majority, have escalated into a full-blown uprising.\n\nIn Algeria, police broke up a rally by thousands of pro-democracy supporters, using clubs and shields to disperse the crowd. The government, led by President Abdelaziz Bouteflika, has promised to lift the state of emergency, which has been in place since 1992, but has warned that a ban on protests in Algiers will remain in place.\n\nThe international community has condemned the use of force by governments in the region, with US President Barack Obama calling on Bahrain to respect the \"universal rights\" of its people and embrace \"meaningful reform.\" The European Union has also called for restraint and dialogue in the region.\n\nThe protests in the Middle East have been driven by a desire for democratic change and an end to authoritarian rule. The region's governments, which have long been criticized for their human rights records and corruption, are"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Most teens don't find music history in their dad's stuff, but Maggie Poukkula's dad is Seattle musician Tony Poukkula, who was a friend of Kurt Cobain. Her find? Photos of Nirvana's first concert, performed by then 20-year-old Cobain and his new band in March 1987 in Tony Poukkula's basement. \"My dad showed me them awhile back, but he never mentioned that's what was going on in the photos,\" Maggie Poukkula, 19, tells Rolling Stone. \"I found out because of all the articles. I didn't realize it was such a historical thing. I thought they were just cool pictures of my dad and Kurt jamming together.\" She tweeted the photos last week, and the post quickly went viral. That first concert featured the early Nirvana lineup of Cobain, bassist Krist Novoselic, and drummer Aaron Burckhard playing original songs and Led Zeppelin covers. Similar early-days footage of Cobain and Co. featured in the recent documentary Montage of Heck has renewed interest in the grunge band. The Emmy-nominated project includes home movies, never-before-heard music, and intimate interviews with Cobain's family and friends, notes SPIN. After being critically acclaimed at festivals across the country, the film will return to theaters on Aug. 7, adds Rolling Stone. (Want a little more Cobain history? Here's the mix tape he made at 21.)"], "length": 751, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Rare Nirvana Photos and Web Archiving Efforts Make Headlines**\n\nTwo separate news stories have made waves in the music and technology worlds. First, rare photographs from Nirvana's first concert in March 1987 have been unearthed by Maggie Poukkula, the 19-year-old daughter of Tony Poukkula, a member of Seattle band Laytem who hosted the fledgling outfit's first show in his basement. The photos, which feature Kurt Cobain and Poukkula's father playing together, have been shared on social media and provide a unique glimpse into the early days of the iconic band. The photos come on the heels of a deluge of rare Cobain and Nirvana artifacts, many of which were incorporated into the acclaimed documentary \"Montage of Heck.\"\n\nMeanwhile, the Internet Archive has launched an effort to archive web pages and preserve links to referenced content. The goal is to fix broken links on the web by creating a permanent record of pages as they are created and referencing the archived versions when links are broken. This effort, part of the \"No More 404\" initiative, aims to preserve the integrity of online content and provide a way for users to access original versions of pages that have been changed or removed.\n\nIn related news, \"Montage of Heck\" is set to return to movie theaters starting August 7th, following a successful festival run and limited theatrical release. The documentary, which features unprecedented access to Kurt Cobain's archives, has been widely praised for its unique insight into the life and music of the Nirvana frontman."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Both of TV's \"Fat Ladies\" have sung. Clarissa Dickson Wright, one half of the BBC's \"Two Fat Ladies\" cooking duo, died in Edinburgh Saturday at age 66, the BBC reports. Wright was a former lawyer who filmed four of the \"Fat Ladies\" series, going on food-related road trips across the UK in a motorbike and sidecar with Jennifer Paterson, before Paterson died in 1999 from cancer. The New York Times describes Wright as a \"rebel,\" both hosts as \"irreverent and eccentric,\" and the recipes as \"sometimes confounding.\" Wright's eclectic working life also included stints as a cook, an author, and a cookbook shop manager; she also ran a catering business, was a guild butcher, and once worked on a yacht in the Caribbean. In fact, she recently said, \"I've had a fantastic life and I've done everything I could have wanted to do and more.\" It wasn't until her 40s, after she'd recovered from alcoholism, that she got into cooking seriously. As for the perhaps-controversial title of the show that brought her fame? \"If you're fat you're fat,\" she once said. \"I hate this modern-day political correctness, that you don't call things by their proper name.\" Her agent remembers Wright similarly in a statement: \"Loved dearly by her friends and many fans all over the world, Clarissa was utterly non-PC and fought for what she believed in, always, with no thought to her own personal cost.\" There's no word on Wright's cause of death, but the Guardian reports that she had been undergoing treatment at a hospital since the beginning of the year."], "length": 2870, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Clarissa Dickson Wright: A Life of Passion, Food, and Controversy**\n\nClarissa Dickson Wright, the larger-than-life co-star and co-chef of the popular British television show \"Two Fat Ladies,\" has passed away at the age of 66. Born on June 24, 1947, in London, Dickson Wright was a brilliant cook, food historian, and champion of hunting and shooting. She was known for her fiery temper, strong opinions, and unapologetic approach to life.\n\nDickson Wright's life was marked by both triumph and tragedy. She grew up in a wealthy family, but her father's abuse and alcoholism had a profound impact on her. She became a barrister at 21, but her drinking career soon took over, leading to a series of bankruptcies and a tumultuous personal life. However, after quitting drinking on her 40th birthday, Dickson Wright discovered a new passion for cooking and went on to become a celebrated food writer and television personality.\n\nAs one half of the \"Two Fat Ladies\" duo, Dickson Wright and her partner Jennifer Paterson traveled the UK in a motorcycle-sidecar, cooking up a storm and entertaining audiences with their irreverent humor and eccentricity. The show, which ran from 1996 to 1999, was a huge success and led to several books, including \"Cooking With the Two Fat Ladies\" and \"A History of English Food.\"\n\nAfter Paterson's death in 1999, Dickson Wright continued to work in television, fronting several shows, including \"Breakfast, Lunch and Dinner\" and \"Clarissa and the Countryman.\" She was also a vocal advocate for rural life and hunting, causing controversy with her views on the badger cull and her suggestion that people should eat the meat of culled badgers.\n\nThroughout her life, Dickson Wright was known for her intelligence, wit, and unapologetic approach to life. She was a true original, and her passing has left a void in the world of food and television. As her agent said, \"Her fun and laughter, extraordinary learning and intelligence, will be missed always, by so many of us.\""}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A hiker's fatal fall in March has been blamed on using a smartphone app map instead of an actual map. The BBC reports Jane Wilson and her husband Gary were hiking on Tryfan in Wales when it started to get dark and they decided to skip the summit. While attempting to descend the mountain, Gary was looking at an Ordnance Survey app while Jane went a short way ahead to make sure the path was safe, according to the Telegraph. The Manchester Evening News reports Gary heard his wife fall, got to safety, and called for help. Rescuers found the 53-year-old librarian's body at the bottom of a 30-foot cliff with a fractured skull. At a hearing Friday, Jane's was ruled an accidental death due, in part, to using an app instead of a map. \"Apart from potential difficulties caused by poor detail on an electronic map, batteries on mobile phones have a nasty habit of running out just when you need them most,\" the Telegraph quotes a spokesperson for Mountain Rescue England and Wales as saying. A detective says the app map \"would have been small and not as detailed\" and took the couple on a route that was not the safest. A director with Ordnance Survey says hikers should always use the app in conjunction with an actual map \"due to the nature of mobile devices when navigating the real world.\" Authorities also say Jane wasn't wearing appropriate footwear for the hike. (Go inside the futile search for \"Inchworm\" on the trail.)"], "length": 1566, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Mountain Walker Dies in Fall While Using Smartphone App**\n\nA 53-year-old woman, Jane Wilson, fell to her death while attempting to navigate a mountain in Snowdonia, Wales, using a smartphone app. Wilson, an experienced mountain walker and librarian at the University of Manchester, was with her husband, Gary, on March 25 when they decided not to reach the summit of Tryfan. Instead, they used Mr. Wilson's phone to guide them across the mountain's west face, a route known for its loose rocks and steep inclines.\n\nAccording to the inquest, Wilson went ahead of her husband to look for a safe route and asked him if it was okay to proceed. However, she fell 20-30 feet down a vertical cliff and tumbled another 150 meters down into a rocky area. Her husband, who had limited first-aid knowledge, climbed to a safer ledge and raised the alarm, but unfortunately, Wilson's injuries were too severe.\n\nThe inquest heard that the couple had been mountain walking and scrambling for six years and that Wilson was a fit and active woman. However, the coroner noted that the use of a smartphone app to navigate the mountain may have contributed to the accident. The app's image was small and not as detailed as a proper map, and the couple may have misjudged the route.\n\nMountain rescue experts have warned against relying solely on electronic navigation aids, urging walkers to carry a paper map and compass as well. The Ogwen Valley Mountain Rescue Team, which responded to the call, has also highlighted the dangers of the west face of Tryfan, which is responsible for about 30% of the team's incidents.\n\nThe coroner recorded a conclusion of accidental death, and the incident serves as a reminder of the importance of careful planning and preparation when venturing into the mountains."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Mitch Daniels has some advice for those who are writing Rick Perry's political eulogy: Hold your horses. \"It's way too early to know, or to issue, to pronounce last rites over one performance,\" Daniels said today. \"There's still many of these (debates), too many maybe. ... I'd cut him some slack and give him a little time.\" Politico notes that Daniels also had kind words for Democrat Sen. Mark Warner's work on the Gang of Six, saying, \"It's very encouraging, by the way, to hear a guy like Sen. Warner speak to it as clearly as he just did. I wish he were president.\" More fun on your Sunday dial, as per Politico: Michael Bloomberg on President Obama's 2012 chances: \"He would be a credible, formidable candidate. You're going to have a real horse race no matter who the Republican nominee is.\" Reince Priebus to Debbie Wasserman Schultz on 2012: \"It sounds like the new slogan is no longer 'hope and change.' It's, 'Hey, it could've been worse.' Great bumper sticker Debbie, I hope it works for you.\" David Plouffe on a White House shakeup: \"I don't expect that.\" Obama is \"confident in his team, in the direction we've laid out here.\" Warner on Congress' standoff over a short-term spending bill: \"It is embarrassing. The Senate is saying, 'Why should we build schools in Iraq on the credit card but expect that rebuilding schools in Joplin, Mo.,'\" will be paid for. Warner blames \"a group, more centered in the House, in terms of some of these Tea Party Republicans, who say on every issue, 'We're going to make this a make-or-break.'\" Lindsey Graham on Pakistan: \"We need to put Pakistan on notice. We're going to have to put all options on the table. including defending our troops. It's now a time of choosing, so I hope they choose wisely.\""], "length": 1454, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Summary of Current News**\n\n**Politics**\n\n* New York Mayor Michael Bloomberg praised President Barack Obama as a \"credible, formidable candidate\" for reelection, saying he would be a strong opponent in the 2012 election.\n* Bloomberg also praised Texas Gov. Rick Perry and former Massachusetts Gov. Mitt Romney for their past achievements, despite their struggles in the Republican presidential campaign.\n* Indiana Gov. Mitch Daniels defended Perry's debate performances, saying it's too early to write him off.\n* Republican National Committee Chairman Reince Priebus mocked Obama's 2008 election slogan, saying it won't resonate with voters in 2012.\n\n**Congress**\n\n* Sen. Mark Warner (D-VA) called the threat of a government shutdown \"embarrassing\" and blamed tea party-affiliated Republicans for the stalemate.\n* Sen. Lamar Alexander (R-TN) countered that Majority Leader Harry Reid (D-NV) is responsible for holding up the bill.\n\n**Foreign Policy**\n\n* Sen. Lindsey Graham (R-SC) said the US needs to leave \"all options on the table\" in dealing with Pakistan after US military officials accused the country's intelligence officials of assisting terrorist operations.\n* President Obama's senior adviser, David Plouffe, said the US may continue reducing aid to Pakistan if it can't do more to impede anti-American terrorism.\n\n**White House Staff**\n\n* Plouffe said he doesn't expect significant changes in top White House staff or the Cabinet ahead of the 2012 reelection campaign.\n\n**Economy**\n\n* Plouffe urged Congress to pass Obama's American Jobs Act, saying the President is \"confident in his team\" and has a \"good plan\" to create jobs.\n\n**Other News**\n\n* Sen. Mark Warner (D-VA) praised Sen. Mark Warner (D-VA) for his work with the Gang of Six senators to cut spending from the federal budget.\n* New Jersey Gov. Chris Christie, who is considering entering the GOP presidential race, was praised by Bloomberg as a \"good governor.\""}
{"input": "", "answers": ["\"That new soda tax in Seattle is working out about as well as Chicago's,\" proclaims a headline at Hot Air. It's not a compliment. The tax, which went into effect Jan. 1, slaps an additional 1.75 cents on each fluid ounce of sugar-sweetened drinks, a group that includes soda, sports drinks, and kombucha. That's nearly double the one-cent levy the Chicago-encompassing Cook County tried, a tax it repealed after about two months. The Chicago Tribune reported at the time that Costco's nine affected locations saw their sales of beverages impacted by the tax fall 34%, while sales jumped 38% at locations just beyond the county line. And Costco is again at the fore of what's happening: Because bulk purchases contain significantly more ounces than an individual one, the impact is starker, as KIRO discovered when it spotted an updated Costco sign for Gatorade in Seattle. The sign details Costco's price for the 35-pack of 16-ounce bottles—$15.99—and separately lists the city's $10.34 tax, for a new total of $26.33. But that's not all the sign says: It also directs consumers to nearby locations that are outside city limits and exempt from the tax. The tax is meant to fight obesity and raise funds for worthy expenditures, but the blog for Citizens Against Government Waste sees the ends quite differently: \"Let’s be clear. Soda taxes don’t make people healthier. They don't raise revenue—they drive it outside city limits. They don’t help ease inequality—they make the poor poorer.\" But some in government seemingly remain hopeful: KXLY reports a bill was reintroduced Monday that would push the tax statewide. (Speaking of Seattle and beverages, the world's largest Starbucks is no longer located there.)"], "length": 3161, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Soda Tax Backlash: Seattle and Cook County Experience Failures**\n\nIn recent months, two major cities in the United States have implemented a sweetened beverage tax, only to face widespread backlash from consumers and businesses. Seattle, Washington, and Cook County, Illinois, both introduced a tax on sugary drinks, aiming to reduce sugar consumption and raise revenue for important projects. However, the results have been disastrous.\n\nIn Seattle, the tax, which adds 1.75 cents per ounce to sweetened beverages, has led to a significant increase in prices, with some drinks doubling in cost. Costco, a popular retailer, has taken a creative approach by posting signs directing customers to locations outside the city limits where they can buy the same products without the tax. As a result, many Seattle residents are voting with their feet, buying drinks in the suburbs and depriving the city of the revenue it claims to need.\n\nIn Cook County, the tax, which was implemented in August 2017, was repealed just two months later after a tremendous public outcry. The tax, which added a penny per ounce to sugar and artificially sweetened beverages, led to a 34% decline in sales of affected beverages at Costco's nine Cook County locations. Many retailers and restaurants reported significant losses, with some even laying off workers.\n\nThe failures of these soda taxes have been attributed to several factors, including the regressive nature of the tax, which disproportionately affects low-income families. Additionally, the tax has been criticized for not applying to sugar-heavy beverages sold at Starbucks, creating an uneven playing field.\n\nProponents of the tax argue that it is a necessary measure to reduce sugar consumption and improve public health. However, critics argue that the tax is a form of \"nanny statism\" that oversteps the role of government in regulating individual behavior.\n\nThe failures of these soda taxes have significant implications for policymakers and businesses. As one Costco executive noted, \"You're displacing shopping from one area, you're creating congestion in another, and it's just counterproductive.\" The experience of Seattle and Cook County serves as a warning to other cities and states considering similar taxes.\n\nIn related news, a bill to introduce a statewide soda tax in Washington state has been re-introduced, but local businesses and residents are expressing concerns about the potential impact on their communities. As one business owner noted, \"It's just another cost that we're trying to overcome.\""}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A birthday party in California turned tragic after the guest of honor was run over and killed by his friend. Police say Jonathan Carlyle Merkley was celebrating his 34th birthday early Sunday at a hotel near San Diego when a woman decided to leave the party. Merkley wanted her to stay and reportedly walked toward her BMW and laid down in front of what the Orange County Register describes as a moving car. The woman didn’t stop, ran him over, and kept going. Police cite witnesses as saying both had been drinking, Fox 5 reports. Merkley, who suffered major chest trauma, died about 45 minutes later at a hospital. Police tracked the woman down and a car that \"may have been involved\" was impounded, cops tell the San Diego Union-Tribune. The woman was not arrested but police said on Tuesday that the investigation is continuing. \"There's a lot we still need to find out,\" says San Diego Police Sgt. Tim Underwood."], "length": 885, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Tragic Birthday Party Incident: Man Killed in Hit-and-Run**\n\nA 34-year-old man, Jonathan Carlyle Merkley, was killed in a hit-and-run incident on Sunday morning after trying to prevent his female friend from leaving his birthday party at the Staybridge Suites hotel in San Diego. According to police, Merkley, a resident of Garden Grove, had been celebrating his birthday with friends at the hotel when the unidentified woman, who was also a friend, attempted to leave. Merkley, in an attempt to stop her, walked into the path of her BMW and laid down in front of the moving car.\n\nTragically, the woman did not stop and ran over Merkley, causing major trauma to his chest. He was rushed to a hospital, where he died about 45 minutes later. The woman, who has not been named, was identified by police and was interviewed by detectives, but she has not been arrested as of Tuesday.\n\nPolice have impounded a car that \"may have been involved\" in the crash, and the investigation is ongoing. The woman's culpability in the incident is still being evaluated, and it is unclear whether she knew she had hit Merkley.\n\nThe incident has sparked outrage in the community, with many expressing shock and sadness at the senseless tragedy. A hotel guest, Gloria Williams, was quoted as saying, \"I think this is crazy, we don’t value each others lives.\"\n\nThe San Diego Police Department is urging anyone with information about the incident to come forward and contact them or Crime Stoppers at 888-580-8477.\n\nAs the investigation continues, the community is left to mourn the loss of a young man who was celebrating a milestone birthday with friends."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["It turns out missing people are pretty easy to find when they appear every week in your living room. The San Francisco Chronicle reports a 22-year-old woman reported missing in November in California was found this week—because she's a current contestant on The Bachelor. The strange story started when Bekah Martinez's mom reported her missing to the Humboldt County Sheriff's Office on Nov. 18. According to BuzzFeed, Martinez's mom said her daughter had gone to the area to work on a marijuana farm and she hadn't heard from her in a week. The Sheriff's Office was unable to find Martinez or contact anyone who might know her, so Martinez was added to the California Department of Justice's list of missing people. That list was the basis for a story in the North Coast Journal about the high number of missing people in Humboldt County. The newspaper shared the story Thursday on Facebook and asked readers if they recognized any of the missing people. Amy O'Brien did. \"I was like wait a minute, she looks so familiar,\" O'Brien tells the Chronicle. \"I instantly thought of The Bachelor.\" The Journal informed the sheriff's office, which contacted Martinez and removed her from the list. \"MOM. how many times do I have to tell you I don’t get cell service on The Bachelor??,\" Martinez tweeted after learning she was a missing person. The Bachelor started filming in September, and it's unclear if Martinez was still filming when she was reported missing. She tweets \"the scariest thing\" about being a missing person \"is that my efforts to conceal The Worst Drivers License Photo Of All Time have been thwarted\""], "length": 4630, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Humboldt County's High Rate of Missing Persons: A Complex Issue**\n\nHumboldt County, California, has been identified as having the highest per-capita rate of missing persons reports in the state. According to a recent analysis by the North Coast Journal, between 2000 and 2016, Humboldt County averaged 717 missing persons reports per 100,000 residents, compared to the statewide average of 384. The county's high rate of missing persons is attributed to various factors, including the local cannabis industry, which attracts people from throughout the state and nation, often leading to extended periods of disconnection from family and friends.\n\n**The Humboldt 35**\n\nThe North Coast Journal's investigation focused on the 35 individuals listed as missing in Humboldt County on the California Attorney General's website. Among them is Rebekah Martinez, a 22-year-old woman who was reported missing in November 2017 after she failed to contact her mother. However, it was later discovered that Martinez was actually a contestant on the reality TV show \"The Bachelor.\" Her case highlights the complexities of missing persons reports, where individuals may be intentionally avoiding contact with family and friends.\n\n**Investigations and Challenges**\n\nLaw enforcement officials in Humboldt County emphasize that many missing persons cases are resolved within days or weeks, with most individuals being found safe and sound. However, some cases remain unsolved, and the process of searching for missing loved ones can be frustrating for families. Vikki Joseph, whose brother Jeff Joseph disappeared in 2014, has been working closely with the Humboldt County Sheriff's Office to investigate his case, but she feels that the process has been slow and uncooperative.\n\n**Cannabis Industry Connection**\n\nThe cannabis industry has been identified as a primary factor contributing to Humboldt County's high rate of missing persons. Many individuals involved in the industry come to the area to work on remote mountains, where cell phone service is limited, and may be out of contact with family and friends for extended periods. Law enforcement officials acknowledge that these cases can be particularly challenging to investigate, as individuals may be intentionally avoiding contact.\n\n**National Database and DNA Matching**\n\nThe National Missing and Unidentified Persons System (NamUs) is a federal database that connects missing persons cases with caseworkers who compare them against reports and look for patterns. The system also allows family members to submit DNA samples for comparison against unidentified bodies. In one case, a 15-year-old boy who went missing in 2011 was positively matched to a DNA sample"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Let's hope Snooki sorta planned that baby she's reportedly carrying (stop giggling). Because if she didn't, that fetus has to be nearly swimming in booze by now. While Forbes is concerned about what's to become of the diminutive reality star's \"brand,\" others are worried about the drinking habits of the pregnant 24-year-old, who seems to spend very few minutes sober on Jersey Shore. \"Is there a test for fetal alcohol syndrome at three months in utero?\" wonders crabbygolightly.com. \"Quick, word association test,\" demands studybreaks.com, \"because all that comes to mind\" for the pregnant \"diva of debauchery\" is \"fetal alcohol syndrome\"—a leading cause of mental retardation. The reported pregnancy poses problems for Snooki and JWoww's spin-off, and for Jersey Shore, where the cast spends \"approximately 97.2% of their time being absolutely trashed,\" notes studybreaks. Snooki has some qualms about her hard-partying ways, once telling Ellen DeGeneres: \"I want to remember my night, and sometimes I just don't. It sucks. You're like, 'What did I do? Why did I wake up in a garbage can?'\""], "length": 8992, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Reality TV Star Snooki's Pregnancy and Fetal Alcohol Syndrome**\n\nNicole \"Snooki\" Polizzi, a reality TV star from the show \"Jersey Shore,\" has announced that she is pregnant with her first child. Despite her initial denials, sources close to the star confirm that she is indeed expecting a baby with her boyfriend, Jionni LaValle. This news comes as a surprise to many, as Snooki has built her career on her partying and reckless behavior, which may now be impacted by her pregnancy.\n\nThe pregnancy has raised concerns about the potential risks of fetal alcohol syndrome (FAS), a condition that can occur when a mother drinks alcohol during pregnancy. FAS is a group of conditions that can result from prenatal alcohol exposure, including physical and neurodevelopmental problems. According to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), FAS is the most severe form of the condition, and it can cause a range of problems, including growth deficiency, facial abnormalities, and central nervous system damage.\n\nThe risk of FAS is higher when a mother drinks heavily during pregnancy, and it is estimated that 1.5% of births in the United States are affected by FAS. However, the exact mechanism of FAS is still not fully understood, and more research is needed to determine the effects of moderate drinking during pregnancy.\n\nSnooki's pregnancy has also raised questions about her ability to provide for her child and her career plans. As a reality TV star, Snooki has built her fame on her partying and reckless behavior, but her pregnancy may now impact her ability to continue this lifestyle. In an interview with Ellen DeGeneres, Snooki admitted that she may occasionally overdo it and that living with reckless abandon is not always as fun as it may seem.\n\nIn related news, the CDC has estimated that 3 million women in the United States are at risk of having a baby with FASD, and the Surgeon General has recommended that women abstain from alcohol use while pregnant or while planning a pregnancy. The only certain way to prevent FAS is to avoid drinking alcohol during pregnancy, and women who are planning to become pregnant are advised to abstain from alcohol use.\n\nOverall, Snooki's pregnancy has raised important questions about the risks of FAS and the importance of avoiding alcohol use during pregnancy. As a reality TV star, Snooki's behavior has been closely watched, and her pregnancy may now impact her ability to continue her career."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Myrna Arias didn't like the GPS app on her phone that constantly tracked her, so she uninstalled it. The problem: Arias' iPhone was issued by her employer, which required her to run the app constantly, and after she removed it, the California woman was fired. Now she's suing her former employer, money transfer service Intermex, for invasion of privacy, unfair business practices, and retaliation, among other things, Ars Technica reports. Arias started researching the app, then called Xora—its website touts the app's ability to let employers \"instantly see where their employees are and where they have been\"—and eventually she and some of her colleagues asked a trainer from the app company to clarify. Boss John Stubits then, per the suit, \"admitted that employees would be monitored while off duty and bragged that he knew how fast she was driving at specific moments.\" Arias says she has no problem being monitored while at work, but she complained to Stubits about being tracked in her free time; he was unsympathetic and said she should have no problem because of how much she was being paid by Intermex. \"Her manager made it clear that he was using the program to continuously monitor her, during company as well as personal time,\" the complaint states, noting that he told Arias she needed to keep her phone on at all times in case a customer called. Arias' attorney tells Courthouse News that there was no way to turn the app off, as it would constantly run in the background. \"She found it very offensive that they were treating her like a felon,\" the attorney says. Arias says her boss \"scolded\" her for uninstalling the program last year, and fired her soon after. (Elsewhere, a woman got fired over a text mistakenly sent to the boss.)"], "length": 1970, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Employee Fired for Refusing to Install Location-Tracking App**\n\nA former sales executive for Intermex Wire Transfer LLC, a money-transfer company, has filed a lawsuit against her former employer alleging that she was fired for refusing to install a location-tracking app on her company-issued iPhone. Myrna Arias, who worked as a sales executive and account manager from February 2014 to May 2014, claims that her boss, John Stubits, required her to install the Xora app, which tracked her every move 24 hours a day, seven days a week, even when she was off the clock.\n\nAccording to the lawsuit, Arias researched the app and asked Stubits if Intermex would be tracking her whereabouts when she was off the clock. Stubits allegedly admitted that employees would be monitored while off duty and bragged that he knew how fast she was driving at specific moments. Arias likened the app to a prisoner's ankle bracelet and informed Stubits that his actions were illegal. However, Stubits replied that she should tolerate the illegal intrusion because Intermex was paying her more than her previous employer.\n\nArias claims that she was scolded by Stubits when she uninstalled the app to protect her privacy, and that she was fired a few weeks later. Her attorney, Gail Glick, stated that an employer can legally monitor employees at work if there is a legitimate business interest, but that Intermex's actions were a complete violation of California and federal laws against invasion of privacy.\n\nThe lawsuit alleges invasion of privacy, retaliation, labor code violations, intentional interference with contract, wrongful termination, and unfair business practices. Arias seeks an injunction and general, special, and punitive damages in excess of $500,000.\n\n**Background on the App**\n\nThe Xora app, now known as ClickSoftware Solution, is a web-based management application that enables office staff to track employees' locations and monitor their activities. The app allows employees to record their daily work and job-specific hours, receive daily job assignments on their phone, and capture customer or job information on their phone. However, the app also has a GPS function that tracks employees' locations 24/7, even when they are off the clock.\n\n**Industry Response**\n\nThe use of location-tracking apps in the workplace has raised concerns about employee privacy and the potential for abuse. Some companies, such as Intermex, may use these apps to monitor employees' activities and ensure that they are meeting productivity standards. However, others may use these apps"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A judge on Tuesday allowed a man charged in freeway shootings that rattled Phoenix last year to be released from jail amid questions about evidence authorities say links him to the crimes. The judge overseeing the case of Leslie Merritt Jr. reduced his bond to zero and said he can return to his home under electronic monitoring, the AP reports. He was expected to be released later in the day. The reduction of the bond—once $1 million—was a major victory as defense lawyers contend that ballistic tests cast doubt on the claim by authorities that Merritt was behind four of the freeway shootings. \"With all due respect your honor, there's no evidence against him to show he's responsible for this,\" defense lawyer Jason Lamm said. \"He is no more the I-10 shooter than, respectfully, you are.\" Merritt lifted up his shackled arms in celebration as he walked from the courtroom. After the hearing, family members hugged and shook the hands of defense lawyers. Merritt has pleaded not guilty to drive-by shooting, aggravated assault, and other charges. The shootings caused panic on Phoenix-area freeways, where 11 vehicles were hit in August and September. Detectives took Merritt into custody on Sept. 18; in court the next day, Merritt adamantly denied shooting any cars, telling the judge, \"I'm the wrong guy.\" His lawyers immediately began raising questions about the evidence, citing ballistics information and phone records they say provided an alibi for their client. (Also Tuesday, a motorist suspected of shooting at other vehicles on an Alabama highway died after a shootout with officers.)"], "length": 955, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Freeway Shooting Cases: Update and New Incident**\n\nIn recent developments, a judge in Phoenix, Arizona has reduced the bond of Leslie Allen Merritt Jr., a man charged with freeway shootings that occurred in August and September last year. Merritt, who has been in jail for seven months, was allowed to be released from jail under electronic monitoring after the judge reduced his bond to zero. Merritt's defense lawyers have been questioning the evidence linking him to the crimes, citing ballistic tests and phone records that they say provide an alibi for their client.\n\nThe shootings, which caused panic on Phoenix-area freeways, resulted in 11 vehicles being hit. Authorities had initially declared that the shootings were the work of a domestic terrorist, and Merritt was arrested on September 18. However, his lawyers have been raising doubts about the evidence, pointing to road rage shootings that occurred on Phoenix-area freeways after Merritt's arrest.\n\nMeanwhile, in a separate incident in Alabama, a driver suspected of firing a gun at motorists on a highway and exchanging gunfire with officers has been killed. The incident occurred in Blount County, about 45 miles northeast of Birmingham, and began around 7 a.m. on Tuesday. The suspect's vehicle crashed, and he exchanged gunfire with law officers before being pronounced dead. The suspect's name and other details have not been released.\n\nThe two incidents highlight the ongoing issue of freeway shootings and road rage incidents in the United States. While the Arizona case has raised questions about the evidence and the suspect's guilt, the Alabama incident serves as a reminder of the dangers of such incidents and the need for law enforcement to take swift action to protect the public."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["News that a shooting left multiple people dead may sound depressingly familiar, but the one outside Baltimore on Thursday is unique in at least one way: The shooter was female. Police say the 26-year-old woman, who has not been identified, died of a self-inflicted gunshot wound to the head. Harford County Sheriff Jeff Gahler said she was a temporary employee at the Rite Aid distribution center where the shooting took place, reports the Baltimore Sun. Police say the suspect killed three people, and three others remain hospitalized with wounds not believed to be life threatening. Details and developments: When, where: The Rite Aid distribution center where the shooting took place is at a business complex in Perryman, about 30 miles from Baltimore, reports WBAL. Deputies got there at 9:06am, within 5 minutes of the first call, said the sheriff. He said that deputies fired no shots and that the shooter used a 9mm Glock semiautomatic handgun. Why: Police aren't speculating about a motive. “Normally, she was a nice person, but she came in in a bad mood,” Mike Carre, an employee at a nearby business, tells the Washington Post, recounting what Rite Aid workers told him. \"She wanted to pick a fight,\" he said. \"And then she started shooting.” CNN quotes a law enforcement official who describes her as a disgruntled employee; the site says she worked there as a security guard. The woman lived in Baltimore County, and the gun was legally registered to her."], "length": 3414, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Multiple Shootings in US Workplaces in 24 Hours**\n\nIn a shocking series of events, three workplace shootings occurred in the US within 24 hours, leaving multiple people dead and injured. The first shooting took place on Thursday morning at a Rite Aid distribution center in Harford County, Maryland, where a 26-year-old temporary employee, Snochia Moseley, shot and killed three people and injured three others before taking her own life. Moseley, who was a disgruntled employee, used a 9mm Glock pistol and multiple magazines to carry out the attack.\n\nThe shooting occurred at around 9:06 a.m. at the Rite Aid facility in Perryman, Maryland, where approximately 1,000 employees work. Deputies and first responders arrived on the scene within five minutes, and the shooter was found with a self-inflicted gunshot wound, dying at an area hospital. Two victims were pronounced dead at the scene, and four others were taken to hospitals with non-life-threatening injuries.\n\nThis shooting is the third high-profile workplace shooting in the US in 24 hours. On Wednesday, a gunman shot and killed three people at a software company in Middleton, Wisconsin, and a man opened fire outside a judge's office in a municipal building in Masontown, Pennsylvania, injuring four people, including a police officer.\n\nThe motive behind Moseley's attack is still unknown, but authorities are investigating her background and possible connections to the victims. The Rite Aid facility has been closed, and grief counselors are being made available to employees.\n\nThe shootings have sparked outrage and concern about gun violence in the US. Former US Rep. Gabrielle Giffords, who was shot in the head in 2011, said that the level of gun violence in the US is not normal and that every American should be outraged.\n\nGovernor Larry Hogan has offered his support and assistance to the victims and their families, and the Harford County Emergency Operations Center has been activated to coordinate the response to the shooting.\n\nThe incident has also raised questions about workplace security and the need for more stringent measures to prevent such attacks. The Rite Aid facility did not have metal detectors or security cameras in the building where the shooting occurred, and witnesses have expressed shock and concern about the lack of security measures.\n\nAs the investigation continues, authorities are working to determine the motive behind Moseley's attack and to prevent similar incidents from occurring in the future."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The bad news about acetaminophen just keeps on coming: A new study finds that use of the drug during pregnancy is linked to \"ADHD-like behavioral problems\" in children, CNN reports. The Danish study looked at data from more than 64,000 children and found that those who had prenatal exposure to acetaminophen had a 13% higher risk of exhibiting ADHD-like behaviors, a 29% higher risk of being prescribed ADHD medication, and a 37% higher risk of receiving a diagnosis of hyperkinetic disorder, a severe form of ADHD, USA Today reports. The risk increased the later in pregnancy the drug was taken and the longer it was taken. Women who used acetaminophen for 20 weeks or longer had a 50% higher chance of their children being prescribed ADHD medication. But the authors did not find a cause-and-effect relationship, and they note that \"exhibiting ADHD-like behaviors\" is not the same as actually having ADHD. Right now, doctors consider acetaminophen the \"safest\" pain reliever for pregnant women, NBC News reports; the study author says pregnant women \"shouldn't worry at this point.\" What could account for the link? The authors think acetaminophen may interfere with key maternal hormones that impact fetal brain development. Another recent study also found that frequent acetaminophen use during pregnancy was linked with a 70% higher risk of behavioral problems in children."], "length": 1951, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**New Study Raises Concerns about Acetaminophen Use During Pregnancy**\n\nA recent study published in the American Medical Association journal JAMA Pediatrics has found that frequent use of acetaminophen, commonly found in over-the-counter pain relievers such as Tylenol, during pregnancy may be associated with an increased risk of attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) and similar behavioral problems in children. The study, which analyzed data from over 64,000 children in Denmark, found that mothers who took acetaminophen for longer periods and later in pregnancy were more likely to have children diagnosed with ADHD or prescribed ADHD medications.\n\nWhile the study does not show a clear cause-and-effect relationship between acetaminophen use and ADHD, the researchers suggest that the medication may interfere with maternal hormones that are critical for fetal brain development. The study's authors also note that the data suggests that taking acetaminophen for longer periods and later in pregnancy is associated with higher risks.\n\nThe study's findings have sparked concerns among health experts, who are urging pregnant women to be cautious when using acetaminophen. \"If I were a woman who was pregnant, I would try to avoid taking painkillers as much as I can until we know more about this,\" said Dr. Beate Ritz, a professor and chair of the epidemiology department at the University of California, Los Angeles Fielding School of Public Health.\n\nHowever, other experts have cautioned against over-interpreting the study's findings. \"The cited literature is not relevant to the human condition,\" said Dr. Max Wiznitzer, a pediatric neurologist at Case Western Reserve University School of Medicine. \"I'm afraid that (women) will think, somehow, that they caused their child's problem when the study does not tell us that.\"\n\nThe study highlights the importance of not taking a drug's safety during pregnancy for granted and suggests that pregnant women may want to consider alternative ways to manage pain, such as massages, baths, and acupuncture. \"There are nonpharmacological ways to deal with pain,\" said Dr. Jeffrey Chapa, head of maternal-fetal medicine at Cleveland Clinic Children's Hospital. \"I think we have to focus a little bit more on that as opposed to just medications.\"\n\nThe manufacturer of Tylenol, McNeil Consumer Healthcare, has stated that the medication has a favorable safety profile when used as directed, but has also acknowledged that the study's findings are \"interesting\" and warrant further investigation.\n\nIn light of the study's findings, pregnant women"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Bill Cosby was labeled a \"violent sexual predator\" by the judge who sentenced him to three to 10 years in prison on Tuesday, but his publicist had a different description: one of the \"greatest civil rights leaders\" in history and one of the \"greatest educators of men and boys.\" Outside the courtroom in Norristown, Pa., publicist Andrew Wyatt claimed the 81-year-old Cosby had faced the \"most racist and sexist trial\" in American history, the New York Times reports. Wyatt claimed that three psychologists who testified against Cosby in the sexual assault trial were trying to \"make money off of accusing black men of being sexual predators.\" He also read a statement from Camille Cosby, the disgraced entertainer's wife, alleging that an audio recording played in the trial had been doctored. Wyatt, who previously called Cosby's trial a \"public lynching,\" accused Judge Steven O'Neill of being part of a \"sex war\" and linked the sentencing to the treatment of Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh. Vox reports that the strangest moment came when Wyatt likened his client to Jesus. \"They persecuted Jesus, and look what happened,\" he said. \"Not saying Mr. Cosby is Jesus, but we know what this country has done to black men for centuries.\" Cosby, who left the Montgomery County Courthouse in handcuffs, spent his first night behind bars at SCI Phoenix, a state prison that opened two months ago in suburban Philadelphia, reports WPVI. (Cosby's mug shot was released Tuesday afternoon.)"], "length": 1313, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Bill Cosby Sentenced to 3-10 Years in Prison, Defense Team Accuses Racism and Sexism**\n\nOn Tuesday, Bill Cosby, 81, was sentenced to 3-10 years in state prison for drugging and sexually assaulting a woman, Andrea Constand, at his home outside Philadelphia in 2004. Cosby's defense team, led by publicist Andrew Wyatt, responded with fiery accusations of racism and sexism, claiming that Cosby's conviction was an unjust product of the #MeToo era.\n\nWyatt compared Cosby's trial to the accusations of sexual misconduct against Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh, saying that both men were victims of a \"sex war\" happening in Washington. He also called Cosby's trial the \"most racist and sexist trial in the history of the United States,\" and claimed that Cosby was a \"great educator of men and boys\" and one of the \"greatest civil rights leaders.\"\n\nCosby was declared a \"sexually violent predator\" under state law and will serve his sentence at SCI Phoenix, a new 3,830-bed state prison in suburban Philadelphia. He will be housed in a single cell in a unit adjacent to the infirmary and will be allowed phone calls and visitation in accordance with policy.\n\nThe defense team's accusations of racism and sexism have been met with criticism, with some calling them a desperate attempt to deflect attention from Cosby's crimes. Cosby's conviction has been hailed as a victory for the #MeToo movement, which has brought attention to the widespread problem of sexual assault and harassment.\n\nCosby's sentence marks the end of a long and tumultuous trial, which began in April and resulted in a guilty verdict. The case has sparked a national conversation about sexual assault, racism, and the role of power and privilege in perpetuating these issues.\n\n**Key Developments:**\n\n* Bill Cosby sentenced to 3-10 years in state prison for drugging and sexually assaulting a woman\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A day after being accidentally introduced as \"President Clinton,\" Hillary seems to be taking pains to kill talk of a future run in New Zealand's media, reports Reuters. Asked whether she'd \"ruled out\" running through 2016, she replied, \"Oh yes, yes. I'm very pleased to be doing what I'm doing as secretary of state.\" Asked in another interview whether she could be the first female president, she answered, \"Well, not me. But it will be someone ...\" The significance? Probably not much, writes Laura Rozen in Politico, noting how one of Clinton's answers is tied to her being secretary of state—a post she likely won't hold in 2016. On another front, Clinton said the midterm losses were \"big—but not out of the pattern of historical political elections.\" She added, \"I think the president made very clear was he made decisions which were essential for the well-being of the American people.\""], "length": 1038, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Hillary Clinton Rules Out Presidential Run, Defends Obama**\n\nIn a series of interviews in New Zealand, US Secretary of State Hillary Clinton has ruled out running for president in 2012 or 2016. Despite speculation fueled by the Democrats' losses in the mid-term elections, Clinton stated that she is \"very pleased\" to be serving as Secretary of State and has no plans to run for the top office.\n\nClinton's comments were made in response to questions from New Zealand television, where she was asked if she would consider running for president in 2016. She replied, \"Oh yes, yes. I'm very pleased to be doing what I'm doing as Secretary of State.\" Clinton also acknowledged that the Democrats' losses in the mid-term elections were significant, but defended President Barack Obama's decisions, saying that they were necessary to prevent worse economic consequences.\n\nClinton's visit to New Zealand was also marked by a humorous moment when New Zealand Prime Minister John Phillip Key accidentally introduced her as \"President Clinton\" before correcting himself. Clinton's visit was part of a nearly two-week Asia-Pacific tour, which will conclude in Australia.\n\nClinton's comments on her presidential ambitions are likely to put to rest speculation about her future plans. While she has been a popular figure in Democratic politics, her decision to rule out a presidential run suggests that she is committed to serving as Secretary of State for the remainder of her term.\n\nClinton's defense of Obama and her comments on the mid-term elections suggest that she is working to put the best face on the Democrats' losses. While the party's poor showing has raised questions about who will run for president in 2012 and 2016, Clinton's comments suggest that she is focused on her current role as Secretary of State and is not considering a presidential run."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Amy Schumer took the long way around to announce she's pregnant with husband Chris Fischer. The comedian and actress broke her baby news Monday on the Instagram stories of friend and journalist Jessica Yellin, the AP reports. Yellin, of the site NewsNotNoise.org, showed at the end of a list of Schumer's recommended congressional and gubernatorial candidates the line: \"I'm pregnant-Amy Schumer.\" Schumer had teased the announcement on her own Instagram page, captioning a photo of hers and Fischer's heads atop Prince Harry's and Meghan Markle's bodies, \"About to announce some exciting news on @jessicayellin insta page. Please follow her for up to the minute #newsnotnoise she breaks down what’s really going on. She agreed to post a lil noise today for me! Follow her and VOTE!!\" \"We are 15 days out from the midterms and there is a lot going on,\" Yellin explained before going into Schumer's \"lengthy\" list of recommendations, per Bustle. \"I wanted to share some news from our community. Maybe it's noise, but it's happy noise,\" Yellin continued. \"These are the recommendations of Amy Schumer, one of the most consistent and early supporters of #NewsNotNoise. Now, read all the way to the bottom. You'll see there's some news down there. Congratulations, Amy.\" Schumer is known for her liberal politics: She was recently arrested protesting the confirmation of Judge Brett Kavanaugh to the US Supreme Court. The 37-year-old married Fischer, a chef, in February. (Here's why Schumer won't appear in any Super Bowl ads.)"], "length": 1133, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Amy Schumer Makes Big Announcements: Pregnancy and Politics**\n\nComedian Amy Schumer has made two significant announcements in recent days. First, she revealed on Instagram that she is expecting her first child with husband Chris Fischer. However, she didn't just stop at sharing the happy news. Schumer also used the platform to promote her support for the #NewsNotNoise campaign, a movement to educate people about politics and encourage them to vote in the upcoming midterm elections.\n\nSchumer's announcement was made in conjunction with former CNN Chief White House Correspondent Jessica Yellin, who shared Schumer's picks for candidates in the midterm elections on her Instagram page. Yellin also explained the #NewsNotNoise campaign, which aims to break down complex political jargon and provide clear information to voters.\n\nIn addition to her pregnancy announcement, Schumer has been a vocal advocate for various social and political causes. She has been a strong supporter of Colin Kaepernick's peaceful protest against racism and police brutality, and has spoken out against the Trump Administration's policies, including the nomination of Brett Kavanaugh to the Supreme Court. Schumer has also been a vocal advocate for gun control and has supported the transgender community.\n\nSchumer's decision to tie her pregnancy announcement to a voting announcement is not surprising, given her commitment to politics and social justice. By using her platform to promote voting and encourage people to get involved in the democratic process, Schumer is continuing to make her voice heard and inspire others to do the same.\n\n**Key Takeaways:**\n\n* Amy Schumer is expecting her first child with husband Chris Fischer.\n* Schumer used her pregnancy announcement to promote the #NewsNotNoise campaign and encourage people to vote in the midterm elections.\n* Schumer has been a vocal advocate for various social and political causes, including Colin Kaepernick's peaceful protest and the #NewsNotNoise campaign.\n* Schumer has spoken out against the Trump Administration's policies, including the nomination of Brett Kavanaugh to the Supreme Court.\n* Schumer's commitment to politics and social justice continues to inspire others to get involved in the democratic process."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Chicken giant Tyson Foods says it will retrain all of its workers who deal with live birds after an animal rights group released hidden footage from within its facilities on Wednesday, USA Today reports. According to the Washington Post, the video from Compassion Over Killing shows workers choking, punching, and kicking chickens, plus running them over with forklifts and leaving them in piles to die. One worker in the video says it's \"inhumane\" to kill a chicken by stepping on its head while he kills a chicken by stepping on its head. Tyson calls the behavior \"inexcusable\" and says it fired 10 employees at the Virginia facilities shown in the video. Animal rights activists have infiltrated Tyson Foods to film such videos at least five times in a little over a year."], "length": 1671, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Tyson Foods Faces Criticism for Animal Abuse at Chicken Farms**\n\nTyson Foods, one of the largest chicken-processing companies in the US, is facing criticism and allegations of animal abuse at its facilities in Virginia. An undercover investigation by animal rights group Compassion Over Killing (COK) has revealed shocking footage of Tyson employees mistreating chickens, including throwing, punching, and kicking them, as well as sticking plastic rods through their beaks and leaving injured birds to die.\n\nThe investigation, which was conducted in May and June at four Tyson facilities in Virginia, shows workers engaging in inhumane behavior, including standing on chickens' heads to suffocate them. One worker is heard warning an undercover investigator that \"you can't let nobody see you do that\" as he stepped on a chicken's head, suffocating it.\n\nTyson Foods has responded to the allegations by firing 10 employees who were involved in the mistreatment of chickens and discontinuing the practice of inserting plastic tubes in roosters' beaks, known as beak modification. The company has also announced that it will retrain all of its live poultry workers on animal welfare policies and is evaluating further steps to ensure animal well-being procedures are being followed throughout its operations.\n\nCOK has condemned the behavior of the Tyson employees and called for the company to be held accountable. \"This level of violence and abuse is so egregious, it violates Virginia state animal protection laws,\" said Erica Meier, COK's executive director. \"The company should be held accountable — and consumers deserve to know the truth about the horrors inflicted upon these animals.\"\n\nTyson Foods has stated that it takes animal well-being seriously and is committed to ensuring that its employees behave properly. However, COK has questioned whether there is any way to humanely kill and eat animals, and has called for consumers to consider leaving animals off their plates.\n\nThe investigation is the latest in a series of undercover investigations into animal abuse at Tyson-connected facilities. In the past year, animal rights groups have infiltrated Tyson facilities and secretly recorded footage of workers abusing chickens and cramped living conditions for chickens before they are slaughtered.\n\n**Key Developments:**\n\n* Tyson Foods fires 10 employees involved in mistreatment of chickens\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Donald Trump's lawyer has apparently decided that it's his turn to make the gaffes. In an interview with CNN Wednesday, notoriously combative Trump Organization attorney Michael Cohen interrupted host Brianna Keilar when she said that the campaign was \"down,\" Politico reports. \"Says who?\" he asked. \"Polls. Most of them. All of them?\" she replied. \"Says who?\" Cohen asked again. When she repeated it was polls, he responded, \"OK, which polls?” and Keilar said: “All of them.\" USA Today reports that Twitter users were quick to mock Cohen and are now replying \"Says who?\" to all his tweets. Cohen, in what Law Newz calls \"typical lawyer fashion,\" also took issue with Trump's overhaul of campaign staff being called a \"shake-up,\" insisting it was actually a \"change-up.\" Cohen also raised eyebrows when he told Kielar that Trump knows about \"the African-American problem in this country,\" the Independent reports. \"What I meant to say is the problem that exists in the African-American community,\" he later said. (Michael Moore says he \"knows for a fact\" that Trump never really wanted to be president.)"], "length": 1533, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Donald Trump's Campaign in Disarray: \"Says Who?\"**\n\nIn a series of awkward exchanges, Donald Trump's lawyer and special counsel, Michael Cohen, clashed with CNN's Brianna Keilar over the Republican nominee's struggling campaign. The controversy began when Cohen was asked about the recent shake-up in Trump's campaign staff, which included the hiring of new officials such as Kellyanne Conway and Stephen Bannon.\n\nWhen Keilar pointed out that Trump's campaign was \"down\" in the polls, Cohen responded with a series of incredulous questions, including \"Says who?\" and \"Which polls?\" Keilar repeatedly replied that it was \"polls. Most of them. All of them,\" but Cohen continued to press for more specific information.\n\nThe exchange quickly went viral on social media, with Twitter users mocking Cohen's responses with the hashtag #SaysWho. The trend even inspired a series of humorous knock-knock jokes, with one user responding to Cohen's \"Says who?\" with \"THE POLLS. ALL OF THEM.\"\n\nDespite the awkward exchange, Cohen attempted to spin the hiring of Conway and Bannon as a positive move, saying that Conway was a \"brilliant individual\" who understood the data coming in. However, the controversy has done little to reassure Trump voters that his campaign is back on track.\n\nIn fact, recent polls suggest that Trump is struggling in key swing states, including Virginia and Colorado, where Hillary Clinton leads by double-digits. However, Trump did win a recent poll in Indiana, where he was ahead of Clinton by 11 points.\n\nThe shake-up in Trump's campaign staff comes just a few months before the election, and it remains to be seen whether the new hires will be enough to turn the campaign around. For now, the \"Says who?\" controversy has become a symbol of the Trump campaign's struggles to connect with voters and respond to criticism."}
