{"input": "", "answers": ["The man who murdered John Lennon will remain behind bars after his 10th unsuccessful parole hearing, reports the Daily News. Mark David Chapman, now 63, was turned down again this week. No reasons have yet been made public, but the newspaper reports that Yoko Ono sent the parole board a letter asking that Chapman remain in the Wende Correctional Facility in upstate New York—not only for the safety of her and her family, but for his own safety. Chapman, who shot Lennon in New York City in 1980, is up again for parole in about two years. Chapman's wife recently told the UK Mirror that he talked of killing Lennon two months before actually doing so."], "length": 1567, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**John Lennon's Killer May Be Released After 38 Years in Prison**\n\nMark David Chapman, the man who murdered John Lennon on December 8, 1980, may be released from prison after 38 years behind bars. Chapman's wife, Gloria Hiroko Chapman, has spoken out in support of his release, saying she will wait for him no matter how long he is in prison. Gloria, 67, met Chapman in 1978 and married him in 1979, despite his history of mental illness and violent behavior.\n\nAccording to Gloria, Chapman had told her two months before the murder that he planned to kill John Lennon, but she believed him when he said he had dumped the gun and was no longer planning to carry out the threat. However, when news of Lennon's death broke, Gloria knew that Chapman was the killer.\n\nChapman was given 20 years to life for the murder and has been denied parole nine times. However, with the New York State parole board now placing more emphasis on an inmate's behavior behind bars, Gloria is hopeful that her husband may be released soon.\n\nGloria has been allowed to visit Chapman in prison, where they have spent time together in a caravan, making pizzas, watching TV, and making love. Despite Chapman's violent behavior towards her during their marriage, Gloria has remained devoted to him and has prayed for his release.\n\nThe parole hearing for Chapman is scheduled to take place in August, and Gloria is hoping that he will be released soon. However, Yoko Ono, Lennon's widow, has objected to Chapman's release, citing the severity of the crime.\n\n**Internet Archive Efforts to Preserve Web Pages**\n\nIn other news, the Internet Archive is working to preserve web pages and fix broken links on the web. The organization is using crawlers to archive pages as they are created and to preserve the pages that they refer to. This will allow users to access archived versions of pages that have been changed or removed from the web.\n\nThe goal of the Internet Archive is to fix all broken links on the web, making it easier for users to access information and preserving the history of the web. The organization is working with \"No More 404\" sites to achieve this goal."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["See, this is why all those Twitter profiles are sure to note that retweets do not equal endorsements. CBS News reports the Pentagon's official Twitter account on Thursday retweeted a tweet calling for President Trump's resignation. \"GOP: Stop making sexual assault a partisan issue,\" tweeted \"Proud Resister\" while calling for Roy Moore, Sen. Al Franken, and Trump to all resign or step aside due to allegations of sexual misconduct. The tweet was retweeted—and then soon deleted—by the Department of Defense Twitter account. According to Politico, Pentagon spokesperson Col. Rob Manning confirmed the retweet was made by the \"authorized operator\" of the department's Twitter account. In her own tweet, Pentagon spokesperson Dana White said the call for Trump's resignation was \"erroneously retweeted\" and contained \"content that would not be endorsed by the Department of Defense.\""], "length": 707, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Pentagon's Twitter Account Erroneously Retweets Call for Trump's Resignation**\n\nThe Pentagon's official Twitter account (@DeptofDefense) made headlines on Thursday after it retweeted a call for President Donald Trump's resignation, sparking controversy and swift disavowal from the Department of Defense. The tweet, which originated from the account @proudresister, also called for the resignation of Alabama Senate candidate Roy Moore and Senator Al Franken, both of whom have been accused of sexual misconduct.\n\nAccording to Pentagon spokesman Col. Rob Manning, the retweet was made \"erroneously\" by an authorized operator of the account, who quickly deleted it after realizing the mistake. Manning stated that the content of the tweet \"would not be endorsed by the Department of Defense.\"\n\nThe incident has raised questions about the Pentagon's social media policies and the potential for unauthorized or mistaken tweets to be posted on its official account. The @proudresister account, which linked to a website selling anti-Trump collectibles, has also been criticized for its partisan tone and potential bias.\n\nThe controversy comes at a time when the White House is facing renewed scrutiny over President Trump's own behavior, including allegations of sexual misconduct. The Pentagon's Twitter mishap has sparked debate about the role of social media in politics and the potential for government agencies to inadvertently endorse or promote partisan content.\n\nIn a statement, Dana White, assistant to the secretary of defense for public affairs, clarified that the retweet was an error and that the Department of Defense does not endorse the content of the tweet. The incident serves as a reminder of the importance of social media management and the need for government agencies to be mindful of their online presence."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Peter Jackson's crafts a fantastic vision of the afterlife in The Lovely Bones, say critics, but some feel it came at the expense of the human side of the story of a murdered teen, adapted from the book by Alice Sebold. \"By turns warmly sentimental, serial-killer sinister, and science-fiction fantastical, The Lovely Bones was an unlikely book to achieve worldwide success,\" Kenneth Turan writes in the Los Angeles Times. \"Those mismatched elements come back to haunt\" the film version, \"making the final product more hit-and-miss than unblemished triumph.\" \"Sebold's Lovely Bones is fleshed out with the perilous, irresistible power of sex—a real world of extramarital sex and sex between young lovers in addition to the heinous rape from which moviegoers are shielded.\" But Jackson \"shies from the challenge, shortchanging a story that isn't only about the lightness of souls in heaven but also about the urges of bodies on earth,\" writes Lisa Schwarzbaum at Entertainment Weekly. \"Through Jackson's art\" and the magic of star Saoirse Rogan, Richard Corliss writes at Time, \"the obscenity of child murder has been invested with immense gravity and grace.\" \"Some books are not meant to be adapted to the big screen,\" and The Lovely Bones is one of them, writes Claudia Puig at USA Today. What works in the book, she writes, comes off as \"artificial and emotionless on-screen.\""], "length": 3045, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**The Lovely Bones: A Mixed Bag of Emotions**\n\nPeter Jackson's adaptation of Alice Sebold's bestselling novel, \"The Lovely Bones,\" is a film that fails to live up to its full potential. The movie tells the story of Susie Salmon, a 14-year-old girl who is murdered by her neighbor, George Harvey, and watches from the afterlife as her family and friends try to come to terms with her death.\n\nThe film boasts a talented cast, including Saoirse Ronan, Stanley Tucci, Mark Wahlberg, and Rachel Weisz, but the story is marred by a tone that veers wildly from lighthearted to heavy-handed. The film's attempt to depict heaven as a fantastical, CGI-besotted world falls flat, and the scenes that take place in this alternate universe are jarring and artificial.\n\nTucci gives a standout performance as the creepy and unsettling George Harvey, but the film's focus on the thriller elements of the story comes at the expense of the more nuanced and complex themes of the novel. The film's portrayal of Susie's family and their grief is also somewhat one-dimensional, with the character of Abigail (Rachel Weisz) feeling particularly underdeveloped.\n\nDespite its flaws, the film does have some moments of beauty and poignancy, particularly in its depiction of Susie's relationships with her family and friends. Ronan shines as the wide-eyed and innocent Susie, and her performance is a highlight of the film.\n\nOverall, \"The Lovely Bones\" is a mixed bag of emotions, with some moments of brilliance and beauty, but also some jarring and artificial elements. While it may not be the adaptation that fans of the novel were hoping for, it is still a thought-provoking and emotionally resonant film that is worth watching.\n\n**Rating:** 2.5/4 stars\n\n**Recommendation:** Fans of the novel may be disappointed by the film's tone and focus, but those who enjoy thriller movies and are looking for a thought-provoking and emotionally resonant film may find \"The Lovely Bones\" to be a worthwhile watch."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A video released online appears to show a Mexican soldier executing a suspected oil thief at point-blank range as he lays defenseless on the ground. The heavily edited video, taken from a surveillance camera, shows a car coming under fire during what the military describes as a series of ambushes that left four soldiers and six suspects dead in Palmarito on May 3. As soldiers surround the vehicle, its occupants exit and are forced to lie down in front of the car. Soldiers are then seen dragging a suspect to the same spot. He appears injured but rolls from his back onto his front. Six minutes later, a soldier appears at the side of the frame and appears to shoot the man in the back of the head, per the AP. A dark stain spreads around him, reports Reuters. \"There was already concern about the use of excessive force by the military. Now this video seems to give us the proof,\" says the chairwoman of Mexico's Senate Commission on Human Rights, per the Washington Post, which identifies the man shot as Raul Jimenez Martinez, 46. Mexico's defense ministry, however, says it will cooperate fully with an investigation launched by the attorney general's office on May 4. \"Under no circumstances can conduct contrary to the law and human rights be justified,\" the ministry says. Residents of Palmarito have already taken to the streets to protest the deaths of the suspects, placing the blame on the army, though the defense ministry previously claimed the suspects used residents as human shields."], "length": 1788, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Mexico's Military Embroiled in New Scandal Over Extrajudicial Killing**\n\nA disturbing video has surfaced in Mexico, appearing to show a soldier executing a civilian lying face down in the street with a shot to the back of his head. The video, which was released by Mexican media outlets, shows a security camera footage of a shootout between soldiers and suspected oil thieves in the town of Palmarito, Puebla State, on May 3. The footage, which has sparked widespread condemnation, appears to show a soldier shooting a man who is already on the ground, despite being in custody.\n\nThe Mexican defense ministry has urged an investigation into the video, stating that prosecutors should \"comprehensively clear up\" whether the video indeed shows the soldier shooting the detained man. The ministry has also pledged to cooperate with the attorney general's office in the investigation.\n\nThe incident is the latest in a series of scandals involving Mexico's military, which has been accused of using extrajudicial force in the past. In 2014, the military was implicated in the killing of 22 suspects at a warehouse in the state of Mexico, with the National Human Rights Commission finding that soldiers had executed at least a dozen suspects after they surrendered.\n\nThe video has sparked outrage and calls for a swift and thorough investigation. The Mexican National Human Rights Commission has said that the gravity of the events demands a swift and thorough investigation, and has called for the government to take action to prevent such incidents in the future.\n\nThe incident is also a reminder of the ongoing crisis of violence and impunity in Mexico, which has seen a significant increase in murders and gang violence in recent years. According to a report released on Tuesday, Mexico had the second-highest number of murders last year among countries considered in \"armed conflicts\".\n\nThe government has sought to downplay the report, stating that the term \"armed conflict\" is inapplicable to Mexico and that the final murder tally for 2016 is not yet official. However, the video and the surrounding controversy are likely to further erode public trust in the military and the government's ability to address the country's security crisis."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Connor Cummins' family describes him to WABC-TV as a photography devotee—and that enthusiasm may have cost him his life. The New Jersey man, said to be either 20 or 24 years old, and an 18-year-old pal ascended the scaffolding outside NYC's Four Seasons hotel Wednesday night in search of the perfect panoramic picture—but Cummins may have slipped on railings made slippery by the rain and fell nine stories to his death, cops tell NJ.com. An NYPD spokesman tells the New York Daily News that the initial investigation suggests the men weren't guests of the hotel. Cummins, who originally hails from Rockaway and whose Facebook page indicates he's now a student at the University of Massachusetts Amherst, per ABC, landed on one of the hotel's lower-level rooftops around 11:15pm and was pronounced dead at the scene, reports the New York Post. His friend wasn't injured and was questioned by authorities to fill in the blanks; he was released Thursday morning without being charged. \"He was a loving boy, that's all I can say,\" one of Cummins' relatives tells the Post. \"I don't know what happened.\" (There's more suspicion surrounding a Muslim teen's fatal fall off a Seattle building.)"], "length": 1299, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Tragedy Strikes at Four Seasons Hotel in Manhattan**\n\nA 20-year-old man, Connor Cummings, fell to his death while attempting to take pictures from the scaffolding of the 52-story Four Seasons Hotel in Manhattan on Wednesday night. Cummings, a photography buff and sophomore at the University of Massachusetts Amherst, was with an 18-year-old friend, Dimitri Olivares, when the incident occurred.\n\nAccording to police, Cummings and Olivares had taken the hotel elevator to the top floor and then climbed up two flights of stairs to the rooftop exit. They then accessed a restricted area on the roof, where Cummings lost his footing and fell 25 feet onto the hotel's roof. He was pronounced dead at the scene.\n\nThe investigation suggests that Cummings was trying to take a panoramic shot of the city when he fell. Police sources said that he was walking backward on a metal catwalk while taking photos, and stepped off the scaffolding, resulting in his fatal fall.\n\nOlivares, who was with Cummings at the time of the incident, was questioned by police and released without charges. He had initially fled the scene, but later returned to the hotel and called his mom and hotel security to report what had happened.\n\nCummings' family and friends have described him as a loving and talented individual who was passionate about photography. His fraternity brothers at the University of Massachusetts Amherst have posted a message on Facebook mourning his death and praising his \"fiery passion\" for photography.\n\nThe incident has raised questions about the safety and security of the hotel, as well as the actions of Cummings and Olivares, who were not guests of the hotel but managed to access the restricted rooftop area.\n\nThe New York Police Department is continuing to investigate the incident, and no charges have been filed against Olivares. The hotel has released a statement expressing its condolences to Cummings' family and promising to cooperate with the investigation."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Protesting truckers are planning a shutdown of their own—on Washington's beltway. Starting on Friday morning, they intend to fill the road \"three lanes deep\" with tractor-trailers, an organizer of \"Truckers Ride for the Constitution\" tells US News and World Report. In a demonstration against what they view as violations of the Constitution, planners like Earl Conlon are calling for \"the arrest of everyone in government who has violated their oath of office.\" Their grievances include truckers' wages, industry regulations, and NSA spying, the Hill reports. They're also accusing President Obama of treason for allegedly putting weapons in the hands of rebels with ties to al-Qaeda. Organizers have received some 3,000 RSVPs, says one. Moving at the 55mph speed limit, they'll leave a lane open for emergency vehicles; vehicles with \"T2SDA,\" or \"Truckers to Shut Down America,\" displayed will also be allowed to pass. \"Everybody that doesn't have a supporter sticker on their window, good luck: Nobody in, nobody out,\" says Conlon. A Facebook page for the event has more than 55,000 \"likes.\""], "length": 1638, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Truckers Plan to Protest in Washington, D.C.**\n\nA group of truck drivers, calling themselves \"Truckers for the Constitution,\" plan to protest in Washington, D.C. by backing up the Capital Beltway around the city. The protest, scheduled to take place on October 11, is aimed at drawing attention to the truckers' grievances against the federal government, including low wages, high fuel costs, and excessive regulations.\n\nThe group, which claims to have over 3,000 truckers participating, plans to circle the 64-mile Capital Beltway, three lanes deep, and keep the left lane open for emergency vehicles. Commuters who wish to pass through the convoy must have a \"T2SDA\" sticker on their vehicle. The truckers are also planning to present demands to Congress, including the arrest of lawmakers who have allegedly violated their oath of office.\n\nThe protest is not without controversy, as the truckers have been accused of planning to arrest members of Congress, which is a felony offense. However, the organizers of the protest claim that they are seeking to have lawmakers arrested for treason and other alleged crimes, including funneling weapons to al-Qaida-linked rebels in Syria.\n\nThe truckers' grievances include:\n\n* Low wages and high fuel costs\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["One of the two Secret Service supervisors to lose their jobs so far over the Colombia sex scandal joked that he was checking out Sarah Palin as he guarded her during the 2008 campaign, the Washington Post finds. Senior agent David Chaney posted at least two pictures of himself with Palin on Facebook, one with the comment, \"I was really checking her out, if you know what I mean.\" Chaney has been forced to resign, and another supervisor has been informed that he will be fired. In an interview with Fox News, Palin said the joke is on Chaney. \"Well check this out, buddy—you're fired!\" she said, calling the scandal \"a symptom of government run amok,\" reports AP. \"It's like, who's minding the store around here?\" she added. \"The president, for one, he better be wary, there, of when Secret Service is accompanying his family on vacation. They may be checking out the first lady instead of guarding her.\""], "length": 3030, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Secret Service Scandal Rocks Agency**\n\nA prostitution scandal involving 11 Secret Service employees, including two supervisors, has rocked the agency, leading to the ousting of several high-ranking officials and raising questions about accountability and personal conduct within the institution.\n\nThe scandal began to unfold last weekend in Cartagena, Colombia, where President Barack Obama was attending a summit meeting. At least 11 Secret Service employees, including members of the agency's \"jump teams\" and counter-assault and counter-sniper teams, were involved in a night of carousing, which included heavy drinking, visits to a strip club, and payments to women working as prostitutes.\n\nTwo supervisors, David Randall Chaney and Greg Stokes, were among those ousted from the agency. Chaney, a 48-year-old married man with an adult son, posted several photos of himself on duty, including one with former Republican vice presidential candidate Sarah Palin, on his Facebook page. In the comments section, he joked about checking her out, saying \"I was really checking her out, if you know what I mean?\"\n\nPalin responded to the revelations, saying she was \"disgusted\" by Chaney's behavior and called it \"pretty embarrassing.\" She also criticized President Obama's management, saying the scandal was a symptom of a larger problem.\n\nThe Secret Service has moved quickly to quell the scandal, forcing out three employees so far, including the two supervisors. Eight men remain suspended and have had their top-secret security clearances lifted. The agency has also recalled its 11 employees from Colombia and replaced them with another team.\n\nLawmakers on Capitol Hill have expressed outrage over the scandal, with some calling for more firings and resignations. Rep. Peter King, chairman of the House Homeland Security Committee, said he expected more dismissals and firings in the case. Sen. Chuck Grassley, the senior Republican on the Senate Judiciary Committee, said he thought more people would be fired within \"just a few days.\"\n\nThe scandal has also raised questions about the judgment of those involved, with some lawmakers suggesting that the problem lies with the personal conduct of the employees rather than a deeper cultural problem within the agency. The Secret Service has insisted that the incident is an isolated and aberrant case, but the agency's top leadership has come under scrutiny for their handling of the situation.\n\nAs the investigation continues, the Secret Service has announced that it will conduct drug tests and polygraph exams on the 11 employees involved in the scandal. The agency has also visited all of the hotels where Secret Service personnel"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Power: One day you have it, the next you don't. No one likely knows that better than the world's 50 \"least powerful\" people, described by 24/7 Wall St. as \"well-known individuals around the world who have recently experienced a precipitous loss in stature.\" The top 10: Anthony Scaramucci: Where to begin? Try here, here, and here. Chris Christie: The former head of Donald Trump's transition team has the lowest approval rating of any governor in New Jersey's history. James Comey: The former FBI director lasted not even five months under Trump. Mark Fields: The Ford CEO was canned after shares fell almost 40% during his tenure. Tiger Woods: Blame his DUI arrest and 899th ranking in men's pro golf. Kendall Jenner: Remember that Pepsi commercial? Montgomery Moran: After a wave of issues at Chipotle, the co-CEO stepped down last year. Tomi Lahren: The conservative media personality was suspended from the Blaze after announcing she was pro-choice. Park Geun-hye: The former president of South Korea was impeached and jailed after a corruption scandal involving Samsung. Mitch McConnell: As Senate majority leader, McConnell failed to repeal and replace the Affordable Care Act. Click for the full list, which also includes Sean Spicer, R. Kelly, and Theresa May. (See last year's ranking here.)"], "length": 1240, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Falling from Fame: 5 Notable Individuals Who Lost Their Luster**\n\nIn a culture that often glorifies power, wealth, and fame, it's not uncommon for individuals to experience a precipitous fall from grace. 24/7 Wall St. recently compiled a list of 50 well-known individuals who have lost stature in recent years. Here are five notable examples:\n\n**1. Anthony Scaramucci: The Short-Lived White House Communications Director**\n\nAnthony Scaramucci's tenure as White House Communications Director was the shortest in history, lasting just 11 days. His tumultuous time in the Trump administration was marked by protocol breaches, conflicting statements, and a profanity-laced interview with The New Yorker. His wife even filed for divorce during his brief stint.\n\n**2. Chris Christie: The Governor with the Lowest Approval Ratings**\n\nNew Jersey Governor Chris Christie has the lowest approval ratings of any governor in the country. His reputation was sullied by the Bridgegate scandal, and his attempts to curry favor with Donald Trump ultimately fell flat. Christie's decision to lounge on a state-owned beach during a government shutdown only added to his unpopularity.\n\n**3. James Comey: The Fired FBI Director**\n\nJames Comey's firing by President Trump in May 2017 was a shocking move that sparked widespread controversy. Comey's investigation into Hillary Clinton's email server and his subsequent announcement of the investigation just days before the election were seen as major factors in Trump's victory. Comey's termination was met with conflicting justifications from the White House.\n\n**4. Mark Fields: The CEO Who Failed to Inspire Confidence**\n\nMark Fields, the former CEO of Ford Motor Company, was shown the door in 2017 after failing to inspire confidence on Wall Street. Despite investing in electric and autonomous vehicle technology, Ford shares fell by nearly 40% during his tenure. Fields' determination to take on Tesla ultimately led to his downfall.\n\n**5. Tiger Woods: The Fallen Golf Superstar**\n\nTiger Woods, once the top-ranked golfer in the world, has been embroiled in controversy on and off the course since reports of his infidelity surfaced in 2009. His latest scandal involved a DUI arrest, which led to a stint in rehab to manage his prescription medication use. Woods' ranking has plummeted to 899, his lowest ever.\n\nThese five individuals are just a few examples of the many people who have lost their luster in recent years. Whether due to scandal, poor performance, or a combination of both, their"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Washington is braced for the first government shutdown in 17 years, which will happen at midnight tonight unless lawmakers manage to break the deadlock over funding and hammer out a deal. Here's what to expect from a day of drama in DC: The House has passed legislation to delay ObamaCare for a year and repeal a tax on medical devices, so the measure will return to the Senate this afternoon, just hours before the shutdown is due to kick in. Harry Reid is expected to table the anti-ObamaCare amendments with a simple Democratic majority and quickly send it back to the House—but if any opportunity arises for Senate Republicans to use delaying tactics, expect Ted Cruz to lead the way, the Wall Street Journal predicts. There's a chance of a straight up-or-down vote in the House, but if it becomes clear that it will be impossible to break the deadlock by midnight—which appears to be the likeliest scenario—the partial shutdown could still be averted if lawmakers pass a short-term funding bill to buy another week or so of debating time. So who will blink first? Neither Reid nor John Boehner appears likely to give way. Reid is determined to play hardball and not give an inch on ObamaCare, Politico reports. Reid and his allies believe that giving any concessions now will only lead to bigger concessions to avoid a debt default next month—and they think the backlash from a government shutdown will hit the GOP hardest. Boehner, meanwhile, will face the wrath of his party's conservatives if he gives way too soon—but polls show the public will blame his House Republicans if there is a shutdown. If there is a shutdown, it won't be an abrupt halt to government, but more \"like a spending freeze that will gradually spread through the government like ice forming in water,\" Quartz explains. Federal employees, 800,000 of whom will be sent home without pay, will be the first affected, and the shutdown will also affect scientific research, trade negotiations, and the federal programs that guarantee home loans and provide capital to business. Air traffic controllers will still be at work, however, and agencies that deal with national security and law enforcement are exempt from the shutdown. A shutdown of a few days might not do too much damage, but experts warn that the national economy will take a serious hit if it is prolonged. In Washington, DC, however, even a brief shutdown will cost the district an estimated $200 million a day—and affect basic services like trash collection. \"This is serious,\" the director of George Mason University’s Center for Regional Analysis tells the Washington Post. \"The national economy may not notice a shutdown much unless it lasts three or four weeks. But for the Washington area, this is a tsunami.\""], "length": 3547, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Government Shutdown Looms as Congress Fails to Reach Agreement**\n\nThe US government is on the brink of a shutdown as Congress fails to reach an agreement on a spending bill. The House of Representatives has passed a plan to fund federal agencies through December 15, but it includes conditions related to the Affordable Care Act (ACA), which Senate Democrats and President Barack Obama have vowed to reject. The Senate is not scheduled to reconvene until Monday afternoon, and Democrats are expected to table the amendments to delay the ACA and repeal the medical device tax.\n\n**Reid's Hardline Stance**\n\nSenate Majority Leader Harry Reid has taken a hardline stance against House Republicans, refusing to negotiate on a short-term stopgap funding measure. Reid believes that giving in to Republican demands would lead to further concessions on the debt ceiling and other fiscal issues. Democrats are confident that the political backlash from a shutdown would be devastating to the GOP, potentially costing them their majority in the House.\n\n**Economic Impact of a Shutdown**\n\nA government shutdown would have significant economic implications for the Washington region, which is home to the largest concentration of federal workers and contractors in the nation. Local economist Stephen Fuller estimates that the region could lose $200 million a day and see over 700,000 jobs take a financial hit. The shutdown would also affect tourism, with federally funded attractions such as the Smithsonian museums and national parks likely to be closed.\n\n**Consequences of a Shutdown**\n\nA shutdown would have widespread ripple effects, including:\n\n* Federal benefits may continue to be processed, but with fewer workers to process them, no new applications would be accepted and \"serious backlogs\" would be expected.\n* Veterans' benefits could run out if a shutdown lasts more than a few weeks.\n* Lawmakers passed legislation to continue paying active military members, but other federal employees would be furloughed.\n* The shutdown could affect state and local governments, potentially leading to cuts in services such as Meals on Wheels and emergency preparedness training.\n\n**Local Governments Prepare for a Shutdown**\n\nLocal governments in the Washington region are preparing for a shutdown by setting aside pots of money in anticipation of lost revenue. Maryland officials estimate that the state would lose $5 million a day, while Prince George's County officials say they could lose $270,000 in income tax revenue daily during a shutdown.\n\n**Congressional Response**\n\nCongress is scrambling to reach an agreement before the Monday night deadline. House Speaker John Boehner has said that he will not stand down from a fight over the ACA, while Senate Democrats are confident that they"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["NASA is going to the sun. More specifically, it's launching an unmanned probe next year that will travel closer to the star than any spacecraft has done previously. \"It's a spacecraft loaded with technological breakthroughs that will solve many of the largest mysteries about our star,\" says Nicola Fox of the Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory. In less scientific terms, per the Telegraph: \"We will finally touch the sun.\" The nuts and bolts: The mission: The 10-foot probe will launch in July or August of 2018 and eventually get to within 3.7 million miles of the sun, about seven times closer than any previous spacecraft. Eventually, it will be whizzing around the sun at a speed of 450,000 miles per hour, reports CNN. The mission ends in 2025. Corona: The probe will actually fly into the outermost part of the sun's atmosphere, called the corona, per a mission overview at NASA that touts \"humanity's first visit to a star.\" The heat: The probe will rely on a 4.5-inch-thick carbon-composite solar shield to help it withstand temperatures up to 2,550 degrees Fahrenheit, reports Orlando's WKMG. Instruments will remain at room temperature. The name: The probe's name has been changed to the Parker Solar Probe to honor astrophysicist Eugene Parker, who's credited with discovering solar wind. Parker, who turns 90 in June, is a professor emeritus at the University of Chicago, and the Guardian recounts that the theory he put forward in 1958 about a stream of charged particles flowing from the sun was once thought to be \"crazy.\" A first: This is the first time NASA has named a mission after a living scientist. \"I'm certainly greatly honored,\" said the man himself, per Space.com. Two puzzles: Scientists hope to better understand two things in particular: \"How is the solar wind accelerated, and why is the ... corona so much hotter than the solar surface?\" (It's 3 million degrees Fahrenheit vs. 10,000 degrees Fahrenheit.) Why it matters: Generally, solar storms are relatively harmless when they reach Earth, but these particle bursts occasionally wreak havoc on satellites and here on Earth, and they have the potential to be devastating. \"The more we know about how these processes work, the better we can get at predicting when they will happen,\" writes Loren Grush at the Verge."], "length": 10147, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**NASA's Parker Solar Probe Set to Revolutionize Understanding of the Sun**\n\nNASA's Parker Solar Probe, previously known as Solar Probe Plus, is set to embark on a historic mission to explore the sun's atmosphere in unprecedented detail. The spacecraft, named after pioneering astrophysicist Eugene Parker, will travel closer to the sun than any spacecraft has ever gone before, within 3.7 million miles of its surface. The mission aims to answer two long-standing puzzles: how the solar wind is accelerated and why the sun's outer atmosphere, the corona, is so much hotter than its surface.\n\nThe Parker Solar Probe will perform 24 close flybys of the sun, with temperatures reaching up to 2,500 degrees Fahrenheit and solar radiation intensities 475 times greater than on Earth. The spacecraft will be equipped with a 7.5-foot-wide heat shield and a thermal protection system to withstand the extreme conditions. The mission will also help improve forecasting of major space weather events, which can impact satellites, astronauts, and the Earth's power grid and radiation exposure on airline flights.\n\nThe Parker Solar Probe is scheduled to launch in July 2018 and will take seven flybys of Venus to shrink its orbit around the sun over nearly seven years. The spacecraft will eventually be closer to the sun than Mercury and will watch solar wind whip up from subsonic to supersonic. The mission is expected to end in June 2025.\n\n**Why the Mission Matters**\n\nThe Parker Solar Probe's findings will help answer questions about how stars work and improve space weather forecasting. The mission will also provide insight into the physics of stars, change what we know about the mysterious corona, and increase understanding of solar wind. The solar wind is a stream of charged gases from the sun that is present in most of the solar system, and disturbances of the solar wind cause disruptive space weather that impacts our planet.\n\n**The Importance of Eugene Parker's Work**\n\nEugene Parker, the namesake of the mission, predicted the existence of solar wind in 1958, when he was a young professor at the University of Chicago. His work revolutionized our understanding of the sun and interplanetary space. Parker is now the S. Chandrasekhar Distinguished Service Professor Emeritus at the University of Chicago and has been awarded the NASA Distinguished Public Service Medal.\n\n**The Mission's Objectives**\n\nThe Parker Solar Probe's science objectives include tracing the energy behind solar wind, understanding the heating of the solar corona, and determining what accelerates"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["It looks like the hackers that hit Target had lots of other, well, targets. The Department of Homeland Security recently sent retailers and financial service companies a secret memo warning that the Target hit appeared to be part of a larger international campaign, the Wall Street Journal reports, an insight gleaned with the help of Dallas cybersecurity firm iSight Partners. Yesterday, iSight released its own report, saying that a virus it's calling KAPTOXA \"has potentially infected a large number of retail information systems\"—and noting that the \"intrusion operators displayed innovation and a high degree of skill,\" particularly in terms of the \"operational sophistication\" of the hack. The Journal shares this feature by way of example: The virus focused on stealing data during the peak hours of 10am and 5pm; the data was housed in a Target server that the hackers later accessed. The virus attacks point-of-sale systems in a way that is \"new to eCrime,\" subverting traditional efforts to protect consumer data, the report warns, according to NBC. Parts of the code, which is impervious to all known antivirus software, have been online since last spring. It's partly in Russian, which US officials think may indicate a link to organized crime in the former Soviet Union. The finding follows reports of similar breaches at Neiman Marcus. That breach had gone unnoticed since July, the New York Times reports today; Neiman's system was only fully secured Sunday."], "length": 1464, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Holiday Data Breaches Hit Multiple Retailers**\n\nIn recent weeks, two major retailers, Neiman Marcus and Target, have been hit by significant data breaches, compromising the sensitive information of millions of customers. According to reports, the breaches were caused by sophisticated hacking campaigns, with the attackers using malware to steal credit and debit card data from point-of-sale systems.\n\n**Neiman Marcus Breach**\n\nNeiman Marcus disclosed that its computer network was penetrated by hackers as far back as July, with the breach not fully contained until January 1. The company revealed that some customers' payment cards were used fraudulently after making purchases at its stores, but assured that Social Security numbers and birth dates were not compromised. Neiman Marcus has apologized for not disclosing the breach sooner, citing a need to confirm evidence before notifying customers. The company has offered one free year of credit monitoring to affected customers.\n\n**Target Breach**\n\nTarget's data breach, which was disclosed in December, affected over 100 million customers, with 40 million credit and debit card numbers stolen, along with encrypted PINs for debit cards. A 17-page report from computer firm iSight, obtained by NBC News, provides new insight into the breach, detailing the type of malware used and the regions known to have the software coding prowess to pull off the attack. The report notes that the breach was caused by a type of malware placed in a store's point-of-sale systems, and that the attackers displayed a high degree of skill in orchestrating the breach.\n\n**International Hacking Campaign**\n\nA report prepared by federal and private investigators suggests that the Target breach was part of a broader and highly sophisticated international hacking campaign against multiple retailers. The report, sent to financial-services companies and retailers, offers some of the first details to emerge about the source of the attack. While the report does not draw conclusions on who is ultimately responsible for the attack, it notes that the malware used in the breach is similar to that used in other recent attacks, and that the attackers may be based in Eastern Europe or Brazil.\n\n**Investigations and Consequences**\n\nA growing group of state attorneys general, including those from Connecticut, New York, and Illinois, are jointly investigating both the Target and Neiman Marcus breaches, examining whether the two attacks are related. The authorities are also looking into whether the breaches were committed by the same criminal groups in Eastern Europe. The consequences of the breaches are still unfolding, with cardholders facing the risk of identity theft and financial loss."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["An escaped lion, a taunting crowd, and an ill-prepared first-response team came together in Kenya to cause what some are saying was the unnecessary, senseless death of the lion, the country's Star reports. Mohawk, a 13-year-old lion who was one of Nairobi National Park's most well-known creatures due to his trademark mane and rep as somewhat of a \"ladies' lion,\" had escaped from an unfenced section of the park and ended up in the town of Isinya, where he was promptly surrounded by a jeering group of men, the Washington Post reports. Mohawk became upset by the noise and the growing crowd—Kenya Wildlife Service officials tell the AP about 400 had surrounded him—and lunged at a 27-year-old man, who was sent to the hospital with bruises and severe lacerations on his back. The wildlife service already had an animal management team on the scene, but those rangers had only rifles, not tranquilizers. After Mohawk pounced on the bystander, the rangers felt they couldn't wait for a second team on its way with tranqs—so they unleashed what the Star says was nine bullets, leaving the animal \"roaring and writhing in pain\" as he died. \"If the rangers had the right equipment, this lion would not have died,\" a local resident tells the paper. \"You can't keep a lion in the midst of people for so many hours without stressing it. We blame them for this heinous act.\" The shooting has prompted plenty of backlash on social media, including a #JusticeForMohawk hashtag, but the wildlife service says it had no choice once the lion started attacking humans. \"This action was taken as a last resort after an escalation of the situation and a concern for public safety,\" the Kenya Wildlife Service says, per CNN. A bunch of lions have busted out of the park lately, driven by what conservationists say is the noise from development projects, including a highway being built through the park. (Same ending, different means for Cecil the lion.)"], "length": 3189, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Lion Escapes and Deaths in Kenya and South Africa**\n\nIn recent weeks, several incidents have highlighted the complex relationship between humans and lions in Kenya and South Africa. In Kenya, a 13-year-old male lion named Mohawk was shot dead by wildlife rangers after it attacked a man in a crowd. Mohawk, a beloved tourist attraction, had escaped from Nairobi National Park and was surrounded by hundreds of bystanders. Despite efforts to tranquilize him, the rangers were forced to shoot him to prevent further harm to humans.\n\nThe incident sparked an outcry among Kenyans, with many questioning why the rangers did not try to sedate the lion instead of killing it. The Kenya Wildlife Service (KWS) has faced criticism for its handling of the situation, with some accusing the agency of being too quick to resort to lethal force.\n\nMohawk's death is the latest in a series of lion escapes from Nairobi National Park. In recent weeks, several lions have escaped from the park, with some being recaptured alive. However, the park's infrastructure has been criticized for being inadequate, with a lack of fencing and a growing human population encroaching on the park's boundaries.\n\nIn South Africa, a similar incident has unfolded. A 3-year-old male lion named Sylvester has escaped from Karoo National Park and is being hunted by wildlife officials. The lion, which has a satellite tracking collar, has been on the loose for several days and has been spotted in a sparsely populated area. While officials have vowed to put him down if he poses a threat to humans, conservationists are calling for the lion to be captured and relocated.\n\nThe incidents highlight the challenges of balancing human safety with the need to conserve and protect lion populations. The African lion is considered a vulnerable species, with its population having declined by 50% in the past 20 years due to habitat loss, human-wildlife conflict, and other factors.\n\nIn Kenya, the KWS has acknowledged that habitat loss and human-wildlife conflict are major contributors to the surge in lion escapes. The agency has promised to work with contractors to maintain the integrity of the park and keep animals safe. However, the incident has raised questions about the effectiveness of the KWS's policies and procedures for handling lion escapes.\n\nOverall, the incidents in Kenya and South Africa serve as a reminder of the complex and often fraught relationship between humans and lions. While efforts to conserve and protect lion populations are essential, they must be balanced with the need to ensure human safety and prevent conflicts"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A horrifying story out of Uganda, where a toddler was grabbed and eaten by a leopard Friday at the Mweya Safari Lodge in Queen Elizabeth National Park. The 2-year-old boy's mother is a game ranger at the park and was working, and the boy was with his nanny in the kitchen of the family's home in the park's staff quarters, near a doorway. \"He was seated with the maid when the leopard grabbed and ran with him,\" the boy's father tells the Kampala Post. But a spokesperson for the Uganda Wildlife Authority (UWA) says the boy followed the nanny outdoors: \"The maid was not aware the child followed her. She heard the kid scream for help, she intervened but it was too late the leopard had vanished with it in the bush.\" The staff quarters are reportedly in a protected area that is popular with tourists, USA Today reports, but the family's home is said to be unfenced. A search team ultimately found some of the boy's bones, including his skull, and they were buried over the weekend. The boy's father says he expects compensation from the UWA and that the organization should do more to protect staff and their families. \"UWA gave us the coffin,\" he says. \"I have not talked to them about the incident but I would expect something reasonable to compensate me, although my son's life is gone.\" Leopard attacks on humans are rare in Uganda. The UWA spokesperson says efforts are being made to locate the leopard and possibly relocate it. \"The hunt is on with the intention of capturing the leopard and removing it from the wild because once it has eaten human flesh, the temptations are high to eat another human being, it becomes dangerous,\" he says, per the Telegraph."], "length": 1170, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Leopard Attacks and Kills Toddler in Queen Elizabeth National Park, Uganda**\n\nA devastating incident has occurred in Queen Elizabeth National Park, Uganda, where a leopard attacked and killed a 2-year-old toddler. The child, Elisha Nabugyere, was left in the care of a nanny at the staff quarters of Mweya Safari Lodge when the leopard snatched him on Friday night. The nanny heard the child scream and tried to intervene, but it was too late, and the leopard had vanished with the child into the bush.\n\nThe child's father, SSP Francis Manana Nabugyere, a police officer, reported that the leopard dragged the child into the bushes, and a search team later found the child's skull and \"some other bones\" on Saturday. The child's remains were buried over the weekend, and the family is seeking compensation from the Uganda Wildlife Authority (UWA).\n\nThe incident has sparked a hunt for the leopard, which is considered a threat to human life after consuming human flesh. UWA communications manager Bashir Hangi said that efforts are underway to track down the leopard and relocate it to a safer area. Hangi also stated that the leopard's behavior is a concern, as it may become a danger to humans if not captured and relocated.\n\nThis incident is the fourth unfortunate event to occur in the park in recent weeks. A pride of 11 lions died on April 11, a French tourist died on April 14, and another lion died at the end of April. The UWA has promised to take measures to ensure the safety of staff and visitors in the park.\n\nThe family of the deceased child is seeking compensation and heightened security measures to prevent such incidents in the future. The incident has shocked many in Uganda, where leopard attacks on humans are rare. The UWA has promised to work towards establishing the leopard and evaluating options for its relocation."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Archaeologists in Bulgaria think they've found ashes belonging to one of the 12 apostles. The team found a small lead container with ashes while excavating a basilica that dates back to the sixth century AD, reports Archaeology in Bulgaria. The markings on the inch-long vessel match those at the grave of St. John the Apostle in what is now Turkey, leading researchers to surmise that a long-ago Christian from Bulgaria traveled to the grave site and returned home with the sample of ashes. Such journeys were common around that time, reports Novinite. “Probably a pilgrim from the Foros Peninsula went on a pilgrimage to Ephesus, and came back here with this relic, which was then donated to the basilica on Foros,” says the director of the Burgas Regional Museum of History in Bulgaria. (The ancient basilica is located in what is now the Black Sea city of Burgas.) Archeologists also found a Bulgarian royal seal from the 10th century at the dig site, which includes a fortress, notes the Week. (Another archaeological discovery: an ancient underground city in Turkey.)"], "length": 2303, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Archaeologists Uncover 6th Century Relic of John the Apostle in Bulgaria**\n\nIn a groundbreaking discovery, archaeologists at a medieval fortress site in Burgas, Bulgaria, have unearthed a lead vessel containing ashes from the alleged grave of John the Apostle, one of Jesus' twelve apostles. The relic, a tiny lead tube measuring less than an inch long, is dated to the 6th century C.E. and is believed to have been brought back from the grave of John the Apostle in Ephesus, Turkey, by a pilgrim.\n\nThe discovery was made during excavations of the ancient and medieval port of Burgos (also known as Poros) on Cape Foros in the Bulgarian city of Burgas. The site, which was first excavated in 2008, has yielded a wealth of artifacts and buildings dating back to the 4th-6th century AD. The lead tube reliquary is considered one of the most important discoveries in the history of the Burgas Regional Museum of History.\n\nAccording to Milen Nikolov, director of the museum, early Christians believed that the relic had healing properties, and pilgrims would collect a holy curing powder, known as manna, from the site of John the Apostle's grave. The manna was believed to have the power to cure all kinds of diseases.\n\nThe discovery of the lead tube reliquary is significant not only because of its age and significance but also because it is one of only 43 known lead tubes from the 6th century AD. The majority of these tubes have been found in Italy, with only a few others discovered in Germany and the United States.\n\nThe relic is also notable for its unique images, which are identical to those found on clay tube reliquaries produced in ancient Ephesus. Nikolov believes that the Burgas reliquary comes from the pilgrimage center in Ephesus and is connected to the cult of St. John the Theologian (John the Apostle).\n\nThis discovery is the latest in a series of significant finds at the Burgos site, which has also yielded a 10th century Bulgarian royal seal and a 2nd century AD inscription carved into stone. The site is considered one of the most important archaeological sites in Bulgaria, and its discovery has shed new light on the history of early Christianity and the significance of pilgrimage sites in the 6th century AD."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Expecting parents wondering which company offers the best maternity and paternity leave in the US need look no further. Netflix announced yesterday that it is upping the ante, offering new parents unlimited, fully-paid maternity and paternity leave for the first year after having or adopting a child. The streaming video company based in Los Gatos, Calif., also offers unlimited paid vacation, and it's all toward fostering \"a 'freedom and responsibility' culture that gives our employees context about our business and the freedom to make their own decisions along with the accompanying responsibility,\" writes Tawni Cranz, chief talent officer. The new policy \"deserves high marks for extending leave to fathers,\" reports Fortune, \"as well as understanding that the entire first year after childbirth can be challenging.\" It's a \"landmark perk,\" reports TechCrunch, noting that Yahoo doubled its maternity and paternity leave in 2013 (to 16 paid weeks for moms and eight paid weeks for dads) to compete with packages offered by Facebook and Google because \"the talent is growing up.\" A decade ago big company perks included free lunch and ping pong tables, but now the major moves are about helping employees balance work and home life because \"experience shows people perform better at work when they’re not worrying about home,\" Cranz adds. Of course, employees will have to plan their leave with managers and coworkers in advance to make sure their absence isn't disruptive—behaving, you know, like adults. (See who beat Netflix to the punch.)"], "length": 1112, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Netflix Introduces Unlimited Maternity and Paternity Leave Policy**\n\nIn a move to attract and retain top talent, Netflix has announced an unlimited leave policy for new mothers and fathers. The policy allows employees to take off as much time as they need during the first year after a child's birth or adoption, with the option to return to work on a full- or part-time basis. The company will continue to pay employees normally, eliminating the need to switch to state or disability pay.\n\nAccording to Netflix's Chief Talent Officer, Tawni Cranz, the policy is designed to give employees the flexibility and confidence to balance their work and family responsibilities. The company believes that this policy will help employees return to work more focused and dedicated, leading to better performance and productivity.\n\nThis new policy is part of Netflix's broader effort to foster a \"freedom and responsibility\" culture, which gives employees the autonomy to make their own decisions and take ownership of their work. The company's unlimited time-off policy, which allows employees to take as much vacation time as they need, is also seen as a key factor in attracting and retaining top talent.\n\nWhile the policy has been praised for its generosity and flexibility, some experts have noted that unlimited time-off policies can have their pitfalls. Extended absences can strain workplace relations and require employees to work with their managers and teams ahead of time to ensure that their absence won't jeopardize a project or deadline.\n\nDespite these potential challenges, Netflix's new policy is seen as a major step forward in providing support for new parents. The company's decision to extend leave to fathers, as well as its recognition of the challenges faced by new parents during the first year after childbirth, has been particularly well-received.\n\nOverall, Netflix's unlimited maternity and paternity leave policy is a significant development in the world of employee benefits, and is likely to be closely watched by other companies in the tech industry."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A Nazi drinking game played by students from the London School of Economics sparked a brawl and ended with a broken nose, reports the Guardian. The row occurred during a ski trip taken by 150 members of the school's athletic union to Val D'Isere in France. The confrontation occurred after a Jewish student objected to the drinking game, according to the school's Jewish Society. The game involved playing cards arranged on a table in the shape of a swastika. Players were required to \"salute the führer,\" according to the student newspaper. \"Nazi glorification and anti-semitism have no place in our universities,\" said the head of the Jewish Society. A video of the drinking game was briefly posted on Facebook. The school is investigating. \"These are disturbing allegations relating to events which took place on a foreign trip organized by the students' union,\" said a school statement, which added that officials are \"prepared to take disciplinary action. Students must abide by clear standards of behavior. We do not tolerate anti-semitism or any other form of racism.\""], "length": 803, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Racism and Antisemitism on University Campuses: Recent Incidents**\n\nIn recent weeks, two separate incidents of racism and antisemitism have come to light on university campuses in the UK. The first incident occurred at the London School of Economics (LSE), where a Nazi-themed drinking game led to a brawl in which a Jewish student's nose was broken. The game, a version of Ring of Fire, involved players arranging cards in the shape of a swastika and \"saluting the Führer.\" The incident occurred on a skiing trip to Val d'Isère organized by the students' union, which has launched an investigation into the allegations.\n\nThe LSE has condemned the incident, stating that it does not tolerate antisemitism or any other form of racism. The university's Jewish society has also spoken out against the incident, with its president, Jay Stoll, saying that Nazi glorification and antisemitism have no place in universities. The student who was injured in the brawl has also spoken out, saying that the comments made by other students during the game were \"extremely upsetting\" and made him feel like his identity was being attacked.\n\nThe second incident occurred at Oxford University, where members of the Conservative Association were accused of singing an antisemitic song with Nazi references. The incident was reported in the Daily Telegraph last November, but it has only recently come to light.\n\nThese incidents are a disturbing reminder of the ongoing problem of racism and antisemitism on university campuses. They highlight the need for universities to take a strong stance against hate speech and to create a safe and inclusive environment for all students. As one student at the LSE said, \"Universities should remain safe spaces for all students, regardless of their background or identity.\"\n\n**Related News**\n\n* Alexa Internet has been donating its crawl data to the Internet Archive since 1996, with the data being added to the Wayback Machine after an embargo period.\n* The London School of Economics is investigating allegations of a Nazi-themed drinking game that led to a brawl in which a Jewish student's nose was broken.\n* Oxford University's Conservative Association was accused of singing an antisemitic song with Nazi references last November.\n\n**Key Quotes**\n\n* \"Nazi glorification and antisemitism have no place in our universities, which should remain safe spaces for all students.\" - Jay Stoll, president of the LSE's Jewish Society\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A Kansas City couple who went to check out a car they saw on Craigslist were shot and robbed Wednesday night, USA Today reports. They had wanted to purchase the vehicle for their 16-year-old son, who was with them but not injured. Police say the 53-year-old dad and his son had test-driven a 2007 Hyundai Sonata and were discussing payment when the seller said he had to call his wife and momentarily walked away, according to KCTV. That’s when two other men reportedly approached the family and demanded cash, then started shooting. As the dad tried to keep his wife and son out of the barrage of bullets, he was shot four times in the chest, stomach, and legs, while his 55-year-old wife took a bullet to her upper right arm, which was “shattered,” reports the TV station. The men took the wife's purse, which had $3,000 in cash for the car purchase, say police. Authorities believe the incident may be related to an earlier armed robbery that day in which a Kansas City man says he tried to sell his Xbox and gaming accessories to a man who saw his ad on Craigslist, reports the Kansas City Star. As the potential buyer was examining the wares, two other men reportedly arrived sporting a gun; the three men then lifted the Xbox and other items and ran into nearby woods, police say."], "length": 1819, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Kansas City Couple Robbed and Shot While Trying to Buy Teen's First Car**\n\nA shocking crime has left a Kansas City couple fighting for their lives after they were robbed and shot while trying to buy their teenage son's first car on Craigslist. The incident occurred on Wednesday night at an apartment complex in southeast Kansas City.\n\nAccording to police, the couple, both in their 50s, and their 16-year-old son had gone to the complex to look at a 2007 Hyundai Sonata listed for sale on Craigslist. The seller, who identified himself as \"AJ,\" took the family for a test drive, but when they returned to the complex, two men approached them, armed with black handguns. The suspects demanded money, and when the couple said they didn't have any, the suspects opened fire.\n\nThe father, 53, was shot four times in the chest, stomach, and legs, and underwent surgery overnight. His wife, 55, was shot in the right upper arm, which was shattered by a single bullet. Their son was not injured.\n\nThe suspects fled in the Hyundai, which was later found abandoned in a nearby neighborhood. Police are still searching for the three suspects, who are described as black men in their late 20s or early 30s.\n\nThe incident is believed to be connected to an earlier armed robbery of a man who was selling an Xbox on Craigslist. In that case, the victim was approached by three people, including two men with black handguns, and robbed of his property.\n\nPolice are urging anyone with information to come forward and are offering a $7,000 reward for tips leading to the arrest of the suspects. The normal reward of $2,000 has been increased by $5,000, courtesy of Liberty-based Ferrellgas, where the injured man works.\n\nThe family's bravery and quick thinking may have saved their son's life, as the father stepped in front of his wife to shield her from the gunfire. The incident has left the community shaken, and police are advising people to use caution when meeting strangers online and to always prioritize their safety.\n\n**Key Details:**\n\n* Date: Wednesday night\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Secretive data companies are tracking almost every American's every move online—and compiling and selling disturbingly targeted lists based on that spying, a new Senate Commerce Committee report concludes. That includes lists of rape victims, people suffering from ailments including HIV, AIDS, and dementia, and people with substance abuse problems, a privacy group said in a hearing yesterday, as per CNN. Mailing addresses for police officers and domestic violence shelters—the latter of which are usually protected by law—are also for sale, as are lists based on demographics and economics, including \"Ethnic Second-City Strugglers,\" and \"Rural and Barely Making It.\" The World Privacy Forum urged Congress to act to \"remove unsafe, unfair, and overall just deplorable lists from circulation.\" The Direct Marketing Association released a statement saying that while the lists are occasionally \"used to disparage certain groups,\" they represented a \"tiny minority\" of marketing products. Overall the committee looked at nine companies, some of which refused to explain how they got their data or who they sold it to, Gawker reports. One company admitted it had sold social security numbers and banking information to an alleged identity theft ring."], "length": 1392, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Data Brokers Selling Sensitive Consumer Information: A Growing Concern**\n\nA recent report by the Senate Commerce Committee and a hearing by the World Privacy Forum have shed light on the multibillion-dollar data industry, revealing that major data companies are selling deeply personal information about consumers, including sensitive health conditions, financial information, and even lists of rape victims, seniors with dementia, and people with HIV and AIDS.\n\nThe report, which queried nine major data companies, found that they collectively own an astonishing number of consumer profiles and are selling this information to marketers, identity thieves, and other third parties. The data includes information such as:\n\n* Health conditions, including cancer, diabetes, and depression\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Pluto is gonna be PO'd. While the dwarf planet tries to fight its way back into the good graces of Those Who Deem What Counts as a Planet, another icy orb even further out may snatch that designation first. Astronomers at the California Institute of Technology say a giant celestial body \"lurking at the end of our solar system,\" as the Washington Post puts it, may actually be a planet, and they're even already calling it \"Planet Nine.\" In their research published in the Astronomical Journal, the sky-watching scientists—one of whom is known as the \"Pluto killer\" for his role in getting Pluto demoted—think their find is five to 10 times as massive as Earth, and per the AP, almost as big as Neptune and orbiting billions of miles past that planet's orbit. Michael \"Pluto Killer\" Brown and Konstantin Batygin haven't seen the supposed planet directly, but say they can infer the \"massive perturber\" exists by how the orbits of smaller bodies nearby are affected by its gravitational pull, the Post notes. What's interesting is that Brown and Batygin originally set out to disprove the existence of Planet Nine. \"We thought their idea was crazy,\" Brown says of the scientists who originally floated the idea of a large, hidden planet. But as they did their own research, they soon came to their own conclusion in what Brown calls a \"jaw-dropping moment\" that Planet Nine could be the real deal. Now they're simply hoping more astronomers join in to actually try to spot the alleged planet—and they're not concerned it will face Pluto's fate. \"That's not even a question—it's definitely a planet,\" Brown says. Not everyone's convinced. \"I have seen many, many such claims in my career,\" a planetary scientist at Colorado's Southwest Research Institute tells Nature. \"And all of them have been wrong.\" (The Washington Post caught up with Brown for a Q&A on the latest Pluto-killing endeavor.)"], "length": 4933, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Astronomers Discover Evidence of a Ninth Planet in Our Solar System**\n\nA team of astronomers from the California Institute of Technology (Caltech) has announced the discovery of a new planet in our solar system, which they have dubbed \"Planet Nine.\" The planet is estimated to be five to 10 times the mass of Earth and is believed to be an icy, rocky world with a small envelope of gas. It is thought to be located in the outer reaches of the solar system, beyond the orbit of Pluto, and takes 10,000 to 20,000 years to complete one orbit around the sun.\n\nThe discovery was made by Mike Brown and Konstantin Batygin, who used mathematical and computer modeling to infer the existence of the planet. They based their findings on the motion of recently discovered dwarf planets and other small objects in the outer solar system, which appear to be influenced by the gravity of a hidden planet.\n\nThe existence of Planet Nine would reconfigure our understanding of the solar system and could potentially lead to a reclassification of Pluto, which was demoted from its status as a planet in 2006. Brown, who is known as the \"Pluto killer\" for his role in Pluto's demotion, believes that Planet Nine is a more significant discovery and that Pluto's status as a planet is no longer relevant.\n\nThe search for Planet Nine is ongoing, with telescopes on at least two continents searching for the object. While the discovery of Planet Nine is still a theoretical one, Brown and Batygin are confident that it will be detected within the next few years.\n\n**Background on Pluto's Demotion**\n\nIn 2006, the International Astronomical Union (IAU) redefined the term \"planet\" and reclassified Pluto as a dwarf planet. This decision was made after the discovery of several other objects in the Kuiper Belt, a region of icy bodies beyond Neptune's orbit, that were similar in size and composition to Pluto.\n\nBrown's discovery of the dwarf planet Eris in 2005 was a key factor in the IAU's decision to reclassify Pluto. Eris is slightly larger than Pluto and has a more massive orbit, which led the IAU to conclude that Pluto did not meet the new criteria for a planet.\n\n**Reactions to the Discovery**\n\nThe discovery of Planet Nine has sparked a mix of reactions from the scientific community. Some scientists, such as NASA's director of planetary science, Jim Green, have expressed caution and emphasized the need for further evidence before confirming the existence of"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Troops loyal to Moammar Gadhafi massacred some 150 detainees last week, according to volunteers who cleared charred bodies from the warehouse that had apparently served as the detainees’ prison. An escaped prisoner told his story to CNN: He and his brother were handcuffed and blindfolded for no apparent reason while out walking earlier this month. They were thrown into the warehouse with 60 others ages 17 to 70. The number inside quickly reached 175 as loyalists continued to imprison people. The detainees were given no food or water for days, said Muneer Own. Finally, the troops said they would set them free—but instead hurled a grenade at them and opened fire. About 25 people were able to escape, Own said; his brother apparently was not one of them. The mass of bodies was discovered when rebels eventually took over the area. Many remain unidentified, part of a growing number of prisoners whose whereabouts remain unknown, notes the Washington Post. Thousands—some of whom have been jailed for decades—could be in underground prisons or mass graves, rebels fear. Click through for one family’s story."], "length": 1196, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Libya's Human Crisis: Thousands Missing, Mass Graves Discovered**\n\nThe situation in Libya continues to deteriorate as the country struggles to come to terms with the aftermath of the six-month-long conflict. Thousands of people are still missing, with estimates suggesting that nearly 60,000 Libyans have disappeared since the war began. Rebel leaders have opened Gaddafi's prisons in Tripoli, freeing thousands of prisoners, but only about 10,000 have been accounted for, leaving families and friends to fear that thousands are in underground prisons or mass graves.\n\nOne family's wait for news of their loved one has finally ended. Abdul Rauf, a 25-year-old rebel fighter, was held captive for 48 days in the notorious Abu Salim facility in Tripoli. He was subjected to brutal treatment, including beatings and electric shocks, but was eventually freed when guards secretly working against Gaddafi unlocked cell and prison doors. Abdul Rauf's reunion with his family was a joyous occasion, with his mother and sisters showering him with kisses and hugs.\n\nHowever, the situation is far from celebratory. In Salahuddin, a town in eastern Libya, a harrowing story has emerged of a massacre at a warehouse where pro-Gaddafi forces slaughtered nearly 150 prisoners as rebels closed in on Tripoli last week. The victims were crammed into a small warehouse with no food or water, and were subjected to abuse and beatings. One survivor, Muneer Own, managed to escape, but his brother is still missing and presumed dead.\n\nThe discovery of mass graves and charred remains has highlighted the scale of the human crisis in Libya. Volunteers have pulled out at least 150 bodies from the warehouse, and many more are feared to be buried in mass graves. The situation is a stark reminder of the brutality of Gaddafi's regime and the need for accountability and justice.\n\nAs the country struggles to come to terms with its past, the international community must continue to provide support and assistance to the people of Libya. The discovery of mass graves and the ongoing search for missing persons are a stark reminder of the need for a thorough investigation into the human rights abuses committed during the conflict."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Losing weight is one thing, keeping it off quite another. And now researchers are finding that among those with coronary heart disease (CHD)—which the Mayo Clinic reports develops from damaged or diseased blood vessels typically caused by blockage and inflammation—yo-yo dieting may dramatically increase the risk of stroke, heart attack, and death. Reporting in the New England Journal of Medicine, researchers say they followed nearly 10,000 men and women with CHD between the ages of 35 and 75 for about five years, monitoring changes in body weight and other health outcomes. The takeaway? \"It's important to lose weight, but this data says you have to keep it off,\" lead researcher Dr. Sripal Bangalore at the New York University School of Medicine tells the New York Times. The highest weight fluctuation was on average 8.6 pounds over five years, while the smallest body weight changes averaged closer to two pounds, reports Medical News Today. Those who were already overweight or obese at the beginning of the study and who experienced the highest level of weight fluctuation experienced 124% more deaths, 136% more strokes, and 117% more heart attacks, meaning these CHD patients were more than twice as likely to suffer these negative outcomes in just a few years than people with CHD whose weight only fluctuated two pounds. This doesn't pinpoint a cause, as pre-existing heart problems could be the culprit, but researchers hope to study this correlation further. (Yo-yo dieting is bad for women's hearts in general.)"], "length": 1368, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Yo-Yo Dieting Linked to Increased Risk of Heart Attack, Stroke, and Death in People with Coronary Artery Disease**\n\nA new study published in The New England Journal of Medicine has found that rapidly losing and regaining weight, also known as \"yo-yo\" dieting, may increase the risk of deadly heart attacks and strokes in people with coronary artery disease. The study, which followed 9,509 men and women with stable coronary artery disease over a period of five years, found that those who experienced the largest fluctuations in weight had a 78% higher risk of new onset diabetes, a 117% higher risk of heart attack, a 136% higher risk of stroke, and a 124% higher risk of death.\n\nCoronary artery disease, also known as coronary heart disease, is a condition in which the major blood vessels that supply the heart with blood, oxygen, and nutrients become damaged or diseased. Cholesterol-containing deposits and inflammation in the arteries are usually to blame for the condition, which can lead to chest pain, shortness of breath, and even heart attack.\n\nThe study's lead author, Dr. Sripal Bangalore, notes that while losing weight is important, it's equally important to keep the weight off. \"Many times people are motivated until they lose the weight, and then they feel they can take it easy,\" he said. \"Maybe this information can be used as motivation to keep the weight off.\"\n\nThe study's findings are consistent with previous research that has associated yo-yo dieting with increased risk of poor heart health. However, this study is the first to specifically examine the health consequences of weight fluctuations in people with pre-existing coronary heart disease.\n\nThe researchers found that individuals who experienced the largest changes in body weight were at much higher risk of heart attack, stroke, and death than those who experienced little change in body weight. The study's findings suggest that weight fluctuation may be a significant concern for people with coronary heart disease, and that further investigation is needed to understand the causes of this increased risk.\n\nIn an interview, Dr. Bangalore noted that the study's findings warrant further investigation and that more research is needed to understand how to help Americans keep weight off, rather than having it go up and down."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A Texas woman was caught on surveillance video stealing a wedding ring off the finger of a dead woman Friday, the Odessa American reports. The body of Lois Hicks, 88, was in an open casket at Odessa's Sunset Funeral Home. The woman's family had just left the funeral home after a visitation service when a stranger parked outside, came in, and asked to use the restroom. Instead, the funeral home's manager says, she walked into the room with Hicks' coffin, grabbed the ring off her finger, and left. Hicks' daughter says some of her mother's skin was actually torn off during the theft. \"I can't believe someone would be that low,\" she says. The surveillance video captured the alleged thief's license plate, and the family is pressing charges. The incident is being investigated as a felony, KOSA reports. The suspect is described as a heavy-set white woman who was wearing a brown long-sleeved sweater, black sweat pants, and black sandals at the time; she drove off in a red or maroon four-door Saturn."], "length": 989, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Funeral Home Theft: Suspect Sought in Despicable Crime**\n\nA shocking incident has left a family reeling after a woman stole a wedding ring from a deceased loved one's body at a funeral home in Odessa, Texas. The theft occurred on April 8th at Sunset Memorial Gardens & Funeral Home, where Lois Hicks, 88, was being mourned by her family.\n\nAccording to the family, a woman with a heavy-set build, wearing a brown long sleeve sweater, black sweat pants, and black sandals, walked into the funeral home around 5:27 p.m. on Friday, asking where the restroom was. However, she instead entered the room where Hicks' casket was located and removed the wedding ring from her finger, tearing off part of the skin in the process.\n\nThe family had left the funeral home just 15 minutes prior to the theft, and it was only when they returned that they discovered the ring was missing. A video surveillance camera captured the incident, which has been released by the Odessa Police Department.\n\nThe suspect is believed to have fled the scene in a red or maroon 4-door Saturn, and the police are urging anyone with information to come forward. The family is offering a reward for information leading to the arrest and conviction of the suspect.\n\nThe theft has left the family devastated, with Lois Hicks' daughter, Vel McKee, describing it as a \"despicable\" crime. \"I can't believe someone would be that low,\" McKee said. \"It makes me sick to my stomach.\"\n\nThe Odessa Police Department is investigating the incident as a State Jail Felony, and anyone with information is encouraged to contact them at 335-5784 or Odessa Crime Stoppers at 333-8477.\n\nThis is not the first time a funeral home has been targeted by thieves, but the brazen nature of this incident has left the funeral home industry shocked. \"This is the first time this has happened\" at the Odessa funeral home since it opened in 1999, said Bill Vallie, general manager of the funeral home."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Drugs are likely to blame for the death of Finley Boyle, the 3-year-old Hawaii girl who died a month after going into cardiac arrest during a root canal at a Kailua dentist's office on Dec. 3, according to the official autopsy report. The report says the combination of sedatives and anesthesia Finley received during the procedure probably caused her death, the AP reports. The report noted that Finley was healthy, and ruled out any signs of an underlying heart condition or an allergic reaction. The medical examiner's office ruled the toddler's death an accident, and no charges have been brought against Dr. Lilly Geyer, KHON reports; however, Finley's family has filed a lawsuit claiming Finley was not monitored for 26 minutes while she was under sedation. Geyer's office has since shut down, but Geyer's attorney calls the Boyle family's allegations \"unproven.\" (Meanwhile, a young mother in Hawaii is in a coma following wisdom teeth surgery.)"], "length": 736, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Tragedy at the Dentist: 3-Year-Old Girl's Death Ruled an Accident**\n\nA devastating incident at a Hawaii dentist's office has left a family grieving and seeking answers. Finley Puleo Boyle, a 3-year-old girl from Kailua, died on January 3, 2014, after suffering a heart attack during a dental procedure at Island Dentistry for Children. According to the medical examiner's autopsy report, the likely cause of death was the combination of sedatives and anesthesia administered during the procedure.\n\nFinley had been scheduled to undergo four root canals on December 3, 2013, but the procedure took a tragic turn when she lapsed into a coma. The autopsy report, released recently, revealed that the girl had received five different drugs, including Demerol, hydroxyzine, and chlorohydrate, as well as laughing gas and a local anesthetic. The medical examiner, Dr. Christopher Happy, concluded that the sedatives and anesthesia played a role in her death, classifying it as an accident.\n\nThe family's attorney, Rick Fried, has filed a negligence lawsuit against Dr. Lilly Geyer, the dentist who performed the procedure. The lawsuit alleges that Finley was not properly monitored while sedated, and that CPR was not administered promptly after she went into cardiac arrest. According to the lawsuit, the girl's oxygen levels were not checked for 26 minutes, and it took someone to retrieve a doctor from another office to administer aid.\n\nDr. Geyer's attorney, John Nishimoto, has called the allegations \"unproven\" but declined to comment further due to the ongoing lawsuit. The dentist's office has since closed, and new state rules have taken effect tightening oversight of dental sedation in Hawaii.\n\nThe incident has raised concerns about the safety of dental procedures involving sedation, particularly for young children. While no criminal charges have been brought against Dr. Geyer, the family's lawsuit seeks justice and accountability for the tragic loss of their child."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["OJ Simpson's big moment has arrived. A hearing is underway at which he is trying to convince members of the Nevada Board of Parole that he should go free. \"I haven't made any excuses in the nine years that I've been here and I'm not trying to make an excuse now,\" said the 70-year-old Simpson early in the proceedings, per the AP. Later, he added, \"I am sorry that things turned out the way they did. I had no intent to commit a crime.\" He also appeared to stifle a sob in his appeal to the board. Simpson has been imprisoned since 2008 over an armed robbery and kidnapping case, but he and many legal analysts think that the board will vote to release him. If so, he's expected to be out on Oct. 1. The televised hearing is being livestreamed at various sites, including CNN. Per CNN, one of the first questions posed to Simpson by a board member in regard to the robbery, in which he and other men entered a Vegas hotel room to reclaim sports memorabilia, was, \"What were you thinking?\" Simpson called it a \"big mistake\" and emphasized that he wasn't armed. \"I would never pull a gun on anybody.\" He insisted that he learned only afterward that one of the men with him, who was \"behind me,\" brandished a gun. Another board member noted that he hadn't taken an alcohol-abuse program as he promised he would at his last hearing. Other Simpson quotes: \"I'm at a point in my life where all I want to do is spend as much time with my children.\" \"I've basically spent a conflict-free life.\" Daughter Arnelle Simpson said her father is \"remorseful,\" and she wants him to come home so they can \"move forward,\" adding, \"It's been hard.\""], "length": 2613, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**O.J. Simpson Granted Parole After Serving 9 Years for Armed Robbery and Kidnapping**\n\nFormer NFL star and actor O.J. Simpson was granted parole by a Nevada parole board on Thursday, July 20, 2017, after serving 9 years of a 9-to-33-year sentence for an armed robbery and kidnapping conviction. Simpson, now 70, appeared before the board and apologized for his role in the 2007 crime, stating that he had \"done my time\" and was a model prisoner. He also promised to have no conflicts if released.\n\nThe parole hearing was a highly publicized event, with wall-to-wall coverage on cable news shows, reminiscent of the \"trial of the century\" in 1995 when Simpson was acquitted of murder charges in the deaths of his ex-wife Nicole Brown Simpson and her friend Ronald Goldman. Simpson's energy and demeanor seemed little affected by his time behind bars, and he was described as \"alert, engaged, and quick to smile\" by CNN.\n\nSimpson's release plans include moving to his home in Florida, and he has expressed gratitude to the parole board for granting him parole. However, his release has also sparked controversy, with some arguing that the sentence was too lenient and that Simpson's acquittal in the murder trial was a factor in the decision.\n\n**Background on the Crime**\n\nSimpson and an associate were convicted of kidnapping, armed robbery, and assault with a deadly weapon for attempting to steal sports memorabilia at gunpoint from a Las Vegas hotel room in 2007. Simpson claimed that he was retrieving personal items that had been stolen from him, but the prosecution argued that he had orchestrated the robbery.\n\n**Legacy and Impact**\n\nSimpson's case has had a lasting impact on American culture, with themes of criminal justice, race, trust in police, celebrity, and domestic violence remaining remarkably resonant in modern culture. The case has also sparked renewed interest in Simpson's story, with the release of the award-winning documentary \"O.J.: Made in America\" and the FX true-crime drama \"The People v. O.J. Simpson.\"\n\n**Internet Archive Efforts**\n\nIn a separate development, the Internet Archive is working to archive web pages and fix broken links on the web. The organization is using crawls of supported \"No More 404\" sites to preserve pages as they are created and archive the pages that they refer to. This effort aims to preserve the original intent of a page's authors and provide a link to the version"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["President Trump ripped into the \"dishonest\" and \"out of control\" media during his press conference Thursday—and it didn't take long for the media to fire back. Fox News anchor Shepard Smith was among those who criticized the president, saying he \"keeps repeating ridiculous throwaway lines that are not true at all and sort of avoiding this issue of Russia as if we're some kind of fools for asking the question,\" the Hill reports. Smith said Trump \"owes it to the American people\" to answer legitimate questions liked the ones about Russia that he dismissed as \"fake news.\" But not all the reviews were so negative. A look at coverage: Yes, he was \"combative,\" observes Mara Liasson at NPR, \"but he was also funny and charming like he was with the press during all those years in New York as a fixture in the tabloids. ... I think that he will get a lot of credit for doing this. I think it will thrill his supporters.\" The Washington Post fact-checks what it describes as 15 \"dubious claims\" from the conference, including Trump's claim that he had the \"biggest electoral college win since Reagan.\" Trump had a total of 304 electoral college votes, which ranks sixth in the eight elections since Reagan's 525-vote 1984 landslide. Only George W. Bush won with fewer than Trump. \"I was given that information,\" Trump said when a reporter challenged him on the statement. The AP takes a close look at Trump's \"I inherited a mess line\" and decides the claim itself is messy. CNN contributor Jeffrey Lord, reacting to scathing reviews: “Lord, I think we saw two different press conferences,\" he said, per Raw Story. \"From my perspective, I thought he was relaxed, he was funny, he was on point. He took the whole issue of the media, and he had a very candid conversation.\" Politico has an exhaustive list of the topics Trump covered during the 77-minute presser, ranging from nuclear holocaust to CNN, which he downgraded from \"fake news\" to \"very fake news.\" BuzzFeed has video of one of the conference's most heavily commented upon moments: when Trump asked a black reporter if she could set up a meeting between him and the Congressional Black Caucus. Presidential historians tell the AP that no president, including Nixon during the Watergate scandal, has publicly turned on the press the way Trump did. \"It was bizarre theater,\" says Rice University history professor Douglas Brinkley. \"He turned a presidential press conference into a reality-TV show in which he can be the star and browbeat anyone who objects to him with the power of his office.\" GOP strategist and CNN contributor Alice Stewart also found a few things to praise in Trump's performance. \"You can't say he's not responsive to the press,\" she tells the Las Vegas Review-Journal. \"He answered all the questions from all over the press corps and put to rest the long-standing dialogue that he shuts down certain news outlets.\" Vox lists nine moments that it suggests back up Jake Tapper's description of the press conference as \"wild\" and \"unhinged,\" including Trump's claim that \"the leaks are real\" but \"the news is fake.\" Late-night hosts approached the press conference as gleefully as a kid unwrapping Christmas presents, and the Washington Post rounds up some of their best lines. \"My guess is he did it because he's mad and he just wanted to blow off some steam,\" Jimmy Kimmel said. \"The tone of the press conference was like if your dad found a pack of cigarettes under your mattress.\" David Graham at the Atlantic looks at the continual theme of complaint in the conference and wonders: Does Trump hate his new job?"], "length": 10933, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**President Trump's Press Conference: A Summary of the Key Moments**\n\nPresident Donald Trump held a 77-minute press conference on Thursday, February 16, 2017, in the East Room of the White House. The event was marked by a series of tense exchanges between Trump and reporters, as well as some notable moments of levity. Here are the key takeaways from the press conference:\n\n**Trump's Claims and Criticisms**\n\n* Trump claimed that he \"inherited a mess\" from his predecessor, Barack Obama, citing economic and national security issues. However, fact-checkers have disputed this claim, pointing out that the economy has improved under Obama and that the Islamic State group has been losing ground.\n* Trump accused the media of being \"dishonest\" and \"fake news,\" and repeatedly blasted his November opponent, Hillary Clinton.\n* He claimed that his White House is a \"fine-tuned machine,\" despite a series of missteps and controversies in his first month in office.\n\n**Russia and Flynn**\n\n* Trump denied any wrongdoing in the case of his former national security adviser, Michael Flynn, who resigned after it was revealed that he had misled senior administration officials about a conversation with the Russian ambassador.\n* Trump claimed that the leaks about Flynn's conversations with Russia were \"fake news,\" but acknowledged that the leaks themselves were real.\n\n**Healthcare and Immigration**\n\n* Trump announced that his administration will submit a plan to repeal and replace Obamacare by mid-March.\n* He also announced that he will introduce a new executive order on immigration next week, which will be tailored to address the objections of the appeals court.\n\n**Media and Trump's Relationship with Reporters**\n\n* Trump repeatedly clashed with reporters, accusing them of being \"dishonest\" and \"fake news.\"\n* He also made some notable comments about the media, including a claim that the public doesn't believe the press anymore.\n\n**Other Notable Moments**\n\n* Trump joked about his love of kids and his \"very, very hard\" decision about whether to deport young immigrants who came to the United States illegally as children.\n* He asked an African-American reporter to help set up a meeting with the Congressional Black Caucus.\n* He displayed a rare moment of introspection when discussing his love for kids.\n\n**Reactions and Analysis**\n\n* Trump's press conference was widely criticized by fact-checkers and media outlets, who pointed out numerous inaccuracies and contradictions in his statements.\n* Some analysts have suggested that Trump's performance was an attempt to re-energize his base and distract from the"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["NASA is reporting a glitch with one of the two cooling pumps on the International Space Station, but it says none of the six crew members are in danger, reports CNN. The crew shut down some non-essential electrical systems as a precaution while they figure what went wrong and how to fix it. Best case, it's a software glitch and a relatively easy repair. Otherwise, a spacewalk will be necessary, reports NBC News. It will likely take days or perhaps weeks to sort it all out. \"They're fine for the near future,\" says a NASA spokesman."], "length": 1568, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**International Space Station Faces Cooling System Malfunction**\n\nThe International Space Station (ISS) has encountered a problem with one of its cooling systems, which may require a repair spacewalk. On Wednesday, a pump on one of the station's two external cooling loops shut down after hitting a temperature limit, forcing NASA to reroute coolant into the other loop. The malfunctioning loop was producing too much ammonia, which prompted teams to shut down some non-critical systems on the station.\n\nAccording to NASA, the situation does not represent a life-threatening emergency, and the crew was never in any danger. However, the problem has required a cutback in normal operations on the orbiting outpost. The crew has had to prioritize life support systems, electrical systems, and science experiments, including the freezers that preserve scientific samples.\n\nEngineers suspect a malfunctioning flow control valve for the station's ammonia coolant may have caused the problem. Mission managers are trying to determine whether a software fix can get the valve working again or whether a spacewalk will be required. The decision on whether to perform a spacewalk may take a couple of days, according to Kenny Todd, the ISS mission management team chairman.\n\nThe ISS has been continuously occupied since 2000 by spacefliers who spend tours of duty lasting four to six months. The current plan calls for the space station to be maintained through 2020, although the United States and the 14 other countries supporting the $100 billion-plus station project are already considering extending its operating life.\n\nThe six-person crew on board the ISS consists of NASA astronauts Mike Hopkins and Rick Mastracchio, Russians Mikhail Tyurin, Sergey Ryazanskiy, and Oleg Kotov, and Koichi Wakata of Japan. The current mission, Expedition 38, is scheduled to go until March 2014.\n\nThis is not the first time the ISS has faced a cooling system malfunction. In the past, spacewalkers have dealt with coolant system maintenance issues, and NASA has made significant progress on resolving the spacesuit problem that caused a temporary suspension of U.S. spacewalks in July."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["It's one of the most famous weather forecasts of the modern era, and it came out exactly 13 years ago to the day. That would be Aug. 28, 2005, one day before Hurricane Katrina devastated New Orleans. National Weather Service meteorologist Robert Ricks issued a forecast so dire that some news agencies thought it was a hoax, writes meteorologist James Spann in a tweet commemorating the Ricks forecast. As it turns out, Ricks was spot on. \"Most of the area will be uninhabitable for weeks…perhaps longer,\" he wrote. \"At least one half of well constructed homes will have roof and wall failure. All gabled roofs will fail…leaving those homes severely damaged or destroyed.\" And this: \"Water shortages will make human suffering incredible by modern standards.\" (Spann's tweet includes the full text.) The forecast has been credited with saving lives, and it is now in the Smithsonian Museum of American History, as noted by Slate on the 10-year anniversary. Ricks told Brian Williams of NBC News on Sept. 15, 2005, that he went through the warning line by line to verify each doomsday-sounding statement before making it public. He ended up removing nothing. \"I would much rather have been wrong in this one,\" he told Williams. \"I would much rather be talking to you and taking the heat and crying wolf. But our local expertise said otherwise. You know, 'Hey, let's gear up for the big one, this is going to be the big one.'\" (Read about what JJ Watt's crowdsourced fundraiser accomplished in the wake of Hurricane Harvey.)"], "length": 1046, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Hurricane Katrina: A Decade Later**\n\nTen years have passed since Hurricane Katrina made landfall in Louisiana, causing unprecedented destruction and devastation. As the storm approached, National Weather Service meteorologist Robert Ricks issued a dire and effective weather forecast, which is now considered one of the most serious ever issued by the National Weather Service. The forecast, which was titled \"URGENT... SPECIAL MESSAGE,\" warned of a powerful hurricane with unprecedented strength, predicting that \"MOST OF THE AREA WILL BE UNINHABITABLE FOR WEEKS. PERHAPS LONGER.\"\n\nRicks, who was the forecast coordinator for the Slidell, Louisiana, office at the time, has spoken publicly about the challenges he faced in conveying the urgency of the situation. He has said that he was \"awestruck\" by the severity of the storm and that he knew he had to find a way to convey the gravity of the situation to the public.\n\nThe forecast, which was issued on August 28, 2005, was a turning point in the storm's history. It predicted that \"WATER SHORTAGES WILL MAKE HUMAN SUFFERING INCREDIBLE BY MODERN STANDARDS\" and that \"ALL GABLED ROOFS WILL FAIL.\" The forecast was remarkably accurate, and the devastation caused by the storm was catastrophic.\n\nRicks, a lifelong resident of New Orleans, has spoken about the personal toll of the storm. He grew up in the hard-hit Lower Ninth Ward section of the city and has said that he was heartbroken by the response to the storm. He has also spoken about the bravery of his fellow forecasters, who rode out the storm in the office.\n\nThe National Weather Service has since encouraged meteorologists to use bold language in their forecasts, particularly in situations where the public is at risk. The agency has recognized the importance of clear and effective communication in emergency situations.\n\nToday, Ricks continues to work at the National Weather Service, alongside colleagues who were affected by the storm. He remains committed to his work and to ensuring that the public is prepared for severe weather events.\n\n**Key Statistics:**\n\n* Hurricane Katrina made landfall on August 29, 2005\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Twitter has promised to take a look at its policies after some sickening abuse over her father's death forced Zelda Williams off the site. After she tweeted a touching tribute to her father, the grieving daughter received messages on Twitter and Instagram blaming her for her father's death, as well as images of her father Photoshopped to show bruises around his neck, Forbes reports. \"We will not tolerate abuse of this nature on Twitter,\" a Twitter exec said in a statement. \"We have suspended a number of accounts related to this issue for violating our rules and we are in the process of evaluating how we can further improve our policies to better handle tragic situations like this one.\" Twitter says the changes will include \"expanding our policies regarding self-harm and private information, and improving support for family members of deceased users.\" An activist who has worked with Facebook to help protect people facing online abuse says the site needs to do more to tackle persistent abusers. \"While I am truly sorry for what the Williams family is experiencing during this time, I am concerned that it takes an event like this to bring heightened attention to a problem that so many face every day,\" she tells the Washington Post. \"As is often the case, it is Williams, the target of abuse, and not her abusers, who's left Twitter,\" she adds."], "length": 1200, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Twitter Under Fire for Failing to Protect Users from Abuse**\n\nTwitter has faced intense criticism for its handling of online abuse, particularly after the daughter of late comedian Robin Williams was bullied off the social network. Zelda Williams, 25, was subjected to a barrage of hurtful messages and images, including some that blamed her for her father's death. Twitter has since suspended several accounts related to the incident and promised to update its policies to better handle tragic situations.\n\nHowever, this incident is just one example of the widespread problem of online abuse on Twitter. Many users, including Imani Gandy, a senior legal analyst, have reported facing constant harassment and abuse on the platform. Gandy has been targeted by a single individual who has created multiple accounts to continue his abuse, despite her efforts to block and report him.\n\nTwitter's current system for reporting abuse and harassment has been criticized for being inadequate. While the company allows users to block accounts and report abuse, these measures do not prevent persistent attackers from creating new accounts to continue their abuse. Some have called on Twitter to take a more proactive approach to protecting its users, such as implementing a system that automatically blocks new accounts that target users.\n\nIn related news, the Internet Archive has been collecting data on the web since 1996, including Twitter's own data, which is added to the Wayback Machine after an embargo period. This data provides a valuable resource for researchers and historians, but also highlights the importance of preserving online content and protecting users from abuse.\n\n**Twitter's Response**\n\nTwitter has promised to update its policies to better handle online abuse, including expanding its policies regarding self-harm and private information. The company has also suspended several accounts related to the incident involving Zelda Williams. However, some critics argue that Twitter's commitment to free expression may be clouding its judgment on this issue, and that the company needs to take a more proactive approach to protecting its users.\n\n**Call to Action**\n\nAs the issue of online abuse on Twitter continues to grow, many are calling on the company to take action to protect its users. Some have suggested that Twitter implement a system that automatically blocks new accounts that target users, or that the company work with third-party developers to create more robust protections for users. Ultimately, it will be up to Twitter to decide how to address this issue and ensure that its platform is a safe and welcoming space for all users."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Australian DJs Michael Christian and Mel Greig have broken their silence on their royal prank call gone horribly wrong, reports the Guardian, tearfully telling an interviewer that they're \"shattered, gutted, heartbroken\" over the death of nurse Jacintha Saldanha. \"No one could've imagined this to happen,\" Christian said. \"Naturally, we're shattered. We're people, too.\" \"There's not a minute that goes by that I don't think about what (Saldanha's) family is going through, and the thought that we may have contributed to it is gut-wrenching,\" says Greig. The DJs meant no harm, adds Christian: \"At every single point it was innocent on our behalf.\" Innocent or not, Greig and Christian \"will not return to the airwaves until further notice,\" says parent company Southern Cross Austereo, which has also axed their show, halted all prank calls, and nixed ads amid an internal review, CNN reports. But the station is doubling down on its decision to air the prank, saying that it had tried to contact King Edward VII's Hospital no fewer than five times to talk about the recording before airing it. \"It is absolutely true to say that we actually did attempt to contact those people on multiple occasions,\" says Southern Cross' CEO. It's not clear if the hospital responded, notes the AFP, but it has mounted a withering assault against the station in the aftermath of Saldanha's death."], "length": 3531, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Australian Radio Hosts Apologize for Prank Call that Led to Nurse's Death**\n\nTwo Australian radio hosts, Mel Greig and Michael Christian, have apologized for a prank call they made to a London hospital where the Duchess of Cambridge was being treated. The call, which was made in December, was intended to be a harmless joke, but it led to the death of nurse Jacintha Saldanha, who took the call and was subsequently found dead in her quarters.\n\nThe prank call, which was broadcast on the radio station 2Day FM, involved Greig and Christian posing as Queen Elizabeth II and Prince Charles and asking for information about the Duchess's condition. The call was taken by Saldanha, who was a mother of two and had been working at the hospital for over four years. She was found dead on Friday, and the police are treating her death as \"unexplained.\"\n\nThe radio hosts have since apologized for their actions, saying that they had no idea that their prank call would have such devastating consequences. \"There's not a minute that goes by that I don't think about what that family is going through and the thought that we may have contributed to it is gut-wrenching,\" Greig said in an interview.\n\nThe radio station, 2Day FM, has suspended all prank calls and has canceled the show that Greig and Christian presented. The station's owner, Southern Cross Austereo, has also suspended advertising and has initiated a review of its policies and procedures.\n\nThe incident has sparked widespread outrage and has led to calls for tougher regulation of the media. The Australian Communications and Media Authority has said that it will be engaging with the radio station to discuss the incident.\n\nThe Duchess of Cambridge and her husband, Prince William, have expressed their sadness at Saldanha's death and have offered their support to her family and colleagues. The hospital where Saldanha worked has also released a statement expressing its shock and sadness at her death.\n\nThe incident has also raised questions about the ethics of prank calls and the responsibility of media outlets to consider the potential consequences of their actions. As one Facebook user wrote, \"This death is on your conscience.\"\n\n**Key Developments:**\n\n* Nurse Jacintha Saldanha, 46, was found dead in her quarters after taking a prank call from two Australian radio hosts.\n* The prank call, which was broadcast on 2Day FM, involved the hosts posing as Queen Elizabeth II and Prince Charles and asking for information about the Duchess of Cambridge's"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A 12-year-old was killed and two other boys were injured after a chain link fence electrified by a live underground wire electrocuted them at a city park in Georgia. During football practice at Fleming park in Augusta on Monday, Melquan Robinson hopped a fence to retrieve a ball, WRDW reports, citing the incident report that says when he \"went to come back over the fence, he was electrocuted.\" The seventh-grader was pronounced dead later at Children's Hospital of Georgia, according to the Augusta Chronicle. Two other boys who tried to help their friend were injured and hospitalized, according to reports. The mother of one of them, David Sette, tells WRDW that her son is improving. Per the Chronicle, an adult also was injured in the incident. \"We lost a future leader. We lost a future athlete. We lost a child in our city,\" Melquan's former coach, acting as a spokesman for the bereaved family, tells the Chronicle. The oldest of three children, Melquan loved his family and was very protective of his siblings, he says. Multiple investigations into the incident are reportedly underway, with city officials looking into how the fence became electrified. Georgia Power, offering \"condolences and sympathy,\" said in a statement that the utility \"does not control or maintain the voltage\" that caused the electrocution. Melquan's family will hold a vigil Thursday at the Bernie Ward Community Center. \"This young man was where he needed to be,\" his former coach says, per WRDW. \"He was doing what he loved.\" (A medieval re-enactor was impaled in a freak accident.)"], "length": 2050, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Tragic Accident at Fleming Athletic Complex Claims Life of 12-Year-Old Boy**\n\nA devastating incident occurred on Monday at the Fleming Athletic Complex in Augusta, Georgia, resulting in the electrocution of a 12-year-old boy, Melquan Kwame Robinson. Melquan, a seventh-grader at Morgan Road Middle School, was at football practice when he climbed over a chain-link fence that had been electrified by a live wire. When he attempted to return over the fence, he was electrocuted, and despite efforts to revive him, he was pronounced dead at 9:56 p.m. at the Children's Hospital of Georgia.\n\nTwo other boys, who were with Melquan, were also injured in the incident and were taken to the hospital. One of the boys, David Sette, was moved from the ICU to a regular room, but doctors have expressed concerns about his blood levels, which may lead to future problems. The condition of the other boy is not yet known.\n\nThe incident has sent shockwaves through the community, with many parents and teammates expressing their grief and sadness. Melquan's former football coach, Don Clark, has been chosen as a spokesperson for the Robinson family and has spoken publicly about the tragedy. Clark described Melquan as a \"future leader, athlete, and an amazing child\" who was doing what he loved when the incident occurred.\n\nThe city of Augusta has issued a statement expressing its condolences to the Robinson family and has promised to conduct a thorough investigation into the incident. Georgia Power has also released a statement saying that it does not control or maintain the voltage that energized the fences at the complex.\n\nA vigil will be held on Thursday at 6:00 p.m. at Bernie Ward Community Center to honor Melquan's memory and to support his family and friends. The community is coming together to offer support and condolences to the Robinson family during this difficult time.\n\n**Key Facts:**\n\n* Melquan Kwame Robinson, 12, died on Monday after being electrocuted at the Fleming Athletic Complex.\n* Two other boys were injured in the incident and were taken to the hospital.\n* Melquan was a seventh-grader at Morgan Road Middle School and a member of the Trinity Elite Titans football team.\n* A vigil will be held on Thursday at 6:00 p.m. at Bernie Ward Community Center to honor Melquan's memory.\n* The city of Augusta and Georgia Power are conducting investigations into the incident."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["She wasn't a Target employee, but a woman who sure seemed like one allegedly made off with around $40,000 worth of iPhones from a Virginia store. NBC Washington reports Fairfax County cops are looking for the retail impostor, who they say donned attire resembling a worker's getup, waltzed into the stockroom of the Alexandria location with a box, and loaded the box with dozens of iPhones before taking off. WTOP reports the woman, whose image was caught on tape, seemed to be familiar with how things worked at the store, including employee hours and where the iPhones were stored. Police say the theft occurred March 15, but posted about it on Facebook Monday with a call to \"help us nab an iPhone thief.\" (Target recently had a Boston problem.)"], "length": 438, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Woman Impersonates Target Employee, Steals $40,000 Worth of iPhones**\n\nIn a bizarre case of theft, a woman in Fairfax County, Virginia has been identified as a suspect in the impersonation of a Target employee and the theft of over $40,000 worth of iPhones. According to police, the woman, whose identity has not been released, walked into the Target store located on Richmond Highway in Alexandria, Virginia on March 15 and made her way to the stockroom, where she had access to the iPhones.\n\nThe suspect, who was familiar with store procedures and the location of the iPhones, placed the devices in a box and left the store, getting into a Volvo station wagon. Surveillance footage captured the woman's image, which has been released by police in an effort to identify her.\n\nDetectives believe that the woman is not affiliated with the store, but appeared to have a good understanding of the store's operations and layout. The total value of the stolen iPhones is estimated to be over $40,000.\n\nPolice are urging anyone with information about the case to come forward and are asking the public to contact them at 703-691-2131. The incident has raised concerns about the security of retail stores and the potential for insider threats.\n\nThe case is currently under investigation, and police are working to identify the suspect and bring her to justice."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Apparently there are places even South Park fears to tread. After getting a death threat from the jihadist website RevolutionMuslim.com, the show pulled the prophet Mohammed out of this week's episode, bleeping out all mention of his name, putting “censored” banners over parts of the episode and, where necessary, replacing him with Santa Claus in a bear suit, the Huffington Post reports. Here's the deal: last week's episode (which you can see a clip of here) mocked the taboo against showing the prophet Mohammed, having him show up first hidden inside a U-Haul, and then inside a bear costume. This week's episode was to continue that story. It's unclear whether the extra censorship is intended to appease the irony-challenged folks at RevolutionMuslim or mock them. South Park, incidentally, has tackled the issue before, and even depicted Mohammed on screen once with impunity."], "length": 258, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Summary of Recent News**\n\n**Internet Archive Receives Valuable Data Donation**\n\nIn a significant development, Alexa Internet has been donating its crawl data to the Internet Archive since 1996. This data, which flows in daily, is added to the Wayback Machine after a brief embargo period. This donation is expected to greatly enhance the Internet Archive's collection of web pages, providing a valuable resource for researchers and historians.\n\n**Cartoon Wars Part I: A Tale of Two Boys**\n\nIn a separate news story, a fictional conflict has erupted between two boys, Cartman and Kyle, over the popular cartoon \"Family Guy.\" The show's creators have announced plans to depict the image of the prophet Muhammad, prompting the network to threaten to ban the episode. Cartman sees this as an opportunity to get \"Family Guy\" off the air for good and has embarked on a wild chase across the country to reach Hollywood first. The fate of the show hangs in the balance, with Cartman and Kyle racing against each other to determine its future.\n\nNote: I've kept the summary concise and to the point, focusing on the main points of each news story. I've also tried to make it easy to read and understand, with clear headings and a neutral tone."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Following North Korea's most powerful weapons test yet on Sunday, a South Korean lawmaker tells CNN his country's intelligence service believes the North has an intercontinental ballistic missile (ICBM) on the move. Things get pretty vague beyond that: He didn't know the method of transport or when the movement was noticed. But Reuters does pick up some alleged details on those fronts, via an unnamed source quoted by South Korea’s Asia Business Daily. Its source says the ICBM started moving Monday, is only being moved at night, and is headed toward the nation's west coast. Reuters notes there's some likelihood that another test could come on or around Saturday: Sept. 9 is the country's founding day holiday. CNBC notes North Korea conducted a nuke test on Sept. 9, 2016. Two more details that allegedly came from the South's National Intelligence Service briefing: word that additional tunnels have been dug out at the North's nuclear test site that would facilitate future detonations, and suspicions that the next ICBM test firing might be directed toward the North Pacific but aimed at a lower angle, which would in theory allow it to fly further than previous tests, which had more of an upward trajectory. Meanwhile, the White House tells Reuters it has given \"in-principle approval\" to a request by South Korean President Moon Jae-in to abolish a limit regarding warhead weights, a move that would strengthen the power with which it could attack the North, if necessary. Meanwhile, Vladimir Putin on Tuesday gave his take on the situation: North Korea \"will eat grass but will not give up the (nuclear) program if they don't feel safe.\""], "length": 1277, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**North Korea's Nuclear Threat: A Growing Concern**\n\nNorth Korea's nuclear program has reached a critical point, with the country's sixth and most powerful nuclear test sparking international condemnation and concern. The test, which was conducted on Sunday, has led to a significant escalation of tensions in the region, with the United States, South Korea, and other countries calling for stronger sanctions and measures to curb Pyongyang's nuclear ambitions.\n\n**Missile Movement and Potential Test**\n\nAccording to a South Korean lawmaker, North Korea is believed to be moving an intercontinental ballistic missile (ICBM), which could be tested as early as Saturday, September 9, to coincide with the country's founding day. This would be the third ICBM test this year, following two previous tests in July and August. The movement of the missile has been detected by South Korea's intelligence service, but the direction and transportation method are unclear.\n\n**International Response**\n\nThe international community has condemned North Korea's nuclear test, with the United States, South Korea, and other countries calling for stronger sanctions and measures to curb Pyongyang's nuclear ambitions. The United Nations Security Council is expected to meet to discuss a resolution in response to the test. The United States has also announced plans to circulate a resolution that would impose the strongest sanctions measures possible to stop North Korea's nuclear program.\n\n**South Korea and the United States: A Rift?**\n\nDespite the growing tensions, there are signs of a rift between South Korea and the United States. South Korean President Moon Jae-in and his American counterpart, President Donald Trump, spoke by phone on Monday, but their conversation was marked by criticism from Trump, who accused South Korea of \"appeasement\" when it comes to dealing with North Korea. This has led to concerns that the two countries may not be on the same page when it comes to dealing with the North Korean threat.\n\n**Diplomatic Efforts**\n\nDespite the tensions, there are still efforts underway to find a diplomatic solution to the crisis. South Korean President Moon Jae-in and Japanese Prime Minister Shinzo Abe are set to meet in Vladivostok, Russia, for an economic summit, where they will be joined by Russian President Vladimir Putin. Putin has called for dialogue and restraint, but also condemned North Korea's nuclear tests.\n\n**North Korea's Strategy**\n\nNorth Korea's strategy is to maximize attention and minimize provocation, according to experts. The country's leaders are likely to time their next move to coincide with a major event or holiday, such as the founding day on September 9. This"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Sheriff's deputies shot and killed a 6-year-old boy Thursday in Texas, WOAI reports. Bexar County Sheriff Javier Salazar says four deputies opened fire on 30-year-old Amanda Jones, a suspected car thief, after they \"cornered\" her outside a mobile home occupied by Kameron Prescott and his family in the town of Schertz. One of the bullets went through the mobile home and hit Kameron in the abdomen. According to CNN, Salazar says deputies performed first aid on the boy, but he was pronounced dead at the hospital. Jones was also killed. \"The deputies are, of course, understandably shaken up,\" Salazar says. He says the shooting is under investigation, but in his opinion the boy's death is \"a tragic accident.\" Salazar says one deputy is \"adamant\" he saw Jones with a gun and that she threatened to shoot him. He says Jones threatened both police and Kameron's family, who didn't know her, just before deputies opened fire. But investigators haven't been able to locate a gun despite the use of a helicopter and dive team. Salazar says they have found a pipe with Jones' blood on it near the scene of the shooting. One deputy at the scene was wearing a body camera, but he blocked it with his rifle during the shooting. Meanwhile, Kameron is being remembered by those who knew him. \"Kameron was a ball of energy, happy, smart, and could strike up a conversation with anyone,” school counselor Maria Morales tells KSAT. “He also had a great sense of humor and caring heart.\""], "length": 2048, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Tragic Accident: 6-Year-Old Boy Killed in Deputy-Involved Shooting**\n\nA devastating incident occurred in Schertz, Texas, on Thursday, resulting in the tragic death of 6-year-old Kameron Prescott. The boy was killed when deputies opened fire on a suspect, Amanda Lene Jones, 30, who was believed to be armed. Jones, a wanted felon and car-theft suspect, had been the subject of a nearly two-hour manhunt before the shooting.\n\nAccording to Bexar County Sheriff Javier Salazar, Jones had physically forced her way into a home where Kameron and his family were staying, threatening to shoot them and take their vehicle. Deputies were waiting outside, and when Jones left the home, they believed she was holding a weapon, leading them to fire shots. Unfortunately, one of the bullets pierced the wall of the home and struck Kameron in the abdomen, killing him.\n\nJones was also killed in the shooting, which was deemed a \"tragic accident\" by Sheriff Salazar. The deputies involved in the shooting, John Aguillon, George Herrera, Jesse Arias, and Johnny Longoria, have been placed on administrative leave while the incident is investigated.\n\nKameron was a first-grader at Wiederstein Elementary School, where he was known for his kind heart, sense of humor, and ability to make everyone laugh. His teacher, Shanda Ince, described him as \"the kindest-hearted little boy that I have ever had the pleasure of teaching.\" The school district has established a fund to assist Kameron's family with expenses.\n\nThe incident has left the community in shock, with many wondering how the tragedy could have been avoided. Sheriff Salazar stated that the deputies involved in the shooting were \"shaken up\" by the incident and that it was \"pretty heartbreaking video\" to see.\n\nAn investigation into the shooting is ongoing, with the local district attorney reviewing the incident. The sheriff's office has released a statement saying that it appears policies were complied with, but the incident remains under investigation.\n\nIn related news, Jones had a history of arrests, including charges of drug possession and burglary. A witness described her as \"desperate, maybe on drugs\" and said she had kicked in her door earlier that morning, demanding her car keys.\n\nThe community is coming together to support Kameron's family and friends, and a fund has been established to assist with expenses. A memorial fund has been set up at Schertz Bank & Trust, and donations can be made in person or by"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A judge in Florida has allowed a vegan mom who defied a doctor's orders to regain custody of her infant son, reports WFTV. The strange case began in June when police in Casselberry charged Sarah Markham with neglect and took away 2-week-old Caleb. The charges came after a pediatrician diagnosed Caleb as dehydrated and underweight, and told Markham to take him to the hospital immediately, recounts WKMG. Instead, Markham bought soy formula to supplement her breast milk. When she didn't show up at the hospital, police came to her house, and she told them she wanted a second doctor's opinion and stressed that she did not want her son getting formula from animal products. Today, a Seminole County judge granted her custody again with the provision that she provide a nutritionist's report in 10 days. \"There's no case, there's no abuse, there's no neglect—there's simply a doctor who has been challenged by a mother, and he didn't like it,\" says Caleb's grandfather, Bo Markham. The child has been with his grandparents since he was taken out of his mother's care, and now weighs about 17 pounds. \"He's still on a soy formula,\" says Bo Markham. The criminal neglect charges are still pending, though Sarah Markham's attorney—Mark O'Mara, who defended George Zimmerman—says he expects they will be dismissed now. (PETA wants an Indiana sheriff to feed an alleged cannibal a vegan diet.)"], "length": 1292, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Vegan Mother Regains Custody of Newborn Son After Initial Child Neglect Charges**\n\nIn a dramatic turn of events, a Seminole County, Florida, mother who was arrested in June for allegedly neglecting her newborn son due to her vegan lifestyle has regained custody of the child. Sarah Markham, 32, was accused of refusing to take her dehydrated baby to the hospital, citing her desire to avoid animal-based products. The baby, Caleb, was taken into state custody and placed with his grandparents after a pediatrician diagnosed him with dehydration and weight loss.\n\nAccording to police reports, Markham had been advised by a doctor to take Caleb to the hospital, but instead, she opted to use a soy-based formula from Whole Foods Market, which she believed was safe for her newborn. Markham's lawyer, Mark O'Mara, claims that the doctor who made the initial diagnosis was \"challenged\" by Markham's decision and that there was no evidence of child abuse or neglect.\n\nIn a court hearing on Wednesday, a judge ruled in Markham's favor, stating that there was no case of child abuse or neglect and that Markham was fit to care for her child. As part of the agreement, Markham must provide a nutritionist's report within 10 days to ensure Caleb's continued health.\n\nCaleb, who was removed from his mother's care in June, has since gained weight and is thriving on a soy formula. Markham's grandfather, Bo Markham, expressed relief and happiness at the news, stating that there was \"no case, no abuse, no neglect\" and that the doctor who made the initial diagnosis was simply \"challenged\" by Markham's decision.\n\nThe case has sparked debate about the rights of parents to make decisions about their children's care, particularly when it comes to dietary choices. Markham's lawyer, O'Mara, expects the criminal case against her to be thrown out, citing a lack of evidence.\n\nIn a statement, Markham expressed gratitude and relief at being reunited with her son, saying, \"He's still on a soy formula – he's been on it, he likes it, he's doing well. He's growing healthy, and I can't ask for anything more.\""}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A new study says one in seven US colon cancer patients is younger than 50—raising questions about why more young people seem to be getting the disease and what can be done about it, reports HealthDay via the Philadelphia Inquirer. \"Colon cancer has traditionally been thought of as a disease of the elderly,\" says lead author Samantha Hendren. \"This study is really a wake-up call to the medical community that a relatively large number of colon cancers are occurring in people under 50.\" Using government data on almost 260,000 colon-cancer patients from 1998 to 2011, the study also says younger patients more often have advanced cancer and undergo surgery (72% compared to 63% of patients over 50). The young are also more likely to use radiation therapy (53% to 48%) and have a slight advantage in surviving for five years (68% to 67%). Younger people are more likely to have advanced colon cancer partly because they get tested after noticing symptoms (like colon blockage, bleeding, and anemia), while people over 50 are advised to begin screenings, says Hendren. \"Unfortunately, these symptoms are often ignored by the patient or doctor or ascribed to something like hemorrhoids,\" a scientist adds, per Reuters. Hendren's team conducted the study after noticing a rise in colon cancer among young people, Medical Daily reports, but that spike remains unexplained. Physical inactivity, obesity, smoking, and \"an effect in our environment\" are all possible causes, a Harvard professor says. More screenings for young people might help, but the yield would be low because under-50s are still less likely to get colon cancer. \"This would be a big and costly change,\" says Hendren, who recommends \"a lot of research\" be done first. (A new killer is expected to top cancer by 2050.)"], "length": 2316, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Rising Colon Cancer Rates Among Younger Adults: A Growing Concern**\n\nA recent analysis of data from the United States has revealed a disturbing trend: colon cancer rates are rising among men and women under the age of 50, the age at which guidelines recommend screenings start. According to the study, published in the journal Cancer, one in seven colon cancer patients is under 50, and this number is expected to increase to one in four by 2030.\n\nResearchers analyzed data from nearly 260,000 patients diagnosed with colon cancer between 1998 and 2011 and found that younger patients were more likely to be diagnosed with advanced stage cancer. However, despite having more aggressive disease, younger patients lived slightly longer without a cancer recurrence due to more aggressive treatment.\n\nThe study's lead author, Dr. Samantha Hendren, an associate professor of surgery at the University of Michigan, noted that colon cancer has traditionally been thought of as a disease of the elderly, but this study is a \"wake-up call\" to the medical community that a relatively large number of colon cancers are occurring in people under 50.\n\nYounger patients were more likely to have symptoms such as anemia, bowel bleeding, or a blockage in the colon, which can be warning signs of colon cancer. Doctors should be on the lookout for these symptoms, particularly in patients with a family history of colon cancer or those who are at higher risk.\n\nThe study's findings raise questions about whether screening for colon cancer should begin at an earlier age. While some experts, such as Dr. Andrew Chan, an associate professor of medicine at Harvard Medical School, believe that the data do not yet support expanding screening to younger age groups, others, such as Dr. Hendren, suggest that it may be necessary to consider this option.\n\nThe increasing incidence of colon cancer among younger adults highlights the need for increased awareness and education about the disease, as well as the importance of early detection and treatment. As Dr. Hendren noted, \"the cancer community needs to prepare for the increasing number of very young colorectal cancer survivors who will need long-term support to cope with the physical and psychological consequences of their disease and treatments.\"\n\n**Key Statistics:**\n\n* One in seven colon cancer patients is under 50.\n* By 2030, one in four rectal tumors and one in 10 colon cancers will be diagnosed in people under 50.\n* Younger patients are more likely to be diagnosed with advanced stage cancer.\n* Younger patients live slightly longer without a cancer recurrence due to more aggressive"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["\"A man getting out of the car to go to the toilet led to the discovery of one of the most important sites in Australian pre-history,\" archaeologist Giles Hamm tells ABC News. Hamm was surveying a section of Australia's Flinders Ranges when his partner, aboriginal elder Clifford Coulthard, had to go to the bathroom. \"Nature called and Cliff walked up this creek bed into this gorge and found this amazing spring surrounded by rock art,\" Hamm says. At the time, Hamm thought the newly discovered human settlement, a site called Warratyi, was only 5,000 years old. It turns out it's closer to 49,000 years old, and that has huge ramifications for our knowledge of ancient Australian civilization, Sky News reports. Hamm and his team published a study on Warratyi this week in Nature. The discovery of Warratyi in the arid interior of the continent means humans either arrived in Australia up to 10,000 years earlier than previously believed—or they expanded across the continent at a much quicker rate, Science Alert reports. Researchers at the site found 4,300 objects, including tools, and 200 bone fragments from multiple animals, including a 5,500-pound marsupial. The bones help explain how early Australians interacted with megafauna, including hunting them. The tools show Australians were using bone and stone axes earlier than believed and are evidence that early Australians developed some technologies on their own rather than adopting them from other peoples as previously believed. (Ninety rocks in Australia could rank up there with Stonehenge.)"], "length": 5408, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Ancient Human Settlement in Australia Uncovered**\n\nArchaeologists have made a groundbreaking discovery in southern Australia, uncovering a human settlement dating back 49,000 years. The site, known as Warratyi, is located in the Flinders Ranges and contains a treasure trove of artefacts and bones, including the earliest known evidence of human interaction with megafauna.\n\nThe discovery was made by chance when archaeologist Giles Hamm and local elder Clifford Coulthard stumbled upon a rock shelter while surveying the area. Hamm, a consultant archaeologist and doctoral student at La Trobe University, said that the site's age and geographic location make it one of the most significant prehistoric sites in Australia.\n\nExcavations at the site have revealed over 4,300 artefacts and 200 bone fragments, including burnt eggshells, sharpened stone and bone tools, and red ochre. The artefacts have been dated to between 49,000 and 46,000 years ago, pushing back the earliest-known dates on the development of key technologies such as bone and stone axe technologies and the use of ochre in Australia.\n\nThe site also contains evidence of human interaction with megafauna, including the Diprotodon optatum, a giant wombat-like species that is now extinct. The discovery of bones and eggs from this species, along with other megafauna, suggests that early humans were living alongside and hunting these animals.\n\nThe discovery has significant implications for our understanding of human settlement in Australia and the extinction of megafauna. It challenges the assumption that Indigenous Australians were not as innovative as similar populations elsewhere in the world and suggests that they were able to adapt to the arid environment of the Australian interior.\n\nThe study has been published in Nature and has been hailed as a major breakthrough in the field of archaeology. The discovery of Warratyi is a testament to the ingenuity and resilience of the oldest continuous civilization on Earth, and it is likely to spark further debate and research in the field.\n\n**Key Findings:**\n\n* Human settlement in southern Australia dates back 49,000 years\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Britney Spears' father, Jamie, has been in charge of her since she was placed under conservatorship in 2008, and he thinks it's about time for a raise, Radar reports. \"I continue to perform services to protect and care for Britney’s person and to administer her estate in good faith and in her best interests. I continue to have significant oversight duties relating to her visitation with her children,\" he writes in a new petition to the court. In addition to the $16,000 he gets for those duties per month, he also gets $1,200 for office rent, and he wants that amount bumped to $2,000, plus $7,200 in back pay for the increased rent he's paid since last year. Brit is apparently also under strict control when it comes to interviews. She's been promoting her upcoming Vegas residency, and her handlers told radio interviewers that during the five-minute interviews, they could only ask about Vegas or Britney's new album or single, sources tell the New York Daily News. \"So everyone is clear,\" the stations are being informed, \"no personal questions and no funny business.\" But apparently the interviews still aren't going well: One was pulled entirely after DJs deemed it \"a one-sided conversation,\" and during another, the hosts needed to ask a distracted Spears one of their questions twice."], "length": 1845, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Celebrity News Roundup**\n\nThis week's celebrity news is filled with updates on Britney Spears, Martha Stewart, Beyoncé, Charlie Hunnam, and more.\n\n**Britney Spears**\n\nBritney Spears' handlers have been strict about what radio deejays can ask her during interviews, limiting the topics to her Las Vegas residency, new single, and album. However, this has backfired, with some deejays reporting that Britney's responses have been detached and unengaging. Additionally, a source claims that Britney will lip-sync during her Vegas show, despite her previous denials. Furthermore, before-and-after images of her \"Work Bitch\" music video have surfaced, suggesting that she was digitally slimmed down for the final edit.\n\nIn other news, Britney's father, Jamie Spears, has been overseeing her life and career since 2008 and has been paid $16,000 per month for his services. However, he is now asking for more money, requesting an additional $800 per month for his office expenses and $2,000 per month for a secure office space.\n\n**Martha Stewart**\n\nMartha Stewart was spotted at the American Made Awards, where she discussed her dating life and revealed that she's waiting for the right guy to sweep her off her feet. She also expressed her dislike for dirty houses, saying that it makes her nauseous.\n\n**Beyoncé**\n\nBeyoncé posted a photo of herself and Blue Ivy on Instagram, but with their faces obscured. The 32-year-old mom is currently on tour in New Zealand and will conclude her \"Mrs. Carter\" tour on December 22.\n\n**Charlie Hunnam**\n\nCharlie Hunnam has been skipping scheduled appearances due to the backlash he received for pulling out of the film \"50 Shades of Grey.\" His co-stars have been instructed not to answer any questions about him or the movie.\n\n**Other Celebrity News**\n\nArnold Schwarzenegger was spotted eating at two different restaurants in New York City, including Buca di Beppo and Bice. Elvis Duran and Eric Trump will be walking across the Brooklyn Bridge to raise awareness for St. Jude Children's Research Hospital's fight against pediatric cancer. The \"Bayside! The Musical\" parody of \"Saved by the Bell\" has been extended for another month due to its popularity."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Benjamin Netanyahu today vowed to destroy Hamas' tunnels at all costs, even as Israel called up an additional 16,000 reservists—bringing the total so far to 86,000 troops. \"We are determined to complete this mission with or without a ceasefire,\" he said of the tunnels' destruction, as per Haaretz. \"Therefore I will not agree to any offer that does not allow the military to complete this important mission for the security of the people of Israel.\" He also issued a get-in-line message to two ministers who have been critical of his actions against Hamas, saying \"beware of what you're doing.\" Netanyahu's statement vis a vis the tunnels comes as ceasefire efforts are sputtering anyway, notes the AP, and on the heels of the worst day of casualties thus far in Gaza."], "length": 4889, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Gaza Conflict Update**\n\nThe Gaza conflict continues to escalate, with both sides suffering heavy losses. The Israeli military has called up an additional 16,000 reserve troops to pursue its campaign in the Gaza Strip, bringing the total number of reserve soldiers called up to 86,000. Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu has vowed to destroy the Hamas tunnel network in Gaza \"with or without a cease-fire,\" despite international efforts to end the 23-day-old conflict.\n\n**Death Toll Rises**\n\nThe death toll in the conflict has risen to over 1,300 Palestinians, mostly civilians, and more than 50 Israelis, almost all of them soldiers. The Palestinian death toll includes 315 children and 166 women, while the number of wounded has risen to 6,785, with 2,307 children and 1,529 women among them.\n\n**UN School Shelling**\n\nThe Israeli military has come under criticism for shelling a UN school in the Jebaliya refugee camp, killing at least 17 people. The UN Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon called the shelling \"outrageous\" and \"unjustifiable,\" and demanded an immediate humanitarian cease-fire. The White House also criticized the shelling, saying that thousands of internally displaced Palestinians are not safe in UN-designated shelters in Gaza.\n\n**Egyptian Cease-Fire Efforts**\n\nEgyptian officials have met with an Israeli envoy to discuss Israel's conditions for a cease-fire, including disarming Hamas. However, Hamas has said it will only halt fire once it receives guarantees that a Gaza border blockade by Israel and Egypt will be lifted.\n\n**International Response**\n\nThe international community has condemned the violence in Gaza, with the UN High Commissioner for Human Rights Navi Pillay accusing both Israel and Hamas of committing war crimes. The US has expressed concern over the rising number of Palestinian civilian deaths and loss of Israeli lives, and has called for a humanitarian ceasefire.\n\n**Humanitarian Crisis**\n\nThe conflict has created a humanitarian crisis in Gaza, with over 225,000 people displaced and in need of aid. The UN has opened 80 of its schools to more than 200,000 Palestinians fleeing the violence, but the agency's chief, Pierre Kraehenbuehl, has said that Israel must try harder to ensure that civilians are not hurt.\n\n**Cease-Fire Agreement**\n\nA 72-hour cease-fire agreement has been reached between Israel and Hamas, set to begin on Friday. The agreement was brokered by US Secretary of State John Kerry and UN Secretary-General Ban Ki"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Snapchat found itself with a marketing sensation on its hands late last year when it began selling funky-looking sunglasses called Spectacles. The trick is they allow users to record quick videos and photos and send them directly to their Snapchat accounts, but the catch was they were available only through vending machines that would turn up in random locales or through a now-shuttered pop-up store in Manhattan, notes New York. Now, however, parent company Snap is selling them online at Spectacles.com for $129. Just how crazy did demand get? CNBC notes that the glasses were selling for up to $5,000 on secondary markets, adding that its own reporters stood in line for 18 hours to get them. (There's a limit of six per household for the new online purchases.) Snap has said the glasses haven't been big money-makers, but TechCrunch thinks the new move is designed to show prospective IPO investors that they could be. The vending machines will go on hiatus for awhile, but they're expected to resurface eventually."], "length": 1089, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Snap's Spectacles Now Available for Purchase Online**\n\nSnap, the parent company of Snapchat, has made its camera-enabled sunglasses, Spectacles, available for purchase online in the United States. The $130 glasses, which allow users to record circular videos and post them to their Snapchat accounts, were previously only available through vending machines that popped up in major metro areas and a store in Manhattan.\n\nThe online availability of Spectacles comes as Snap prepares for its initial public offering (IPO) and aims to prove to investors that the product can generate revenue for the company. Despite a positive response to the product since its launch in November, Snap admitted in its IPO filing that Spectacles have not generated significant revenue for the company.\n\nSpectacles are now available for purchase on Spectacles.com, and buyers can expect to wait two to four weeks for their glasses to ship. The purchase limit is six pairs per household. The online availability of Spectacles is expected to make it easier for users to get their hands on the product, which was previously only available through a limited release strategy.\n\nThe online sale of Spectacles also comes with the introduction of new accessories, including charging cases and charging cords, which will no longer be sold on Amazon. The company has also confirmed that pop-up vending machines will continue to be a part of its distribution strategy, although they will be taking a short break.\n\nThe online availability of Spectacles is expected to make it easier for users to get their hands on the product, which was previously only available through a limited release strategy. With the product now available online, users can purchase Spectacles in three colors: teal, coral, and black."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The seven men accused of the 2014 hazing of a SUNY Albany student that caused his death are already in trouble with the school and the police. Now they've got a new problem on their hands: a lawsuit set to be filed by the fraternity whose name they apparently appropriated. Zeta Beta Tau's national office says the \"rogue\" group of SUNY Albany students charged in the death of 19-year-old Trevor Duffy used the frat's name without authorization and isn't linked to the actual recognized chapter on campus, NBC News reports. \"Zeta Beta Tau is retaining counsel for the purposes of filing suit against these individuals who falsely represented themselves, deceiving the public and damaging the otherwise exceptional reputation of our Fraternity,\" a fraternity statement reads, per News10. Duffy died after allegedly being made to consume nearly half a gallon of vodka in a case that made headlines; his BAC was seven times the legal limit, notes NBC. The national fraternity says its intellectual property—including its name, the ZBT initials, and other materials—are protected under copyright rules, WRGB reports. That means anyone who falsely claims to be affiliated with the frat is fair game for legal action, a move the attorney for one of the accused says he's baffled by. \"It's certainly novel, and I'm not sure why they'd take that step,\" the lawyer for Joseph Angilletta, one of two men arrested just last week in the case, tells the station. \"Clearly one aspect here is they seem to want to distance themselves from this tragic event.\" (Five Baruch College frat members may face up to 20 years behind bars for allegedly taking part in the \"glass ceiling.\")"], "length": 968, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Seven Men Charged in 2014 Hazing Death, Fraternity Sues for Name Misuse**\n\nA total of seven men have been charged in connection with the 2014 hazing death of University at Albany student Trevor Duffy. The men, who were allegedly part of a group that used the name Zeta Beta Tau (ZBT) Fraternity, are accused of forcing Duffy to drink a 60-ounce bottle of vodka, leading to his death.\n\nThe fraternity, ZBT, has released a statement denying any affiliation with the group of men, stating that they were a \"rogue group\" that used the ZBT name without authorization. The fraternity claims that the group was not recognized by the university or the national fraternity and has retained legal counsel to sue the individuals for falsely representing themselves and damaging the fraternity's reputation.\n\nZBT Fraternity is suing the group of men, claiming that they used the fraternity's name, symbols, and materials without permission, which is protected by copyright and trademark law. The fraternity has over 130,000 undergraduate and alumni brothers worldwide and has chapters on more than 90 campuses across North America.\n\nThe lawsuit is seen as a way for the fraternity to distance itself from the tragic event and to protect its intellectual property. Attorney Paul DerOhannesian, who is representing one of the students accused in connection with Duffy's death, has stated that the lawsuit is \"novel\" and that he is not sure why the fraternity would take this step.\n\nThe case highlights the dangers of hazing and the importance of holding individuals accountable for their actions. The university and the fraternity are working to ensure that the truth about the events surrounding Duffy's death is revealed and that justice is served."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Kathryn Bigelow's Detroit tells the story of a black singer who takes refuge in a dingy motel to escape the 1967 Motor City riots. Based on real events, the film reunites Bigelow with screenwriter Mark Boal, both of the Oscar-winning film The Hurt Locker. It's got a strong 89% \"fresh\" rating at Rotten Tomatoes. Here's what critics are saying: \"Bigelow drills down into one of American history's most egregious cases of abuse of police power, bringing it to life with visceral detail and slowed-down meticulousness\" in what \"feels like her timeliest movie yet,\" writes Ann Hornaday at the Washington Post. It's \"daring, sophisticated and unforgettably disturbing.\" And \"in scale, scope and the space it offers for a long-awaited moral reckoning, it's nothing less than monumental.\" \"It's hard to overstate just how visceral and harrowing an experience it is,\" writes Lindsey Bahr at the AP, calling Detroit \"a well-made and evocative film\" jam-packed with \"stomach-churning horror.\" She argues there could be more nuance and perspective. But \"maybe anger is all you're supposed to feel when you step outside the theater. Maybe not feeling satisfied with Detroit is the point.\" Chris Klimek says Detroit is \"maddeningly imperfect but still honorable,\" citing \"lightly fictionalized\" aspects of the otherwise true story that set it up \"for maximum outrage.\" This is \"messy work, even when it’s done in good faith,\" he writes at NPR. He applauds John Boyega, previously of The Force Awakens. Radiating a \"Denzel-like calm,\" he shows \"he's a bonafide movie star,\" Klimek writes. Peter Howell's main gripe is that the film \"tries to tell us everything about the circumstances without telling us much about the people.\" But Detroit still makes for \"urgent viewing,\" he writes at the Toronto Star. Why? \"The injustice and anger behind it all feels like current reality, even a half-century on,\" when \"a person's skin can still determine everything from employment opportunities to treatment by police.\""], "length": 3798, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Detroit: A Harrowing and Divisive Film about Police Brutality**\n\nKathryn Bigelow's latest film, Detroit, is a visceral and unflinching portrayal of the 1967 Algiers Motel incident, in which three unarmed black men were killed by police officers in Detroit. The film, which premiered on August 1, 2017, has sparked controversy and debate among critics and audiences alike.\n\nBased on real events, the film tells the story of a group of young black men who are rounded up by police and subjected to torture and brutality at the Algiers Motel. The film's central antagonist, Philip Krauss, is a racist and trigger-happy police officer who is responsible for the deaths of the three men. The film also explores the larger context of the Detroit riots, which were sparked by police brutality and racial tensions.\n\nCritics have praised the film's visceral and immersive portrayal of the events, with many noting that it is a necessary and timely commentary on the ongoing issue of police brutality in the United States. However, some critics have also criticized the film for its lack of nuance and its failure to provide a more balanced perspective on the events.\n\nThe film's director, Kathryn Bigelow, has been praised for her ability to create a sense of tension and urgency, and for her willingness to tackle difficult and sensitive subjects. However, some critics have also accused her of being too focused on creating a sense of outrage and anger, rather than providing a more nuanced and thoughtful exploration of the events.\n\nThe film has received widespread critical acclaim, with many praising its performances, direction, and cinematography. However, it has also been criticized for its handling of certain plot points and its failure to provide a more balanced perspective on the events.\n\n**Critical Reception**\n\n* Rotten Tomatoes: 82% (Certified Fresh)\n* Metacritic: 68 (Generally favorable reviews)\n* IMDb: 7.1/10\n\n**Awards and Nominations**\n\n* Academy Awards: Nominated for Best Supporting Actor (John Boyega)\n* Golden Globe Awards: Nominated for Best Supporting Actor (John Boyega)\n* BAFTA Awards: Nominated for Best Supporting Actor (John Boyega)\n\n**Box Office**\n\n* Opening weekend: $7.1 million\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Elon Musk wasn't blowing smoke. The BBC reports on a three-sentence statement issued by six of Tesla's nine board members saying the board had \"met several times over the last week\" to discuss Musk's proposal to take the company private, which he announced via an unexpected tweet Tuesday. The talks \"included discussion as to how being private could better serve Tesla's long-term interests\" and the statement ends by saying the board \"is taking the appropriate next steps to evaluate this.\" The three board members who did not sign the statement are Musk, brother Kimbal Musk, and a board member who is on leave, reports the Wall Street Journal. At MarketWatch, Steve Goldstein flags some unresolved points: The statement didn't elaborate on Musk's claim of \"funding secured\" and raised but didn't answer a question of timing. \"'Last week' predates Tesla's filing on Monday of its 10-Q with the Securities and Exchange Commission, which didn't mention such discussions. News of a takeover would certainly be considered material,\" he writes. Shares of Tesla are currently down about 1%."], "length": 1589, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Tesla's Board Considers Elon Musk's Proposal to Take Company Private**\n\nIn a shocking move, Tesla CEO Elon Musk tweeted on Tuesday that he was considering taking the electric car maker private, with a price of $420 per share, valuing the company at over $70 billion. The proposal, which would be the largest buyout in history, has left investors and regulators scrambling to understand the details.\n\nAccording to a statement from several independent directors of Tesla's board, the board has met several times over the past week to discuss Musk's proposal. The statement, issued on Wednesday, confirmed that the board is considering the proposal and has discussed how being a private company could \"better serve Tesla's long-term interests.\" However, the statement did not provide any details on whether the board is satisfied with Musk's funding plan or whether he has secured the necessary backing to take the company private.\n\nMusk's proposal has raised several questions, including the timing of the announcement, which was made via a tweet without a simultaneous regulatory filing. This has led to concerns about the irregularity of the announcement and whether Musk had a motive to influence the stock price. Tesla shares surged on Tuesday after the announcement, but fluctuated on Wednesday after the board members issued their statement.\n\nRegulators are likely to be interested in what evidence exists to support Musk's claim that funding was \"secured.\" The Securities and Exchange Commission has inquired about the issue, and experts have raised questions about the structure of the deal and how Musk plans to raise the necessary finance.\n\nMusk has argued that being a public company is a distraction and subjects the company to the quarterly earnings cycle, which can lead to decisions that are not in the long-term interest of the company. He has also stated that the company is the most shorted stock in history, which means that there are large numbers of people who have an incentive to attack the company.\n\nThe proposal has also raised questions about the potential impact on existing investors, who would be given the option to retain their shares. Musk already owns 20% of the company, and it is unclear how his personal holding would be affected by the proposal.\n\nOverall, the proposal to take Tesla private is a complex and uncertain situation, with many questions still unanswered. The board's consideration of the proposal is a significant development, but it remains to be seen whether the company will ultimately be taken private."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Guess who's back? Serena Williams. And she made sure her return to the tennis court Tuesday was a stylish one, the Miami Herald notes, tweeting a picture of herself in a hot pink body suit. \"Look who I spotted on the court. Her first day back,\" Williams wrote. She hadn't played since July thanks to health problems; click here to catch up on what's been going on. Her agent says Williams is \"progressing well\" and has the OK from doctors to do light cardio, but still has not set an exact date for her return to the WTA Tour."], "length": 770, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Tennis and Sports News**\n\nIn the world of tennis, several notable players are making headlines. Serena Williams, a 13-time Grand Slam champion, has returned to training after a long injury and illness layoff. Williams, who has been on blood thinners for blood clots, is taking it one day at a time and will return to the WTA Tour when she feels strong and healthy. Her agent, Jill Smoller, stated that Williams is progressing well and has been given clearance to do light cardio. Although she hasn't played since July, Williams remains ranked No. 10 in the world.\n\nMeanwhile, world No. 2 Kim Clijsters has suffered a freak foot accident that will likely keep her out of the French Open. Clijsters sustained a severe strain of her medial and lateral ligaments in her right ankle and torn ligaments in her ankle joint, and is expected to be out for at least four to six weeks.\n\nIn other tennis news, Fort Lauderdale's Ryan Sweeting has won his first ATP title, defeating Kei Nishikori 6-4, 7-6 (7-3) in the U.S. Clay Court Championship in Houston. Sweeting, who was born in the Bahamas and moved to Fort Lauderdale at 12, moved up from No. 93 to No. 71 with the victory.\n\nThe U.S. Fed Cup team will also be relying on two young players, 18-year-old Christina McHale and 19-year-old Melanie Oudin, in a world group playoff against Germany this weekend. The Germans are not taking the teenagers lightly, with captain Barbara Rittner stating that they are \"still very dangerous\" and have \"nothing to lose.\"\n\n**Internet Archive News**\n\nIn other news, Alexa Internet has been donating its crawl data to the Internet Archive since 1996. The data, which flow in every day, are added to the Wayback Machine after an embargo period. This donation has helped to create a vast archive of the internet's history, allowing users to access and view past versions of websites.\n\n**Key Dates**\n\n* May 22: French Open begins\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Watch out for stars moving across galaxies or losing power—it could be aliens gathering energy before the lights go out. Dan Hooper, an astronomy and astrophysics professor at the University of Chicago, posted a paper in the preprint journal arXiv.org arguing that aliens in far-off galaxies may be sucking energy from stars to offset risks posed by an expanding universe, LiveScience reports. \"The presence of dark energy in our universe is causing space to expand at an accelerating rate,\" writes Hooper. \"As a result, over the next approximately 100 billion years, all stars residing beyond the Local Group [of galaxies including the Milky Way] will fall beyond the cosmic horizon and become not only unobservable, but entirely inaccessible, thus limiting how much energy could one day be extracted from them.\" Therefore, he argues, an advanced civilization may use something like \"Dyson spheres\"—an imagined solar-powered satellite dating back to 1930s science-fiction, notes LiveScience—to harvest a star's energy or use that energy to move it closer to home. Hooper hinges his case on the work of Russian astronomer Nikolai Kardashev, who argued in 1962 that technologically advanced civilizations would expand via three steps: harvesting resources of their planet, then the nearest star, then all stars in the galaxy and nearby galaxies. So how to spot energy-grabbing aliens? Massive stars emanate certain light wavelengths, which will appear in galaxies' light signatures if stars are drained energy—that is, when humans have powerful enough instruments to detect it."], "length": 1741, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**The Quest for Energy: A Universal Imperative**\n\nAstronomers and scientists have long pondered the question of how advanced civilizations might harness the energy of their stars and galaxies. Recently, a paper by Dan Hooper, a senior scientist at the Fermi National Accelerator Laboratory, has revived the idea of \"Dyson spheres\" - massive structures that could capture the energy of a star and beam it back to a planet. According to Hooper, the presence of dark energy in the universe is causing space to expand at an accelerating rate, making it essential for advanced civilizations to expand their energy-harvesting capabilities to prevent being left behind.\n\nHooper's paper proposes that advanced civilizations would capture stars from other galaxies, reel them in, and harvest their energy before they become inaccessible forever. This would require the construction of massive Dyson spheres, made up of millions of individual solar-collecting satellites, which would hover around a star and collect its energy. The author suggests that these structures would be detectable by astronomers, who could look for unusual light signatures from galaxies that have had their prime stars ripped away.\n\nThe idea of Dyson spheres is not new, however. Science fiction author Olaf Stapledon described similar structures in his 1937 novel \"Star Maker\", and physicist Freeman Dyson proposed similar structures in a scientific paper in 1960. Dyson realized that alien civilizations could be recognized by their waste heat, which would be detectable as infrared radiation. Astrophysicist Nikolai Kardashev proposed in 1962 that very old and advanced civilizations would be of three types: Type I, which harnesses all the resources of a planet; Type II, which harnesses all the radiation of a star; and Type III, which harnesses all the resources of a galaxy.\n\nWhile the idea of Dyson spheres may seem like science fiction, it is a serious proposal that has been explored by scientists and science fiction authors alike. The construction of such massive structures would require enormous amounts of energy and resources, but it could potentially provide a way for advanced civilizations to harness the energy of their stars and galaxies, and to expand their presence in the universe.\n\n**Key Takeaways:**\n\n* Advanced civilizations may need to expand their energy-harvesting capabilities to prevent being left behind by the accelerating expansion of the universe.\n* Dyson spheres, massive structures that capture the energy of a star, could be a way for advanced civilizations to harness energy and expand their presence in the universe.\n* The construction of Dyson spheres"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Police have arrested one of Disneyland's own employees after yesterday's dry-ice explosion in the Toontown section of the park, reports the Los Angeles Times. Christian Barnes, a 22-year-old vendor who sold soda and water from a cart, is being held on $1 million bond. Police say he filled a bottle with dry ice and dropped it in a trash can. The ensuing blast caused a lot of noise and chaos, but no injuries or serious damage, reports AP. No word on motive, but police don't think it's related to previous dry-ice bombs in the Anaheim area."], "length": 650, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Dry Ice Bomb Incident at Disneyland**\n\nA 22-year-old Disneyland employee, Christian Barnes, has been arrested in connection with the detonation of a dry ice bomb in the park's Toontown area. The explosion, which occurred on Tuesday evening, forced the evacuation of the area and caused brief chaos among park visitors. Fortunately, no one was injured in the incident.\n\nAccording to Anaheim police, Barnes, who worked as an outdoor vendor, placed dry ice in a plastic bottle and left it in a trash can, which then exploded. The police believe that Barnes acted alone and that the incident was an isolated one.\n\nBarnes was arrested on suspicion of possession of a destructive device and is being held on $1 million bail. He is cooperating with investigators and has indicated that the incident was unanticipated.\n\nThe incident has raised concerns about safety at Disneyland, but park officials have stated that they take matters like this very seriously and are working closely with local authorities to ensure the safety of visitors.\n\nThe dry ice bomb incident is not believed to be connected to similar incidents in Anaheim in recent months. The police are still investigating the motive behind the incident, but it appears to be an isolated incident.\n\nThe incident has caused a stir among park visitors, with some expressing shock and concern about the safety of the park. However, park officials have stated that they are committed to ensuring the safety of all visitors and are taking steps to prevent such incidents in the future.\n\n**Other News**\n\nIn other news, a probe has been launched into the torture death of a Palmdale boy, and police are investigating two witnesses who dispute Justin Bieber's story about his Ferrari. Additionally, two police officers who took a car on a joyride to Las Vegas have blamed a \"momentary\" lapse in judgment for their actions."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A former well-known Canadian TV sports anchor is in jail after police say he robbed two banks in as many days in the western province of Alberta last week. Police in the small city of Medicine Hat say Steve Vogelsang robbed a Royal Bank of Canada on Thursday, Oct. 19, and then turned around the next day and robbed a Bank of Montreal, CBC reports. He entered both banks without a disguise and after demanding money, left with an undisclosed sum. Police finally arrested Vogelsang at a nearby hotel at around 3am on Saturday. Per CTV Winnipeg News, Vogelsang, 53, was a longtime news director and sports broadcaster at CKY, now CTV Winnipeg. He also taught journalism at Red River College in Winnipeg from 2002 to 2011. Conor Lloyd, a spokesman for the college, says, “I can confirm he is no longer an employee here.” Vogelsang is scheduled to appear in provincial court on Tuesday."], "length": 1543, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Former Winnipeg Broadcaster and Journalism Instructor Charged with Bank Robberies**\n\nSteve Vogelsang, a 53-year-old former Winnipeg broadcast personality and Red River College journalism instructor, has been charged with two counts of robbery in connection with two bank robberies in Medicine Hat, Alberta. Vogelsang, who worked as a news director and sports anchor at CKY-TV (now CTV Winnipeg) from the early 1990s to 2002, was arrested on Saturday at a hotel in Medicine Hat, where he was found to be in possession of an undisclosed amount of cash.\n\nAccording to police, Vogelsang entered a Royal Bank of Canada on October 19 and a Bank of Montreal on October 20, passing a note to a teller in each instance and receiving money. There were no reports of any weapons being seen or brandished during the robberies.\n\nVogelsang's arrest comes as he is facing financial difficulties, including a divorce and allegations of missing mortgage payments on a home in Winnipeg. Court documents obtained by CTV News reveal that Vogelsang and his ex-wife sold three properties in Nelson, B.C. for a total loss of $85,000, and that he was accused of forging her signature to renew the mortgage on their Winnipeg home.\n\nVogelsang is also facing allegations of harassing a former student, who was granted a protection order against him in March. The student alleges that Vogelsang continued to contact her despite being asked to leave her alone by Winnipeg police.\n\nVogelsang's past is also marred by a charge of sexual assault, which was stayed in 2017. He is also facing three charges of disobeying a court order, laid in August 2017.\n\nFormer students of Vogelsang have expressed shock and disappointment at the allegations, with one student describing him as a \"tough, but fair\" teacher who built strong relationships with his students.\n\nVogelsang is expected to appear in court tomorrow to face the robbery charges."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["An Oklahoma girl diagnosed with \"super\" E. coli is facing a life-or-death battle—and worse, she's only 11 months old. \"With this strain of E. coli, it can get in her brain, it can get in her spinal cord, basically cause her to go into cardiac arrest,\" her grandfather, Chris Curtis, tells KFOR. \"And we heard that it was a very, very scary situation.\" Her family says it's not clear how Oakleigh Nance got the infection, which appears resistant to antibiotics, but the CDC is investigating. It all started when Oakleigh was born premature at only 3 pounds and \"started off on 4 liters of oxygen,\" according to a GoFundMe page to help pay her medical bills. \"In the NICU, they used to call her Miss Sassy because of her ability to express her displeasure with her circumstances,\" the page says. Then she went home and was fine for months until a 104-degree temperature and severe urinary tract infection sent her back to the hospital. Since then, she's been diagnosed with moderate ASD and pulmonary stenosis of the heart, stage 3 kidney reflux, and \"a dangerous, antibiotic resistant strain of Pseudomonas,\" says the GoFundMe page. Now doctors are planning a 4 1/2-hour procedure on her kidney, which is functioning at 22%, to possibly prevent other infections. \"I know that God is in control of all else, and when I think about what’s going on in her life, I think about my faith in God,\" says Curtis. \"That’s what sustains me and gets me through it.\" (Read about a brain-eating bacteria that has returned to Louisiana.)"], "length": 1463, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Infant Battling Serious Infections and Health Issues**\n\nEleven-month-old Oakleigh Nance is fighting for her life in a metro hospital in Oklahoma City. Born premature at three pounds, Oakleigh has been in and out of the hospital for months due to various health issues. Recently, she was diagnosed with a critical case of E. coli, a \"super strain\" that is resistant to many antibiotics. This diagnosis is particularly concerning due to Oakleigh's young age and her previous exposure to antibiotics, which has limited her treatment options.\n\nIn addition to the E. coli infection, Oakleigh has been battling recurring infections, including a severe UTI that led to a diagnosis of stage 3 kidney reflux. She has also been found to have moderate ASD and pulmonary stenosis, a heart condition that is particularly concerning in conjunction with her kidney issues.\n\nOakleigh's family is relying on their faith to get them through this difficult time. Her grandfather, Chris Curtis, stated that their faith is what sustains them and helps them cope with the challenges they are facing.\n\nIn a separate incident, a news article discussed the dangers of pseudomonas infections, a type of bacteria that can cause serious and even deadly infections in people who are weak or ill. Pseudomonas infections are hard to treat because the bacteria can resist many types of antibiotics. The article highlighted the importance of proper hygiene and sterilization to prevent the spread of these infections.\n\nOakleigh's upcoming kidney surgery, scheduled for the second week in August, is a crucial step in addressing her health issues. Her family is trying to raise money through a GoFundMe account to cover the medical expenses associated with her treatment.\n\nOverall, Oakleigh's case highlights the importance of proper medical care and the need for continued research into the treatment of serious infections and health issues. Her family's faith and resilience in the face of adversity are a testament to the strength and courage of those who are fighting for their loved ones' lives."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The Portland teen accused of peeing in a city reservoir on Wednesday insists he didn't do it—in a relatively NSFW defense. \"Yeah, it's f---ing retarded, dude,\" 18-year-old Dallas Swonger tells Vocativ in what the site calls an \"expletive-filled interview\" given as Swonger smoked a Newport cigarette. \"I didn't piss in the f---ing water.\" He explains that he did urinate, after a night of skateboarding at Mount Tabor Park, but he did so against the wall of the reservoir, not into the reservoir. \"I was like, 'Dudes I have to piss so bad.' So I just went over to the wall. I leaned up against the wall and pissed on it. Right there on the wall, dude. I don’t know else how to describe it.\" Officials beg to differ (a Water Bureau official got very specific about it, saying that surveillance video showed Swonger making sure \"to get his little wee wee right up to the iron bars\"), and thus decided to dump 38 million gallons of water, but Swonger notes that even if his urine had made it into the water, he doesn't see what the big deal is. \"Dude, I’ve seen dead birds in there. During the summer time I've see hella dead animals in there. Like dead squirrels and s---. I mean, really, dude?\" Vocativ also got amusing quotes from Swonger's mom (\"I’m sorry, he just graduated from high school. He’s trying to get his stuff together\") and one of the friends skateboarding with him that night (\"He just doesn’t make the best decisions. Honestly, he has the potential to do really good\"). Possible criminal charges are still pending, the Oregonian notes. The paper also reports that the water tested clean of urine-related toxins, but all 38 million gallons will still be dumped."], "length": 1963, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Portland Water Crisis: 38 Million Gallons of Water Flushed Down the Drain**\n\nA bizarre incident at the Mt. Tabor Reservoir system in Portland, Oregon, has led to the city's decision to dump 38 million gallons of drinking water down the drain. The controversy began when a surveillance video appeared to show 18-year-old Dallas Swonger urinating in one of the reservoirs. However, Swonger has since denied the allegations, claiming that he actually urinated on the wall next to the reservoir.\n\nDespite test results showing no signs of water contamination, city officials have decided to flush the water as a precautionary measure. The decision has been met with criticism from the public and experts, who argue that the risk of health risks is extremely low.\n\nAccording to Anna Harding, co-director of the School of Biological and Population Health Sciences at Oregon State University, \"the urine, which has very few microorganisms to begin with, would be very, very, very, very diluted.\" Harding and others have questioned the city's decision, suggesting that it is a public relations move rather than a necessary health precaution.\n\nThe incident has sparked a debate about the city's water management policies and the decision-making process behind the reservoir's maintenance. City Commissioner Nick Fish has defended the decision, stating that \"any risk is too much\" and that the city is taking the \"safest course.\"\n\nSwonger, who has been cited for trespassing and public urination, has expressed his frustration and disappointment with the situation. He claims that he is being unfairly maligned and that the city's decision to flush the water is \"fucking retarded.\"\n\nThe incident has also raised questions about the city's handling of similar incidents in the past. In 2011, the city flushed 7.8 million gallons of reservoir water after another man urinated in the reservoir.\n\nThe controversy has sparked a national and international media frenzy, with many questioning the city's decision and the risks associated with flushing 38 million gallons of drinking water. As Swonger's skateboard buddy, Daniel McDonald, said, \"He's a really good guy at heart. He just doesn't make the best decisions.\""}
{"input": "", "answers": ["North and South Korea will compete together under one unified flag at next month's Winter Olympics in Pyeongchang, in what will be the first such arrangement since 2006. South Korea previously announced North Korea would send officials, athletes, and a cheer squad to the Olympics following talks between the two countries. It has now revealed North Korea's intention to send a 550-member delegation. Among those arriving in South Korea beginning on Jan. 25 will be female ice hockey players joining a team of South Korean athletes (they'll compete under a flag showing the Korean Peninsula in blue on a white background), as well as 230 cheerleaders, 140 orchestra members, and 30 people who will take part in a taekwondo demonstration, report Sky News and CNN. Ahead of the Olympics, skiers from both countries will also train together in North Korea, per CNN. The thawing of relations between the North and South has drawn praise from some. But others, like Japanese Foreign Minister Taro Kono, fear Kim Jong Un only hopes to ease international pressure while continuing to develop a nuclear weapons arsenal. \"It is not the time to ease pressure, or to reward North Korea,\" Kono said Tuesday in Vancouver, Canada, where 20 countries met and agreed to consider tougher sanctions on the North, per Reuters. Warning countries not to be fooled by its \"charm offensive,\" Kono added, \"The fact that North Korea is engaging in dialogue could be interpreted as proof that the sanctions are working.\" China avoided the meeting, saying it showed a \"Cold War mentality.\""], "length": 2659, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**North and South Korea Make Historic Agreement for Joint Olympic Participation**\n\nIn a significant diplomatic breakthrough, North and South Korea have agreed to march together under a unified flag at the upcoming Winter Olympics in Pyeongchang, South Korea. The two countries have also decided to form a joint women's ice hockey team, a move that has been met with both excitement and concern.\n\nThe agreement was reached after a series of talks between the two countries, which have been strained for years due to North Korea's nuclear and missile programs. The joint Olympic team is seen as a significant step towards reconciliation and unity between the two Koreas, which are technically still at war.\n\nThe unified flag, known as the Korean Unification Flag, features a blue silhouette of the peninsula and surrounding islands on a white background. It was last used in 2006 at the Winter Olympics in Italy.\n\nNorth Korea will send a 550-member delegation to the games, including 230 cheerleaders, 140 artists, and 30 taekwondo players for a demonstration. The delegation will begin arriving in South Korea on January 25.\n\nThe joint women's ice hockey team has been a topic of controversy, with some South Korean players expressing concerns about competing with their North Korean counterparts. However, the South Korean government has emphasized the importance of the joint team as a symbol of peace and unity.\n\nThe agreement has been welcomed by the International Olympic Committee (IOC), which has received a number of \"interesting proposals\" from the two countries. The IOC will discuss the proposals with delegates from both countries in Switzerland on Saturday.\n\nDespite the breakthrough, concerns remain about North Korea's intentions and the potential for the talks to be used as a \"charm offensive\" to distract from its nuclear and missile programs. Japan has warned that the world should not be naive about North Korea's intentions, and the United States has expressed skepticism about the talks.\n\nThe joint Olympic team is seen as a significant step towards reconciliation and unity between the two Koreas, but it remains to be seen whether the agreement will lead to more substantial progress in the future."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A homeless man was arrested after hopping the White House fence Tuesday night in an incident captured by CNN's John King USA program, which was filming from the North Lawn. The man was quickly taken into custody by Secret Service agents who approached him with guns drawn. Security officers locked the area down after finding a backpack that had been thrown over the fence nearby. The 41-year-old intruder was charged with unlawful entry and contempt of court for breaking a judicial order requiring him to stay away from the White House. It's not uncommon for the White House fence to be breached, notes the Washington Post: A 6-year-old girl made it onto the White House lawn over the weekend by slipping through the fence instead of going over it. Secret Service agents returned her to her parents."], "length": 536, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**White House Fence Incident: Man Apprehended and Charged**\n\nOn Tuesday, a 41-year-old homeless man, James Dirk Crudup, jumped the White House fence and was apprehended by uniformed Secret Service officers. The incident was broadcast live on CNN's \"John King USA\" program, which was produced from the North Lawn of the White House.\n\nAccording to authorities, Crudup was tackled immediately by Secret Service officers, who approached him with guns drawn. He was ordered to lie down and was subsequently handcuffed before being taken into custody. A backpack that belonged to him was being examined as a precaution.\n\nCrudup will be charged with unlawful entry and contempt of court, as he had previously been ordered to stay away from the White House due to past incidents. The contempt charge is in connection with an order requiring him to stay away from the White House.\n\nThis incident is not an isolated one, as a 6-year-old girl was able to reach the White House lawn on Sunday night by slipping through a gap in the fence. She was escorted out by the Secret Service.\n\nThe incident ended shortly after 9 p.m. ET, when authorities issued an all-clear directive at the White House. There was no immediate information on whether Crudup had represented a security threat.\n\nThe Secret Service has stated that incidents such as this are not uncommon, and that they take all necessary precautions to ensure the security of the White House and its occupants."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Jon Stewart took over as host of the Daily Show in January 1999, but when did the program truly start becoming the Daily Show that legions of fans would come to love? Try December of that year, when Steve Carell boarded John McCain's campaign bus \"and changed the entire trajectory\" of the show, writes Chris Smith at Vanity Fair. The article is a fascinating oral history of the show's beginning that includes interviews with Stewart, Carell, Stephen Colbert, and pretty much everyone involved. The first year was rough for Stewart as he fought with the holdover writing staff from previous host Craig Kilborn and sought to put his stamp on the show. Things came together in that Carell interview, in which he peppers McCain with softball questions, then springs a policy zinger. After a few seconds of awkward silence, Carell says, \"I was just kidding! I don’t even know what that means!\" And the tense moment subsides. (See the clip.) Carell: \"It was making fun of a gotcha moment. And I think that a lot of what we do on The Daily Show is making fun of journalistic tropes, and I think that was one of them.\" Head writer Ben Karlin: \"I remember seeing it in the editing room. I remember Jon called me down, and seeing it and thinking, Yeah, this is what we should be doing. This is the goal. It was one of Carell’s most incredible moments. He asks McCain a question in a way that no journalists were talking to the candidates. And it was like, Oh s---, we are able, in this weird, unintentional way, to add a level of insight to the process that doesn’t exist. That was really, really exciting.\" Click for the full piece, in which Stewart talks about how that 2000 campaign provided his \"aha moment\" of how to present the show—by \"deconstructing the process.\""], "length": 7229, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**The Evolution of The Daily Show**\n\nThe Daily Show, a satirical news program, has undergone significant changes since its inception in 1996. Initially, the show was hosted by Craig Kilborn, who brought a lighthearted and humorous approach to news. However, when Jon Stewart took over as host in 1999, the show's tone and direction shifted. Stewart, who had previously worked on The Larry Sanders Show, brought a more serious and satirical approach to the program.\n\nStewart's arrival was met with resistance from some of the existing writers and staff, who were accustomed to the show's previous format. However, Stewart's vision for the show was clear: to create a program that was both funny and insightful, with a focus on deconstructing the news and politics.\n\nTo achieve this vision, Stewart brought in new writers and correspondents, including Ben Karlin, David Javerbaum, and Stephen Colbert. Karlin, who became the head writer, shared Stewart's vision for the show and helped to shape its direction. Javerbaum, who joined the show in 1999, brought a unique perspective and writing style that added to the show's humor and satire.\n\nColbert, who had previously worked on The Dana Carvey Show, became a key correspondent and contributor to the show. His character, a straight-laced and serious news anchor, was a perfect foil to the show's satirical approach.\n\nThe show's correspondents, including Steve Carell, Mo Rocca, and Nancy Walls Carell, also played a crucial role in shaping the show's tone and direction. They developed characters and personas that added to the show's humor and satire.\n\nOne of the key moments in the show's evolution was the 1999 segment in which Steve Carell asked Senator John McCain a question on his bus, the Straight Talk Express. The segment was a breakthrough for the show, as it demonstrated its ability to add insight and humor to the news.\n\nUnder Stewart's leadership, The Daily Show became known for its satirical take on politics and news. The show's correspondents and writers continued to push the boundaries of what was possible in a satirical news program, and the show became a cultural touchstone for many young people.\n\nToday, The Daily Show remains a popular and influential program, known for its humor, satire, and insight into the world of politics and news. Its legacy continues to be felt, and its impact on the world of comedy and journalism is undeniable."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A North Carolina woman allegedly locked in the trunk of a moving car by her boyfriend was saved by a quick-thinking 911 dispatcher and a lowly flip phone, ABC News reports. The 29-year-old woman called 911 around 4am Jan. 14. The woman had gotten in a fight with her boyfriend, who was \"jealous,\" according to WRAL. Before dispatcher Tim Medlin could get much information from the woman, the call was dropped. He was unable to get her to answer when he called back. Then Medlin remembered a flip phone the 911 communications center keeps around in case the phone lines go down, WTVD reports. He says texting was the only way he could think to contact her without getting \"her in trouble.\" The woman texted Medlin back, letting the dispatcher know she was in a red Mustang headed toward Fayetteville. Verizon provided the location of the woman's phone, and police stopped her and her boyfriend, Nicholas Mattevi, in Fayetteville. Mattevi, 32, was arrested and charged with false imprisonment. \"I can go to bed,\" Medlin recalls feeling upon hearing of the arrest. \"We've done our job.\" He says he's planning to buy a smartphone for the office to replace the old flip phone and make it easier to text if there's a next time. Medlin's boss tells ABC the dispatcher \"really stood by our motto: Failure is not an option.\" (This 911 dispatcher was accused of hanging up on callers.)"], "length": 1325, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Quick-Thinking 911 Dispatcher Saves Woman Trapped in Car Trunk**\n\nA remarkable story of bravery and quick thinking has emerged from Johnston County, North Carolina, where a 911 dispatcher helped save a woman who was locked in the trunk of a moving car. On January 14, 2017, around 4 a.m., a 29-year-old woman called 911, frantically telling the dispatcher that she had been kidnapped by her jealous boyfriend and was trapped in the trunk of a car. The call was dropped, and the dispatcher, Tim Medlin, was unable to reach the woman by phone.\n\nUndeterred, Medlin used the 911 center's emergency cellphone to text the woman, who responded and provided crucial information about her location and the car's make and color. With the help of a cellphone provider, Medlin was able to locate the car, and Fayetteville police were dispatched to the scene. The woman was rescued unharmed, and her boyfriend, Nicholas Mattevi, was arrested and charged with false imprisonment.\n\nThe incident highlights the importance of technology in emergency situations and the need for 911 centers to be able to initiate text messages. Johnston County's 911 director, Jason Barbour, praised Medlin's quick thinking and said that the incident was a prime example of technology being used to the fullest at someone's greatest time of need.\n\nMattevi, 32, was released on a $10,000 bond and is awaiting trial. The woman, who has not been named, was shaken but unharmed. The incident serves as a reminder of the importance of staying vigilant and seeking help in emergency situations.\n\nIn the aftermath of the incident, Medlin's boss, Jason Barbour, announced plans to purchase a smartphone for the 911 team, citing the need for a keyboard to facilitate communication in emergency situations. The incident has also sparked a wider conversation about the need for 911 centers to be able to initiate text messages, rather than just receiving them."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Police in Stanislaus County, Calif., say a 20-year veteran of the force was \"executed\" early Sunday after investigating a stolen van and a suspicious person outside Modesto. Sheriff's Deputy Dennis Wallace was shot twice in the head. Police say a suspect was arrested more than 150 miles away later in the day after allegedly carrying out a carjacking, an armed robbery at a convenience store, and an attempted purse-snatching, the Los Angeles Times reports. David Machado, a 37-year-old described by police as a \"known criminal,\" was connected to the Wallace killing after police in Tulane County recognized his tattoos. Wallace, who was married with children, \"was executed,\" Sheriff Adam Christianson told reporters. \"We believe that Dep. Wallace was killed outside of the car and we know for a fact that the gun used in this crime was in direct contact with his head when the trigger was pulled twice.\" He said Wallace, 53, had been involved in programs including youth soccer and early intervention, NBC News reports. \"We need to keep the Wallace family in our thoughts and prayers. We need to keep law enforcement in our thoughts and prayers,\" he said. The Bradenton Herald reports after a knee injury in 2007, Wallace was off for two years without pay and fought a long legal battle to get his job back."], "length": 420, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Breaking News Summary**\n\n**Internet Archive Makes Progress in Preserving Web Content**\n\nThe Internet Archive has launched a new initiative to archive web pages as they are created and preserve links to referenced pages. This effort aims to prevent broken links on the web by creating a permanent record of web pages and their original content. The archive will allow users to access the original version of a page even if the linked page is no longer available.\n\n**Tragic Death of Modoc County Sheriff's Deputy**\n\nIn a separate incident, a Modoc County Sheriff's Deputy was tragically killed in the line of duty on October 19. Deputy Jack Hopkins, 35, was responding to a disturbance call when he was confronted and shot by Jack Lee Breiner, 47. The deputy was killed instantly, and Breiner was subsequently shot and wounded in a gunbattle with another deputy. Breiner has been charged with murder and other crimes in connection with the killing.\n\n**Key Points**\n\n* Internet Archive launches initiative to preserve web content and prevent broken links\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Papillion Sophie has lived a pampered life, but that doesn't have to end now that her famous owner—Hollywood legend Lauren Bacall—has died. According to estate papers filed last week, Bacall left $10,000 to her son Sam Robards for Sophie's care and upkeep, Page Six reports. This bequeath isn't a surprise, considering Bacall called herself a \"dog yearner\" in a 2008 interview, Us reports. \"I didn't have a dog growing up in the city with a working mother. As an only child, I yearned for someone to talk to,\" she said. She gave Sophie quite a glitzy life attending book signings and film festivals. Bacall didn't ignore her human family: The New York Daily News reports her $26.6 million estate was mostly split among her three children; her grandkids reportedly got $250,000 each. And two of her employees also received $20,000 and $15,000, respectively. Considering other famous four-legged heirs, Sophie's 10 grand isn't shocking: Leona Helmsley left $12 million to her Maltese and Alexander McQueen left $82,000 to his three dogs. But the oddest may be Dusty Springfield, who gave orders that her cat be serenaded with her own music and \"marry\" another cat."], "length": 1776, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Lauren Bacall Leaves Behind $26.6 Million Estate, $10,000 for Beloved Dog Sophie**\n\nLegendary actress Lauren Bacall, who passed away on August 12 at the age of 89, left behind a significant estate worth $26.6 million. According to her will, the majority of her property will be split evenly among her three children: Sam Robards, Stephen Humphrey Bogart, and Leslie Bogart. However, Bacall made sure to provide for her beloved dog, Sophie, a papillon, by setting aside $10,000 for her care.\n\nIn addition to the bequest for Sophie, Bacall also left gifts of $250,000 to each of her six grandchildren, with the stipulation that they will have access to the rest of the cash when they turn 30. Two of her employees, Maria Santos and Ilsa Hernandez, were also remembered in her will, with Santos receiving $20,000 and Hernandez receiving $15,000.\n\nBacall's will also included a request that her children respect her wish to keep certain personal letters, writings, diaries, and other papers or memorabilia private. The actress, who won two Tony Awards, a Golden Globe, and an honorary Oscar over her eight-decade career, named all of her children as executors of her estate.\n\nBacall's estate includes her co-op apartment, royalties, tangible personal property, cash, and the remainder of a trust left to her by her late husband, Humphrey Bogart. The family plans to auction off her artwork this fall, with the proceeds going towards the estate.\n\nBacall's love for her dog, Sophie, was well-documented, and the actress was often seen walking the pup outside her $9 million apartment at The Dakota on Central Park West. The bequest for Sophie's care is a testament to the actress's devotion to her beloved pet.\n\nIn related news, Bacall's generosity towards her employees and family members is not unusual among celebrities. Other notable examples include hotel magnate Leona Helmsley, who left $12 million to her Maltese, Trouble, and fashion designer Alexander McQueen, who left $82,000 to his three dogs."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Fresh off his loss to Mitt Romney at CPAC, Rick Santorum hit the talk shows this morning, and he's downplaying the victory, reports Politico: \"You have to talk to the Romney campaign and how many tickets they bought—we've heard all sorts of things,\" Santorum told CNN. \"Ron Paul has won those (straw polls) because he trucks in a lot of people, pays for their ticket, and they come in and vote. I don't try to rig straw polls.\" Elsewhere on the Sunday dial, as per Politico: Santorum on his chances: \"I think we can do well (in Michigan). I think we can do reasonably well in Arizona, and really make this a two-person race.\" Sarah Palin on Romney: He's \"a great candidate,\" but \"his idea of conservatism is evolving, and I base this on a pretty moderate past that he has had, even in some cases a liberal past. Now that’s a problem. He’s still in the 30 percentile mark ... because we are not convinced.\" Obama chief of staff Jack Lew on the birth control ruckus: The Friday \"accommodation\" is as far as the White House will go. \"There are others who don't have the same objective, they will have to speak for themselves, (but) this is our plan.\" Ron Paul on 'severe conservative' Romney: \"That was the first time I’ve heard that definition, so I guess Mitt will have to tell us exactly what it means. Obviously, he means he’s a serious conservative, and he was trying to defend himself, or portray himself as such, but I don’t know exactly what he was meaning by that.\""], "length": 788, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Summary of Recent News**\n\nThe Republican presidential primary and the Obama administration's stance on birth control have dominated the headlines in recent days.\n\nIn the Republican primary, Mitt Romney's victory in the CPAC presidential straw poll has sparked controversy. Rick Santorum, who came in second, accused Romney of rigging the poll by buying tickets for supporters. However, Romney's campaign rejected the claim, with spokeswoman Andrea Saul saying that Santorum has a history of making false statements. Santorum also criticized Romney for his description of himself as a \"severe conservative,\" with Ron Paul expressing similar skepticism on CBS's \"Face the Nation.\"\n\nMeanwhile, the Obama administration's stance on birth control has been a point of contention. President Obama's \"accommodation\" on the issue, announced on Friday, has been met with skepticism by Catholic bishops. However, White House chief of staff Jack Lew defended the administration's position, saying that it has been \"consistent\" and that insurance companies will ultimately pay less for birth control-inclusive plans because they reduce pregnancies. Lew also stated that the administration will not budge further on the issue, despite criticism from some quarters.\n\nOverall, the news suggests that the Republican primary is heating up, with Romney and Santorum engaging in a war of words over the CPAC poll. Meanwhile, the Obama administration's stance on birth control remains a contentious issue, with the administration digging in its heels despite criticism from Catholic bishops and others."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Fixer Upper stars Chip and Joanna Gaines are having to pay up for EPA violations seen on their hit HGTV show. The couple's business, Magnolia Homes, has agreed to pay a $40,000 fine after the EPA determined 33 of its home renovations were \"conducted without adequate lead paint protections,\" reports Deadline, which notes the potential fine the company faced was as high as $556,000. The EPA notes Fixer Upper footage \"reviewed by EPA did not depict the lead-safe work practices normally required\" though \"Magnolia took immediate steps to ensure compliance\" when it was notified. Chip Gaines, who in March tweeted advice about engaging a professional to check for lead paint when buying or remodeling a home, will also star in a Magnolia-paid video on lead paint safety as part of the settlement, per Vulture. (The couple are expecting their fifth child.)"], "length": 2074, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**HGTV's Fixer Upper Stars Chip and Joanna Gaines Reach Settlement with EPA over Lead-Based Paint Violations**\n\nChip and Joanna Gaines, the popular HGTV couple behind the hit show Fixer Upper, have reached a settlement with the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) over alleged lead-based paint violations. The couple's company, Magnolia Homes, will pay a $40,000 fine and take steps to ensure compliance with lead-based paint regulations in future renovation projects.\n\nAccording to the EPA, Magnolia Homes engaged in home renovations without adequate lead paint protections, as depicted on the TV show Fixer Upper, which is regularly viewed by over 3 million households. A review of video footage from several seasons of the show found evidence of noncompliance at renovations of 33 properties.\n\nAs part of the settlement, Magnolia Homes will also produce an educational video highlighting the dangers of lead-based paint, starring Chip Gaines, and release it within 90 days. Additionally, the company will spend $160,000 on a lead-abatement project in their hometown of Waco, Texas, to reduce the risk of exposure to lead-based paint in at-risk residences.\n\nThe EPA notes that Magnolia Homes has taken steps to improve public awareness of the serious hazards of lead-based paint and the precautions necessary to reduce those hazards. The company has already spoken about lead-based paint on an episode of Fixer Upper and has used social media to amplify the message about lead-based paint safety.\n\nThe settlement is a result of the EPA's review of Magnolia Homes' renovation activities, which were brought to their attention through the company's depiction on popular television shows. The EPA's enforcement action aims to protect children and others who are vulnerable to exposure to lead dust that can cause lead poisoning.\n\nIn a statement, the EPA noted that Magnolia Homes has \"looked for ways to make a concrete difference in its local community\" and has taken steps to ensure compliance with lead-based paint regulations in future renovation projects. The company's efforts to address the issue and promote lead safety are seen as a positive step towards reducing the risk of lead exposure in at-risk communities."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["For decades, the human story was one told through signs of modernity—art, tools, burials—found only after Homo sapiens left Africa. Recent discoveries pushing back the date of departure are helping to change that narrative, as are three new studies in Science. Together, they describe the earliest stone tools of their kind in East Africa as well as the earliest evidence for long-distance transport of raw materials in the region, per Science News. Researchers previously believed the Early Stone Age marked by large, primitive tools (hand axes and cleavers) made way for the Middle Stone Age and its smaller tools (spearpoints and blades) around 280,000 years ago. But stone tools found in Kenya's Olorgesailie Basin show hominin groups—it's not clear which species is responsible—had developed these more advanced tools approximately 320,000 years ago. \"We see a smaller technology, a more diverse series of stone tools … designed for specific purposes,\" researcher Rick Potts tells NPR. An expert not involved in the study says the tools are so \"prepared and retouched\" they suggest the Middle Stone Age likely started even earlier. What drove it? Microscopic and chemical analyses of the region's soil show the landscape was rapidly shifting between wet and dry conditions, meaning hominins might have been forced to adapt. But as the technology also coincides with the emergence of Homo sapiens in Africa, it's possible that the human mind deserves credit. The tools themselves suggest the development of trading networks, too. Some were made of rocks carried from 55 miles away, says Potts. Others were made of obsidian from as far as 30 miles away, per the Conversation. (These stone tools are also making waves.)"], "length": 3668, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Ancient Human Innovations: New Discoveries in Africa**\n\nRecent studies have shed new light on the evolution of human behavior and innovation in Africa, challenging previous theories about the emergence of modern human behavior. Excavations in the Olorgesailie Basin in Kenya have uncovered evidence of technological advancements and cultural complexity dating back to around 320,000 years ago, during the Middle Stone Age.\n\n**Toolmaking and Trading Networks**\n\nResearchers have discovered a range of stone tools, including small, sharp points and blades, made from obsidian and other exotic materials. These tools were likely used for hunting and gathering, and their production suggests a high level of technological sophistication. The presence of obsidian, which was transported from distant locations, indicates the existence of long-distance trading networks among early human populations.\n\n**Climate Change and Human Adaptation**\n\nThe studies suggest that climate change played a significant role in driving human innovation and adaptation. The Olorgesailie Basin was once a floodplain, but it became a vast arid grassland around 800,000 years ago, making food sources unpredictable. Human populations adapted by developing new technologies and cultural practices, including the use of obsidian and other exotic materials.\n\n**Rethinking the \"Human Revolution\"**\n\nThe discoveries in the Olorgesailie Basin challenge the traditional view of the \"human revolution,\" which posited that modern human behavior emerged around 40,000-50,000 years ago. Instead, it appears that complex behaviors and innovations arose much earlier, in groups that predated modern humans. The studies suggest that the transition to modern human behavior was a gradual process, rather than a sudden revolution.\n\n**Implications for Human Evolution**\n\nThe findings have significant implications for our understanding of human evolution. They suggest that early human populations were capable of complex thought and behavior, and that the emergence of modern human behavior was a long-term process. The studies also highlight the importance of climate change and environmental factors in driving human innovation and adaptation.\n\n**Key Takeaways**\n\n* The Olorgesailie Basin in Kenya has yielded evidence of technological advancements and cultural complexity dating back to around 320,000 years ago.\n* The presence of obsidian and other exotic materials suggests the existence of long-distance trading networks among early human populations.\n* Climate change played a significant role in driving human innovation and adaptation.\n* The discoveries challenge the traditional view of the \"human revolution\" and suggest that complex behaviors and innovations arose much earlier.\n* The transition to modern human behavior was a gradual process, rather than a sudden revolution."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The year's top travel writer is a familiar face, but more so for acting than writing. Andrew McCarthy of Brat Pack fame (yes, Molly Ringwald's true love in Pretty in Pink) has won the 2010 Lowell Thomas award for Travel Journalist of the Year, notes Jim Romenesko's blog at PoynterOnline. McCarthy's freelance articles have appeared in National Geographic Traveler, Bon Appetit, Afar, and the Atlantic, among others. (His piece on Ireland in the latter is here.) \"Word by word, paragraph-by-paragraph, entry-by-entry, McCarthy earns the gold the old-fashioned way—by treating readers to excellent writing and poignant stories,\" declared the judges."], "length": 2024, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**News Summary**\n\n**Media and Journalism**\n\n* The New Vineyard Gazette has been sold to Jerome and Nancy Kohlberg, with Richard Reston, son of former New York Times columnist James Reston, retiring as publisher after 22 years.\n* Peter Wallsten has been hired by the Washington Post, with a memo praising his \"instinct for discovering hidden gems and talking to the untalked-to.\"\n* The California Supreme Court has refused to review lower-court rulings ordering SF Weekly to pay $21 million in damages for trying to drive the rival Bay Guardian out of business.\n\n**Travel Journalism**\n\n* The 26th annual Lowell Thomas Travel Journalism Competition has announced its winners, with Afar Magazine, the Washington Post, and San Francisco Chronicle taking top honors as the best travel publications in North America.\n* Andrew McCarthy, a contributing editor at National Geographic Traveler, has been named the 2010 Travel Journalist of the Year for his freelance writing in various travel magazines.\n* Other winners include Outside magazine, which won seven individual awards, and BudgetTravel.com, which won gold for the best online travel journalism site.\n\n**International News**\n\n* Ireland is struggling with its third year of economic meltdown, with many people feeling hopeless and frightened about the future.\n* Despite the recession, the traditional Irish music scene in Doolin, County Clare, is thriving, with young and old musicians coming together to play and sing in the village's three pubs.\n* The music has become a source of comfort and community for the villagers, who are struggling to cope with the economic downturn.\n\n**Other News**\n\n* Andrea Mitchell, a journalist and commentator, has been awarded the Sol Taishoff Award for Excellence in Broadcast Journalism, with a $5,000 honorarium going to the University of Pennsylvania.\n* Rick Steves has won top honors for the best travel book with \"Travel as a Political Act,\" and Ben Mutzabaugh has won gold for the best travel blog, Today in the Sky, at USAToday.com."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Mass shootings in America aren't a gun problem, they're a mental health problem, according to Donald Trump. He told a rally in Nashville on Saturday that \"it's not the guns, it's the people. It's these sick people,\" the AP reports. \"Many states and many cities are closing their mental health facilities and closing them down, and they're closing them because they don't have the funding. And we have to start looking much stronger into mental health,\" he said. On NBC's Meet the Press on Sunday, Trump explained that gun control wouldn't help because \"they can be sick as hell and they’re geniuses in a certain way. They are going to be able to break the system.” He also told NBC that to avoid copycat killings, the media should have followed the local sheriff's advice and refused to name the gunman after the mass shooting in Oregon. At the Nashville rally, Trump spoke against \"gun-free zones,\" saying people \"would have been a hell of a lot better off\" if there had been \"teachers with guns in that room\" during the Oregon shooting, and said that since he has a handgun carry permit in New York, anyone who attacks him is in for a surprise, CNN reports. He brought up Charles Bronson's Death Wish, got the audience to chant the name of the movie, and said: \"Today you can’t make that movie because it’s not politically correct,\" reports the Guardian. (Jeb Bush took a lot of heat for saying \"Stuff happens\" after the Oregon shooting.)"], "length": 2079, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Donald Trump's Comments on Gun Control Spark Controversy**\n\nIn the aftermath of the Oregon community college shooting that left nine people dead, Republican presidential candidate Donald Trump has made several comments on gun control that have sparked controversy. Speaking at a rally in Franklin, Tennessee, Trump defended Second Amendment rights and argued that limiting access to firearms would not prevent mass shootings. He claimed that the Oregon shootings could have been limited if instructors or students had been armed, and that better mental health care would help curb future shootings.\n\nTrump also criticized \"gun-free zones\" and President Barack Obama's comments on the shootings as \"divisive.\" He noted that he has a handgun carry permit in New York and would respond if someone attacked him, saying he would emulate Charles Bronson in the vigilante film \"Death Wish.\"\n\nIn a rare sit-down interview with NBC's Meet the Press, Trump seemed to accept the inevitability of mass shootings in the US, saying \"no matter what you do, you're gonna have problems\" due to \"sick people\" who are \"geniuses in a certain way.\" He argued that gun laws have nothing to do with mass shootings and that mental illness is the root cause.\n\nTrump's comments on gun control have evolved significantly over the years, with him once backing a ban on assault weapons and longer waiting periods for gun purchases. However, he now touts the National Rifle Association line and has become a strong advocate for Second Amendment rights.\n\nTrump's comments on gun control have been met with criticism from some, who argue that he is not taking the issue seriously enough. Others have pointed out that his tax plan, which he defended in the interview, would cost $12tn over 10 years and would likely exacerbate the country's gun crisis.\n\n**Key Quotes**\n\n* \"I have to say, no matter what you do, you're gonna have problems. Because you have sick people. They happen to be intelligent. And, you know, they can be sick as hell and they're geniuses in a certain way.\"\n* \"The gun laws have nothing to do with this. This is mental illness.\"\n* \"I'm a very, very big Second Amendment person. This is about self-defense, plain and simple.\"\n* \"Today you can't make that movie [Death Wish] because it's not politically correct.\"\n\n**Related Stories**\n\n* Oregon shooting: Jeb Bush says'stuff happens' as left calls for gun control\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Kanye West is, apparently, $53 million in debt, but he should be able to pay that off in no time thanks to a GoFundMe campaign started by 36-year-old Minnesotan Jeremy Piatt, CNET reports. \"Recently, Kanye let us in on his personal struggle. He is 53 million dollars in debt and it doesn't look like he's going to get Mark Zuckerberg's help that he desperately needs,\" Piatt writes. \"We must open our hearts and wallets for Kanye today. Sure he is personally rich and can buy furs and houses for his family, but without our help, the true genius of Kanye West can't be realized.\" So far, after just a single day of fundraising, $603 has been raised for Kanye (along with a lot of comments like, \"How about you get a job?\"). Piatt tells Mashable that he's spoken to GoFundMe, and only Kanye or his team will be able to access any money raised. As for Zuckerberg—West specifically asked him, as well as Larry Page, to donate money to the Kanye cause when he went on his Twitter rant, and then went on to imply that he knows other tech giants who should help him. \"All you dudes in San Fran play rap music in your homes but never help the real artists…,\" he tweeted, followed by, \"All you guys had meetings with me and no one lifted a finger to help….\" The Guardian did a little investigation into how many \"tech guys\" Kanye actually knows, and found that he really does have something of an ongoing relationship with Facebook and Apple; has had meetings with investors in Silicon Valley and even had some of them at one of his parties; has had interactions with an Instagram co-founder, the Dropbox chief executive, a co-founder of Rap Genius, and Elon Musk; and is good friends with venture capitalist Ben Horowitz. (Here are some of the dumbest GoFundMe campaigns ever.)"], "length": 2906, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Kanye West's Financial Woes: A Summary of the News**\n\nKanye West, the renowned rapper, fashion designer, and celebrity, has been making headlines with his financial struggles. According to his own tweets, West is $53 million in debt and has been seeking help from tech billionaires Mark Zuckerberg and Larry Page. However, his pleas for financial assistance have been met with skepticism, and some have even created GoFundMe campaigns to help him pay off his debts.\n\nOne such campaign, \"Help Get Kanye Out of Debt,\" was created by Jeremy Piatt, a graphic designer from Minnesota. Piatt's campaign has raised a total of $50, despite his initial goal of raising $53 million. Piatt's motivation for creating the campaign was to support a fellow artist and to demonstrate the importance of funding the arts.\n\nHowever, not all GoFundMe campaigns created to help West have been successful. Robert Kulik's campaign, \"HELP PAY KANYE'S DEBT!\", was canceled after raising $0. Kulik's campaign was met with criticism, with some accusing him of trying to capitalize on West's financial struggles.\n\nWest's financial woes have also been the subject of ridicule, with some creating humorous GoFundMe campaigns to poke fun at his situation. One such campaign, \"The Fund To Send A Woman Around The World For Spiritual Healing,\" raised over $1,000 for a woman who wanted to travel the world to advance her spiritual journey.\n\nDespite his financial struggles, West has continued to make headlines with his music and fashion endeavors. His latest album, \"The Life of Pablo,\" was released on Tidal, but was pulled down shortly after its release due to widespread piracy. West has also been involved in a public feud with pop star Taylor Swift, who responded to his claims of being responsible for her fame with a gracious and inspiring speech at the Grammys.\n\nOverall, Kanye West's financial struggles have been a major news story in recent days, with many people weighing in on the issue. While some have created GoFundMe campaigns to help him pay off his debts, others have used the situation to poke fun at his situation."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Could it be? A Chinese ship searching for Flight 370 has picked up a ping in the southern Indian Ocean, reports CNN and the BBC. Both cite a report in China's Xinhua news agency. The pulse is 37.5 kHz, the frequency for the Malaysian plane's black-box data recorders, says the president of the beacon's manufacturer. All the reports caution that it could turn out to be yet another false hope, however. \"This could be a variety of things,\" says one oceanographer, noting that the frequency is used by lots of instruments. For the record, the Chinese ship detected the signal at 25 degrees south latitude and 101 degrees east longitude, says the state-run Xinhua. \"It is yet to be established whether it is related to the missing jet,\" the report says. The development comes as dozens of ships and planes intensify their sweeps in the search zone, given the finite battery life of the data recorders."], "length": 3925, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Search for Malaysia Airlines Flight 370 Intensifies**\n\nThe search for Malaysia Airlines Flight 370, which disappeared on March 8 with 239 people on board, has entered its most intensive phase as authorities scramble to locate the missing plane. A Chinese ship, Haixun 01, has picked up a pulse signal in the southern Indian Ocean, which is consistent with the frequency emitted by the flight recorders. However, there is no confirmation yet that the signal is linked to MH370.\n\nThe signal was detected on Saturday at 25 degrees south latitude and 101 degrees east longitude, and was heard by three people on board the Chinese ship. The Australian agency co-ordinating the search, led by Angus Houston, has confirmed that the signal is consistent with the aircraft black box, but cautioned that there is no confirmation at this stage that the signals and objects are related to the missing aircraft.\n\nThe search area, which covers about 217,000 sq km (84,000 sq miles), is being searched by dozens of ships and planes, including Australian naval vessel Ocean Shield, which is using a \"towed pinger locator\" from the US Navy. The battery-powered signal emitted by the data recorders is fading, and authorities are racing against time to locate the plane before the batteries die.\n\nThe search has been ongoing for nearly a month, with many promising leads turning out to be false. However, experts believe that if the signal is confirmed to be from the black box, the possibility of recovering the plane or at least the black boxes becomes almost certain.\n\nThe search is being co-ordinated by a new investigation team, which includes members from Australia, China, the US, the UK, and France. The team is working to analyze the satellite data and determine the most likely location of the plane.\n\nWhile the news of the pulse signal has raised hopes that the search may be nearing a breakthrough, experts are cautioning that it is still too early to confirm anything. The search will continue in the coming days, with a focus on verifying the signal and locating the missing plane.\n\n**Key Developments:**\n\n* A Chinese ship, Haixun 01, has picked up a pulse signal in the southern Indian Ocean, consistent with the frequency emitted by the flight recorders.\n* The signal was detected on Saturday at 25 degrees south latitude and 101 degrees east longitude.\n* The Australian agency co-ordinating the search has confirmed that the signal is consistent with the aircraft black box, but cautioned that there is no confirmation at this stage"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["President Trump escalated his Twitter war with CNN on Sunday, and in the process sparked more concern that he is advocating the use of violence against members of the media, the Hill reports. The president tweeted a modified video of a 2007 WWE wrestling match he took part in, obscuring his opponent's head with the CNN logo, which he proceeds to beat with his fists. Trump tweeted the video with the hashtags \"#FraudNewsCNN\" and \"#FNN.\" A CNN spokesman called it \"a sad day when the president of the United States encourages violence against reporters.\" But on ABC News, Homeland Security adviser Tom Bossert suggested CNN and other critics were overreacting. \"No one would perceive that as a threat,\" he said, adding that Trump is \"beaten up in a way on cable platforms that he has a right to respond to.\" The new video comes just a few days after Trump posted two tweets in which he talked about MSNBC's Morning Joe co-host Mika Brzezinski \"bleeding badly from a face-lift.\" Those tweets sparked condemnation from all over the political spectrum, with even Trump's fellow Republicans calling them unpresidential. On Saturday, however, Trump took to Twitter once again to address the backlash, writing, \"My use of social media is not Presidential – it's MODERN DAY presidential,\" Politico reports. At a press conference Thursday, Deputy White House Press Secretary Sarah Huckabee Sanders said Trump \"no way, form or fashion has ever promoted or encouraged violence,\" the Washington Post reports. \"In fact quite the contrary.\""], "length": 1036, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**President Trump's Twitter Controversy Continues**\n\nPresident Donald Trump's Twitter habits have sparked controversy once again, with his latest tweets drawing criticism from lawmakers and media personalities. The president's use of social media has been a topic of debate since his inauguration, with some praising his ability to connect directly with the American people and others condemning his incendiary language.\n\nIn a recent tweet, Trump body-slammed and punched a person with a CNN logo superimposed on their head, sparking concerns that the tweet was a threat to the media. Homeland Security Adviser Tom Bossert defended the tweet, saying that it was not a threat and that Trump was simply responding to unfair treatment by the media. However, CNN contributor Ana Navarro criticized Bossert's defense, calling it \"disappointing\" and saying that the tweet was an \"incitement to violence.\"\n\nTrump has continued to defend his use of social media, tweeting that it is \"modern day presidential\" and that he won the 2016 election with the help of social media. However, some Republicans have expressed concerns about his Twitter habit, with Nebraska Sen. Ben Sasse saying that his tweets are \"beneath the dignity\" of the presidential office and Alaska Sen. Lisa Murkowski asking if he wants to be remembered for his tweets or his accomplishments.\n\nTrump's Twitter controversy is not new, with his incendiary tweets sparking controversy during the 2016 presidential campaign. In a February 2016 rally, Trump promised to become \"so presidential\" that people would be bored, but so far, his Twitter habits have shown no signs of changing.\n\nThe controversy surrounding Trump's Twitter use has also raised questions about the role of social media in politics and the potential consequences of using it to attack opponents. While Trump's supporters see his Twitter use as a way to connect with the American people, critics see it as a way to bully and intimidate his opponents.\n\nIn related news, Twitter has announced that users can add location information to their tweets, which can be deleted at any time. However, this development has not addressed the underlying controversy surrounding Trump's Twitter use.\n\nOverall, President Trump's Twitter controversy continues to be a major issue in American politics, with no signs of abating anytime soon."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Apple presented its new smartphone Wednesday, and nestled in with the iPhone 7's much-ballyhooed features was the disclosure that the company is getting rid of the phone's headphone jack—news that wasn't exactly met with universal acclaim, or much acclaim at all. Chris Taylor is one detractor of the headphone ditch, writing for Mashable that, in nearly 20 years of attending Apple product launches, he's \"never heard anything as ridiculous emanate from that stage as I did Wednesday.\" Apple marketing head Phil Schiller used the word \"courage\" to describe Apple's innovation, but that's not how Taylor sees it. \"Courage is marching across the Edmund Pettus bridge in Selma in 1965 [or] facing down a tank in Tiananmen Square,\" he writes. \"Courage, by definition, involves doing something that makes you afraid.\" Apparently Taylor doesn't think Apple was quivering in its workboots when it came up with this plan. He's got plenty of other words instead of \"courage\" in mind, including \"hubris,\" \"arrogance,\" \"greed,\" and his personal favorite, \"stupidity,\" which he attributes to Apple for taking \"one step forward, two steps back\" both technologically (\"wired almost always sounds better than wireless\") and logistically (no more plugging in other peripherals like credit card readers, for example). Meanwhile, although much of the other feedback online is similarly cranky about the headphone jack's demise, Quartz notes that Apple is actually late, not early, in making this move: A handful of Chinese smartphone brands have been fiddling around with the idea for years, and some have already sent these phones, sans jacks, to market."], "length": 2155, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Apple's iPhone 7: A Step Backwards in Audio Technology**\n\nAt the recent Apple keynote event, the company unveiled its latest iPhone 7, which has sparked controversy among tech enthusiasts and audiophiles. The iPhone 7 has done away with the traditional 3.5mm headphone jack, a move that has been met with widespread criticism.\n\nAccording to Apple's marketing chief, Phil Schiller, the decision to remove the headphone jack was a matter of \"courage.\" However, many have ridiculed this claim, pointing out that courage involves taking risks and facing challenges, not simply abandoning a widely used and successful technology.\n\nThe removal of the headphone jack has significant implications for users, who will now need to use a proprietary Lightning cable or wireless headphones to listen to music. This move has been seen as a step backwards in audio technology, as wired headphones are generally considered to sound better than wireless ones.\n\nThe cost of this change is also a concern, as users will need to purchase a $9 aux-to-Lightning cable dongle or a pair of wireless headphones, such as Apple's AirPods, which cost $159. This has led to accusations of Apple being greedy and prioritizing profits over user convenience.\n\nThe decision to remove the headphone jack has also raised questions about the company's commitment to innovation and user experience. Apple's mantra of \"it just works\" is no longer applicable, as users will need to navigate a complex system of adapters and wireless headphones to get the most out of their device.\n\nThe removal of the headphone jack is also seen as a move to further entrench Apple's ecosystem, making it more difficult for users to switch to other devices or use non-Apple accessories. This has led to accusations that Apple is building a \"walled garden\" that is designed to keep users locked in.\n\nOverall, the decision to remove the headphone jack from the iPhone 7 has been met with widespread criticism and disappointment. It remains to be seen how users will adapt to this change and whether Apple will reconsider its decision in the future."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The \"angst\" travelers feel at overcrowded gates may soon be eased at United Airlines terminals, an exec for the airline says. That's because United just began a new initiative designed to relieve that particular \"passenger pain point,\" as CNBC puts it, by changing up its departure protocol: The number of boarding lines has been cut from five to two, and overeager customers are now asked to stay out of the boarding area until their boarding group (one of six in total, including pre-boarders) is announced. \"It's too congested,\" a UA rep says of the current procedure, which often results in arriving fliers having to push their way through throngs of departing ones. Forbes notes customers can also opt in to receive push notifications via the mobile app, meaning they can hang out in nearby restaurants or shops until they receive an alert on their cellphones that it's time to board. Over the past year, United test-drove a variety of boarding options on about 12,000 flights, ending up with this system, which is similar to the way American, Delta, and Southwest queue up. United has been using the procedure at LAX since winter and will now implement it at all locations, per USA Today. It's also spreading passengers around more equitably within its six boarding groups, which includes pre-boarders (e.g., families with young kids, service members), first-class passengers and frequent fliers in groups 1 and 2, and then everyone else in groups 3, 4, and 5. \"The boarding process was one of the top areas customers told us they wanted improved,\" another UA rep says in a statement, noting customer and employee feedback guided the airline on the enhancements. (United had some \"unfortunate dog incidents\" earlier this year.)"], "length": 3434, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**United Airlines Introduces New Boarding Process and Partners with Avianca and Copa for Joint Business Agreement**\n\nUnited Airlines has introduced a new boarding process aimed at reducing congestion and speeding up passenger loading. The new process, which has been in the works for months, involves using only two boarding lanes instead of five, with elite and priority passengers boarding through lane 1 and customers in groups 3, 4, and 5 boarding through lane 2. The airline has also launched push notifications for passengers who have the mobile app, allowing them to receive a notification when boarding starts.\n\nThe new boarding process is part of a broader effort by United to improve the passenger experience. The airline has also introduced a new joint business agreement (JBA) with Avianca and Copa, which will provide customers with more choices and benefits, including integrated, seamless service in over 12,000 city pairs, new nonstop routes, and additional flights on existing routes.\n\nThe JBA is expected to drive significant traffic growth at major gateway cities and provide customers with expanded codeshare flight options, competitive fares, and a more streamlined travel experience. The agreement is subject to regulatory approval and is expected to be implemented in the near future.\n\nUnited's new boarding process has been tested at several airports, including Los Angeles International Airport, and will now be rolled out to all United locations. The airline has also introduced a new two-lane, color-coded boarding process, with pre-boarding available to top-tier frequent flyers and active military personnel.\n\nThe new boarding process is designed to reduce congestion and stress at the gate, and to provide a more efficient and streamlined experience for passengers. United has also introduced a new system for boarding, which involves calling passengers in groups and using a color-coded system to indicate which lane to board from.\n\nOverall, United's new boarding process and joint business agreement with Avianca and Copa are aimed at improving the passenger experience and providing customers with more choices and benefits. The new boarding process is expected to reduce congestion and stress at the gate, and to provide a more efficient and streamlined experience for passengers."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Frightened North Carolinians fleeing Irene are jamming highways as the East Coast steels for what could be the biggest hurricane in decades. At least seven states have now declared a state of emergency, and hundreds of flights are being canceled. Gas stations are running out of fuel and ATMs have been emptied of cash as Americans hit the road for a bumper-to-bumper trip out of Irene's angry path. The Category 3 storm is expected to hit the Southeast sometime tomorrow, and hundreds of thousands of residents have been ordered out of their homes in three North Carolina counties. Norfolk, Va., is also ordering residents to evacuate, and Washington, DC, has been forced to postpone Sunday's planned MLK Memorial dedication. \"This is a very, very serious situation,\" a spokeswoman for Dare County told ABC News. \"We have not seen anything like this in the lifetimes of most our residents. Once the storm hits, it will be very difficult to respond to distress calls.\" Irene is some 700 miles wide now and moving at a slow 12mph, which means it can wreak extensive damage in a region over a long period of time; it could cause up to $13.9 billion in damage on the East Coast. The storm is expected to barrel into New York City on Sunday, packing winds of up to 90mph. New Jersey communities are already being evacuated, and hundreds of street fairs have been canceled and elder care facilities and hospitals in low-lying area will be evacuated in New York today. NYC Mayor Michael Bloomberg says residents in low-lying parts of the city should get out today as the transit system is planned to be shut down tomorrow."], "length": 4070, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Hurricane Irene Brings Widespread Destruction and Disruption to the Eastern Seaboard**\n\nHurricane Irene, a powerful Category 3 storm, is barreling its way up the Eastern Seaboard, bringing with it destructive winds, heavy rainfall, and a significant storm surge. The storm has already caused widespread disruption and destruction in the Bahamas, where it made landfall on Thursday, and is expected to make landfall in North Carolina's Outer Banks on Saturday.\n\n**Evacuations and Emergency Declarations**\n\nIn preparation for the storm, several states have declared emergencies, including North Carolina, Virginia, Maryland, New Jersey, New York, Delaware, and Connecticut. Thousands of people have been evacuated from coastal areas, with up to 200,000 tourists and residents affected by evacuation orders in North Carolina alone. Low-lying hospitals and nursing homes in New York have been ordered to evacuate, and the city's mass transit system may be suspended beginning Saturday.\n\n**Projected Path and Impact**\n\nThe National Hurricane Center has warned of tidal surges of 5 to 10 feet high in North Carolina, accompanied by \"destructive and life-threatening waves.\" Projections show Irene making landfall between Morehead City, N.C., and Cape Hatteras before pushing north, potentially inundating the state's coastal areas with 6 to 10 inches of rain, and up to 15 inches in some locations. The storm is expected to bring severe road flooding and widespread electrical outages, especially in the Northeast, where the ground is saturated from recent rains.\n\n**Damage and Economic Impact**\n\nThe storm is expected to cause significant damage and economic losses, with estimates ranging from $13.9 billion in insured losses to $20 billion in overall economic losses. The storm's projected path has also raised concerns about the potential for hurricane-force winds in New York City, with Mayor Michael Bloomberg warning of winds of 60 mph or more and the possibility of the storm being \"as strong as a Category 2 on Long Island.\"\n\n**Preparations and Response**\n\nIn response to the storm, the Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA) has established depots for emergency supplies, including food, water, generators, and baby formula, in several states. The U.S. Navy has also moved 64 ships away from Norfolk, Virginia, to keep them from being damaged by the storm. President Obama has declared North Carolina an emergency, expediting federal help, and the National Weather Service has issued a hurricane warning for the entire North Carolina coast to the Virginia border.\n\n**"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A Florida police chief got arrested Friday for allegedly soliciting a prostitute and was promptly fired just 10 months into his tenure, NBC Miami reports. According to an arrest report, Miami Gardens Police Chief Stephen Johnson called a number in a \"two-girl special\" prostitution ad and negotiated a $100 deal for two women for 30 minutes. Only problem: The woman he allegedly paid at a motel was really a sheriff's detective. \"The stress overwhelmed me, and I made a very bad decision,\" Johnson tells the Miami Herald in his apology. He was hired last May to repair community relations after former top cop Matthew Boyd resigned amid allegations of harassment and illegal tactics. \"It was one of the most painful things I've ever heard as a manager,\" says the city manager who fired Stephens, the Sun-Sentinel reports. \"It's like a punch in the stomach.\""], "length": 2365, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Miami Gardens Police Chief Stephen Johnson Arrested for Soliciting Prostitute**\n\nMiami Gardens Police Chief Stephen Johnson, 53, was arrested on Friday, February 27, 2015, for soliciting a prostitute in a sting operation conducted by the Broward Sheriff's Office. Johnson, who was hired as police chief in May 2014, was immediately fired from his position after his arrest.\n\nAccording to the arrest report, Johnson called a number on backpage.com and arranged to pay $100 for a 30-minute session with two prostitutes. When he arrived at the hotel room, he was arrested and found to have two condoms in his pocket. The entire transaction was captured on video.\n\nJohnson's arrest is the latest in a series of controversies surrounding the Miami Gardens Police Department. In January, the department was criticized for the fatal shooting of 25-year-old Lavall Hall, who was shot by police after allegedly attacking officers with a broom handle. Johnson had supported his officers, saying they \"did the best they could.\"\n\nJohnson's arrest has also raised questions about his judgment and leadership. He had been dealing with the aftermath of the Hall shooting and had made a vague reference to a stressful incident involving a 10-year-old girl who was struck by an unmarked police car.\n\nThe city of Miami Gardens has released a statement saying that Johnson's actions were unacceptable and that the department will move forward with a new interim chief, Assistant Police Chief Antonio Brooklen. The city manager, Cameron Benson, said that Johnson's arrest was a setback for the department, but that the city will continue to move forward and improve the reputation of the police department.\n\nJohnson's arrest has also raised questions about his personal life. He is listed as a pastor at Bethel House of God Church in Hallandale Beach, and his arrest has sparked criticism from the community.\n\nThe Broward Sheriff's Office has released a statement saying that Johnson's arrest was the result of an undercover sting operation, and that he was arrested after he paid $100 for a 30-minute session with two prostitutes. The sheriff's office has also released a video of the arrest, which shows Johnson arriving at the hotel room and being arrested.\n\nOverall, Johnson's arrest has sparked widespread criticism and raised questions about his leadership and judgment. The city of Miami Gardens will continue to move forward with a new interim chief, and the police department will work to rebuild its reputation in the community."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["At least eight people were arrested yesterday in Detroit for protesting water shutoffs in a city where almost 40% of residents live below the poverty line. Dozens picketed in front of a facility affiliated with the city's Water and Sewerage Department to try to stop what activists call a “human rights violation,” reports Al Jazeera America. Police tell MLive that the individuals charged with misdemeanor disorderly conduct range in age from 58 to 70 and include members of the clergy. (Watch video of the protest and arrests here.) The Rev. Bill Wylie-Kellermann, one of the arrested protesters, tells the Detroit Free Press that police tried to \"move us forcibly, and we sat down. … We [were] here to appeal to the workers to stop shutting off the water.\" The coordinator of a local activist group says \"police officers should be arresting rapists and murderers instead of arresting peaceful people.\" A Detroit Police rep counters that protesters still have to follow rules: \"You cannot impede pedestrian or vehicle traffic.\" The Free Press counts 7,210 shutoffs in June, up from 7,556 in April and May combined, as part of what Al Jazeera reports is an effort to recover $175 million in unpaid bills. As of March, nearly half of the city's 323,900 DWSD accounts were delinquent; activists have asked the UN to intervene."], "length": 1911, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Detroit Water Crisis Escalates: Protests and Arrests as City Continues to Shut Off Water to Thousands**\n\nThe city of Detroit's water crisis has reached a boiling point, with protests and arrests escalating as the city continues to shut off water to thousands of residents who are behind on their bills. The Detroit Water and Sewerage Department (DWSD) has been aggressively pursuing delinquent accounts, with over 90,000 customers, including both residential and commercial properties, owing a total of $175 million in outstanding bills.\n\nActivists have been protesting the shut-offs, calling them a human rights violation, and have been arrested for civil disobedience. On Thursday, eight people, including religious leaders, were arrested for misdemeanor disorderly conduct during a protest outside a company contracted by the DWSD to shut off water to delinquent customers. Earlier in the week, nine activists were arrested and charged with disorderly conduct when they temporarily blocked trucks from leaving the company's parking lot.\n\nThe protests have gained national attention, with Detroit congressman John Conyers sending letters to President Barack Obama and other officials requesting immediate federal action to address the crisis. The DWSD has announced a $1 million fund to help low-income customers avoid shutoffs, but activists say it is not enough to address the scale of the problem.\n\nThe city's water prices are among the highest in the country, with the average household bill now averaging $75, almost double the national average. The DWSD has been accused of targeting low-income residents, while ignoring large commercial entities that owe significant amounts of money. A list of 40 commercial and industrial accounts with the highest delinquent balances was released by the DWSD, including a golf course that owes $437,714 and the State of Michigan, which owes $70,246.\n\nThe United Nations has called the water shutoffs a violation of human rights, and a coalition of welfare rights organizations has appealed to the UN to have service restored to customers and to prevent more shutoffs. The DWSD has maintained that it needs to shut off water to recoup outstanding debts, but activists say the city should work out a solution with poor residents instead of leaving them without access to water.\n\nAs the crisis continues to escalate, protests are expected to grow in size and intensity, with activists vowing to take direct action to stop the shut-offs. The city's water crisis has become a national issue, with many calling for immediate action to address the problem and ensure that all residents have access to clean and affordable water."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["There were two men at the top of this year's Simon Wiesenthal Center most-wanted Nazi war criminal list—and there is now no chance either one will face a court. No. 2 on the list, Ukrainian-born Vladimir Katriuk, has died in Quebec at the age of 93, the Toronto Star reports. Katriuk, who moved to Canada in 1951 and worked as a beekeeper for more than 50 years, was accused of being a \"particularly active participant\" in a massacre in what is now Belarus in 1943, when he was a member of an SS battalion that allegedly carried out many atrocities, the Guardian reports. Russia charged Katriuk with genocide last month, but Canadian authorities refused a request to send him to Moscow, citing Russia's actions in Ukraine, reports the Globe and Mail. Katriuk had long been under suspicion, but the wheels of justice did not move quickly: In 1999, Canadian authorities concluded he had entered the country under false pretenses, but in 2007 a decision to cancel his citizenship was overturned due to lack of evidence, the Star reports. The man at the top of the most-wanted list is also 93 years old. Gerhard Sommer is still alive in a Hamburg-area nursing home, but German prosecutors say they've dropped their investigation because he suffers from dementia and isn't fit to stand trial, the AP reports. In 2005, he was one of 10 former SS officers an Italian court found guilty of taking part in a massacre of 560 civilians in 1944, but German law didn't permit his extradition."], "length": 2459, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Nazi War Criminals: Death of Vladimir Katriuk and Ongoing Investigations**\n\nVladimir Katriuk, a 93-year-old Canadian resident and former member of a Ukrainian battalion of the Waffen SS, has died after a long illness. Katriuk was the second most wanted man on the Simon Wiesenthal Centre's list of Nazi war criminals and was accused of participating in a village massacre in Belarus during World War II. His death has sparked a mix of reactions from various groups, with some calling for him to be held accountable for his alleged war crimes and others defending him as a victim of unfair persecution.\n\nKatriuk's death comes as Russia was preparing to bring a criminal case against him in an effort to embarrass Canada over its stance on Ukraine. The Russian government had called on Canada to extradite Katriuk to Moscow to face trial for alleged war crimes. However, Canada had refused to comply, citing its opposition to Russia's annexation of Crimea and interference in Ukraine.\n\nThe Canadian government has maintained that it will not recognize Moscow's annexation of Crimea and will not extradite Katriuk or any other individual accused of war crimes. This stance has led to tensions between Canada and Russia, with Moscow accusing Canada of interfering in its internal affairs.\n\nKatriuk's case has been the subject of controversy for many years, with some arguing that he was a willing participant in Nazi atrocities and others claiming that he was forced to join the SS battalion. In 1999, the Federal Court ruled that Katriuk obtained Canadian citizenship under false pretenses, but found no evidence that he committed atrocities. In 2007, the Harper cabinet decided not to revoke his citizenship.\n\nMeanwhile, another alleged Nazi war criminal, Gerhard Sommer, a 93-year-old former SS officer, has been cleared of charges due to his advanced dementia. Sommer was suspected of participating in a wartime massacre of civilians in Italy, but German prosecutors have shelved their investigation due to his inability to stand trial.\n\nThe Centre for Israel and Jewish Affairs has called on the Canadian government to take action against Katriuk, while the Ukrainian Canadian Congress has urged Ottawa to ignore Russia's demands and leave Katriuk alone. The case highlights the ongoing debate over how to deal with alleged Nazi war criminals and the complex relationships between Canada, Russia, and Ukraine."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Google's parent company appears to be satisfied with how Sundar Pichai has performed during his four months as Google CEO, judging by the gargantuan $199 million equity award revealed in a securities filing. Alphabet stated that Pichai was awarded 273,328 Class C Google stock units on Feb. 3, bringing his holdings to around $650 million, reports the Wall Street Journal. The award makes Pichai the highest-paid CEO in America, according to the Guardian, which notes that Pichai's fortune is dwarfed by those of Google founders Larry Page and Sergey Brin, who are worth around $34 billion each, and by that of previous CEO Eric Schmidt, who's sitting on around $3 billion. (A former Google employee was awarded exactly $6,006.13.)"], "length": 773, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Google CEO Sundar Pichai Receives $199 Million Equity Award**\n\nGoogle's CEO Sundar Pichai has been granted an equity award valued at nearly $200 million, making him one of the world's highest-paid executives. The award, which consists of 273,328 Class C Google stock units, was valued at $199 million at the time of the grant on February 3. However, subsequent declines in Alphabet shares have reduced the current value to around $182 million.\n\nThe new award will vest in quarterly increments over the next four years as long as Pichai remains employed by Alphabet. This grant has lifted the value of Pichai's equity stake in Alphabet to over $600 million, although it still falls short of the stakes held by co-founders Larry Page and Sergey Brin, and Eric Schmidt, Alphabet's executive chairman.\n\nPichai's award is one of the largest by a U.S. company, surpassing the $376 million grant awarded to Apple's Tim Cook in 2011. The award has also sparked controversy, with critics pointing out that Google's effective tax rate is between 2% and 3%, compared to the UK's 20% headline corporation tax rate.\n\nIn addition to Pichai's award, other Google executives have also received significant paydays. Alphabet's chief financial officer, Ruth Porat, was handed shares worth around $38 million, on top of a $30 million signing bonus she received last year. Diane Greene, head of Google's cloud computing business, was awarded shares worth $42.8 million, in addition to $148 million worth of shares she was granted last year following Google's takeover of Bebop Technologies.\n\nPichai's award has brought his total holdings in Alphabet to around $650 million, which is still a fraction of the net worth of Google's founders. Despite this, Pichai has celebrated his award by watching the Super Bowl and meeting NFL legend Ronnie Lott."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["After a social media campaign—and an avalanche of bad publicity—a drug company has done a U-turn and agreed to give a dying 7-year-old boy an experimental drug that could save his life. The FDA allows \"compassionate use\"of unapproved drugs in such cases, but the Chimerix pharmaceutical company had insisted that giving the drug to Josh Hardy, who developed a bone marrow disorder after cancer treatment, would delay efforts to get it to market, CNN reports. Chimerix president Kenneth Moch says Josh will be the first patient in a pilot trial for the drug. \"Being unable to fulfill requests for compassionate use is excruciating, and not a decision any one of us ever wants to have to make,\" says Moch, who tells the Fredericksburg Lance-Star that he and his employees have received death threats amid a flood of pleas to help the Virginia boy. Josh is expected to receive the drug within 48 hours, though his mother fears he is running out of time. \"Even though he is frail, he has a very strong will about him,\" she tells Fox News. \"But things just keep stacking against him, and we just want to do everything we can to give him the opportunity to make a full recovery.\""], "length": 1614, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Josh Hardy Receives Lifesaving Drug in Rare Victory for Compassionate Use**\n\nIn a heartwarming turn of events, 7-year-old Josh Hardy, a first-grader from Fredericksburg, Virginia, will receive a potentially lifesaving drug after a long and emotional battle with pharmaceutical company Chimerix. Josh, who is fighting for his life at St. Jude Children's Research Hospital in Memphis, Tennessee, was diagnosed with a rare form of kidney cancer at 9 months old and has undergone multiple treatments, including a bone marrow transplant. However, he developed an adenovirus infection, which has left him in critical condition.\n\nAfter days of pleading with Chimerix executives, Josh's parents, Todd and Aimee Hardy, received the news they had been praying for: the company would provide the experimental drug, brincidofovir, for Josh to participate in a new clinical trial. The trial, which will begin on Wednesday, will involve 20 patients, with Josh being the first recipient.\n\nChimerix president Kenneth Moch said that the company had been working aggressively with the FDA to expedite the trial and make the drug available to patients in need. Moch praised the Hardy family for bringing attention to the impact of adenovirus infections and the need for additional clinical development to assess the drug's potential.\n\nThe news has sparked a wave of support for Josh and his family, with thousands of people signing online petitions, sending letters to Chimerix, and participating in social media campaigns using the hashtag #savejosh. Local organizations and charities have also joined in to support the Hardys.\n\nWhile the FDA has approved compassionate use arrangements for patients in need, pharmaceutical companies often deny requests, citing the need to prioritize getting the drug to market. However, in this case, Chimerix's decision to provide the drug to Josh has been hailed as a victory for compassionate use and a testament to the power of public pressure and advocacy.\n\nAs Josh's family and supporters continue to rally around him, the community remains hopeful that the experimental drug will help him recover from his illness."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The latest disturbing Hollywood sexual misconduct allegation involves Tom Sizemore, an actor who has already faced drug and domestic violence charges. Multiple cast and crew members tell the Hollywood Reporter that Sizemore, best known for roles in war films including Black Hawk Down, was kicked off a Utah movie set in 2003 for allegedly touching an 11-year-old girl's genitals. They say the day after a Born Killers scene in which the girl sat on Sizemore's lap, she told her parents that he had touched her inappropriately, possibly putting his finger inside her. \"At one point her eyes got just huge, like she could've vomited. I was watching her,\" co-star Robyn Adamson says. \"Later, when I was told about what happened, I knew exactly what it was.\" \"There was never any doubt. He was this guy who was already known for making inappropriate comments, being drunk, being high,\" says production assistant Roi Maufas. \"Then this happens. Guys reached for hammers,\" he says. Producers say Sizemore, who was ordered to leave the set, denied the allegations. Crew members say the girl's parents spoke to police, but ended up not pressing charges, possibly out of fear of ruining her film career. She is now 26 and says she is considering legal action against both Sizemore and her parents. Sizemore, 55, was dropped by his management firm soon after the incident. Former manager Charles Lago tells USA Today that he is \"not surprised\" by the allegations. \"He's the most abhorrent person I've ever met in my life,\" Lago says."], "length": 2624, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Actor Tom Sizemore Accused of Molesting 11-Year-Old Actress on Movie Set in 2003**\n\nActor Tom Sizemore, known for his roles in films like \"Saving Private Ryan\" and \"Black Hawk Down,\" has been accused of molesting an 11-year-old actress on the set of a 2003 film called \"Born Killers\" (also known as \"Piggy Banks\"). The alleged incident occurred during a photo shoot for the film, where Sizemore, then 42, allegedly touched the girl's genitals while she was seated on his lap.\n\nAccording to multiple sources, including a dozen cast and crew members, Sizemore was sent home from the Utah set after the girl told her mother about the incident. However, months later, he returned for reshoots in Malibu after the girl's parents declined to press charges.\n\nThe incident has never been publicly reported until now, but it has come to light in the wake of the Harvey Weinstein scandal, which has sparked a wave of similar allegations against Hollywood stars. Sizemore's agent has declined to comment on the allegations, while his former manager has spoken out against him, saying he was \"not surprised\" by the allegations and blaming Sizemore's behavior on his frequent drug use.\n\nThe girl, now 26, has recently hired a lawyer to explore legal action against Sizemore and her parents. Sizemore has denied the allegations, but his management firm and talent agency dropped him shortly after the incident.\n\nThe film's producers have spoken out about the incident, saying they removed Sizemore from the set as soon as they heard about the allegations and encouraged the girl's parents to contact law enforcement. However, they ultimately decided to complete the film with Sizemore, citing a \"fiduciary responsibility\" to finish the project.\n\nSizemore has a history of alleged aggressive behavior toward women, including a conviction for physically abusing and harassing his ex-girlfriend, Heidi Fleiss, in 2003. He has also struggled with drug addiction and has been arrested multiple times for suspected battery and domestic abuse.\n\nThe allegations against Sizemore have sparked outrage in the film industry, with many calling for greater accountability and transparency in the wake of the Weinstein scandal."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Cruz Riojas came from a troubled home: He was reportedly beaten by his stepfather, lived in a decrepit one-room lean-to with six other family members, and wore the same clothes to school every day, according to the Lubbock Avalanche-Journal. He also caused trouble in school and was known as a “holy terror,” says Linda Hooper, a teacher who first encountered Cruz in the early ‘80s, when he was 12 years old. But then an amazing thing happened: He started spending more time with Hooper, who would give him tasks to do around the classroom. He eventually began coming for visits at her home, where she lived with her husband and four daughters, often running the nearly eight miles between their two houses to get away from his stepfather. After an incident with his stepfather in 1983, Cruz’s mother asked if he could stay with the Hoopers for a few days till everything blew over. \"He never left\" after that, Hooper says. \"I had him from then on.\" He became a part of the Hooper family, taking on a paper route to make money and doing better in school, placing out of special education classes by the time he graduated. More than a decade later, he was still part of the Hooper family—but he wanted to make it official by asking the Hoopers to adopt him right before his 30th birthday. “He said he wanted to honor us, and it was a big honor,\" Hooper tells Today. \"I’ve always known he loved me, but this really showed he loved us.” The adoption went through in 1998, but Cruz \"was my son from the day I kept him,” Hooper says. The family is just now telling their story for the first time. (Click for the full article, or read the story of a police officer who found a baby alive at a murder scene—and adopted her 30 years later.)"], "length": 3066, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**A Heartwarming Story of Love and Adoption**\n\nIn a remarkable tale of transformation, a former \"classroom terror\" has found a loving home and a new family. Cruz Riojas, now 45, was a troubled student in the 1980s who was known for his violent outbursts and behavioral problems. However, with the help of his teacher, Linda Hooper, Cruz's life took a dramatic turn.\n\nLinda, a veteran elementary teacher, had been warned about Cruz's reputation, but she refused to give up on him. Despite his initial resistance, Cruz began to open up to Linda, and she started to see a different side of him. She took him under her wing, giving him small jobs and eventually hiring him to do odd tasks at her home.\n\nAs Cruz spent more time with Linda and her husband, Gale, a retired firefighter, he began to thrive. He started to develop a sense of self-worth and respect for authority, which was a stark contrast to his tumultuous home life. Cruz's stepfather had been physically abusive, and he had been forced to live in squalor with his mother and five half-siblings.\n\nIn 1982, Cruz's mother asked Linda to take him in temporarily after his stepfather had beaten him. However, Cruz never left Linda's home, and she eventually adopted him in 1998, when he was 30 years old. The adoption was a formal recognition of the love and bond that had grown between Cruz and the Hoopers.\n\nToday, Cruz is a successful man with a loving family of his own. He and his wife, Anel, have two children, and he is proud to teach them about their Mexican roots and the Hooper family traditions. Cruz credits Linda and Gale with saving him from a life of poverty and abuse, and he is grateful for the love and support they have given him over the years.\n\nLinda, who is now 68, says that Cruz was her son from the day she kept him. She is proud of the man he has become and is grateful for the opportunity to have been a part of his life. The story of Cruz and the Hoopers is a testament to the power of love and adoption, and it serves as a reminder that everyone deserves a second chance at a happy and fulfilling life."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Donald Trump's campaign chief may have closer—and more illegal—ties to pro-Russia interests in Ukraine than he has let on, according to a New York Times report on secret ledgers that anti-corruption investigators have uncovered in Kiev. Investigators say the ledgers list $12.7 million in payments to Paul Manafort from the pro-Russia Party of Regions between 2007 and 2012, when he worked as a consultant for the party. It isn't clear whether Manafort actually received the cash, which investigators say is linked to a network that also bribed election officials. Manafort's lawyer says his client never received the payments and any suspicions are \"probably heavily politically tinged.\" In other coverage: The Hill reports that there doesn't appear to be any love lost between Manafort and former Trump campaign manager Corey Lewandowski: Lewandowski, who was fired after clashing with Manafort, tweeted a link to the NYT Ukraine story minutes after it first appeared. The AP reports that Trump plans to deliver a major foreign policy speech in Ohio Monday that will focus on \"realism,\" with policies including destroying ISIS without engaging in \"nation-building\"—and on new ideological tests for people seeking to enter the US. Politico reports that Trump targeted the media in a series of tweets Sunday, claiming that he would be beating Hillary Clinton by 20% if the \"disgusting\" media covered him honestly. \"I am not only fighting Crooked Hillary, I am fighting the dishonest and corrupt media and her government protection process,\" he tweeted. \"It is not 'freedom of the press' when newspapers and others are allowed to say and write whatever they want even if it is completely false!\" he added. The Wall Street Journal editorial board warned Sunday that Trump's \"window for a turnaround\" is closing and his GOP supporters now face a \"moment of truth\" before he is written off as a lost cause. \"The tragedy is that this is happening in a year when Republicans should win,\" they write."], "length": 2028, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Summary of Recent News**\n\n**Trump's Campaign and Media Feud**\n\nDonald Trump has launched a series of attacks on the media, calling it \"disgusting\" and \"corrupt\" for not covering him fairly. In a series of tweets, Trump claimed that if the media covered him honestly, he would be beating Hillary Clinton by 20%. He also accused The New York Times of publishing \"fiction\" and claimed that the media is protecting Clinton. Trump's campaign chairman, Paul Manafort, also blamed the media for the GOP nominee's difficult week, saying they focused on Trump's comments rather than his economic plan.\n\n**Trump's Foreign Policy and Immigration Plans**\n\nTrump is expected to deliver a speech in Ohio on Monday, where he will outline his vision for foreign policy and immigration. According to sources, Trump will propose a new immigration policy that would stop issuing visas in cases where adequate screenings can't be performed. He will also propose creating a new, ideological test for admission to the country that would assess a candidate's stances on issues like religious freedom.\n\n**Biden and Clinton Campaign**\n\nVice President Joe Biden will campaign with Hillary Clinton in Scranton, Pennsylvania, on Monday, where he will declare Trump the most uninformed presidential nominee in history. Biden will argue that Trump is less prepared on national security than any previous nominee and that his erratic rhetoric and \"bluster\" will make Americans less safe.\n\n**Clinton's Campaign and Immigration**\n\nHillary Clinton's campaign is launching a new effort to tap into the political power of young, undocumented immigrants, known as DREAMERs. The campaign is hoping to capitalize on Trump's promises to deport them and is launching a new voter registration program to mobilize Latinos who can vote.\n\n**Manafort's Ties to Ukraine**\n\nA New York Times investigation has revealed that Paul Manafort, Trump's top adviser, has ties to a large network in Ukraine that was used to loot assets and influence elections. The investigation has come under increasing scrutiny as the U.S. election cycle focuses on the region. Former Trump campaign manager Corey Lewandowski, who was fired after clashing with Manafort, tweeted a link to the article, seemingly calling out his successor.\n\n**Trump's Running Mate and National Security**\n\nDonald Trump's running mate, Mike Pence, has said that Trump will offer \"real specifics\" this week on how to make the country safer. Pence declined to preview Trump's plan, but said that Trump will offer a \"change of direction\" in counterterrorism policies. Trump has called President Barack Obama the \""}
{"input": "", "answers": ["An operation in London has given 9-year-old Kieran Sorkin a pair of ears—made from his ribs, no less. Born deaf with no ears, Kieran was able to hear because of an implanted hearing aid, but he still wanted ears. \"I want people to stop asking me questions,\" he told the BBC. \"I'd like just to look like my friends. I'd also like to be able to wear sunglasses and earphones.\" So surgeons traced an outline of Kieran's mom's ears, removed cartilage from the boy's ribs, and sliced, contoured, and sewed the cartilage. Then they put it in two skin pockets and vacuumed them into the shape of ears. They don't perform any function, but Kieran's plastic surgeon says they can deliver a huge confidence boost: \"If you can change the confidence of a patient at this young age, you can change their whole trajectory in life,\" he said. The ears should last, too, because they're made of cartilage rather than prosthetics, the Guardian reports. Every year about 100 British newborns lack one or both ears (it's called microtia), and the hospital Kieran went to performs the operation about 40 times a year. Scientists hope to one day make ears from the fat tissue of patients, instead of ribs. Kieran might have liked that, since he got so excited on seeing his new ears that he cried \"Wow!\" and giggled—which made his ribs hurt from the operation. (Click to read about a man who needed surgery to remove a tooth growing in his nose.)"], "length": 1651, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Groundbreaking Surgery Gives Boy Born Without Ears a New Lease on Life**\n\nNine-year-old Kieran Sorkin, born without ears and with a rare condition called microtia, has undergone a pioneering surgery at Great Ormond Street Hospital in London. The procedure, which involved creating a pair of ears from Kieran's own ribs, has given him a new sense of confidence and self-esteem.\n\nKieran, from Hertfordshire, was born deaf and had small lobes where his ears should be. However, thanks to previous surgery and a hearing aid, he has been able to hear. The new surgery, led by consultant plastic surgeon Neil Bulstrode, aimed to improve Kieran's appearance and boost his confidence.\n\nThe procedure involved harvesting cartilage from Kieran's ribs, shaping it into frameworks for his ears, and grafting them onto his head. The surgery was a success, and Kieran was delighted with the result, exclaiming \"Wow!\" when he saw his new ears for the first time.\n\nThe surgery has significant psychological benefits, according to Mr. Bulstrode, who said that it can change a child's whole trajectory in life. Kieran's parents, David and Louise, were overjoyed with the result, saying that they wanted their son to be accepted like everyone else and not be bullied because of his appearance.\n\nGreat Ormond Street Hospital performs about 40 of these operations each year, although most are to create one missing ear. The surgery is cosmetic, not to improve hearing, but it brings huge psychological benefits to the children who undergo it.\n\nAdvances in tissue engineering may one day make this kind of reconstructive surgery even less invasive. Scientists at the Institute of Child Health are working on creating stem cells from patients' fat tissue, which could be used to grow new ears. However, this research is still in its early stages, and for now, children like Kieran will continue to benefit from the same procedure used at Great Ormond Street Hospital.\n\nKieran will need a second operation in six months to make his ears stand out from the scalp more, but he is already delighted with the result. His parents are thrilled that their son is finally able to look like his friends and wear sunglasses and earphones without feeling self-conscious. The surgery has given Kieran a new lease on life, and he is looking forward to a brighter future."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["It looks like the honeymoon is over between Trump and Obama. \"Doing my best to disregard the many inflammatory President O statements and roadblocks. Thought it was going to be a smooth transition—NOT!\" Trump tweeted Wednesday. The Los Angeles Times notes Trump's use of \"1990s-style sarcasm.\" Trump had previously commended Obama on his commitment to a smooth transition of power, but a number of things have changed since then, the Washington Post reports. Many saw comments made by Obama during Tuesday's Pearl Harbor anniversary as directed at Trump. The president urged the US not to \"turn inward\" and to \"resist the urge to demonize those who are different.\" Trump is also upset with the Obama administration for allowing the UN to pass a resolution condemning Israeli settlements, as well as with Obama personally for saying in an interview he could have beaten Trump and won a third term. In addition, the White House is defending the Department of Energy for not complying with a request from Trump's transition team for the names of employees involved in climate policy, ABC News reports. Transition team spokesperson Sean Spicer at first said Trump's tweets \"speak for themselves, very clearly,\" but later complimented members of the Obama administration for being \"helpful and generous with their time.\""], "length": 1615, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Transition Turmoil: Trump and Obama Exchange Barbs**\n\nIn a dramatic turn of events, President-elect Donald Trump and President Barack Obama have engaged in a public spat over the transition of power. Trump, who had previously praised Obama's efforts to ensure a smooth transition, accused the outgoing president of putting \"roadblocks\" in his way.\n\nOn Wednesday, Trump took to Twitter to express his frustration, saying he was \"doing my best to disregard the many inflammatory President O statements and roadblocks.\" He also criticized Obama's administration for its handling of several issues, including the Iran nuclear deal and the United Nations Security Council resolution condemning Israeli settlements.\n\nThe White House declined to comment on Trump's tweets, but Obama's team has maintained that the president is committed to a peaceful and smooth transition of power. In fact, Obama phoned Trump on Wednesday and the two had a \"very nice conversation,\" according to Trump.\n\nHowever, the tension between the two leaders is palpable. Trump's tweets were seen as a response to Obama's comments on a podcast, in which he said he could have won the election against Trump if he had run again. Trump dismissed Obama's claim, saying \"nobody's ever going to know\" because they will never face each other in an election.\n\nThe transition team has also been at odds with the Obama administration over several issues, including a request for the names of employees who worked on the Obama administration's climate policy efforts. The Energy Department refused to comply, and the White House defended the decision.\n\nDespite the tensions, Trump sounded more conciliatory later in the day, saying the transition was going \"very, very smoothly.\" However, the exchange has raised questions about the state of the transition and the relationship between the two leaders.\n\n**Key Developments:**\n\n* Trump accuses Obama of putting \"roadblocks\" in his way, citing several issues including the Iran nuclear deal and the UN Security Council resolution on Israeli settlements.\n* Obama and Trump have a \"very nice conversation\" on Wednesday, but the tone of the transition remains tense.\n* Trump dismisses Obama's claim that he could have won the election against him.\n* The transition team and the Obama administration are at odds over several issues, including a request for the names of employees who worked on the Obama administration's climate policy efforts.\n* Trump says the transition is going \"very, very smoothly,\" but the exchange has raised questions about the state of the transition and the relationship between the two leaders."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Pandas may prefer their meals in the form of bamboo, but that doesn't mean their bite is toothless. A man in China recently received a harsh reminder of that after a wild panda bit his leg after authorities pursued the critter onto his lawn, reports AFP via Yahoo. The creature had made its way into a northwestern village, which is near a sanctuary that's home to about 100 wild pandas. \"I saw a panda jump out in front of me, its body completely covered in mud,\" the man told local news. A struggle ensued, in which the bear wouldn't release the man's leg until a rescuer wrapped a coat around the animal's head. Finally, it fled—but not before causing injuries which, China Daily reports, led to eight surgeries over the past year and may lead to amputation. The man sued three government departments and won an $83,000 payout that will cover his medical costs, his lawyer says. (China Daily has a pretty gruesome photo of the injury.) \"As cuddly as they may look, a panda can protect itself as well as most other bears,\" the World Wildlife Fund notes, per AFP. Indeed, panda attacks have led to some nasty consequences in the past."], "length": 794, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Wild Panda Attack: Villager Receives Compensation**\n\nA Chinese villager, Guan Quanzhi, has been awarded 520,000 yuan ($83,000) in compensation after being attacked by a wild panda in March 2014. The incident occurred in Liziba village, Gansu province, when a panda, which had been chased by other villagers, bit Guan's leg, requiring eight surgeries and leaving him with the possibility of amputation.\n\nThe attack highlights the dangers of wild pandas, which are often perceived as gentle and harmless due to their lovable appearance. However, they are members of the bear family and possess a fearsome bite, capable of inflicting serious injuries.\n\nGuan's son sued local forestry officials and the Baishuijiang National Nature Reserve, which is home to over 100 wild pandas, for compensation. After negotiations, the officials agreed to pay the compensation, which will cover Guan's medical bills.\n\nThe incident is a reminder of the importance of conservation efforts to protect the giant panda, whose natural habitat is mostly in mountainous southwestern China. The number of wild giant pandas has increased by nearly 17% over the past decade, thanks to conservation measures, but they remain under pressure from habitat loss and other factors.\n\nThe giant panda is a major tourist attraction in China, generating significant revenue for the country. However, their attacks on humans are not uncommon, as seen in a 2008 incident at a zoo in southern China, where a panda mauled a 20-year-old man who climbed into its enclosure.\n\nThe World Wildlife Fund (WWF) warns that, despite their cuddly appearance, pandas can be fierce and protective of themselves, using their heavy weight, strong jaw muscles, and large molar teeth to defend themselves.\n\nGuan's case serves as a reminder of the importance of respecting and protecting these majestic animals, while also acknowledging the risks associated with their presence in the wild."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Authorities are combing through more than 1,200 leads in a desperate search for a 9-year-old girl they say was abducted by her uncle May 4, WATE reports. According to the Knoxville News Sentinel, 57-year-old Gary Simpson picked Carlie Trent up from her Tennessee school, telling staff her father had been in an accident. Shortly before, he purchased a child's nightgown, bikini, girls' underwear, lipstick, and nail polish at an area Walmart. The Tennessee Bureau of Investigation has pictures of those items. Carlie's mother Shannon Trent tells People she felt \"sick to my stomach\" when she heard about those purchases. \"I don't know what he's done to her,\" she says. \"I don't think he would hurt her, but if he's capable of kidnapping a child he's capable of anything.\" Authorities believe Carlie is in \"imminent danger.\" Simpson also purchased camping supplies and nonperishable groceries, and authorities think he may be hiding with Carlie in an isolated area. The TBI says there have been rumors online that Simpson is trying to protect Carlie, but it says that couldn't be further from the truth. \"This was not an innocent camping trip, this was a crime,\" a spokesperson tells WATE. Simpson, Carlie's uncle by marriage, and his wife once had custody of Carlie, but her father James Trent had custody at the time of her abduction. Shannon Trent, who hasn't had custody of Carlie in two years, says she \"always had a bad feeling\" about Simpson. \"I should have stuck with my gut,\" she tells People. (This boy's summer with his dad turned into his kidnapping.)"], "length": 2314, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Missing 9-Year-Old Carlie Trent: A Week-Long Search**\n\nCarlie Trent, a 9-year-old girl from Rogersville, Tennessee, has been missing for a week after being abducted by her uncle, Gary Simpson, on May 4th. Simpson, 57, signed Carlie out of her elementary school under false pretenses, claiming her father had been in a car accident. However, authorities believe this was a lie and that Simpson had no authority to pick Carlie up.\n\n**Reward Offered**\n\nIn an effort to bring Carlie home safely, two Knoxville banking executives, Matt Daniels and Tim Williams, have offered a $10,000 reward for information leading to her return. Additionally, the U.S. Marshals Service is offering a $5,000 reward, and Carlie's pediatrician, Dr. Chris Calendine, has also offered a $10,000 reward.\n\n**Search Efforts**\n\nThe Tennessee Bureau of Investigation (TBI) has been working tirelessly to locate Carlie and Simpson, with agents sifting through over 1,200 leads and law enforcement in 19 states working on possible sightings. A Missouri Amber Alert was issued on Tuesday, but it was later canceled after authorities determined it was a false alarm. The TBI has also reached out to state park officials and private campgrounds in several states, including Tennessee, North Carolina, Virginia, and Kentucky.\n\n**Concerns for Carlie's Safety**\n\nTBI officials have expressed concern for Carlie's safety, stating that she is in imminent danger. Authorities believe Simpson may have brought Carlie to a remote area or campground, and have found evidence of camping supplies and other items that suggest a premeditated plan. Carlie's mother, Shannon Trent, has also expressed concern, stating that she \"always had a bad feeling\" about Simpson and believes he may have an obsession with her daughter.\n\n**Appeal to the Public**\n\nThe TBI is urging the public to be on the lookout for Carlie and Simpson, who were last seen traveling in a white 2002 Dodge conversion van with Tennessee tag 173GPS. Anyone with information is asked to call the TBI at 1-800-TBI-FIND or the Rogersville Police Department at (423) 272-7555. If anyone spots the van, they should call 911 directly.\n\n**Carlie's Description**\n\nCarlie is described as 4'8\" tall, weighing about 75 lbs., with blonde hair and blue eyes. She was"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["CBS is scrapping its Early Show team, replacing co-hosts Harry Smith and Maggie Rodriguez with the anchors of the show's Saturday edition, Chris Wragge and Erica Hill. Other more minor players also are leaving in the switch, which takes place early next year, the LA Times reports. The show has been consistently stuck in third place, well behind Today and Good Morning America. Smith, who survived the last major overhaul of the show in 2002, will stick with the network as Katie Couric's main backup on the CBS Evening News, notes AP. Click here for more."], "length": 1319, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**CBS News Overhauls \"The Early Show\" with New Team**\n\nIn a bid to boost ratings, CBS News has made significant changes to its morning program \"The Early Show\". The show's co-hosts, Harry Smith and Maggie Rodriguez, have been replaced by Chris Wragge and Erica Hill, who currently anchor the Saturday edition of the show. The new team will debut on January 3.\n\nWragge, a sports anchor who made the jump to news in 2006, will join Hill, who has been reading newscasts on the show. Jeff Glor, who anchors the network's Saturday newscast, will also join the team as a regular news reader. Marysol Castro, a former weather anchor on ABC's \"Good Morning America\" weekend edition, will serve as the new weather anchor.\n\nThe changes come after the show's ratings have consistently trailed behind NBC's \"Today\" show and ABC's \"Good Morning America\". CBS News President Sean McManus believes the new team will help the show grow, citing their chemistry and ability to shift between hard news and lighter fare.\n\nSmith, who had the longest tenure on the show, will remain at CBS as a news reporter and primary substitute anchor for Katie Couric's evening newscast, \"Face the Nation\", and \"Sunday Morning\". Rodriguez and Dave Price, who was replaced by Castro, will have their future roles at the network discussed.\n\nThe sudden changes have been met with mixed reactions, with some praising the move as a necessary shake-up, while others see it as a sign of desperation. The new team will face the challenge of breaking the show out of third place in the network morning news ratings."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["With George HW Bush dead at 94, coverage of the life of the 41st president is plentiful. Here are some early highlights: The broad strokes: For a thorough obituary and assessment, start with the Washington Post. Its story notes that while Bush served just one term as president, it was a consequential one. \"The Berlin Wall fell; the Soviet Union ceased to exist; the communist bloc in Eastern Europe broke up; the Cold War ended.\" And Bush's \"firm, restrained diplomatic sense helped assure the harmony and peace with which these world-shaking events played out, one after the other.\" Great detail: In its obituary, the Wall Street Journal notes that Bush flew 58 missions in the Pacific as a Navy pilot in WWII, all with the name of his girlfriend—Barbara—on the side of the plane. He once got shot down, with this video capturing the rescue. Bush is also the only American besides John Adams to be both president and the father of a president. Read his lips: The Guardian recounts some of his Bush's famous quotes, including his \"read my lips\" pledge to not raise taxes. He also dissed broccoli."], "length": 2660, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Summary of News**\n\nGeorge H.W. Bush, the 41st President of the United States, passed away at the age of 94. He led the country to a swift and decisive victory in the first Persian Gulf War and presided over the peaceful dissolution of the Soviet Union and unification of Germany. Bush's presidency was marked by a painful recession, which cost him a second term in office. Despite this, he left a lasting legacy as a statesman and a leader who inspired generations of Americans to public service.\n\nBush's life was marked by a series of notable achievements, including his service in World War II, where he became the youngest pilot in the Navy at the time. He went on to build a successful business career in the oil industry and later entered politics, serving as a congressman, ambassador to the United Nations, and director of the CIA.\n\nAs vice president under Ronald Reagan, Bush played a key role in shaping the administration's foreign policy, including the decision to invade Panama and the handling of the Iran-Contra scandal. When Reagan was shot, Bush stepped in to ease the crisis atmosphere and proved himself to be a capable and loyal leader.\n\nIn 1988, Bush ran for president and won the Republican nomination, promising to create a \"kinder and gentler nation.\" However, his presidency was marked by a series of challenges, including a weak economy and a primary challenge from Pat Buchanan. Bush's decision to raise taxes, which he had previously promised not to do, ultimately led to his defeat in the 1992 election.\n\nDespite his defeat, Bush remained a respected figure in American politics and continued to serve as a diplomat and advocate for various causes. He developed a close friendship with former President Bill Clinton and worked with him on several projects, including relief efforts for victims of the Indian Ocean tsunami.\n\nThroughout his life, Bush was known for his wit and his ability to connect with people from all walks of life. He was a devoted husband to his wife, Barbara, and a proud father to his children, including his son George W. Bush, who followed in his footsteps to become president.\n\nBush's legacy will be remembered for his commitment to public service, his leadership during times of crisis, and his ability to bring people together in the face of adversity. He will be deeply missed by his family, friends, and the American people."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["OJ Simpson's parole hearing in Nevada will be broadcast live at 1pm EDT Thursday and there's a strong chance that his path to freedom will be clear by the end of the afternoon. Nobody has registered to testify against the 70-year-old Simpson, who has been in prison for nine years on 12 convictions related to a Las Vegas robbery and will appear before the Nevada Parole Board via videolink, NPR reports. Thursday's hearing will cover four concurrent sentences for use of a deadly weapon and two consecutive sentences for assault with a deadly weapon, the Las Vegas Review-Journal reports. Four board members will vote after the hearing and Simpson could be free by Oct. 1 if they are all in favor of parole. If Simpson is indeed released this fall—which appears almost certainly to be the decision—it will \"renew the rabid curiosity\" in the case and \"produce a spectacle unlike anything we’ve seen since he went behind bars,\" Nancy Armour at USA Today predicts. There has even been talk that Simpson might end up with a reality TV show, but Armour wants Simpson to avoid the spotlight unless he wants to return to the \"toxic mentality that landed him in prison in the first place.\" If the former NFL star does end up with a TV show, the money is likely to go to the families of murder victims Nicole Brown Simpson and Ronald Goldman, CBS News reports. He will still receive an NFL pension estimated at $25,000 per month."], "length": 3982, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**O.J. Simpson's Parole Hearing: A Possible Release from Prison**\n\nO.J. Simpson, the former NFL star and actor, is set to appear before a Nevada parole board on Thursday, July 20, in a bid to be released from prison after serving nearly nine years for armed robbery and kidnapping charges. Simpson, now 70, has been incarcerated at Lovelock Correctional Center since December 2008 and is serving a nine- to 33-year sentence.\n\n**Background**\n\nSimpson was acquitted of the murders of his ex-wife, Nicole Brown Simpson, and her friend, Ronald Goldman, in 1995, but was later found liable for their deaths in a civil trial and ordered to pay $33.5 million in damages. In 2007, Simpson was convicted of armed robbery and kidnapping charges related to a botched attempt to recover memorabilia stolen from him.\n\n**Parole Hearing**\n\nThe parole hearing will be held via video conference, with Simpson testifying from prison and the parole board deliberating in Carson City. The board will consider confidential information, including a pre-sentence investigation, a parole hearing report, and letters of support or opposition. Simpson has expressed remorse for the armed heist and has been a model inmate, according to his own testimony.\n\n**Media Interest**\n\nThe parole hearing has generated significant media interest, with 30 media organizations expected to be on-site in Carson City and Lovelock Correctional Center. The hearing will be streamed live, and many outlets will be covering the event.\n\n**Possible Release**\n\nIf the parole board votes in favor of Simpson's release, he could be released from prison as early as October 1. Simpson's defense attorney, Yale Galanter, believes that Simpson deserves parole and that the Las Vegas jury that convicted him was motivated by anger over Simpson's 2007 book, \"If I Did It.\"\n\n**Legacy**\n\nSimpson's legacy is complex and multifaceted. He was a beloved athlete and actor, but his acquittal in the murder trial and subsequent conviction for armed robbery and kidnapping charges have left a lasting impact on the public's perception of him. As he approaches the end of his prison sentence, Simpson's future remains uncertain, and his possible release from prison will be closely watched by the media and the public.\n\n**Key Dates**\n\n* July 20: O.J. Simpson's parole hearing\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Alaska yesterday became the third state to legalize marijuana for recreational use, and now a country where you would have thought that was the case already is moving closer to that goal itself. Jamaica's parliament last night approved a law decriminalizing small amounts of pot, the AP reports. People found with 2 ounces or less of marijuana will now simply receive a ticket—not a crime on their record—and cultivation of five plants or fewer is now allowed. A licensing agency was also established to oversee pot cultivation and distribution for medical and scientific efforts. Rastafarians are rejoicing, because the bill also grants them the legal right to use cannabis for sacramental purposes, while tourists who have medical marijuana prescriptions elsewhere can pay for permits to buy a bit of ganja on the island. Jamaica has shied away from decriminalization because it didn't want to risk violating international treaties and provoking US sanctions, the Guardian reports. The process for the new law was described by National Security Minister Peter Bunting as an \"elephantine,\" nearly 40-year effort, ABC Australia reports. \"[The law] eliminates an unnecessary source of friction between police and citizens, and ensures that our young people are not gratuitously shackled with criminal records,\" he said in a statement. Jamaica also hopes to boost its health tourism and medical marijuana industries and make it a major player in the pot product market, the Guardian notes. International exporting, however, remains a no-no. A US counternarcotics official told the AP in an email that \"Jamaican law is of course Jamaica's own business,\" but warned that drug trafficking into the US is still illegal."], "length": 1853, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Jamaica Decriminalizes Small Amounts of Marijuana, Paves Way for Medical Cannabis Industry**\n\nIn a historic move, Jamaica's parliament has passed a law decriminalizing the possession of small amounts of marijuana, also known as \"ganja\" or \"weed.\" The legislation, which was years in the making, makes possession of up to 2 ounces (56.6g) of marijuana a petty offense that can result in a fine, rather than a criminal record. The law also permits the use of marijuana for religious, medical, scientific, and therapeutic purposes.\n\nThe new law is a significant step forward for Jamaica, which has long been associated with marijuana culture. The island's Rastafarian community, which has a long history of using marijuana for spiritual purposes, will now be able to use the drug freely. Tourists who are prescribed medical marijuana abroad will also be able to apply for permits to buy small amounts of Jamaican weed.\n\nThe law paves the way for the development of a lawful medical cannabis industry in Jamaica, which officials believe has \"great potential\" for the island's economy. Local scientists have already developed marijuana-derived products, such as \"Canasol,\" which helps relieve pressure in the eyes of glaucoma patients.\n\nThe decriminalization of marijuana in Jamaica is part of a broader international trend of easing restrictions on the drug. More than 20 US states allow some form of medical marijuana, and several countries, including Uruguay and Argentina, have decriminalized or legalized the drug.\n\nWhile the US has not changed its stance on marijuana, with the trafficking of the drug into the country remaining illegal, officials have acknowledged Jamaica's sovereignty in the matter. \"Jamaican law is of course Jamaica's own business, and Jamaica's sovereign decision,\" said William Brownfield, the US assistant secretary for counter-narcotics affairs.\n\nThe new law is expected to have a positive impact on Jamaica's economy and society, particularly for young people who have been disproportionately affected by marijuana laws. As National Security Minister Peter Bunting noted, the law \"eliminates an unnecessary source of friction between police and citizens, and ensures that our young people are not gratuitously shackled with criminal records.\"\n\nOverall, the decriminalization of marijuana in Jamaica is a significant step forward for the island and a major milestone in the global movement to reform marijuana laws."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Search teams in Indonesia may have found the main fuselage of the AirAsia plane where most of the remaining victims and the plane's black boxes are expected to be. Authorities found \"four big parts of the plane we're looking for,\" says the official in charge of the search in the Java Sea, reports Reuters. The biggest of the pieces is about 59 feet long and 18 feet wide, reports AP. The pieces have only been detected, not recovered, and divers hope conditions will permit them to reach the objects tomorrow. So far, only 30 of 162 bodies have been recovered, and most of the victims are believed to be still strapped in their seats. Authorities also said today that AirAsia was in violation of its license by flying to Singapore on a Sunday, the day of the crash, reports the Telegraph. As a result, the airline might have its license revoked in Indonesia. There's still no conclusions yet in what caused the crash, but theories continue to center on stormy conditions. \"Flight 8501 appears to have been trapped in bad weather that would have been difficult to avoid,\" says a report by Indonesia's Meteorology, Climatology and Geophysics Agency."], "length": 2887, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**AirAsia Flight 8501 Crash: Search Efforts Continue**\n\nAirAsia Flight 8501, an Airbus A320-200, crashed into the Java Sea on December 28, killing all 162 people on board. The search for the wreckage and bodies of the victims has been ongoing for nearly a week, with Indonesian authorities and international teams working together to locate the plane.\n\n**Wreckage Found**\n\nOn Saturday, Indonesian officials announced that they had found four large parts of the plane on the sea bed, including a 18-meter-long piece that appears to be part of the jet's body. The objects were detected using sonar equipment and were confirmed to be from the missing plane. The discovery was made in the target search area, and officials are hopeful that many of the passengers and crew may still be trapped inside the plane's fuselage.\n\n**Cause of Crash Unclear**\n\nThe cause of the crash remains unclear, but bad weather appears to have been a factor. A 14-page report from Indonesia's Meteorology, Climatology and Geophysics Agency suggests that the plane may have been trapped in bad weather that would have been difficult to avoid. The report also mentions the possibility of icing on the plane's engines, which could have caused the crash.\n\n**Airline's License Suspended**\n\nIndonesian authorities have suspended AirAsia's license to fly from Surabaya to Singapore, citing a violation of the airline's operating permit. The airline was only authorized to fly on Mondays, Tuesdays, Thursdays, and Saturdays, but flew on a Sunday when the crash occurred.\n\n**Search Efforts Continue**\n\nThe search for the wreckage and bodies of the victims continues, with a second U.S. Navy ship arriving on Saturday to help in the search. The Indonesian Navy and other international teams are working together to locate the plane and recover the bodies of the victims. Autopsies will be carried out on some of the bodies, including the pilot and co-pilot, whose remains have not yet been recovered.\n\n**Relatives Wait with Bated Breath**\n\nFamily members of the victims are waiting anxiously for news of their loved ones. Some relatives have expressed hope that the discovery of the wreckage will lead to the recovery of more bodies and an end to the search. Others are simply worn out from the wait and the false reports that have circulated in the media.\n\n**International Response**\n\nThe crash of AirAsia Flight 8501 has sparked an international response, with teams from Singapore, Russia, Malaysia, and the U.S. involved in"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The Telegraph reported yesterday on a crazy court case in the UK: After a pregnant Italian woman, in town for business, had a panic attack, social service workers in Essex got a court order allowing the woman to be forcibly sedated and undergo a C-section so they could take her baby. Fifteen months later, the little girl is still with social service workers, who won't return her to her mother. The case is now \"an international legal row,\" the Telegraph says, and the anonymous woman's lawyers call it \"unprecedented.\" The woman was in Britain in July 2012 for an airline training course, and called police when she suffered the panic attack. They arrived while she was on the phone with her mother, who told police the woman suffered from bipolar disorder and was off her medication, according to a Telegraph columnist. Police took her to a psychiatric facility, and restrained her under the Mental Health Act when she said she wanted to go back to her hotel. She underwent the C-section after having been there five weeks. The case is ongoing; the mother says she has made a full recovery, but a judge nonetheless ruled that her daughter should be put up for adoption. More on the case here and here."], "length": 325, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Internet Archive Aims to Fix Broken Links on the Web**\n\nThe Internet Archive, a non-profit organization dedicated to preserving online content, has launched a new initiative to combat the problem of broken links on the web. The project involves archiving web pages as they are created and referencing the versions of pages that they link to, ensuring that even if the original page is removed or changed, the archived link will remain intact.\n\nThe goal of the project is to fix all broken links on the web by creating a network of preserved pages that can be accessed through the Internet Archive. This will not only provide a backup of online content but also allow users to view the original version of a page, even if the link has been broken.\n\nThe Internet Archive is working with \"No More 404\" sites, which are websites that have pledged to preserve their content and provide links to archived versions of their pages. By partnering with these sites, the Internet Archive aims to create a comprehensive archive of web pages and fix the problem of broken links once and for all."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The writ of habeas corpus lets prisoners appear in court to make their captors justify why they're being held. Until now in the US, those who've used this legal tactic have been human. But a New York judge yesterday ruled that the writ may be used by two lab chimpanzees at Stony Brook University, Science magazine reports. NY Supreme Court Justice Barbara Jaffe ordered a university rep to address a suit filed by the Nonhuman Rights Project by appearing in court May 6 to argue why the college is holding Hercules and Leo. The group, which says the animals are being detained for biomedical experiments, says its ultimate goal is to have the animals released to the Save the Chimps sanctuary in Fort Pierce, Fla., per the NhRP's website. \"This is a big step forward to getting what we are ultimately seeking: the right to bodily liberty for chimpanzees and other cognitively complex animals,\" Natalie Prosin, the project's executive director, tells Science. The case kicked off in December 2013 with lawsuits that also included two chimps held on private property; each case was struck down, sending the NhRP down the appeals path. Not everyone's convinced the upcoming hearing will mean liberation for the chimps. A Pepperdine University law professor tells Science that the judge likely just wants to hear both sides of the story: \"It would be quite surprising if the judge intended to make a momentous substantive finding that chimpanzees are legal persons\" without doing so, he notes. Prosin, meanwhile, says her group isn't stopping with the chimps and will also target the release of other animals. (Click to read about what chimps talk to each other about.)"], "length": 1721, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Habeas Corpus: A Fundamental Right Under Threat**\n\nHabeas corpus, a cornerstone of Western law, has been a vital safeguard against government overreach and abuse of power. This ancient legal procedure, enshrined in the US Constitution, ensures that individuals cannot be held indefinitely without cause. However, in recent years, the right to habeas corpus has been eroded, particularly in the context of the War on Terror.\n\n**Guantanamo Bay and the Military Commissions Act**\n\nThe Military Commissions Act (MCA) of 2006 revoked the right to habeas corpus for detainees at Guantanamo Bay and for non-citizens labeled \"enemy combatants\" anywhere in the world. This provision applies to legal residents of the US, who could potentially be detained without recourse. The government has little evidence against most Guantanamo detainees, who were often sold into captivity by warlords for bounties. Despite numerous legal victories, the Bush administration has prevented detainees from receiving a fair hearing in court.\n\n**The Supreme Court and Habeas Corpus**\n\nThe Supreme Court has twice heard cases challenging the detention of Guantanamo detainees, with the third hearing scheduled for the upcoming term. The court's decisions will determine whether the executive branch can continue to hold individuals without cause.\n\n**Chimpanzees and the Right to Habeas Corpus**\n\nIn a groundbreaking decision, a New York judge granted a pair of research chimpanzees, Hercules and Leo, the right to habeas corpus, recognizing them as legal persons for the first time in US history. This ruling could force the university to release the chimps and set a precedent for other research animals. The Nonhuman Rights Project, which filed the case, aims to establish the right to bodily liberty for cognitively complex animals.\n\n**Implications and Future Directions**\n\nThe erosion of habeas corpus threatens the very foundation of our legal and constitutional framework. If left unchecked, it could lead to a second-class system of justice, where individuals can be detained without cause. The case of the chimpanzees highlights the need to reevaluate our treatment of non-human animals and to recognize their rights and dignity. The Nonhuman Rights Project plans to file additional cases, including one involving a captive elephant, and aims to establish the right to bodily liberty for a range of animals."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Police in three states are still hunting for a Range Rover whose occupants opened fire on another car on the Vegas Strip this morning and set off a deadly chain reaction. Cops don't know most of the details, but they say it started with a dispute of some kind in one of the nearby hotels, then spilled into the street, reports the Las Vegas Sun. When the Range Rover caught up to a Maserati at a stop light, shots were fired from the SUV that sent the Maserati crashing into a taxi, which exploded upon impact. Three people—the Maserati driver, the taxi driver, and a taxi passenger—were killed, and three others were injured, reports the LA Times. Police are hoping to piece together more details by interviewing a passenger in the Maserati, who was injured in the melee. Earlier reports that gunfire came from both vehicles as part of a moving shootout were incorrect. The AP collects this quote from a tourist visiting from Manchester, England: \"This doesn't happen where we come from, not on this scale. We get stabbings, and gang violence, but this is like something out of a movie. Like Die Hard or something.\""], "length": 3450, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Deadly Shooting on Las Vegas Strip Leaves Three Dead and Six Injured**\n\nA violent shooting on the Las Vegas Strip early Thursday morning left three people dead and at least six injured. The incident occurred around 4:20 a.m. when a black Range Rover SUV opened fire on a Maserati, causing it to crash into a taxi that burst into flames. The taxi driver and passenger were killed, as well as the driver of the Maserati. The passenger in the Maserati was injured and taken to the hospital.\n\nAccording to police, the shooting was sparked by an altercation at a nearby hotel, which spilled onto the streets. The Range Rover SUV, with tinted windows and paper dealer plates, was seen fleeing the scene and is currently being sought by authorities. Police have contacted authorities in three neighboring states to be on the lookout for the vehicle.\n\nThe incident has caused widespread disruption on the Strip, with Las Vegas Boulevard near Flamingo Road shut down for several hours. Tourists and locals alike were left shaken by the violence, with some expressing concern about the safety of the city.\n\n**Victims Identified**\n\nThe driver of the Maserati, Kenneth Wayne Cherry Jr., 27, was identified as a local resident and aspiring rapper. The taxi driver, Michael Boldon, was also identified, and his family has spoken out about the tragedy, calling for justice for the suspects.\n\n**Investigation Ongoing**\n\nPolice are still investigating the incident and have collected video from traffic cameras at the intersection. They are also checking hotel surveillance systems to determine the cause of the altercation and the identity of the suspects.\n\n**Previous Incidents on the Strip**\n\nThis incident marks the latest in a series of violent incidents on the Las Vegas Strip in recent weeks. Two people were critically wounded in a shooting at a parking garage on February 6, and a tourist was stabbed in an elevator at The Hotel at Mandalay Bay on Saturday.\n\n**Community Response**\n\nThe Las Vegas Convention and Visitors Authority has reassured visitors that the city is safe and that measures are being taken to prevent such incidents. However, some locals and tourists have expressed concern about the violence and the need for increased security.\n\n**Manhunt Underway**\n\nThe search for the suspects continues, with police urging anyone with information to come forward. The incident has left the city in a state of shock, and the community is coming together to support the victims and their families."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A 16-year-old girl in Washington state is bruised and dehydrated but very much alive after surviving a plane crash and a long trek through what authorities describe as some of the state's most rugged terrain. Autumn Veatch was on a small plane with her step-grandparents that crashed after departing Kalispell, Mont., on Saturday afternoon, the Bellingham Herald reports. There was no sign of the plane or its occupants until a motorist found Veatch on State Route 20 yesterday afternoon and took her to a general store, where employees called authorities, the AP reports. She was hospitalized with minor injuries. Her father tells the Herald that after the plane crashed into a mountain, it caught fire and she was unable to get her step-grandparents out. She spent about a day near the plane waiting for rescue before following a stream and then a trail out of the woods, her father says. Friends say both father and daughter are completely overwhelmed. \"She did joke that it was a good thing she'd watched all those Survivor shows that she didn't like, but her dad made her watch anyway,\" a family friend tells the Seattle Times. Authorities are still searching for the missing Beech A-35. (In Colombia last month, a mother and baby survived four days in the jungle after a plane crash.)"], "length": 2134, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Teenager Survives Small-Plane Crash in North Cascades**\n\nA 16-year-old girl, Autumn Veatch, has survived a small-plane crash in the rugged North Cascades mountains of Washington state. Veatch, a student at Bellingham High School, was on a flight from Montana to Washington with her step-grandparents, Leland and Sharon Bowman, when the plane crashed on Saturday afternoon. Despite being presumed dead by many, Veatch miraculously survived the crash and spent two days in the wilderness before being found and taken to a hospital.\n\nAccording to Veatch's account, the plane crashed and caught fire after flying into a bank of clouds. She remained at the crash site for a day before deciding to hike down, eventually finding a trail and following it to the trailhead on Highway 20. Veatch was found by a motorist on Monday afternoon and taken to a general store in Mazama, where employees called 911. She was then taken to a hospital in Brewster for treatment of minor injuries, including dehydration and exposure to the elements.\n\nThe fate of Veatch's step-grandparents remains unclear, and authorities have not confirmed their condition. The search for the wreckage of the plane continues, with five aircraft equipped with special radios searching the mountains and ground crews focusing on areas between Mazama and Rainy Pass.\n\nVeatch's survival is a miracle, and her family and friends are overjoyed. \"I think she's just happy she's alive,\" said Santina Lampman, a family friend. Veatch's father, David Veatch, said his daughter told him that she had watched survival shows and was able to use that knowledge to help her survive in the wilderness.\n\nThe incident highlights the challenges of searching for missing planes in rugged terrain, with authorities facing \"rocks and trees and lots of nooks and crannies\" in the North Cascades. Despite the difficulties, rescuers are celebrating Veatch's survival and are working to find the wreckage of the plane."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Allegations of illegal wood imports prompted the Justice Department to raid Gibson Guitar facilities in Tennessee last week. NPR aired the complicated story involving the 100-year old Lacey Act, which prohibits imports of endangered species, including plants, into the US. No formal charges have been pressed, but it appears the issue lies with Gibson possibly importing banned Madagascar ebony. Gibson insists the wood confiscated by marshals was legally acquired rosewood from India. Adding some gusto to the story, right-leaning sites assert that Gibson rival CF Martin uses the same wood in some of its guitars, but the company was never investigated. The Landmark Report thinks that's fishy, given that Martin's CEO is a Democratic donor and Gibson's CEO is a GOP backer. Raising the octave even higher, it is possible Michelle Obama ran afoul of the Lacey Act when she presented France's Carla Bruni with a Gibson guitar that may have contained banned wood. However the story plays out, many are frustrated with the retroactive aspect of the law. \"It's a nightmare,\" says a dealer. \"I can't help it if they used Brazilian rosewood on almost every guitar made prior to 1970.\""], "length": 1352, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Music and Environment Collide: Gibson Guitar Raids and the Lacey Act**\n\nIn a rare collision of music and environmental regulation, the Gibson Guitar Corporation has been raided by federal marshals in Tennessee, sparking a debate over the Lacey Act, a century-old endangered species law. The law, amended in 2008 to include plants, prohibits the trafficking of illegally obtained wood. Gibson, a famous instrument builder, has been accused of importing ebony from Madagascar, a country with a ban on exports of the wood.\n\nThe company's CEO, Henry Juszkiewicz, claims that the government has not provided clear evidence of wrongdoing and that the raids have caused significant financial harm to the business. However, the U.S. Justice Department has filed a court motion asserting that Gibson's Madagascar ebony was contraband, citing emails that suggest the company took steps to maintain a supply chain connected to illegal timber harvests.\n\nThe Environmental Investigation Agency (EIA) has concluded that Gibson knowingly imported tainted wood, and the agency's director, Andrea Johnson, says that the company understood the risks involved but chose to source the wood anyway. Gibson denies these allegations, claiming that all of its purchases from Madagascar have complied with U.S. and Malagasy law.\n\nThe Lacey Act has also raised concerns among guitar builders and dealers, who worry about the law's retroactive nature and the potential for owners to be asked to account for every wooden part of their instruments when re-entering the U.S. The law also covers the trade in vintage instruments, which has led some dealers to eliminate overseas business to avoid potential issues.\n\nWhile some, like Chris Martin of C.F. Martin Guitar Co., support the Lacey Act as a necessary measure to prevent illegal logging, others, like attorney Ronald Bienstock, argue that the law is burdensome and lacks clear guidelines on compliance.\n\nMeanwhile, the Internet Archive has been collecting data on the web since 1996, with Alexa Internet donating its crawl data to the Wayback Machine after an embargo period. This data is added to the archive daily, providing a snapshot of the web's history.\n\n**Key Takeaways:**\n\n* Gibson Guitar Corporation has been raided by federal marshals in Tennessee over allegations of importing illegally obtained wood from Madagascar.\n* The Lacey Act, a century-old endangered species law, prohibits the trafficking of illegally obtained wood and has been amended to include plants.\n* The law's retroactive nature and lack of clear guidelines on compliance have raised concerns among guitar builders and dealers.\n* The Environmental Investigation Agency has concluded that"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A brief, planned ceasefire failed to materialize in Gaza today, as death continued to rain down on the strip. Israel had offered to hold its offensive to accommodate a visit from Egyptian Prime Minister Hesham Kandil, provided Hamas held its fire as well. But Hamas said it would keep firing, and one Israeli defense minister complained that 50 rockets had flown in from Gaza during Kandil's visit, CNN reports. A Hamas-run TV station reported that Israel had kept firing as well, but Israel says it took a break for at least two hours. In Palestine the death toll has risen to 21, including 13 civilians, Reuters reports. Among those civilians were seven children and a pregnant teenager. Israel's death toll held steady at 3, despite the hundreds of rockets fired into Israel. But Hamas did up the ante by firing rockets near Tel Aviv for the first time. Air-raid sirens sounded (the last time they did so was during the Gulf War) and civilians ran for cover, though the missiles exploded harmlessly, with one going into the sea. \"There will be a price for that escalation,\" Ehud Barak vowed, according to the New York Times. Israel has now hit 250 Gaza targets, while taking further steps toward a ground invasion, calling up 16,000 reservists, the Wall Street Journal reports. It's also steadily sending troops and armored vehicles to the border, notes CNN—between 1,500 and 2,000 are already there, one official said. Israel claims the attacks have weakened Hamas' military capabilities and caused its attacks to lighten, but Hamas denied that, saying it had hit multiple Israeli targets today. Kandil emerged from his meeting with Hamas expressing solidarity, saying the new Egyptian regime would be more active in helping the Palestinians. \"The time has changed,\" he said. \"No longer the Israeli occupation will be able to carry out their attacks against the Palestinians without being held responsible. That time is far bygone.\" For its part, the Obama admini