{"input": "", "answers": ["A woman believed to be in her 20s or 30s held employees of a Jared Vault jewelry store in Mebane, NC, at gunpoint on Monday before making off with jewelry piled into a shopping bag. But this wasn't her first rodeo: The FBI says the same brunette has robbed five other jewelry stores across the South since April, including in Florida, Georgia, South Carolina, and Tennessee, netting more than $450,000 worth of bling, per WNCN. A black man in his 30s or 40s, weighing about 250 pounds, was spotted by cameras during three of the robberies, reports NBC News. The FBI says both suspects \"are considered armed and dangerous.\""], "length": 786, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Jewelry Store Robberies Span Multiple States**\n\nA brazen jewelry store robber, described as a white woman in her 20s or 30s, 5-foot-8 and 130 pounds, has been linked to six robberies across the Southeast since April. The suspect, who is considered armed and dangerous, has targeted Jared Vault and other jewelry stores in Florida, Georgia, South Carolina, Tennessee, and North Carolina.\n\nAccording to the FBI, the suspect has stolen over $450,000 worth of jewelry in the six robberies, which have occurred in the following locations:\n\n* Jared Vault in Mebane, North Carolina (Monday)\n* Jared Vault in Tanger Outlets in Bluffton, South Carolina\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Modern Family star Ariel Winter has words for those who criticized the gold minidress she wore to an event for the show Wednesday night, People reports. Photos from the screening of the season eight finale showed that Winter's outfit stood out among the clothes worn by the other cast members who attended (Julie Bowen, for example, wore black slacks with a simple top, while Ty Burrell showed up in jeans). USA Today describes Winter's outfit like so: \"Mesh panels showed off her cleavage as well as the tops of her thighs.\" But some of the commentary on Winter's outfit went beyond simply calling the teen overdressed, with some going so far as to say she looked \"slutty.\" On Thursday night, Winter responded on Instagram. \"Why TF does anyone care that I didn't dress casual like everyone else for the panel? Why do I have to be like everyone else? Why can’t people just let other people feel good about themselves and do what they want?\" the 19-year-old wrote. \"WEAR WHATEVER YOU WANT PEOPLE! As long as you feel good about yourself that’s what matters. I know I did. Don’t ever let anyone stifle who you are and how you express yourself. Rant over.\" (Winter says her \"baby voice\" makes her boyfriend uncomfortable.)"], "length": 1415, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Ariel Winter Takes a Stand Against Haters and Embraces Self-Expression**\n\nActress Ariel Winter, known for her role in the hit TV show \"Modern Family,\" has been making headlines for her unapologetic attitude and fashion choices. Recently, she faced criticism for wearing a glamorous gold minidress to a panel event, with some commenters implying she was overdressed and others making sexist remarks.\n\nHowever, Winter refused to let the haters bring her down. She took to Instagram to clap back at her critics, asking \"Why TF does anyone care that I didn’t dress casual like everyone else for the panel?\" and declaring \"WEAR WHATEVER YOU WANT PEOPLE!\" She emphasized the importance of self-expression and individuality, urging her followers to \"let other people feel good about themselves and do what they want.\"\n\nThis isn't the first time Winter has spoken out against haters. In a previous interview, she discussed her social media presence and how she doesn't let trolls and critics get to her. She stated, \"If I can't win, I'm going to work on a relationship with myself, which I also think is the most important thing we can teach young women and men today.\"\n\nWinter's confidence and self-assurance have earned her a loyal following, and her message of self-acceptance and empowerment is inspiring fans around the world. As she so eloquently put it, \"As long as you feel good about yourself that’s what matters. I know I did. Don’t ever let anyone stifle who you are and how you express yourself.\""}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Will Smith stars as Nicky, an established con man who takes on a new apprentice, Australian newcomer Margot Robbie's Jess, in Focus. They both love the con game, but who's playing who? Here's what critics are saying: Richard Corliss at Time is a fan. \"The mix of longtime star and minx on the rise is one tasty element in the success of a movie that approaches the modest goals and effortless allure of a 60-year-old Hitchcock,\" he writes. Smith \"takes a welcome break from glowering sci-fi roles\" and writer-directors Glenn Ficarra and John Requa \"deserve some credit in letting Will be Will in the star's first charm barrage since 2005's Hitch.\" Steven Rea at the Philadelphia Inquirer admits Smith \"shows some of the movie-star wattage that's been missing from his recent pics. He charms.\" There's also \"combustible chemistry\" between Smith and Robbie, but the movie just \"disappoints,\" Rea writes. \"Even if you're willing to forgive its sinkhole plotholes and farthest-fetched conceits, the film ... ultimately makes no sense.\" Betsy Sharkey, on the other hand, says this \"rom-com-con\" is \"an irresistible reminder of all the reasons we first fell for the Fresh Prince so many years ago.\" Writing at the Los Angeles Times, she notes the romantic role of Nicky fits Smith perfectly, while Robbie is \"more than holding her own\" as his match. \"The scams are Rubik's Cube complicated, but what keeps you guessing is whether the romantic connection between Jess and Nicky is real or just another con.\" But Mick LaSalle at the San Francisco Chronicle isn't convinced. In a review titled \"Will Smith just stole $12 from your pocket,\" he argues Focus is \"ridiculous in every detail. It's a movie with no truth that teaches nothing and shows nothing, that has only its audacity to recommend it.\" Nicky and Jess lack \"impact,\" partly because \"Smith is not a strong leading man,\" he says. Viewers are left \"waiting for the trick, which is not the same as being fooled.\""], "length": 2254, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Focus: A Caper Film with a Star-Studded Cast**\n\nThe movie \"Focus\" has been making waves in Hollywood, with a star-studded cast and a unique blend of charm and wit. The film, directed by Glenn Ficarra and John Requa, follows the story of Nicky Spurgeon (played by Will Smith), a veteran con man who takes on a young apprentice, Jess Barrett (played by Margot Robbie). The movie is a caper film that explores the world of con artists, with a focus on the complex relationship between Nicky and Jess.\n\n**A Departure from Smith's Typical Roles**\n\n\"Focus\" marks a departure from Will Smith's typical roles, which often feature him as a macho, stoic character. In this film, Smith gets to play a more charming and suave character, using his natural charisma to convince audiences of his character's confidence and wit. This is a welcome change for Smith, who has been typecast in many of his previous roles.\n\n**A Star-Studded Cast**\n\nThe cast of \"Focus\" is impressive, with a range of talented actors bringing the characters to life. Margot Robbie, in particular, shines as Jess Barrett, a young and ambitious con artist who learns the ropes from Nicky. The chemistry between Smith and Robbie is undeniable, and their characters' complex relationship is at the heart of the film.\n\n**A Return to the Classic Caper Film**\n\n\"Focus\" is a classic caper film, with a nod to the likes of Alfred Hitchcock's \"To Catch a Thief\" and the iconic con artist movies of the 1950s and 60s. The film's tone is lighthearted and playful, with a focus on the wit and charm of the characters. The movie's success is a testament to the enduring appeal of the caper film genre, and the ability of a talented cast and crew to bring it to life.\n\n**A Modest but Enjoyable Film**\n\nWhile \"Focus\" may not be a groundbreaking or complex film, it is a modest but enjoyable movie that is sure to delight audiences. With its charming cast, witty dialogue, and lighthearted tone, \"Focus\" is a fun and engaging film that is perfect for a night out at the movies."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Tonight's Mega Millions prize could end up being the biggest US jackpot ever. It's already hit $636 million; by tonight, it could rise past the $656 million record, Reuters notes. Let's say, for the sake of argument, that you win it (you won't). When you can breathe again, you'll want to take quick action to ensure that you deal with your newfound millions safely and effectively. At CNN, Steve Almasy offers a checklist: First, sign the ticket. You don't want to risk losing it, or having a \"friend\" claim it. While you're at it, you might want to take a selfie with the ticket, then drop it in a safe deposit box. Then, seek out financial experts, but not too many—you want to keep the news quiet as long as possible. \"Start with one experienced attorney and look for a seasoned certified financial planner,\" Almasy writes. Big question: Is there any way you can legally remain anonymous? If you live in South Carolina or a handful of other states, the answer is yes, but generally you'll have a tough time hiding. You might want to hire a PR person. In fact, you might want to slip out of the country until the hubbub over the prize has died down. Didn't win? Don't forget to check if you've qualified for runner-up cash. Last year saw $800 million in unclaimed lottery winnings. Click for Almasy's full piece."], "length": 1747, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Mega Millions Jackpot Reaches Record-Breaking $636 Million**\n\nThe Mega Millions jackpot has reached a staggering $636 million, making it one of the largest lottery prizes in U.S. history. The record-breaking jackpot was won by at least two people who matched all the winning numbers: 8, 20, 14, 17, and 39, with a Megaball of 7. The winners will have the option to take the cash prize of $341.2 million or receive the estimated value of the cash option plus interest over 30 years.\n\n**What to Do If You Win**\n\nIf you're one of the lucky winners, here are some essential steps to take:\n\n1. **Sign the ticket**: Make sure to sign the ticket immediately to prevent anyone else from claiming it.\n2. **Contact experienced professionals**: Reach out to an attorney and a certified financial planner to help you navigate the process and make informed decisions about your winnings.\n3. **Plan for anonymity**: If possible, consider hiring a media consultant or PR flack to help you manage your newfound wealth and maintain your anonymity.\n4. **Take your time**: You have up to 180 days to claim your prize, so take your time and don't rush into any decisions.\n\n**The Frenzy Continues**\n\nThe Mega Millions jackpot has been growing rapidly, with ticket sales reaching a fever pitch over the weekend. According to Virginia Lottery Director Paula Otto, ticket buying reached 20% more than expected, pushing the prize closer to the record U.S. jackpot of $656 million. The odds of winning are mounting, and the drawing is expected to be one of the most watched in history.\n\n**Unclaimed Prizes**\n\nDespite the massive jackpot, there are still millions of dollars in unclaimed lottery prizes. Last year, $800 million in prizes went unclaimed, including the $1 million prize. If you're one of the lucky winners, make sure to check your tickets carefully to see if you've won a smaller prize.\n\n**The Future of the Jackpot**\n\nIf no one wins the jackpot on Tuesday, it will rise to at least $950 million for the next drawing on Friday. The Mega Millions game is a multi-state lottery, and the prize will continue to grow until someone wins. With the odds of winning mounting, it's anyone's guess who will be the next big winner."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["They volunteered to fight fires, but authorities say they were also purposely setting fires over a period of almost two years. After a tip led to a yearlong investigation, 10 volunteer firefighters in North Carolina's Robeson County were arrested Tuesday and charged with arson and related crimes, ABC 11 reports. So far there are 90 charges involved in the case, which involves firefighters from the Fairmont Rural Fire Department and the Orrum Fire Department, and officials say more charges are expected and more arrests could be made. \"I don't understand that. I mean, the job might be boring at times, but you don't go creating fires just to have something to put out,\" a local, who lives near the Fairmont firehouse, tells WRAL. The firefighters are accused of setting fires in woods and at abandoned structures; WRAL and ABC 11 both have the names of those accused—among them are a police officer and a Department of Corrections officer—and specific charges. Authorities say the alleged crimes cost taxpayers thousands of dollars."], "length": 1537, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Multiple Arrests Made in Robeson County Arson Investigation**\n\nA shocking investigation has led to the arrest of 10 volunteer firefighters in Robeson County, North Carolina, who are accused of intentionally setting fires to abandoned homes and woods over a two-year period. The investigation, which was led by the Robeson County Sheriff's Office, the State Bureau of Investigation, and the NC Forestry Service, resulted in 90 arson-related charges, with more expected.\n\nThe firefighters, who work with the Fairmont Rural Fire Department and the Orrum Fire Department, allegedly set fires to various locations, including:\n\n* Eldorado Rd., Fairmont, NC\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["\"Four trapezoidal bars aligned on a rectangular base\" may not be instantly recognizable. Put that setup in chocolate form, however, and many people would be able to distinguish it as a KitKat. Even if that's the case, the European Union's highest court just ruled that the shape of the sweet treat isn't unique enough to merit trademark protection, meaning other confectioners can potentially produce KitKat knockoffs, the Guardian reports. Nestle, which makes KitKats, has been fighting Cadbury (and then Mondelez, which ultimately bought Cadbury) in the courts for more than a decade on this issue. The BBC notes that Nestle first applied for trademark protection for the candy's shape in 2002, and it was granted that protection four years later. Cadbury's pushback began in 2007, and Wednesday's decision by the European Court of Justice essentially tells the EU trademark office it now has to \"reconsider\" its original decision. What that means, however, is that if the EU Intellectual Property Office finds, based on more recent evidence, that the KitKat bar's shape is recognizable in enough countries, it can retain the trademark protection, per Reuters. The brand name itself of \"KitKat\" is not being contested. (Atari accused Nestle of ripping off one of its popular video games in an ad for KitKat.)"], "length": 1828, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Kit Kat Shape Trademark Battle: A Long-Running Saga**\n\nA decade-long legal battle between Nestlé and Mondelez over the shape of KitKat finger wafers has taken a significant turn. The European Court of Justice (ECJ) has dismissed appeals from both companies, sending the case back to the EU's trademark office. The ruling means that the EU Intellectual Property Office (EUIPO) must review a 2012 decision to uphold Nestlé's trademark on the shape of the four-finger chocolate-covered wafer biscuit.\n\nThe case began in 2002 when Nestlé applied for a trademark in Europe for the KitKat shape. Mondelez, which owns Cadbury, objected to the application, arguing that the shape was not distinctive enough to be trademarked. The EU trademark office initially granted Nestlé the trademark, but Mondelez appealed, and the case has been ongoing ever since.\n\nThe ECJ's ruling found that Nestlé had failed to show that consumers in enough EU countries recognized the shape as distinctive. However, the court also dismissed Mondelez's appeal against some of the grounds for a lower EU court ruling in 2016. The outcome leaves open the possibility that the trademark agency could preserve protection for KitKat's shape, but only if new evidence is presented to show that the shape is distinctive to people in more countries.\n\nThe case has significant implications for brands operating across the EU single market, where there are varying histories in national markets. The dispute has also been mirrored by a similar case in the British courts, where Nestlé objected to a trademark for the purple color used by Cadbury to wrap its Dairy Milk chocolate bars.\n\nThe ruling has been seen as a victory for Mondelez, but the company's CEO has stated that the case is not over yet. Nestlé has also expressed its intention to continue fighting for its trademark. The outcome of the case will have significant implications for the chocolate industry, with the potential for copycat products to flood the market if the trademark is not upheld.\n\n**Key Players:**\n\n* Nestlé: The Swiss-based company that owns the KitKat brand\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Dinosaurs may have been much more like modern birds than we knew—and not just because some had feathers. A new study suggests that mighty dinosaurs of yore didn't roar, contrary to every dinosaur movie you've ever seen. Instead, they made a decidedly less scary sound called a \"closed-mouth vocalization.\" Think of a dove cooing or perhaps an ostrich doing this grunting thing. As the scientists explain in the journal Evolution, per UT News, the sound actually comes out of the neck area after air is pushed through an esophageal pouch. In fact, the animal's mouth is shut the whole time. Lots of birds do this today, and because birds descended from dinosaurs, scientists suspect they may have communicated in a similar way. The fossil record can't prove it, though the Washington Post notes that previous research meshes with the idea, including the belief that dinosaurs had air sacs instead of vocal chords. “A cool thing about this work is the demonstration that closed-mouth behavior evolved many times,” says the lead author. “That suggests it can emerge fairly easily and be incorporated into mating displays\" among different types of animals. A co-author says the study gives the impression of \"a very different Jurassic world. Not only were dinosaurs feathered, but they may have had bulging necks and made booming, closed-mouth sounds.\" But if so, the makers of Jurassic Park didn't get it all wrong: The hiss of the film's velociraptors came from a goose, per Vulture. (Scientists have grown chickens with dinosaur legs.)"], "length": 3836, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**The Roar of the T. Rex: A New Perspective**\n\nFor decades, the Tyrannosaurus Rex has been depicted as a ferocious creature with a thunderous roar, thanks in part to movies like \"Jurassic Park.\" However, new research suggests that the T. Rex's sound may have been far more subdued, with scientists proposing that it may have made sounds similar to those of modern birds, such as pigeons or ostriches.\n\nAccording to a study published in the journal Evolution, the T. Rex and other dinosaurs may have used a type of vocalization called \"closed-mouth vocalization,\" which involves making sounds through the skin in the neck area while keeping the beak closed. This type of vocalization is common in birds, particularly those with large body sizes, and is often used for mating displays or territorial defense.\n\nThe study, led by researchers from the University of Texas and Midwestern University, analyzed the distribution of closed-mouth vocalization in birds and other reptilian groups, and found that it has evolved at least 16 times in archosaurs, a group that includes birds, dinosaurs, and crocodiles. The researchers suggest that the capacity for closed-mouth vocalization may have been present in some extinct nonavian dinosaurs, and may have been used for similar purposes as in modern birds.\n\nIn contrast to the loud, open-mouthed roars often depicted in movies, closed-mouth vocalizations are typically quieter and lower in pitch. The study's authors propose that some dinosaurs may have made sounds similar to those of modern birds, such as the cooing of doves or the booming of ostriches.\n\nThe idea that dinosaurs may have made sounds similar to those of modern birds is not new, and has been suggested by other researchers in the past. However, the new study provides further evidence for this idea, and highlights the importance of understanding the evolution of vocalization in birds and other reptilian groups.\n\n**The Sounds of Jurassic Park**\n\nIn a related story, sound designer Gary Rydstrom has revealed the secrets behind the iconic sounds of the original \"Jurassic Park\" movie. Rydstrom, who won two Academy Awards for his work on the film, used a variety of animal sounds to create the distinctive noises of the movie's dinosaurs, including the T. Rex, Velociraptors, and Brachiosaurus.\n\nRydstrom used a range of animals to create the sounds, including tortoises, horses, geese, and even his own pets. He also used a technique called \""}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Cary Heath was arrested Monday at the Texas middle school where he teaches eighth-grade science a day after police say he murdered two of his neighbors, NBC DFW reports. According to the Fort Worth Star-Telegram, shots were fired shortly before 4am Sunday at a home down the street from where Heath lives. Police arrived to find two men dead in the home's driveway. \"From my understanding, the guy had an assault rife,\" a neighbor who woke up to gunshots tells NBC. \"From the looks of it, due to the shell casings, he unloaded the whole clip.\" Police aren't discussing a motive for the killings, though the first 911 call from the scene was regarding a robbery. The 35-year-old Heath is being held on $1 million bond and has been placed on administrative leave. The school notified parents of the situation via automated phone messages, WFAA reports. One mother says \"it's very scary\" that someone suspected of murder was teaching children. \"They should be safe over there to get their education and come home,\" she says. Heath served in the Air Force for 13 years before recently becoming a teacher."], "length": 875, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Double Homicide in Fort Worth: Teacher Arrested**\n\nOn Sunday, October 23, 2016, a couple was found shot to death in their home in the 800 block of Buffalo Springs Drive in Fort Worth, Texas. The victims' names have not been released. Police were initially called to the scene at 4 a.m. after a report of a robbery, but upon arrival, they found two bodies outside the home.\n\nA witness, Jordan Milner, who lives across the street from the crime scene, reported hearing gunshots and seeing a neighbor, later identified as Cary Joseph Heath, 35, unloading a rifle and killing the two people who lived next door. Heath, a teacher at Permenter Middle School in Cedar Hill, was arrested on Monday, October 24, 2016, on a capital murder charge at the school, where he was employed.\n\nHeath was booked on a $1 million bond and is currently being held at the Fort Worth jail. He has been placed on administrative leave by the Cedar Hill Independent School District, which also notified parents of the incident via an automated phone message. However, some parents reported not receiving the message, raising concerns about the safety of students at the school.\n\nHeath's bio on the school's website has been taken down, but it stated that he was an eighth-grade science teacher in his first year at the middle school and had previously served 13 years in the U.S. Air Force. Police are still investigating the motive behind the double murder and have not released any further information.\n\nThe arrest of Heath, a teacher and a member of the community, has shocked parents and residents, who are concerned about the safety of their children. \"It's very scary,\" said parent Teresa Moreno. \"You have all the kids over there, you don't want anybody's kids to get hurt, whether it's yours or anybody else's. They should be safe over there to get their education and come home.\"\n\nThe Internet Archive, also known as the Wayback Machine, has been collecting data on the internet since 1996, including information on the Fort Worth police department's investigation into the double homicide."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["In an attempt to bury a relic of its past, Vanderbilt University announced Monday that it will pay more than $1 million to remove the word \"Confederate\" from one of its dorms, the Tennessean reports. Chancellor Nicholas S. Zeppos called the inscription on Confederate Memorial Hall \"a reminder of racism, slavery and a very, very bloody Civil War.\" The Nashville university's efforts to change the name in 2002 were halted when the United Daughters of the Confederacy sued. The group’s $50,000 donation in 1933 helped build the dorm. A state appeals court ruled the building could be renamed Memorial Hall only after Vanderbilt gave the United Daughters back their money—$1.2 million in today’s dollars. The university has the cash, thanks to a raft of anonymous contributors who wanted the tie to America’s painful past broken for good. \"It's a symbol that is, for many people, deeply offensive and painful,\" Zeppos told the Tennessean. \"And to walk past it or to have to live in that space is really something that I just don't think is acceptable.” Yet tampering with Confederate imagery remains controversial, and a new state law makes it harder to do so, the AP reports. Efforts by Middle Tennessee State University in Murfreesboro to remove the name of Confederate Gen. Nathan Bedford Forrest from a building could face a tougher climb under the Tennessee Heritage Protection Act, which now requires a vote of two-thirds of the state’s Historical Commission rather than a simple majority."], "length": 1995, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Vanderbilt University Renames Confederate Memorial Hall, Pays $1.2 Million to United Daughters of the Confederacy**\n\nVanderbilt University has announced that it will rename its Confederate Memorial Hall, a dormitory on campus, to simply \"Memorial Hall\" after a 14-year effort to change the name. The university will pay $1.2 million to the United Daughters of the Confederacy, the organization that donated $50,000 to the building's construction in 1933, to relinquish its naming rights. The decision comes as universities across the country grapple with the legacy of the Confederacy and its symbols.\n\nVanderbilt Chancellor Nicholas Zeppos said that the name \"Confederate Memorial Hall\" is a \"symbol and a reminder of racism, slavery, and a very, very bloody Civil War\" and that it is not acceptable to have it on campus. The university has been referring to the building as Memorial Hall since 2002, but was blocked in court from changing the name due to the donation from the United Daughters of the Confederacy.\n\nThe $1.2 million payment was made possible by anonymous donors, and Zeppos said that the university is focused on \"moving Vanderbilt forward\" rather than on what the Daughters of the Confederacy will do with the funds. The decision is seen as a positive step for the university, which has been working to address diversity and inclusion issues on campus.\n\nThe renaming of Confederate Memorial Hall is part of a larger trend of universities across the country re-evaluating the implications of their buildings' names. Middle Tennessee State University, for example, is seeking to remove the name of Confederate Gen. Nathan Bedford Forrest from one of its buildings, but faces a tougher hurdle due to a new Tennessee law that makes it more difficult to remove Confederate symbols and statues from public places.\n\nThe controversy over Confederate symbols and statues has been ongoing since last year's slaying of nine black churchgoers in Charleston, South Carolina. Republican Gov. Bill Haslam has supported removing the Capitol bust of Forrest, and the issue has sparked debate across the South.\n\nThe renaming of Confederate Memorial Hall at Vanderbilt University is seen as a significant step forward for the university and for the country as a whole. It demonstrates a commitment to diversity and inclusion, and sets a precedent for other universities to follow."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A classy move by a Florida cop is making headlines: Laurie Graber, an officer in Plantation, got the call for a particularly low-level crime—someone had ripped the engagement ring off the finger of an 87-year-old woman bedridden with Alzheimer's in a hospital, reports Fox News. By the bruises on Betty Wagner's finger, it wasn't an easy job, and no wonder—the ring had been there for 67 years. \"I just couldn't imagine what kind of depravity you would have to have to take something off of someone so vulnerable,\" Graber tells WSVN. Upon leaving the hospital, Graber went to JCPenney and shelled out for a replacement ring with her own money. She brought it back to the nurses' station with a note, reports AOL.com: \"It's not much, it's not the same. But 67 years of a promise kept should be recognized.\" Husband Arthur, who placed the original on his soon-to-be wife's hand in 1946, says the gesture has helped him deal with the theft. (Another Florida officer bought groceries for a mom caught shoplifting.)"], "length": 1212, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Florida Police Officer Goes Above and Beyond to Replace Elderly Woman's Stolen Engagement Ring**\n\nIn a heartwarming story, a Florida police officer has made headlines for her kind gesture in replacing an elderly woman's stolen engagement ring. The ring, which had been on the woman's finger for 67 years, was taken from her while she was sedated in a hospital bed.\n\nBetty Wagner, 87, has been suffering from Alzheimer's disease and was being cared for at Westside Regional Medical Center in Plantation, Florida. Her husband, Arthur, reported that the ring was stolen from her finger, leaving behind only bruises and a wedding band.\n\nOfficer Laurie Graber was sent to investigate the theft and was moved by the couple's story. She learned that the ring had been purchased by Arthur in 1946 for $400 and had been a symbol of their love for 67 years.\n\nMoved by the couple's story, Officer Graber took it upon herself to replace the stolen ring. She purchased a small heart-shaped diamond ring from J.C. Penney and left it with the nursing staff at the hospital, along with a signed note that read: \"It's not much, it's not the same. But 67 years of a promise kept should be recognized.\"\n\nThe new ring has brought some comfort to Arthur and Betty, who are still waiting for the return of their original engagement ring. The police are still investigating the theft and are reviewing surveillance footage to identify the person responsible.\n\nOfficer Graber's selfless act has been praised by the community, with many hailing her as a hero. Her actions are a testament to the kindness and compassion of law enforcement officers and serve as a reminder of the importance of keeping promises and cherishing love and relationships.\n\n**Key Facts:**\n\n* The stolen engagement ring was purchased by Arthur Wagner in 1946 for $400.\n* The ring was taken from Betty Wagner's finger while she was sedated in a hospital bed.\n* Officer Laurie Graber replaced the stolen ring with a new one from J.C. Penney.\n* The new ring was valued at around $400, a fraction of the original ring's value of $4,500.\n* The police are still investigating the theft and reviewing surveillance footage to identify the person responsible."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Not a huge surprise: Lawyers for Boston marathon bomber Dzhokhar Tsarnaev today appealed his death-penalty conviction, arguing that \"continuous and unrelenting publicity\" biased jurors and made a fair trial in Boston impossible, NBC News reports. \"Put simply, prejudicial media coverage, events, and environment saturated greater Boston, including the social networks of actual trial jurors, and made it an improper venue for the trial of this case,\" say the papers. The lawyers want another trial at a new location, the AP reports. Earlier this year, the Guardian reminded us that Tsarnaev's appeals will likely delay his punishment for \"at least a decade.\" The April 15, 2013, bomb strike in Boston left three dead and more than 200 maimed or injured."], "length": 1067, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Boston Marathon Bomber Dzhokhar Tsarnaev Seeks New Trial and Appeals Death Sentence**\n\nDzhokhar Tsarnaev, the Boston Marathon bomber, is seeking a new trial and appealing his death sentence, citing widespread outrage and prejudicial media coverage in the city where the attack occurred. Tsarnaev's lawyers argue that the trial should have been held outside of Boston due to the intense media attention and public outcry, which they claim made it impossible for him to receive a fair trial. They also argue that the death penalty is unconstitutional, citing a recent dissent by US Supreme Court Justice Stephen Breyer.\n\nTsarnaev was sentenced to death in June for his role in the 2013 bombing, which killed three people and injured over 260 others. His lawyers claim that the trial was unfair due to the \"continuous and unrelenting publicity\" about the bombings, the defendant and his family, and wrenching survivor stories. They also argue that the jurors, who were users of social media, would have been unable to avoid coverage of the case.\n\nThe appeal claims that the trial venue was improper due to the saturation of Boston with prejudicial media coverage, events, and environment, which made it impossible for Tsarnaev to receive an impartial hearing. The defense had repeatedly asked for a change of venue before the trial began, but the requests were rejected by the judge.\n\nTsarnaev's fate will likely take years to reach its conclusion, as only three of 340 prisoners sentenced to federal death row have been executed in the last 50 years. The appeals process is expected to take at least 10 years, and Tsarnaev's team of lawyers has already indicated two strong lines of likely appeal: that the trial should have been held in a different location and that the defense was given insufficient time to mount a full argument in mitigation.\n\nThe case highlights the complexities and challenges of the federal death penalty, which has become increasingly unusual in modern times. The US government has executed only three prisoners in the past 50 years, and the application of capital punishment has been maverick in its deployment, with almost half of the 62 inmates on federal death row being put there by just three states: Texas, Missouri, and Virginia."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["InterContinentalExchange Inc. has agreed to buy NYSE Euronext, the company that owns the New York Stock Exchange, for $8.2 billion, the two companies announced this morning. ICE, an Atlanta-based commodity exchange, says it intends to leave the NYSE's branding alone, and would explore an IPO for Euronext, spinning it off into a continental European entity, the Wall Street Journal reports. It will pay $33.12 a share for the elder exchange, or about a 37% premium on yesterday's close. You might remember ICE from its last attempt to buy the NYSE, which failed thanks to antitrust concerns from regulators. While the 12-year-old company may lack the name recognition of the iconic NYSE, it's much larger in terms of market capitalization, at $9.3 billion to NYSE Euronext's $5.8 billion—a sign, the New York Times observes, of how completely commodity trading has outpaced stock trading in relevance."], "length": 1042, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**$8.2 Billion Merger: IntercontinentalExchange to Acquire NYSE Euronext**\n\nIn a major deal, IntercontinentalExchange Inc. (ICE) is set to acquire NYSE Euronext, the owner of the New York Stock Exchange, in an $8.2 billion takeover. The merger, which is expected to be announced as early as Thursday, would create a global market leader in derivatives trading, eclipsing the market for stocks.\n\nUnder the terms of the deal, ICE would offer approximately $33 per share, with two-thirds of the payment in stock, representing a 37% premium to NYSE Euronext's closing stock price on Wednesday. The acquisition would link NYSE Euronext to one of the industry's fastest-growing exchanges, ICE, which has some of the highest profit margins in the business.\n\nThe merger would mark a significant shift in the financial industry, as the trading of stocks becomes a less attractive business. The New York Stock Exchange, once the public image of a stock market, is now responsible for only about 11% of all stock trading, while NYSE Euronext's electronic Arca platform accounts for another 12%. In contrast, the trading of futures and options, where ICE is focused, has seen a decline in volume, but less than in stocks.\n\nThe combined company would own 14 stock and futures exchanges and five clearing operations, making it the largest player in the industry. The takeover would also seal the triumph of electronic trading over traditional \"open outcry\" floor trading, which has long dominated financial markets.\n\nRegulatory approval and shareholder support are still needed for the deal to go through. However, the merger is expected to face fewer antitrust concerns than previous proposals, as ICE focuses on commodities like oil, natural gas, and cotton, while NYSE Euronext plies mainly in stock and stock options and derivatives.\n\nThe acquisition would expand ICE, which has a little more than 1,000 employees, to a company with 3,077 employees, making it a significant player in the industry. Other exchanges, including the CME Group and the Hong Kong and Singaporean exchanges, may express opposition to the deal, citing antitrust concerns and nationalist concerns."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Apparently still smarting from some of Chris Rock's Oscars barbs, the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences invited 683 people—many of them women and minorities—to join in an unprecedented move Wednesday, Reuters reports. The voting group behind the Oscars is largely old, white, and male and was lambasted this year with the hashtag #OscarsSoWhite after two years in a row of all-white acting nominees. In response, the academy is attempting to—as the AV Club puts it—\" add seemingly goddamn everyone it had, for one reason or another, forgotten to invite into its membership.” The actors, directors, and others invited Wednesday include Idris Elba, Eva Mendes, Oscar Isaac, John Boyega, Ice Cube, Greta Gerqig, Michael B. Jordan, Vivica A. Fox, the Wachowskis, James Wan, Luis Guzmán, Kate Beckinsale, Park Chan-wook, James Hong, Michelle Rodriguez, and not one, not two, but three Wayans brothers. Of the new invitees, 46% are women and 41% are people of color. If all 683 accept their invite, women would account for 27% of the more than 7,000 academy members (up from 25%) and minorities would total 11% (up from 8%). “I'm especially happy to be part of such a diverse group. I actually want to hang out and watch movies with most of the people on this list,\" Arab-German director Lexi Alexander tells the Los Angeles Times. “To be honest, I cried a few tears when I started to get congratulation tweets in Arabic.” Other invitees took to Twitter to share similar sentiments. “Excited to use my vote to nominate talent that reflects the real world we live in—DIVERSITY,\" tweets Brie Larson, who won best actress at this year's Oscars."], "length": 682, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Academy of Motion Pictures Arts and Sciences Takes Steps to Increase Diversity**\n\nIn response to growing criticism and concerns about the lack of diversity within its membership, the Academy of Motion Pictures Arts and Sciences has taken a significant step towards increasing representation. The organization, responsible for awarding the Oscars, has sent out 683 membership invitations to film industry professionals, with a focus on adding more women and people of color to its ranks.\n\nThe list of invitees is a diverse and impressive group, featuring a range of international heavyweights, big-name directors, and up-and-coming talent. Notable directors invited include Catherine Breillat, Park Chan-wook, and Ryan Coogler, while the list of acting invitees includes Luis Guzmán, Idris Elba, and Regina King.\n\nThis move is a direct response to the #OscarsSoWhite movement, which was sparked by the all-white slate of acting nominees in 2015 and 2016. The movement, led by April Reign and supported by celebrities like Spike Lee and Jada Pinkett Smith, called for greater diversity within the film industry and the Academy. In response, the Academy committed to doubling the number of women and people of color within its membership by 2020.\n\nThe release of the latest list of invitees marks a significant step towards achieving this goal, with almost half of the 683 invitations going to women and 41% to people of color. The Academy's efforts to increase diversity are seen as a positive step towards creating a more inclusive and representative film industry."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The three American hikers detained by Iran weren’t captured in Iraq, says Sarah Shourd: They were beckoned over the border by an armed soldier, she tells the New York Times in an effort to set the WikiLeaks version of the story straight. “We did not actually enter Iran until he gestured to us. We were confused and worried and wanted to go back.\" In Shourd's fullest account of her ordeal to date, she goes into detail about their fateful overnight camping trip, describing \"scores of campfires\" near the waterfall they were hiking to—which abutted Iran. “I think we were extremely unlucky,” says Shourd. “I guess I never believed there would be so many hundreds of people close to a border.” Her interview with the Times comes ahead of a court date for her fellow hikers, which was set for Saturday but has been delayed by Iran until Shourd “can return to Iran or,” if she refuses, her case can “be dealt with differently,” said an Iranian official. But her lawyer says the trial should go forward; neither he nor Shourd, he tells Reuters, were notified of the change."], "length": 1346, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Iran Delays Trial of American Hikers, Amid Tensions with US**\n\nIran has delayed the trial of two American hikers, Shane Bauer and Josh Fattal, who were arrested near the Iraqi border in July 2009. The trial was scheduled to begin on Saturday, but a judiciary spokesman said it has been postponed due to the absence of a third suspect, Sarah Shourd, who was released on bail in September and returned to the US. Shourd's lawyer said he was not aware of the trial delay and questioned the decision, saying it was \"illogical\" and that the court should have notified him or Shourd directly.\n\nThe delay comes as tensions between Iran and the US remain high, with the US pushing for Iran to return to talks about its nuclear activities. Iran has said it is ready to restart talks, but has yet to agree on a date and has suggested that the talks should not address the nuclear issue.\n\nThe case of the American hikers has been a point of contention between the two countries, with the US maintaining that they were innocent hikers who were arrested by mistake. The US State Department has never suggested that the hikers were spies, as Iran has claimed, and has instead called for their release.\n\nIn a recent report, the US military suggested that the hikers had intentionally crossed into Iran to create publicity, but the hikers and their supporters have denied this claim. Sarah Shourd, who was released on bail, said that the hikers had no idea they were near the border and had not been warned about anything.\n\nThe hikers' story began as a relaxed camping trip in the Kurdish mountains, where they had been hiking and enjoying the scenery. However, they were eventually arrested by Iranian guards and taken to Evin prison, where Bauer and Fattal remain.\n\nThe case has also highlighted the issue of Iranian prisoners held in US jails, with Iran's Foreign Ministry spokesman saying that the country will pursue all channels to secure their release. One Iranian prisoner, Shahrazad Mir Gholikhan, has spoken out about her treatment in US custody, claiming that she was tortured and mistreated.\n\nThe delay in the trial of the American hikers is seen as a further complication in the already tense relations between Iran and the US. The case is likely to remain a point of contention between the two countries, with the US pushing for the hikers' release and Iran maintaining that they are spies."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A military coup is under way in Mali, as renegade soldiers stormed the presidential palace in the capital of Bamako today, and soon afterward appeared on state TV to announce that they had booted the president and taken over the country, the BBC reports. The soldiers said they were suspending the constitution, dissolving its institutions and instituting a nationwide curfew. They said they were taking power because of President Amadou Toumani Toure's failure to defeat a rebellion by the Taureg tribes in the north, the AP reports. The military “has decided to assume its responsibilities and end the incompetent and disavowed regime of Amadou Toumani Toure,” the troops said, adding that it “does not in any way aim to confiscate power, and we solemnly swear to return power to a democratically elected president as soon as national unity and territorial integrity are established.” The mutiny comes just a month before Toure was set to leave office legally."], "length": 2184, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Mali Coup: Soldiers Seize Control of Presidential Palace**\n\nIn a shocking turn of events, soldiers in Mali have stormed the presidential palace and announced that they have seized control of the country, ousting President Amadou Toumani Toure just one month before he was due to step down at the end of his legal term. The coup is a major setback for one of the region's few established democracies, and has been condemned by the West African regional bloc ECOWAS and the international community.\n\nThe mutiny began on Wednesday when troops at a military camp in the capital, Bamako, started firing into the air in protest over the government's handling of a Tuareg rebellion in the north. The rebellion, which began in mid-January, has been fueled by the return of Tuareg fighters from Libya who had fought in the late Libyan leader Moammar Gadhafi's army. The government has been criticized for its mishandling of the crisis, and the soldiers claim that they are taking control to restore order and stability.\n\nThe soldiers, who have formed a new committee called the National Committee for the Reestablishment of Democracy and the Restoration of the State (CNRDR), have imposed a nationwide curfew and suspended the constitution. They claim that they will hand over power to a democratically elected government once national unity and territorial integrity are established.\n\nThe whereabouts of President Toure are unknown, and a loyalist military source close to the president has told AFP that he is well and in a safe location. However, it is unclear whether the mutinous soldiers have complete control of the presidential palace or have the support of all the Malian forces.\n\nThe international community has condemned the coup, with the US and France urging the soldiers and government to resolve their dispute through peaceful means. The Kenyan government has also confirmed that its foreign minister and three officials are safe in their Bamako hotel after being stranded in Mali.\n\nThe coup has significant implications for the region, as Mali has been a model of democratic rule for the past 20 years. The Tuareg rebellion has forced tens of thousands of people to flee, and has prompted fierce criticism of the government's handling of the crisis. The situation is currently unclear and unfolding quickly, and it remains to be seen how the crisis will be resolved.\n\n**Key Developments:**\n\n* Soldiers storm presidential palace and announce coup\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The internet loves obsessing over Game of Thrones, so Hello Giggles wants to know why more people aren't talking about the fact that \"Tommen is also his own cousin.\" Dean-Charles Chapman currently plays King Tommen Baratheon on the show, a role he took over from a younger actor in 2014. But he also played Tommen's cousin, Martyn Lannister, in a few episodes of the show back in the third season, Mashable explains. Hello Giggles thinks the recasting makes complete sense. “Given the Lannister family’s penchant for incest, having several people in that lineage look the same (like exactly the same) would not be a surprise.” Poor Martyn was killed off after being taken hostage by Robb Stark, and Bustle thinks Chapman better hope for a third role as Tommen is \"probably doomed just like his look-alike cousin.\""], "length": 827, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Game of Thrones Actor Dean-Charles Chapman Played Two Roles in the Show**\n\nDean-Charles Chapman, the actor who played King Tommen Baratheon in Game of Thrones, has been revealed to have played another role in the show. Chapman, who was only 14 when he started playing Tommen in 2014, had previously appeared in the show as Martyn Lannister in Season 3. Martyn was a young lordling who was taken as a hostage by Robb Stark's army and later murdered by Lord Rickard Karstark.\n\nThe revelation was made by fans who noticed the similarities between Chapman's two characters. Chapman's appearance as Martyn in Season 3 was brief, with him appearing in only two episodes. However, his connection to the Lannister family was significant, as Martyn was a son of Kevan Lannister, Tywin's younger brother.\n\nThis discovery has sparked interest in the show's casting and the fact that Chapman was able to play two roles in the show, including actual family members. The Lannister family's penchant for incest has led to several characters sharing similar physical characteristics, making it possible for Chapman to play multiple roles.\n\nThe news has also sparked a discussion about the show's casting and the fact that many fans may have missed this detail. Chapman's IMDb listing for the show confirms his appearances as both Tommen and Martyn.\n\nOverall, this discovery has added another layer of interest to the show's complex characters and storylines, and has highlighted the talent of Dean-Charles Chapman in playing multiple roles in the show."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["It's all up to President Trump now. The House Intelligence Committee voted along party lines Monday to release a controversial memo that alleges wrongdoing on the part of the Justice Department and the FBI in regard to the Russia investigation, reports Politico. Democrats are fuming over the decision. Trump now has five days to decide whether to allow the memo to be released; his approval is necessary because it's a classified document. The memo alleges that the FBI and the Justice Department improperly used government surveillance during the investigation into Russian election interference and contacts with Trump's campaign, per the AP. The vote to release the memo comes after committee Republicans, led by chairman Rep. Devin Nunes, pushed for its disclosure. The memo addresses a dossier of allegations against Trump compiled by a former British spy, and questions over whether it was improperly used to obtain surveillance warrants. The panel's ranking Democrat, Rep. Adam Schiff, announced the vote results Monday while criticizing the Republicans' decision. Democrats generally say the memo cherry-picks facts and presents a distorted view of things."], "length": 1655, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**House Intelligence Committee Votes to Release Controversial Memo Alleging FBI Misconduct**\n\nThe House Intelligence Committee voted along party lines to release a classified memo that alleges misconduct by senior FBI officials involved in the Russia probe. The memo, written by Republicans, claims that the FBI and Justice Department improperly used government surveillance during the investigation into Russian election interference and contacts with President Donald Trump's campaign.\n\nThe vote comes after a contentious debate between Republicans and Democrats, with the latter arguing that the memo mischaracterizes intelligence and is an attempt to distract from special counsel Robert Mueller's probe into Russia's interference in the 2016 election. The memo has been a source of escalating partisan tension in Congress, with Republicans arguing that it shows wrongdoing by FBI officials involved in surveillance of a Trump campaign aide.\n\nThe memo has been shared with FBI Director Christopher Wray and Deputy Attorney General Rod Rosenstein, but they have expressed concerns about its accuracy and potential impact on national security. Wray has asked to review the memo and express his concerns to the committee, but Republicans have rejected this request.\n\nThe White House has said that President Trump favors \"full transparency\" and has urged the Executive Branch to cooperate with Congress to the fullest extent possible. However, the President has not yet decided whether to release the memo, and has five days to object to the committee's request or clear the way for its release.\n\nThe controversy surrounding the memo has sparked concerns about the politicization of the intelligence process and the potential impact on the Mueller probe. Some Republicans, including Rep. Trey Gowdy, have urged caution and warned against using the memo to interfere with Mueller's investigation.\n\n**Key Players:**\n\n* Rep. Devin Nunes (R-CA), Chairman of the House Intelligence Committee\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The US in early July hit $34 billion of Chinese goods with an extra 25% tariff; China responded in kind. CNN reports round two could see the US slap tariffs on another $16 billion in goods as soon as this week, but it's a much bigger figure that's attracting attention Wednesday. Bloomberg cites sources who say the White House is thinking about upping the ante and imposing not an additional 10% tariff on $200 billion worth of Chinese goods but an additional 25%. CNN echoes that news via its source. Bloomberg frames the move, which would be revealed in the next couple of weeks, as intended to \"force officials back to the negotiating table through threats of even higher tariffs\"; the last high-level negotiations took place nearly two months ago. A Chinese foreign ministry rep responded to the news thusly, per the AP: \"If the United States takes further measures that escalate the situation, China will definitely fight back. We are determined to safeguard our legitimate and lawful rights and interests.\""], "length": 1393, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Trade War Escalation: US and China Engage in High-Stakes Negotiations**\n\nThe ongoing trade war between the United States and China has taken a dramatic turn, with both sides engaging in high-stakes negotiations to avert a full-blown trade war. The Trump administration has announced plans to raise tariffs on $200 billion worth of Chinese goods to 25% from 10%, a move that has been met with a warning from China that it will retaliate if Washington imposes new trade penalties.\n\nThe escalation of the trade war has led to a significant increase in tensions between the two nations, with both sides imposing tariffs on each other's goods. The US has already imposed 25% tariffs on $34 billion worth of Chinese goods, while China has responded with its own tariffs on $34 billion worth of US goods. A second round of tariffs on products worth $16 billion could take effect as soon as this week.\n\nDespite the tensions, there are signs that both sides are willing to engage in negotiations to resolve the dispute. Representatives of US Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin and Chinese Vice Premier Liu He are reportedly having private conversations to restart talks. However, the negotiations are complicated by a harder line taken by US Trade Representative Robert Lighthizer, who has jurisdiction over the US's 301 investigation that sparked the tariffs.\n\nThe US is seeking certain concessions from China, including the reduction of the US trade deficit with China, in exchange for backing off additional tariffs. However, China has vowed to respond with the same amount of tariffs on US products if the US imposes new penalties.\n\nThe trade war has had significant implications for the global economy, with the International Monetary Fund warning that it may undermine the strongest economic upswing in years. The US and China have struggled to find a starting point for a new round of talks, with Chinese and US officials giving little recent indication in public that a restart to negotiations might be in the offing.\n\nIn a sign of the trade standoff's impact on Chinese politics, the Politburo has signaled that policy makers will focus more on supporting economic growth amid risks from a campaign to reduce debt and the dispute with Trump. The communique, which followed a meeting of the country's 25 most senior leaders led by Xi, said the nation's campaign to reduce leverage will continue at a measured pace while improving economic policies to make them more forward-looking, flexible and effective in the second half of 2018.\n\nThe outcome of the negotiations remains uncertain, but one thing is clear: the trade war between the US and China"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Sony today unveiled its next-generation gaming system, PlayStation 4, saying the console will be part of a new ecosystem focused on hardware, software, and \"the fastest, most powerful gaming network.\" The new console is the Japanese electronics giant's first major game machine since the PlayStation 3 went on sale in 2006. Today's unveiling is Sony's attempt to steal the spotlight, at least until Microsoft unveils its next Xbox in June at the E3 video game expo in Los Angeles. Among the PS4's revisions is an updated controller that adds a touchpad, motion control, and a \"share\" button. The controller also features a light bar, which means a new PlayStation camera can more easily track the device. Sony has struggled lately to keep up with Microsoft and other rivals such as Apple and Samsung. The company is promising nifty mobile devices, sophisticated digital cameras, and other gadgetry as part of its comeback effort. More details at Gizmodo and TechCrunch."], "length": 1594, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**PlayStation 4: A Next-Gen Console with a Focus on Gaming Experience**\n\nSony has unveiled the PlayStation 4 (PS4), a next-generation gaming console that promises to revolutionize the gaming experience. The PS4 is designed to shift the focus from the living room to the gamer, allowing players to play wherever and whenever they want. The console is built around a powerful x86-based processor, 8-core CPU/GPU, and 8 GB of unified GDDR5 memory, making it a powerful and accessible system for game developers.\n\n**Key Features:**\n\n* **Controller:** The DualShock 4 controller features a touchpad, share button, headphone jack, and a light bar that tracks movement with a PlayStation Move-style controller.\n* **Graphics:** The PS4 boasts impressive graphics capabilities, with demos showcasing stunning visuals, including realistic lighting effects, massive cityscapes, and detailed facial graphics.\n* **Social Gaming:** The PS4 introduces a new social gaming interface, allowing players to share gameplay videos, watch \"celebrities\" gaming, and connect with friends through real names and profile pictures.\n* **Cloud Gaming:** Sony's PlayStation Cloud service will enable streaming access to old titles without the need for discs, although details on how it will work are still unclear.\n* **Background Downloading:** The PS4 will allow games to be downloaded in the background, enabling players to play games while they are being transferred from Sony's servers.\n* **Remote Play:** The PS4 will support remote play, allowing players to continue playing on another screen, with the Vita being the go-to small-screen option.\n* **Personalization:** The PS4 will study players' downloading and playing habits to predict the next titles they will want and download them ahead of time.\n\n**Comparison to PS3:**\n\nThe PS4 is an evolution of the PS3, with improved graphics, faster processing, and expanded features. While it may not be a revolutionary leap forward, the PS4 promises to deliver a more immersive and social gaming experience.\n\n**Release Date and Price:**\n\nThe PS4 is set to release in stores \"Holiday 2013,\" but the price has not been announced."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Scientists at UC Berkeley have made a major advancement in the field of mind reading, reconstructing YouTube videos based on brain scans from people who’d seen them. Researchers would put subjects into an MRI machine and track their brain activity as they viewed videos. Once they’d build a model of how a subject’s mind processed the video, “we could read brain activity for that subject and run it backward … to try to uncover what the viewer saw,” one study coauthor tells ABC News. Using the scans they were then able to reproduce the videos—though they’re blurry. “This is a major leap,” the co-author says. He thinks the technique could eventually be used to reconstruct dreams or memories, if it turns out the brain processes those things similarly to how it processes movies. “It’s our next line of research.” (More details on how the experiment worked, along with a video, at PC Magazine.)"], "length": 1188, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Breakthrough in Brain-Computer Interface Technology**\n\nResearchers from the University of California, Berkeley, have made significant strides in developing a brain-computer interface technology that can read and reconstruct visual experiences from brain activity. In a groundbreaking study, the team used functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) to record brain activity of subjects as they watched YouTube videos. They then developed a computer model that matched features of the videos with patterns of brain activity, allowing them to reconstruct the videos from the brain data.\n\nThe technology, which is still in its early stages, has the potential to revolutionize the way we understand and interact with the brain. The researchers believe that it could one day be used to:\n\n* Reconstruct internal imagery, including dreams and memories\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["We shouldn't be too worried about the sequester—after all, Congress doesn't exactly seem to be recoiling at the thought. And Roger Simon has a theory on why: Lawmakers' own salaries aren't getting cut, he explains at Politico. Sure, their staffers are facing furloughs that amount to a 20% pay decrease, but members of Congress will keep collecting six-figure salaries. Of course our lawmakers \"might have to figure out how to put those plastic coffee pods in the machines themselves, but these people are not fools,\" he writes. \"They will order out. If they can figure out how to work the telephones.\" In fact, House Republicans are positively excited about the sequester, writes Dana Milbank in the Washington Post, noting their agenda yesterday consisted only of renaming a California NASA research center. They're \"pursuing a considered strategy of deliberate idleness,\" after finally realizing that doing nothing means they get \"a 2.5% cut in all federal spending without coughing up a single dollar in tax increases. They have learned to stop worrying and love the sequester.\" Click for Milbank's full piece; Simon's humorous take is here."], "length": 1365, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Congress in Limbo: Sequester Looms as Republicans Refuse to Budge**\n\nAs the March 1 deadline for the sequester approaches, Congress remains in a state of inaction, with Republicans refusing to consider alternative spending cuts and tax increases to replace the automatic cuts. The sequester, which was designed to be a last resort to force Congress to make tough decisions, is now being touted as a victory by Republicans, who see it as a way to cut spending without raising taxes.\n\nMeanwhile, the House of Representatives has been in session for only 20 days this year, with many of those days spent on ceremonial bills and pro-forma sessions. Lawmakers are scheduled to be out of town on Friday, when the sequester is set to take effect, and are planning another recess at the end of March, when the federal government is due to shut down for lack of funding.\n\nThe sequester will cut $42.7 billion from the national defense budget, but will also affect a wide range of other programs, including border security, airport security, Head Start, public housing support, NASA, special education, the FBI, and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. However, the salaries of senators and representatives will not be cut, and congressional staffers will only face a 20 percent pay cut through furloughs.\n\nThe renaming of the Dryden Flight Research Center to the Neil A. Armstrong Flight Research Center was passed by the House, but was seen as a symbolic gesture by Democrats, who noted the irony of renaming a NASA facility while cutting its budget. The bill was sponsored by Majority Whip Kevin McCarthy (R-Calif.) and was passed with little debate, despite the fact that it could have been done with a simple voice vote.\n\nOverall, the sequester and the inaction of Congress have left many Americans feeling frustrated and disillusioned with the political process. As one commentator noted, \"Politicians have lost their power to frighten me. I no longer believe the sky is falling.\""}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Sen. Elizabeth Warren went after Wells Fargo CEO John Stumpf during a Senate hearing Tuesday with the kind of heat she usually reserves for Donald Trump tweetstorms. \"You should resign ... and you should be criminally investigated,\" NPR quotes Warren as saying. Stumpf was in front of the Senate banking committee after Wells Fargo employees created millions of fake accounts for customers without their permission in order to charge them extra fees. Stumpf said he was \"deeply sorry\" for what happened, New Republic reports. But—according to CNBC—he maintained it wasn't a \"scam\"—Warren's word—but a \"way of deepening relationships\" with customers. Warren accused Stumpf of \"gutless leadership\" for passing responsibility for what happened away from himself and other senior executives while blaming \"thousands of $12-an-hour employees who were just trying to meet cross-sell quotas that made you rich.\" The employees' cross-selling scheme was at least partially responsible for Wells Fargo stock going up, making Stumpf's shares alone gain $200 million in value. While more than 5,000 Wells Fargo employees were fired, none of the senior executives are losing their jobs. Warren said the only way Wall Street will be reformed is if CEOs like Stumpf start seeing jail time. She's one of only a few members of the Senate banking committee that hasn't gotten money from Wells Fargo's PAC."], "length": 1931, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Summary of Current News**\n\n**Financial Scandal Rocks Wells Fargo**\n\nIn a heated Senate hearing, Senator Elizabeth Warren (D-MA) grilled Wells Fargo CEO John Stumpf over the bank's cross-selling scandal, which involved creating millions of fake accounts for customers. Warren called for Stumpf's resignation, the return of millions of dollars in bonuses, and a criminal investigation into the bank's executives. She accused Stumpf of \"gutless leadership\" and said that the only way to reform the financial industry is to hold executives accountable with jail time. The scandal has already led to the firing of 5,300 employees and a $185 million penalty for the bank.\n\n**Middle East Tensions Escalate**\n\nPresident Donald Trump is expected to announce that the United States will recognize Jerusalem as Israel's capital, a move that has been met with widespread criticism. The decision is seen as a compromise, as Trump will also sign a six-month waiver to delay the relocation of the US embassy to Jerusalem. The move is expected to spark violent protests and has been condemned by Pope Francis and the Palestinian representative to the UK. The decision comes amidst a broader shift in Middle East politics, with Saudi Arabia and the US growing closer to Israel and presenting a peace plan that favors Israeli interests.\n\n**Key Takeaways**\n\n* Wells Fargo CEO John Stumpf faces calls for resignation and a criminal investigation over the bank's cross-selling scandal.\n* Senator Elizabeth Warren accuses Stumpf of \"gutless leadership\" and says that executives should face jail time for massive frauds.\n* President Trump is expected to announce that the US will recognize Jerusalem as Israel's capital, sparking widespread criticism and protests.\n* The move is seen as a compromise, with Trump signing a six-month waiver to delay the relocation of the US embassy to Jerusalem.\n* The decision comes amidst a broader shift in Middle East politics, with Saudi Arabia and the US growing closer to Israel and presenting a peace plan that favors Israeli interests."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A hacker going by The Dark Overlord claims to have stolen and released 10 episodes of the new season of Orange Is the New Black more than a month before its official release on Netflix. Variety reports the hacker was demanding an unspecified ransom from Netflix, uploading the first episode of the upcoming fifth season to a file-sharing site Friday as proof. The next nine episodes were uploaded early Saturday morning after The Dark Overlord claims Netflix refused to pay them. The hacker says they're \"quite ashamed to breathe the same air\" as Netflix, as they were being entirely \"reasonable and merciful\" with their ransom offer. Neither Variety nor the AP have been able to—legally—confirm the authenticity of the episodes uploaded by The Dark Overlord. The new Orange Is the New Black episodes were apparently stolen months ago. Netflix says a production vendor \"had its security compromised\"; Variety identifies that vendor as Larson Studios, a post-production facility. The Dark Overlord says they weren't able to steal the final three episodes of the season because they weren't finished yet. The Dark Overlord told the AP in February they wouldn't release any of the stolen episodes; it's unclear why that changed. The hacker also claims to have stolen TV series from ABC, National Geographic, Fox, and IFC via the hack at Larson Studios. They're reportedly seeking a \"modest\" ransom for those as well. The leak of new Orange is the New Black episodes could hurt Neflix's subscriber numbers and stock price. The FBI is investigating."], "length": 1236, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Netflix Hacker Leaks \"Orange Is the New Black\" Season 5 Episodes**\n\nA hacker, operating under the pseudonym \"The Dark Overlord,\" has leaked the first 10 episodes of Netflix's hit series \"Orange Is the New Black\" season 5 on the Pirate Bay file-sharing site. The leak comes after the hacker claimed to have stolen the episodes from post-production studio Larson Studios in late 2016, and demanded an unspecified ransom from Netflix to prevent the full season from being released online.\n\nAccording to reports, the hacker had initially threatened to release the episodes unless Netflix paid a ransom, but the company refused to comply. The hacker then went ahead and released the episodes, which are scheduled to be officially released on June 9.\n\nThe leak is a significant blow to Netflix, which is counting on \"Orange Is the New Black\" to help it add 3.2 million subscribers from April to June. The company's stock price is also likely to be affected if the leak dents its subscriber growth.\n\nThe hacker, who has also claimed to have stolen episodes from other studios, including ABC, Fox, National Geographic, and IFC, has been identified as a cybercriminal who has been active in the online underworld for some time. The hacker's motives are unclear, but it is believed that the leak may be more about bragging rights in the cybercrime community than a genuine attempt to extort money from Netflix.\n\nNetflix has confirmed that a production vendor used by several major TV studios had its security compromised, and the company is working with law enforcement authorities to investigate the breach. The FBI is also involved in the investigation.\n\nThe leak is a significant development in the world of online piracy, and it remains to be seen how Netflix will respond to the situation. The company's stock price is likely to be closely watched in the coming days as the situation unfolds."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Forget satellite images and aerial searches—the best way to find Malaysia Airlines Flight MH370 may be with mathematical techniques dating back to the 18th century, the BBC reports. That's how Air France flight 447 was found in 2009, using \"Bayesian statistics\" to measure the probability of the plane being in one place or another. Named after Presbyterian minister and mathematician Thomas Bayes, the technique allowed experts to apply several factors to each point on a map: For example, what was the chance it crashed from mechanical failure? How far do planes tend to crash from their last known location? What was the chance that search teams missed debris in various locations? It's like picking a restaurant by balancing how full it is, what your favorite restaurant-review website says, and so on—except that experts hunting for Airbus A330 did that for each point where plane may have crashed in the Atlantic, Five Thirty-Eight notes. It was so hard that the US team of statisticians invited by France gave up, until they de-emphasized one statistic: that a plane's black box emits a signal after a crash 90% of the time. They changed their findings, and presto, the plane was found. Bayesian techniques have helped people find World War II U-boats, men overboard, and sunken treasure, but there's no evidence that Malaysia is employing them now. \"I suspect that they just guess, like professional baseball managers used to do before Moneyball,\" says a biostatistician."], "length": 3727, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**The Search for Malaysia Airlines Flight 370: A Statistical Approach**\n\nThe disappearance of Malaysia Airlines Flight 370 on March 8, 2014, remains one of the greatest aviation mysteries in history. Despite extensive searches, the location of the plane and the cause of its disappearance remain unknown. However, statistical tools, particularly Bayesian statistics, may hold the key to solving the mystery.\n\n**Bayesian Statistics: A Powerful Tool**\n\nBayesian statistics, named after 18th-century Presbyterian minister Thomas Bayes, is a mathematical approach that allows for the assessment of various scenarios and the combination of contradictory information. This method has been successfully used in the past to locate missing objects, including an Air France plane that disappeared in 2009.\n\n**The Air France Case Study**\n\nIn 2009, Air France Flight 447 disappeared while flying from Rio de Janeiro to Paris. Despite finding debris, the plane's location remained unknown. A team of statisticians from Metron Inc, led by Colleen Keller, was called in to help. Using Bayesian statistics, they created a probability map that narrowed down the search area to a 40-mile radius. Although the initial search was unsuccessful, the team revised their model and found the plane in 2011, solving the mystery of the crash.\n\n**Applying Bayesian Statistics to the Malaysia Airlines Case**\n\nThe same principles used in the Air France case study are being applied to the search for Malaysia Airlines Flight 370. However, the complexity of the case, including the new estimates of the plane's location and the mounting evidence of a deliberate act, makes the Bayesian calculations and estimates more challenging.\n\n**Challenges and Limitations**\n\nDespite the potential of Bayesian statistics, there are challenges and limitations to its application. The search area is vast, and the unpredictable ocean currents make it difficult to simulate the movement of debris. Additionally, the subjective nature of Bayesian statistics requires expert judgment and imagination, which can be a limitation.\n\n**Expert Opinions**\n\nExperts in the field have differing opinions on the use of Bayesian statistics in the search for Malaysia Airlines Flight 370. Some, like Tony O'Hagan, professor emeritus of probability and statistics at the University of Sheffield, believe that Bayesian techniques are the perfect solution for adapting to changing information. Others, like Bradley Efron, a Stanford University statistician, are skeptical, citing the limitations of Bayesian statistics in situations where new evidence drastically changes the situation.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe search for Malaysia Airlines Flight 370 is a complex and challenging task. While Bayesian statistics may hold the key"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Damascus has been rocked by bombings for the past two days, with at least two large blasts hitting Syria's military headquarters there this morning. The Free Syrian Army claimed responsibility for the attack, said to be the largest since July explosions killed President Bashar al-Assad's brother-in-law and other key aides; the BBC reports that Assad would have been able to hear today's explosions from his palace. Gunfire broke out afterward, and Maya Nasser, a TV correspondent for the Iranian Press TV, was shot dead by sniper fire during a live broadcast, report the New York Times and Press TV. Other government facilities were hit by bombs yesterday, proving the opposition can still get past government security, but the regime is downplaying the scope of the attacks while the opposition says many of Assad's forces have been killed. In other news from Syria: UN Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon called for international action at the General Assembly yesterday, calling the conflict \"a regional calamity with global ramifications,\" the AP reports. Click for more from the General Assembly on Syria. Clashes have also been reported near Syria's border with Israel, leading the Israel Defense Forces to issue a statement declaring that \"fire from Syria leaking into Israel will not be accepted.\""], "length": 1465, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Syrian Civil War Escalates: Twin Blasts Rock Damascus, Journalist Killed**\n\nThe Syrian civil war has taken a deadly turn as twin blasts rocked the capital city of Damascus, sparking hours of sporadic gunbattles and a raging fire at the heavily guarded army command headquarters. The explosions, which were heard several miles away, were followed by heavy gunfire and casualties on both sides.\n\nAccording to eyewitnesses, the blasts were caused by a car bomb and an explosive device, and were likely carried out by rebels fighting to topple President Bashar Assad's regime. The army command building was in flames, sending huge columns of thick black smoke over the city.\n\nThe blasts were the latest in a series of attacks on security sites and symbols of regime power, as rebels seek to turn the tide in the fighting. The conflict, which began in March 2011, has killed nearly 30,000 people and has become a regional calamity with global ramifications.\n\nIn a separate incident, Iranian journalist Maya Nasser, a correspondent for Press TV, was killed by a sniper while reporting on the twin blasts in Damascus. The network's Damascus Bureau Chief, Hosein Mortada, was also injured in the attack. Press TV has accused Turkey, Saudi Arabia, and Qatar of providing militants with weapons to kill civilians, military personnel, and journalists.\n\nThe Syrian government has blamed the blasts on \"terrorists\" and has vowed to pursue those responsible. However, the international community remains divided on how to address the crisis, with the UN Security Council paralyzed by Russian and Chinese vetoes of Western-backed resolutions.\n\nThe conflict has drawn attention from world leaders, including US President Barack Obama, who has pledged support for Syrians trying to oust Assad. UN Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon has called for international action to stop the war, but it remains unclear what steps will be taken to address the crisis."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A strong smell outside a California supermarket turned out to be someone who had decomposed in one of the store's entrance columns, ABC 7 reports. Police were called Saturday to the WinCo Foods in Lancaster after a plumber, told it was likely a septic problem, helped knock brick from the column and saw a shoe and a leg inside. \"It's been over 100 degrees up here every day,\" says LA County Sheriff's Lt. John Corina. \"I can't imagine being inside that column and just baking. It's a strange development, and if he died that way, it's a horrible way to die.\" Police say the victim may be a man who was stopped for a fake license plate Monday night, NBC Los Angeles reports. He crashed the vehicle, ran away from deputies, and got into the supermarket. The man \"ran up the steps where the managers [are],\" an eyewitness tells CBS Los Angeles. \"And somehow he got on the roof. Last I heard, he got away.\" From up there, he could have reached the store fascia and descended into the column. Whether he died from the fall or baked inside is unclear; he was found on his feet with an arm stuck behind his head. \"I saw some gooey liquid and it smelled really foul ... it was oozing out of the pillar onto the pavement,\" says a witness. \"It smelled like death.\""], "length": 1484, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Decomposing Body Found in Supermarket Column in Lancaster**\n\nOn Saturday, August 11, 2018, a decomposing body was discovered inside a brick pillar at a WinCo Foods supermarket in Lancaster, California. The body, later identified as 35-year-old Raymundo Rivera, was found by a plumber and a handyman who were called to investigate a strong odor coming from the column. The manager of the store had initially thought it was a sewage leak.\n\nAccording to investigators, the body is believed to be that of a suspect who led deputies on a foot chase on Monday, August 6, after a traffic stop. The suspect, who was driving with a fake license plate, crashed the car and escaped onto the roof of the supermarket. Deputies lost sight of the suspect, and it is believed that he may have fallen or become trapped in the column, where he remained for several days.\n\nThe Los Angeles County Sheriff's Department is leading the investigation, and detectives are working to determine the cause of death. An autopsy is pending, and the coroner's office will have to determine whether Rivera died from a fall, becoming trapped, or from other causes.\n\nThe discovery of the body has sent shockwaves through the community, with many customers expressing concern and sadness. The store was closed for several days as investigators worked to recover the body and process the scene.\n\nAnyone with information about the incident is encouraged to contact the Los Angeles County Sheriff's Department's Homicide Bureau at (323) 890-5500 or Crime Stoppers at (800) 222-TIPS (8477)."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The US has sent two warships to the Black Sea and put planes on standby in Europe as it prepares to evacuate its Olympians from Sochi in the event of an attack. But officials fear their preparations might not be enough, reports NBC News, which describes the effort as \"logistically mind-boggling and diplomatically delicate.\" The big issue: The US can't do much without Russian permission, and it doesn't expect to get that. \"Something that looks like the US cavalry riding to rescue Russia … well, it's just hard to imagine that happening,\" one Russian expert said. The US skiing and snowboarding teams have signed up their own private security company, Global Rescue, which claims it has six aircraft ready for an evacuation if necessary. But having planes isn't the only issue; it's having runways. Sochi's airport is fairly small, so deciding which of the 85 countries competing to use it would be a nightmare. Other teams are also taking special security precautions, the Moscow Times reports. Britain, for example, is sending extra security personnel with its delegation. A German spokesman, meanwhile, said the country trusted Russia, but would \"look closely\" at the situation. \"We will have no aircraft, that is all that I can say.\""], "length": 2600, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Sochi Winter Olympics Security Concerns**\n\nThe upcoming Sochi Winter Olympics have raised concerns about security, with fears of a potential terrorist attack. The US and other countries are taking precautions to ensure the safety of their athletes and visitors.\n\n**US Security Measures**\n\nThe US Olympic Committee has declined to discuss the details of security for American athletes, but has stated that the safety and security of Team USA is their top priority. The US government has sent FBI agents to Sochi and Moscow for the duration of the Games, and has made air and naval assets available to help Russia prevent possible terror attacks. The US ski and snowboard team has hired private security firm Global Rescue to protect its athletes in case of emergency. Global Rescue will have five aircraft on standby to assist the team in case of an evacuation.\n\n**Russian Security Measures**\n\nRussia has introduced extensive safeguards to ensure the security of the Games, including deploying 40,000 police officers and 70,000 soldiers to patrol the area. The Russian government has also raised the possibility of sharing bomb-detecting technology with the US, which would require American military personnel to be on the ground to help.\n\n**International Concerns**\n\nSeveral countries, including the US, Britain, and the Netherlands, are taking additional security measures to protect their athletes and visitors. Britain will send security staff to Sochi as part of its delegation, while the Netherlands will rely on the host country's authorities to provide security. Israel, which experienced tragedy at the 1972 Summer Olympics in Munich, is confident that Russian security agencies will do their job properly and that the Games will be safe.\n\n**Private Security Firms**\n\nPrivate security firms, such as Global Rescue, are also providing additional security to athletes and teams. Global Rescue has been hired by the US ski and snowboard team to provide security and evacuation services in case of an emergency.\n\n**Concerns and Fears**\n\nDespite the security measures in place, concerns and fears about a potential terrorist attack remain. The US has described the terror threat as higher than at any previous Olympics, and the FBI will have a presence in Sochi. The Russian government has also raised the possibility of a large-scale attack, which would trigger unimaginable complications.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe Sochi Winter Olympics are set to take place in a high-threat environment, with concerns about security and the potential for a terrorist attack. While the Russian government has introduced extensive safeguards to ensure the security of the Games, international concerns and fears remain. Private security firms, such as Global Rescue, are providing additional security to athletes"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A New Orleans judge officially approved Robert Durst's February plea deal on a gun charge and sentenced him to seven years in prison on Wednesday, Reuters reports. For more than a year, the real estate heir has languished in a New Orleans prison after being nabbed with a handgun in his hotel room. He was busted for that felony just one day before the series finale of HBO's The Jinx, in which he famously appeared to confess to three murders by saying, \"What the hell did I do? Killed them all, of course.\" US District Judge Kurt Engelhardt also agreed that Durst can be transferred to a low-security prison with medical facilities in San Pedro, Calif., where the 73-year-old's attorneys say his \"advanced age and poor health\" can be better addressed, per the Advocate. In California, Durst will face a murder charge in the 2000 death of friend Susan Berman, the AP reports. (A Texas judge believes Durst once dumped a cat's head on her doorstep.)"], "length": 2091, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Robert Durst Sentenced to 7 Years in Prison, to Serve Time in California**\n\nReal estate heir Robert Durst, 73, was sentenced to 7 years in prison on a federal gun charge in New Orleans on Wednesday. However, he will not be serving his time in Louisiana. U.S. District Judge Kurt Engelhardt agreed to recommend that Durst be transferred to a low-security federal prison in California, where he faces a murder charge in the 2000 death of his friend Susan Berman.\n\nDurst pleaded guilty to the gun charge in February and had been held in Louisiana since his arrest in March 2015. He was found to be in possession of a handgun and other items, including a latex mask and marijuana, in his hotel room. As part of his plea deal, Durst agreed to forfeit the items and will not face prosecution in New York, Texas, and Louisiana for related charges.\n\nDurst's attorneys have maintained his innocence in Berman's death and have requested that he be housed at FCI Terminal Island, a federal prison with medical facilities near Los Angeles. The prison is about 30 miles from downtown Los Angeles, where Durst will face trial in Berman's death.\n\nDurst's sentencing comes after a renewed interest in his case following the conclusion of the HBO documentary series \"The Jinx.\" The series chronicled several police investigations of Durst, including the dismemberment killing of a male neighbor in Texas in 2003 and the disappearance of his wife, Kathleen, in New York in 1982.\n\nDurst's attorney, Dick DeGuerin, said that the deal \"cleared the decks\" for Durst to focus on his trial in California. DeGuerin has maintained that Durst did not kill Berman and does not know who did.\n\nDurst is expected to be arraigned in Los Angeles on August 18 and will likely be transferred to California within a few weeks. His case has sparked widespread attention due to the allegations of murder and the high-profile nature of the case.\n\n**Key Facts:**\n\n* Robert Durst sentenced to 7 years in prison on a federal gun charge\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The Republican-led House Benghazi committee released its final report Tuesday after two years of investigations. The 800-page report lays out the committee's findings on the 2012 attacks in Libya that left four Americans dead. Here's what you need to know: Benghazi is \"unlikely to be a potent tool for weakening Clinton\" in the general election, as the committee and report have little credibility and aren't seen as serious by many, the Los Angeles Times reports. Easily distilling the massive report, the Washington Post lays out its five \"most serious accusations,\" including that the CIA \"missed warning signs\" and \"misread how dangerous Libya ... was at the time.\" New evidence of “culpability or wrongdoing” on Clinton's part is lacking in the report, despite it being “one of the longest, costliest, and most bitterly partisan congressional investigations in history,\" according to the New York Times. Given a lack of new evidence against Clinton, the Weekly Standard uses the report as a tool for blaming Obama. “The Obama administration knowingly provided the American people a false story about the Benghazi attack, its causes, and its consequences,\" the Standard states. How various government agencies responded to the Benghazi crisis is one of the more interesting aspects of the report. According to Politico, a conference call in the midst of the attack included a conversation about whether Marines should wear their uniforms if deployed. Marines changed in and out of their uniforms multiple times while waiting for an answer and were never deployed. According to the Hill, a Democratic lawmaker on the House Benghazi committee calls it \"one of the saddest exercises I've ever engaged in\" during 20 years in Congress. He accuses Republicans of hiding the final report from Democrats to avoid having it fact-checked. Zeroing in on what it calls the \"most revealing paragraph\" in the report, Vox concludes there's a pattern of spinning \"non-damning facts as damning\" for the Obama administration. The paragraph in question admits there were no US forces close enough to prevent the attack but insists that, in itself, was a failure on the part of the White House. \"I think it's time to move on,\" the AP quotes Clinton as saying after the release of the report, which she says includes nothing not previously discovered by an accountability board."], "length": 3608, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Benghazi Attack Investigation: New Details Emerge**\n\nThe House Benghazi Select Committee has released a final report on the 2012 attack on the US diplomatic compound in Benghazi, Libya, which killed four Americans, including Ambassador Chris Stevens. The report, which is 800 pages long, criticizes former Secretary of State Hillary Clinton and other officials for not adequately heeding concerns about growing extremism in Libya.\n\nThe report also accuses Clinton of falsely blaming the violence on an anti-Muslim video, which was used to justify the attack in other parts of the world. However, the report's findings do not alter the public's understanding of the attack, according to Rep. Elijah Cummings (D-MD), the ranking Democrat on the committee.\n\nCummings, who has been critical of the committee's partisan approach, said that the report is \"long past due\" and that the committee's work is finally coming to an end. He also accused the committee's chairman, Rep. Trey Gowdy (R-SC), of trying to prevent accountability for the panel's GOP lawmakers.\n\nThe report's findings are based on a two-year investigation, which cost $7 million in taxpayer funds. The investigation has been marked by controversy and partisanship, with Democrats accusing Republicans of trying to politicize the report.\n\n**Contrasting Public and Private Statements**\n\nThe report highlights the contrast between the public and private statements made by top Obama administration officials, including Secretary Clinton, in the aftermath of the attack. While Clinton and other officials publicly blamed the attack on an anti-Muslim video, private emails and documents reveal that they knew better.\n\nFor example, Clinton's call sheet for a call with the President of Libya on the night of the attack makes no mention of a protest or video, while her public statement at the time blamed the attack on an anti-Muslim video. Similarly, a private email from White House Advisor Benjamin Rhodes to the State Department press office on September 14, 2012, states that the protests were \"rooted in an Internet video, and not a broader failure of policy.\"\n\n**Marine Uniforms and Benghazi**\n\nThe report also reveals that State Department officials were concerned about the potential impact of Marine uniforms on the diplomatic mission in Benghazi. During an emergency conference call on the night of the attack, officials discussed whether Marines should wear civilian clothing instead of their uniforms, citing concerns that the uniforms could \"hurt diplomacy in the region.\"\n\nHowever, the report notes that the military never deployed to Benghazi, and that the clothing issue did not affect the speed or timeliness of any"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A former police bloodhound handler told a court on Monday that during the search for UVa student Hannah Graham, the animal detected her scent outside the car and apartment of suspect Jesse Matthew—and the scent of \"fear and adrenaline\" where she was allegedly attacked. The handler was testifying during a pretrial hearing where lawyers for Matthew, who is already serving three life terms in prison for a 2005 sexual assault, unsuccessfully challenged the search warrants police obtained for Matthew's home and vehicle, the AP reports. He said the 7-year-old dog, Shaker, traced Graham's scent from downtown Charlottesville to a mulch pile at an industrial site, where the smell of fear was strongest. Matthew, 33, faces a capital murder charge in the 2014 death of Graham, whose body was found on an abandoned property around six weeks after she disappeared. He has also been charged with the 2009 murder of a Virginia Tech student. At Monday's hearing, another police witness testified that, contrary to what the defense claimed, the scent trail was consistent with surveillance videos that showed Graham wandering around, reports WSET. The officer shared text message records indicating that she was lost the night she disappeared.The Washington Post notes that Matthew is seen walking alongside Graham in some of the surveillance videos."], "length": 1203, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Hannah Graham Case: New Details Emerge in Pretrial Hearing**\n\nA pretrial hearing for Jesse Matthew Jr., the man charged with killing University of Virginia student Hannah Graham, revealed new details about the investigation. Matthew, 34, is facing capital murder charges in Graham's 2014 disappearance and death, and prosecutors are seeking the death penalty.\n\nAccording to testimony from former Louisa County Sheriff's Office Detective Buck Garner, a police bloodhound named Shaker detected Graham's scent at various locations, including Matthew's apartment, car door, and an industrial site near downtown Charlottesville. The dog's findings provided the basis for police to obtain a search warrant for Matthew's apartment and vehicle.\n\nGarner testified that Shaker followed Graham's scent from the Downtown Mall to the industrial site, where the dog detected a scent indicating \"fear and adrenaline.\" The dog also found Graham's scent on the passenger door of Matthew's vehicle, in the doorway to his home, and near a dumpster at his apartment complex.\n\nThe defense argued that the search warrant was obtained through misstatements and reckless disregard of the truth, but Albemarle County Circuit Judge Cheryl Higgins ruled that there was no evidence of deliberate misstatements.\n\nMatthew also faces first-degree murder charges in the 2009 death of Virginia Tech student Morgan Harrington, and is serving a life prison term for a 2005 attempted murder and sexual assault in northern Virginia.\n\nThe hearing also revealed that Graham's text records showed that she was lost and wandering around the downtown area the night of her disappearance, and that there are surveillance videos of her in different areas, but with some unexplained gaps in time.\n\nThe case is ongoing, with a pretrial hearing scheduled for Monday. Matthew's defense is expected to continue challenging the search warrant and the evidence against him."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["A white-collar crook is facing extra prison time for having his son shoot him in the legs with a 20-gauge shotgun, the BBC reports. Shannon Egeland, 43, was already facing 10 years for taking part in a vast mortgage-fraud scheme when he had his 17-year-old son shoot him by a road in Caldwell, Idaho, in an attempt to avoid prison. \"The psychological and emotional destruction defendant caused this minor child is unimaginable,\" US attorney Scott Bradford wrote in a sentencing memo. Back in 2009, Egeland was co-owner of an Oregon development company that defrauded banks out of $20 million in loans that he and a partner used to fund a lavish lifestyle. Facing prison, Egeland took out disability insurance and had his son aim for his legs. Egeland told police he'd been attacked after stopping to assist a pregnant driver, but investigators got suspicious when his pricey BMW, his wallet, and his cellphone weren't taken, the Idaho Statesman reports. Then they learned about his recent insurance, and the whole thing unravelled. Now Egeland is facing an extra 3 years and 10 months for his roadside scheme and had his leg amputated from the shotgun blast. Bradford called him a \"self-absorbed, opportunistic narcissist,\" the Oregonian reports, but Egeland—who is now on three medications for mental health—has cast his own judgment. \"What bothers me the most is my son—the pain is on him,'' he says. \"If I could take it all back, I would, but I can't. That will haunt me the rest of my life.''"], "length": 1541, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Convicted White-Collar Criminal Sentenced to Additional Time for Staged Insurance Scam**\n\nShannon Egeland, a 43-year-old convicted white-collar criminal, was sentenced to an additional three years and 10 months in prison for his role in a staged insurance scam. Egeland, who was already serving a 10-year federal prison term for his involvement in a mortgage fraud case, had his teenage son shoot him in the leg in an attempt to delay his prison sentence and collect on a disability insurance policy.\n\nThe incident occurred on July 31, 2014, when Egeland's 17-year-old son shot him in the lower legs with a 20-gauge shotgun in Caldwell, Idaho. Egeland had applied for the disability insurance policy just a week earlier, falsely claiming he had not been arrested in the past decade. The scam was quickly detected, and Egeland was left with a permanent injury, requiring the amputation of his left leg.\n\nEgeland's defense team argued that he was suffering from a \"perfect storm\" of medical and mental health issues, as well as financial difficulties, at the time of the shooting. However, prosecutors described Egeland as a \"menace to society\" who had been on a crime spree since 2004 and had recklessly manipulated his son.\n\nIn court, Egeland expressed remorse for his actions, stating that the pain and guilt of his actions would haunt him for the rest of his life. He also acknowledged that his son had suffered greatly as a result of his actions, saying \"The pain is on him. If I could take it all back, I would, but I can't.\"\n\nEgeland's sentence was handed down by U.S. District Judge Anna J. Brown, who also ordered him to pay $90,011 in back child support. The judge urged Egeland to seek mental health treatment and counseling while in prison and to submit to a new mental health evaluation upon his release.\n\nEgeland's case highlights the severity of his crimes, which included a mortgage fraud scheme that raised millions from local banks through bogus business plans and falsified loan applications. His actions have had a profound impact on his son, who was forced to participate in the staged shooting, and on the community, which was victimized by his crimes."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The Tesla Model X that slammed into a highway barrier in California last week and killed the 38-year-old Apple engineer at the wheel had been on autopilot at the time of the crash, Tesla confirms in a blog post. But the company says driver Walter Huang \"received several visual and one audible hands-on warning earlier in the drive,\" adding that \"the driver’s hands were not detected on the wheel for six seconds prior to the collision.\" Tesla also laid some blame on state highway officials, asserting that \"the reason this crash was so severe is because the crash attenuator, a highway safety barrier which is designed to reduce the impact into a concrete lane divider, had been crushed in a prior accident without being replaced.\" The fact that the vehicle was on autopilot is likely to be controversial because, as ABC News notes, driver Huang had reportedly been concerned about it. His brother told KGO that Huang had brought his Model X back to the dealership complaining that when he used the feature, the car kept swerving toward the very same barrier that it eventually struck in the fatal collision. The NTSB is investigating the crash, including the fire that broke out after the collision. The accident follows another in Arizona in which a self-driving Uber struck and killed a pedestrian."], "length": 2070, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Tesla Autopilot Crash: Investigation Ongoing**\n\nA fatal crash involving a Tesla Model X on March 23rd has sparked a heated debate about the safety of autonomous vehicles. The driver, 38-year-old Walter Huang, an Apple engineer, died when his vehicle slammed into a barrier on the 101 Highway near Mountain View, California. The National Transportation Safety Board (NTSB) is investigating the accident, which occurred while the vehicle was on Autopilot.\n\n**Tesla's Response**\n\nTesla has released a statement acknowledging that the vehicle was on Autopilot at the time of the crash. The company claims that the severity of the accident was due to the barrier, which was not repaired after a previous accident. Tesla's statement also highlights the safety record of its Autopilot system, citing data that shows Tesla owners are 3.7 times less likely to be involved in a fatal accident.\n\n**Investigation Findings**\n\nAccording to investigators, the driver had received several visual and one audible hands-on warning earlier in the drive, but the driver's hands were not detected on the wheel for six seconds prior to the collision. The vehicle logs show that the driver had about five seconds and 150 meters of unobstructed view of the barrier, but no action was taken.\n\n**Family's Concerns**\n\nHuang's family has come forward, stating that he had complained to the dealership about the vehicle's Autopilot system veering towards the same barrier on multiple occasions. His brother, Will, said that Walter had taken the vehicle to the dealership seven to 10 times to address the issue, but they were unable to duplicate the problem.\n\n**CalTrans Response**\n\nThe California Department of Transportation (CalTrans) has released a statement saying that replacement work was scheduled for the barrier, but was delayed due to weather conditions. The previous accident that damaged the barrier took place 11 days before Huang's fatal crash.\n\n**Autonomous Vehicle Safety**\n\nThe crash has raised concerns about the safety of autonomous vehicles. Tesla has defended its Autopilot system, citing data that shows it makes the world safer for vehicle occupants, pedestrians, and cyclists. However, the incident has sparked a renewed debate about the risks and benefits of autonomous vehicles.\n\n**Investigation Continues**\n\nThe NTSB is still investigating the accident, and it is unclear what caused the vehicle to crash into the barrier. The investigation is ongoing, and it is expected to take several weeks to determine the cause of the accident."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["After Robin Williams' sudden death, the tributes are rolling in, with many recalling a comic genius grappling with private challenges. A sampling: When it comes to comedy, \"there wasn't a faster brain on the planet,\" writes Tony Hicks in the Contra Costa Times. We'll be hearing \"a litany of tributes about one of the great comedians who ever lived. Much of it will be some of the same adjectives used when any great performer dies—especially before their time. But in Williams' case, most of it will be true.\" Early in his career, \"it was clear that Mr. Williams was one of the most explosively, exhaustingly, prodigiously verbal comedians who ever lived,\" AO Scott writes in the New York Times. And beyond that, \"his essential persona as an entertainer combined neediness and generosity, intelligence and kindness, in ways that were charming and often unexpectedly moving.\" Williams may have gotten his start long before Aladdin, but he gave a \"generous and humble\" gift to the millennial generation, writes Daniel D'Addario at Salon. \"Williams will be remembered for his edgy comedy and for dramatic turns, but for people my age, Williams was our childhood,\" from Mrs. Doubtfire to Jumanji to Flubber. In the Los Angeles Times, Kenneth Turan recalls a moment when, facing an onslaught of photographers, Williams asked him—in the voice of a fly from a 1950s sci-fi flick—to \"help me, Ken. Help me.\" The experience stuck with him and seems particularly resonant today, he writes."], "length": 1921, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Robin Williams: A Legacy of Joy and Genius**\n\nRobin Williams, the beloved actor and comedian, has left an indelible mark on the entertainment industry. At the age of 63, Williams passed away, leaving behind a legacy of joy, laughter, and unforgettable performances. Born to please, Williams was a master of his craft, effortlessly shifting between comedy and drama, and captivating audiences of all ages.\n\n**A Career Spanning Decades**\n\nWilliams' career spanned over four decades, with a remarkable range of films that showcased his incredible talent. From his early days as a stand-up comedian to his Oscar-winning performances in \"Good Will Hunting,\" Williams was a true original. His filmography includes iconic movies such as \"Good Morning, Vietnam,\" \"Dead Poets Society,\" \"Aladdin,\" and \"Mrs. Doubtfire,\" which cemented his status as a beloved family entertainer.\n\n**A Connection with His Audience**\n\nWilliams' unique ability to connect with his audience was a hallmark of his career. He had a special gift for making his characters relatable and endearing, often playing roles that were both humorous and poignant. His performances in films like \"Hook\" and \"Jumanji\" showcased his ability to tap into the imagination of children, while his dramatic turns in \"The Fisher King\" and \"Good Will Hunting\" demonstrated his range as a serious actor.\n\n**A Complex and Restless Personality**\n\nThose who knew Williams described him as a complex and restless individual, always seeking new experiences and knowledge. His stand-up comedy was a testament to his quick wit and improvisational skills, which he honed through years of performing at the Comedy Store and other venues. Williams' ability to think on his feet and respond to situations with humor and creativity was a hallmark of his comedy style.\n\n**A Final Farewell**\n\nAs news of Williams' passing spread, fans and colleagues alike were left to mourn the loss of a true original. His death was met with shock and sadness, with many remembering his incredible talent and generosity of spirit. Williams' legacy will live on through his films, which continue to entertain and inspire audiences around the world. His impact on the entertainment industry will be felt for generations to come, and his memory will be cherished by all who knew him."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Police in the country's smallest state capital are investigating something that hasn't happened there in living memory: a murder. Police in Vermont say they have issued an arrest warrant for 29-year-old Jayveon Caballero, who is accused of fatally shooting Markus Austin in Montpelier early Sunday after an altercation, the Times Argus reports. Witnesses told investigators that Austin, 33, assaulted Caballero’s girlfriend, Desiree Cary, during the altercation outside a bar in the nearby city of Barre early Sunday. Police say Caballero later confronted Austin outside his apartment and shot him dead. The body was found in the parking lot of Austin's apartment complex. A Vermont State Police spokesman says Caballero, who knew Austin before the shooting, fled the town and probably the state after the shooting, WCAX reports. The police spokesman says Caballero is believed to have boarded a Greyhound bus bound for New York City on Sunday morning, which made numerous stops in other states along the way. Later Sunday, Cary, 22, was arrested on apparently unrelated charges of selling heroin and crack cocaine. With around 7,500 residents, Montpelier has the smallest population of any state capital, the AP notes. Police Chief Anthony Facos says this is the city's first murder since a woman shot her husband in the 1920s."], "length": 1264, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Murder Suspect on the Run in Montpelier, Vermont**\n\nA murder suspect, Jayveon Caballero, 29, is still at large after allegedly shooting and killing a former Vermont Frost Heaves basketball player, Markus Austin, 33, in Montpelier, Vermont. The shooting occurred outside an apartment complex in the early hours of Sunday morning, and police have been searching for Caballero ever since.\n\nAccording to police, Caballero and Austin had been involved in an altercation outside a bar in Barre earlier that morning, during which Austin allegedly assaulted Caballero's girlfriend, Desiree Cary, 22. Caballero then confronted Austin outside his apartment and shot him, before fleeing the scene.\n\nPolice have issued a second-degree murder arrest warrant for Caballero, with a bail set at $500,000. They believe he may have boarded a Greyhound bus in White River Junction on Sunday morning, headed for New York City, but it is unclear if he got off at a stop before that.\n\nCary, who was the target of an ongoing drug distribution investigation, was taken into custody on Sunday evening and has been held in custody on charges of selling crack cocaine and heroin. She is scheduled to be arraigned in court today.\n\nThe shooting is the first murder in Montpelier in almost a century, with the city's police chief stating that the last murder in the city occurred in the 1920s. The investigation is ongoing, and police are asking anyone with information about Caballero's whereabouts to come forward.\n\n**Key Facts:**\n\n* Jayveon Caballero, 29, is wanted for second-degree murder in connection with the shooting death of Markus Austin, 33.\n* Caballero and Austin had been involved in an altercation outside a bar in Barre earlier on Sunday morning.\n* Desiree Cary, 22, Caballero's girlfriend, was taken into custody on Sunday evening on charges unrelated to the shooting.\n* Caballero may have boarded a Greyhound bus in White River Junction on Sunday morning, headed for New York City.\n* A second-degree murder arrest warrant has been issued for Caballero, with a bail set at $500,000.\n* The shooting is the first murder in Montpelier in almost a century."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Look up at the sky on Jan. 31 and you may witness a sight unseen for 150 years. For the first time since 1866, a total lunar eclipse will occur with the blue moon, or second full moon of the month, which—like the one visible on New Year's Day—will also be a supermoon. Got all that? When the moon is at or near the closest point to Earth in its orbit (aka, a supermoon), it appears 14% bigger and 30% brighter than full moons that occur at the farthest point in the moon's orbit, though the moon will lose brightness as it enters Earth's shadow, according to NASA. Because of the way Earth's atmosphere bends light, the eclipsed moon will take on a reddish hue, something often referred to as a blood moon, making this rare celestial event \"a super blue blood moon eclipse,\" according to the Miami Herald. The next blue moon eclipse won't occur until 2028. The eclipse will last for 3.5 hours, with the total eclipse stretching for 77 minutes, reports Sky & Telescope. Those along the Pacific Rim from Alaska and northwestern Canada to central and eastern Asia, Indonesia, New Zealand, and Australia will get the best view of totality during the middle of the night. However, partial phases of the eclipse should be visible under a clear sky in central North America, western Asia, India, the Middle East and eastern Europe, per Space.com. North American viewers should look up as the moon sets on the morning of Jan. 31, while those on the opposite side of the world should see a partial eclipse as the moon rises. Not in an ideal viewing area? The Virtual Telescope plans to livestream the total eclipse from Australia, which you can watch here, per Quartz. (Bogus glasses damaged a solar eclipse viewer's eyes.)"], "length": 2076, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Rare Celestial Event: Super Blue Blood Moon Total Eclipse on January 31, 2018**\n\nA rare and spectacular celestial event is set to occur on January 31, 2018, when a total lunar eclipse, also known as a Super Blue Blood Moon, will be visible from eastern Asia to western North America. This event is a combination of a blue moon, a supermoon, and a total lunar eclipse, making it a once-in-a-lifetime experience for skywatchers.\n\n**What is a Blue Moon?**\nA blue moon occurs when there are two full moons in a single month, which happens about once every 2.7 years. The second full moon in a month is called a blue moon.\n\n**What is a Supermoon?**\nA supermoon is a full moon that occurs when the moon is at its closest point to Earth, making it appear 14% bigger and 30% brighter than a regular full moon.\n\n**What is a Total Lunar Eclipse?**\nA total lunar eclipse occurs when the Earth passes between the sun and the moon, casting a shadow on the moon's surface. During this event, the moon will take on a reddish hue, earning it the nickname \"blood moon.\"\n\n**When and Where to See the Event**\nThe total lunar eclipse will begin at 11:48am UTC on January 31, 2018, and reach its maximum at 1:30pm UTC. The event will be visible from eastern Asia to western North America, with the best viewing times in eastern Asia, Indonesia, New Zealand, and Australia.\n\n**How to Watch the Event**\nThe Virtual Telescope project will provide a livestream of the event from Australia, starting at 11:30am UTC. Viewers can also watch the event with the naked eye, without any special glasses.\n\n**Tips for Photographing the Event**\nNASA's senior photographer Bill Ingalls recommends using a DSLR camera with a daylight white balance to capture the event. He also suggests putting the moon in context with a local landmark to create an original shot.\n\n**Rare Occurrence**\nThis event is a rare occurrence, with the next total lunar eclipse of a blue moon happening on December 31, 2028, and the next one after that on January 31, 2037. The last time a blue moon passed through the Earth's umbra was on March 31, 1866.\n\n**Get Ready for a Spectacular Show**\nThe Super Blue Blood Moon Total Eclipse on January 31, 201"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["America's ambassador to Syria visited one of the cities worst-hit by the crackdown against anti-government protesters yesterday, reports CNN. Robert Ford went to the city of Hama \"to make absolutely clear with his physical presence that we stand with those Syrians who are expressing their right to speak for change,\" an embassy spokeswoman says. An estimated 500,000 people joined an anti-government rally in the city last Friday. The Syrian government accused Ford of going to the city without authorization, saying the visit is \"clear evidence of the US involvement in the ongoing events in Syria and its bids to aggravate the situations which destabilize Syria.'' An embassy spokeswoman says the Syrian government was told a delegation would be visiting Hama, although no Syrian \"handlers\" were taken and officials weren't informed about the ambassador's presence. The city has been a symbol of opposition since 1982, when a crackdown ordered by the present Syrian leader's father killed around 20,000 people, al-Jazeera notes."], "length": 1397, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Syria Protests: US Ambassador Visits Hama, Thousands Demonstrate Against Government**\n\nThe situation in Syria continues to escalate as thousands of protesters took to the streets in the city of Hama, with reports of 13 deaths and over 500,000 people participating in the largest protest yet against the government of President Bashar al-Assad. The protests, which were sparked by a series of peaceful demonstrations, have been met with a fierce crackdown by security forces, resulting in numerous arrests and deaths.\n\nIn a show of support for the protesters, US Ambassador to Syria Robert Ford visited Hama on Thursday, meeting with residents and visiting a hospital that has treated those wounded in the security forces' crackdown. Ford's visit was seen as a significant gesture of solidarity, with the US State Department stating that it was an effort to \"make absolutely clear with his physical presence that we stand with those Syrians who are expressing their right to speak for change.\"\n\nHowever, the Syrian government has accused the US of interfering in its affairs, with the Foreign Ministry stating that Ford's visit was \"clear evidence of the U.S. involvement in the ongoing events in Syria and its bids to aggravate the situations which destabilize Syria.\" The US has denied these allegations, with a State Department spokesperson stating that the US had notified the Syrian government of Ford's visit and that he was allowed to proceed through checkpoints.\n\nThe protests in Hama have been met with a significant response from the international community, with diplomats from the US and France visiting the city to show support for the protesters. The French ambassador, Eric Chevallier, was also in Hama on Friday to show solidarity with the residents.\n\nThe situation in Hama has been described as \"tangible example of resistance to injustice\" by an activist, with the city becoming a symbol of the opposition movement against the government. The protests have also been marked by reports of violence, with security forces firing on protesters and conducting mass arrests.\n\nIn other developments, the Syrian Observatory for Human Rights has reported that an estimated 1,000 Syrians have fled Hama in fear of another military crackdown, with many seeking refuge in the town of Salamiyah. The rights group has also reported that security forces have killed at least 23 civilians in Salamiyah since Tuesday.\n\nThe situation in Syria continues to deteriorate, with reports of violence and protests spreading to other cities, including Hasrata just outside Damascus. The international community is calling for an end to the violence and for the government to respect the rights of its citizens to peaceful protest."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The FBI is reportedly investigating a sophisticated—but unsuccessful—attempt to hack into the Democratic National Committee's voter database. The DNC learned of the attempt on Tuesday, CNN reports. The attack, which was detected by a cloud service provider and a security research company, involved a fake login page intended to gather usernames and passwords for a service called Votebuilder, which hosts the DNC's voter database. It's a method called \"spearphishing,\" according to the New York Times. \"It was very convincing,\" Mike Murray of the cybersecurity firm Lookout tells CNN, adding that it would be tough to tell the real page from the fake one, even if they were side-by-side. The fake login page was ultimately removed by the cloud hosting company DigitalOcean. The company's chief security officer says the threat was identified before the attack was launched. \"We see no evidence that any sensitive data was stolen,\" he says. A DNC official tells the Times that it is not clear who the hackers were in this case. But, the paper recalls, a 2016 hack of the DNC was ultimately traced to Russia. This latest attempt, DNC Chief Security Officer Bob Lord tells the Times, \"is further proof that there are constant threats as we head into midterm elections and we must remain vigilant in order to prevent future attacks.\" Earlier this week, Microsoft reported that it had uncovered new Russian hacking attempts targeting conservative organizations in the US."], "length": 716, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Election Security Threats on the Rise: FBI Investigates Attempt to Hack Democratic National Committee's Voter Database**\n\nAs the US heads into the midterm elections, concerns about election security and foreign interference have reached a fever pitch. The Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) is currently investigating an incident in which a sophisticated attempt was made to hack into the Democratic National Committee's (DNC) voter database. The incident, which was initially thought to be a real attack, was later revealed to be an unauthorized \"simulated phishing test\" conducted by an unknown entity.\n\nThe attempted hack, which was detected by a cloud service provider and a security research firm, involved the creation of a fake login page designed to trick users into handing over their login details. The page was designed to look like the access page used by Democratic Party officials and campaigns to log into the Votebuilder service, which hosts the database.\n\nThe incident has raised concerns about the vulnerability of election systems and the potential for foreign and domestic manipulation. Microsoft has reported that hackers tied to Russian intelligence have targeted the Senate and conservative think tanks in the US, creating fake websites and engaging in influence operations. Facebook has also identified disinformation efforts targeting people around the world.\n\nExperts warn that the cyber age has changed elections forever, with many systems vulnerable to manipulation. \"The focus has gone from using the cyber realm to steal secrets to using it to impose national power,\" said Rob Joyce, the former White House cybersecurity coordinator.\n\nThe incident has prompted the FBI to issue a warning about the need for vigilance in the lead-up to the midterms. \"This attempt is further proof that there are constant threats as we head into midterm elections, and we must remain vigilant in order to prevent future attacks,\" said Bob Lord, chief security officer for the Democratic National Committee.\n\nAs the election season heats up, concerns about election security and foreign interference are likely to continue to dominate the headlines."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["Another fatality in the Boston bombings has been identified: The family of 29-year-old Krystle Campbell says she was killed as she and a friend were cheering on another friend who was racing, reports WCVB. \"This is just a waste,\" says her mother. “Everyone that knew her loved her. She was always smiling. You couldn’t ask for a better daughter. She was the best.” Campbell was a restaurant manager, reports the Boston Herald, which notes an especially wrenching detail: Her father was initially told she was alive, but when he got to the ICU, he realized it was his daughter's friend who had survived. Campbell is the second of the three people killed to be identified. The first was 8-year-old Martin Richard, and his father, Bill, released a statement today thanking supporters and asking for privacy, reports WBZ. \"My dear son Martin has died from injuries sustained in the attack on Boston. My wife and daughter are both recovering from serious injuries. We thank our family and friends, those we know and those we have never met, for their thoughts and prayers. I ask that you continue to pray for my family as we remember Martin. We also ask for your patience and for privacy as we work to simultaneously grieve and recover. Thank you.\" The third victim was a grad student at Boston University, but the person's identity is being withheld until the family says it's OK to release, reports USA Today."], "length": 2030, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Boston Marathon Bombing: A Tragic Day in American History**\n\nOn Monday, April 15, 2013, two explosions rocked the finish line of the Boston Marathon, killing three people and injuring hundreds more. The blasts, which occurred just before 3 p.m., sent bloodied spectators to a medical tent and brought the city to a standstill.\n\n**Victims Identified**\n\nThe victims of the bombing have been identified as:\n\n* Martin Richard, an 8-year-old boy from Dorchester, who was watching the race with his family.\n* Krystle Campbell, a 29-year-old restaurant manager from Medford, who was waiting near the finish line to cheer on a friend.\n* A Boston University graduate student from China, whose name has not been released pending family approval.\n\n**Family Tragedies**\n\nThe Richard family suffered a devastating loss, with Martin's mother and sister seriously injured in the blasts. The family has been receiving an outpouring of support from the community, with neighbors and strangers leaving flowers and balloons on the front steps of their home.\n\nKrystle Campbell's family was also dealt a devastating blow, with her parents initially being told that she had survived the bombing, only to later learn that she had died. Her mother, Patricia, spoke publicly about her grief, saying that her daughter was a \"very caring, very loving person\" who was \"daddy's little girl.\"\n\n**Response and Investigation**\n\nThe FBI has taken over the investigation into the bombing, with officials saying that there are no known additional threats. The city has increased security at bus and train stations and airports, and Mayor Thomas Menino has vowed that Boston will not be intimidated by terror.\n\nPresident Obama praised the first responders and others who helped the injured, saying that the response to the bombing was a testament to American values. Pope Francis also sent a message of condolence to the city, praying for the dead and the suffering.\n\n**Community Comes Together**\n\nIn the aftermath of the bombing, the community has come together to support the victims and their families. The Salvation Army has mobilized mobile kitchens and volunteers to provide food and emotional support, while the First Corps of Cadets Armory has become a staging area for runners looking for their gear bags and trying to make travel arrangements.\n\nAs the city begins to heal and move forward, the people of Boston have shown strength, compassion, and determination to see justice done."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["There are more obese or overweight people in the world today than there were people of any weight in 1935, the most comprehensive look at worldwide obesity in decades warns. The 188-country study found that there are 2.1 billion overweight or obese people in the world, making up around 30% of the world's population, up from 20% in 1980, USA Today reports. Not a single country has recorded a decline in obesity over the last 30 years. \"We hoped there would be some examples of success that you could latch onto,\" a study co-author says. \"But there's a complete lack of success stories in bringing down obesity.\" The study published in British medical journal the Lancet found that the Middle East and north Africa had seen the biggest weight gains, though the US still has the largest number of obese people, with 87 million of the world's 671 million obese people, reports Reuters. Other countries, however, have even higher obesity rates, including Tonga, where more than 50% of all adults are obese. \"Two-thirds of the obese population actually resides in developing countries,\" says one of the researchers; they warn that obesity rates are rising among rich and poor countries, men and women, and adults and children alike."], "length": 1409, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Global Obesity Epidemic: A Growing Concern**\n\nA recent study published in the Lancet medical journal has revealed a staggering 2.1 billion people worldwide are either obese or overweight, accounting for nearly 30% of the global population. The study, which covered 188 countries from 1980 to 2013, found that obesity is a growing problem worldwide, affecting both rich and poor nations.\n\n**The United States: A Leader in Obesity**\n\nThe United States leads the world in obesity, with 13% of the global obese population, despite having only 5% of the world's population. The country has seen a significant increase in obesity rates, with about one-third of adults being overweight and another third being obese.\n\n**Global Obesity Rates**\n\nThe study found that obesity rates are rising in both developed and developing countries. In the Middle East and North Africa, more than 58% of adult men and 65% of adult women are overweight or obese. Other countries with high obesity rates include:\n\n* Bahrain, Egypt, Saudi Arabia, Oman, and Kuwait, where more than 50% of adults are overweight or obese\n"}
{"input": "", "answers": ["It's been a year since Katie Holmes filed for divorce from Tom Cruise, and apparently Cruise is feeling sentimental. A source tells UK magazine Look the actor sent Holmes an \"emotional letter\" looking to, at the very least, be friends again, Australia's News Network reports. \"It's like he's finally recognized that Katie will always be the love of his life,\" the source says. Even so, \"It won't have been an over the top letter—it has been a year and I'm sure Tom will have been aware that Katie wouldn't be interested in running straight back into his arms.\" Sources close to Holmes say she was \"touched\" by the letter, which doesn't sound promising for Cruise. \"Tom would no doubt take her back in a second, but I don't think that's what Katie wants,\" says one. (Just to keep things in perspective, we'll point out that Look is the sort of magazine that also runs headlines like, \"Royal Bodyguard Warns: 'Kate Middleton's Baby Is In Danger!'\") In other news, Huffington Post notes that it's Cruise's 51st birthday."], "length": 790, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Weekly News Summary**\n\nThis week's news is filled with exciting stories from the world of entertainment, technology, and lifestyle.\n\n**Celebrity News**\n\nTom Cruise has reportedly written an emotional letter to his ex-wife Katie Holmes, in an attempt to rekindle their friendship and possibly more. The letter, which was written after 12 months of separation, is said to have been a shock to Holmes, but has reportedly had a positive effect on her. Sources close to the former couple claim that Holmes was \"genuinely touched\" by Cruise's words, but it's unlikely that she will take him back. The couple divorced in July last year after Holmes filed for divorce.\n\n**Fashion and Lifestyle**\n\nThis week's LOOK magazine is on sale now, featuring the latest fashion trends and must-have buys. The magazine also includes an exclusive interview with Kristen Wiig, who apologizes for her wild past. Additionally, the world's top make-up artist reveals A-listers' secrets, and there's a warning from a royal bodyguard that Kate Middleton's baby is in danger.\n\n**Technology**\n\nIn a separate story, Alexa Internet has been donating their crawl data to the Internet Archive since 1996. The data, which flows in every day, is added to the Wayback Machine after an embargo period. This is a significant development in the world of technology, highlighting the importance of preserving online data for future generations.\n\n**Other News**\n\nThere's also a warning about cyber spies, who may be watching you through your computer. The article advises readers to be cautious and take steps to protect themselves online.\n\nOverall, this week's news is filled with exciting stories from the world of entertainment, technology, and lifestyle. From celebrity news to fashion trends and technological advancements, there's something for everyone."}
{"input": "", "answers": ["The investigation into the mysterious deaths of a billionaire and his wife is now being led by homicide detectives, police say—suggesting the deaths of Barry and Honey Sherman were either murder-suicide or a double murder. Police, who have described the deaths as \"suspicious,\" revealed Sunday that the cause of death for both Shermans was \"ligature neck compression,\" a form of strangulation, the CBC reports. Sources have told the media that the couple were found hanging near an indoor pool in the basement of their Toronto mansion. Police say they haven't found any sign of forced entry, or a note that could explain what happened to 75-year-old pharmaceutical tycoon Barry Sherman and his 70-year-old wife, both renowned philanthropists. The couple's four children have rejected what they say are \"rumors\" that the deaths are being investigated as a murder-suicide, and many of the influential couple's friends agree. \"There is absolutely zero debate in my mind, this was a double homicide,\" says Canadian Sen. Linda Frum, a longtime friend of the couple, per the New York Times. The Globe and Mail reports that just days before the bodies were found, Honey Sherman was enthusiastically making plans for their annual trip to Florida, emailing friends that she would be traveling to the state on Dec. 18 and Barry Sherman would join her a week later. The couple also sent out invitations last week to a dinner party at their winter home in Palm Beach."], "length": 2360, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**Canadian Billionaire Barry Sherman and Wife Honey Found Dead in Toronto Mansion**\n\nBarry Sherman, the 75-year-old founder of generic pharmaceuticals giant Apotex Inc., and his 70-year-old wife Honey were found dead in their Toronto mansion on Friday. The couple's bodies were discovered by a real estate agent who had been helping to sell their North York home, which was recently listed for $6.9 million.\n\nAccording to police, the cause of death for both Barry and Honey Sherman was ligature neck compression, meaning they were strangled. The homicide squad is now leading the investigation, and police have deemed the deaths \"suspicious.\"\n\nThe couple's family has released a statement expressing shock and disappointment at the rumors circulating in the media about the circumstances surrounding their deaths. They stated that their parents shared an enthusiasm for life and commitment to their family and community, and that the rumors of a murder-suicide are \"totally inconsistent\" with their parents' personalities.\n\nBarry Sherman was a renowned workaholic who devoted almost all of his time and energy to Apotex, frequently sleeping for only a few hours a night. He had a net worth of $4.77 billion, making him the 15th-richest person in Canada. Honey Sherman, on the other hand, was a dedicated philanthropist who was involved in various charitable causes, including the Jewish community.\n\nFriends and colleagues of the couple have described them as kind and generous, and have expressed shock and disbelief at the news of their deaths. Toronto Mayor John Tory and Prime Minister Justin Trudeau have also paid tribute to the couple, praising Barry Sherman's contributions to the city and his business career.\n\nAs the investigation continues, police are still tight-lipped about the details of the case, but friends and family are coming to terms with the loss of a couple who were known for their kindness, generosity, and commitment to their community."}
