{"input": "", "answers": ["Police in Vermont say a car ended up almost vertical when the driver swerved quickly in response to her GPS ordering her to \"turn around.\" The car was suspended almost vertically on guide wires attached to a utility pole (see a photo here) in Mendon on Wednesday night, reports the AP. Police say 30-year-old Nabila Altahan of Dorchester, Mass., was headed west on US Route 4 when she passed her intended destination and reacted quickly to the instructions, leaving the road at a significant enough speed to propel the vehicle up the wires. She wasn't injured. (This driver followed her GPS into a lake.)"], "length": 310, "dataset": "multi_news", "pred": "**LOCAL NEWS SUMMARY**\n\n**CAR SUSPENDED FROM TELEPHONE POLE IN MENDON**\n\nA bizarre incident occurred in Mendon on Wednesday evening when a car was suspended from a telephone guy wire after the driver reacted too quickly to a GPS unit's instructions. Nabila Altahan, the driver of a Toyota Corolla, was heading westbound on Route 4 when her GPS device suddenly directed her to turn around, as she had passed her intended destination. Altahan's quick reaction to the directions caused her to leave the road, and the car ended up suspended from the guy wire of a telephone pole across from Sugar & Spice Restaurant.\n\nFortunately, neither Altahan nor her passenger was injured in the incident. State Police and the Rutland City Fire Department responded to the scene, and a witness at the nearby miniature golf course reported seeing the driver jump out of the car as it was suspended from the pole.\n\nThis unusual incident serves as a reminder to drivers to exercise caution when following GPS directions, and to take a moment to consider the consequences of sudden actions on the road.\n\n**NO OTHER NEWS REPORTED**"}
